const DATA = {"modules":[{"id":11,"title":"Managing Support Procedures","lessons":["11A Documentation","11B Professional Communication","11C Operating Systems"],"icon":"fa-clipboard-list","color":"from-blue-500 to-indigo-600"},{"id":12,"title":"Configuring Windows","lessons":["12A Windows User Settings","12B Windows System Settings","12C Applications","12D Cloud Apps"],"icon":"fa-sliders","color":"from-sky-500 to-blue-600"},{"id":13,"title":"Managing Windows","lessons":["13A Management Consoles","13B Command-Line Tools","13C Windows Networking"],"icon":"fa-terminal","color":"from-emerald-500 to-teal-700"},{"id":14,"title":"Supporting Windows","lessons":["14A Network Troubleshooting","14B Remote Access","14C Performance Tools","14D OS Troubleshooting"],"icon":"fa-screwdriver-wrench","color":"from-amber-500 to-orange-600"},{"id":15,"title":"Securing Windows","lessons":["15A Logical Security","15B Windows Security Settings","15C Windows Shares"],"icon":"fa-shield-halved","color":"from-purple-500 to-indigo-700"},{"id":16,"title":"Installing Operating Systems","lessons":["16A Windows Editions","16B OS Installations & Upgrades"],"icon":"fa-hard-drive","color":"from-cyan-500 to-blue-600"},{"id":17,"title":"Supporting Other OS","lessons":["17A Linux Features","17B Package & Network Mgmt","17C macOS Features"],"icon":"fa-brands fa-linux","color":"from-amber-600 to-yellow-500"},{"id":18,"title":"Configuring SOHO Network Security","lessons":["18A Attacks & Threats","18B Wireless Security","18C SOHO Router Security","18D Additional Security Measures"],"icon":"fa-wifi","color":"from-rose-500 to-red-600"},{"id":19,"title":"Managing Security Settings","lessons":["19A Account Security","19B Workstation Security","19C Browser Security","19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security"],"icon":"fa-key","color":"from-purple-600 to-pink-600"},{"id":20,"title":"Supporting Mobile Software","lessons":["20A Mobile OS Security","20B Mobile App Troubleshooting","20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security"],"icon":"fa-mobile-screen","color":"from-teal-500 to-emerald-600"},{"id":21,"title":"Using Data Security","lessons":["21A Data Backup & Recovery","21B Data Handling Best Practices","21C Artificial Intelligence"],"icon":"fa-database","color":"from-indigo-600 to-blue-700"},{"id":22,"title":"Implementing Operational Procedures","lessons":["22A Change Management","22B Safety & Environment","22C Scripting Basics"],"icon":"fa-code","color":"from-slate-600 to-slate-800"}],"questions":[{"id":"g-M11-01","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A manager asks for a document that lists each step a technician must follow to onboard a new employee. Which document type fits best?","context":"A manager asks for a document that lists each step a technician must follow to onboard a new employee. Which document type fits best?","options":["A Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) with step-by-step tasks","A policy, an overall statement of intent for the whole organization","A guideline for areas where no procedure is defined or may be deviated from","A service level agreement (SLA) with uptime and response metrics"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An SOP is a step-by-step list of the actions needed for a task to comply with policy.","optionWhy":["Correct. An SOP is a step-by-step list of the actions that must be completed for a task to comply with policy. A new-user setup checklist is a typical SOP.","A policy is an overall statement of intent. It says what the organization wants, but it does not list the individual steps a technician must take.","Guidelines apply where no procedure exists, or where deviation is allowed. A step-by-step onboarding list is a procedure, so a guideline is the wrong document type.","An SLA defines the level of service that a department or vendor must provide, with metrics such as uptime. It does not list onboarding steps."],"optionRefs":["p. 342, Standard Operating Procedure","p. 342, Standard Operating Procedure","p. 343, Standard Operating Procedure","p. 343, Service Level Agreements"],"ref":"pp. 342–343, Standard Operating Procedure","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-02","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which task belongs on an end-user termination checklist when an employee leaves the company?","context":"Which task belongs on an end-user termination checklist when an employee leaves the company?","options":["Returning and sanitizing the employee's devices before the last day","Allocating devices to the new hire at the start of onboarding","Verifying system requirements before a software install begins","Setting up secure credentials for a new user during onboarding"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Offboarding checklists cover returning and sanitizing devices, releasing licenses, and disabling access.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists returning and sanitizing devices as a typical task on an end-user termination checklist for employees who retire, change roles, or are fired.","Device allocation is a task on the new-user setup checklist, which is used during onboarding, not when an employee leaves.","Verifying system requirements is a step in the procedure for custom software installation. It is not an offboarding task.","Identification and enrollment with secure credentials belongs to the new-user checklist. It is part of onboarding, not offboarding."],"optionRefs":["p. 343, Typical examples of SOPs","p. 343, Typical examples of SOPs","p. 343, Typical examples of SOPs","p. 343, Typical examples of SOPs"],"ref":"p. 343, Typical examples of SOPs","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-03","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A cloud provider's SLA promises the service will be available 99.99% of the time. Roughly how much downtime per year does the SLA allow?","context":"A cloud provider's SLA promises the service will be available 99.99% of the time. Roughly how much downtime per year does the SLA allow?","options":["About 52 minutes of downtime per year, at most, for four nines","About 5 hours of downtime per year for the whole service","Only a few seconds of downtime per year for the whole service","No downtime at all, because the SLA is 99.99 percent in total"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says a four-nines SLA allows a maximum of 52 minutes of downtime per year.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide states that a service with the Rule of 4 Nines (99.99%) allows a maximum of 52 minutes of downtime per year.","The guide's figure for four nines is 52 minutes per year. Five hours is far more downtime than the four-nines allowance.","A few seconds is far less than the guide's four-nines allowance. The guide gives 52 minutes per year for 99.99% availability.","Even a 99.99% SLA allows some downtime. The guide's 52-minute figure is the maximum allowance, not zero."],"optionRefs":["p. 343, Service Level Agreements","p. 343, Service Level Agreements","p. 343, Service Level Agreements","p. 343, Service Level Agreements"],"ref":"p. 343, Service Level Agreements","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-04","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A user calls the help desk because an email will not send. Which step happens first in the ticket process?","context":"A user calls the help desk because an email will not send. Which step happens first in the ticket process?","options":["A unique job ticket ID is generated and an agent is assigned","The agent escalates the ticket to Tier 3 engineers for analysis","The agent assigns a severity level before any ticket exists yet","The user writes the final problem resolution text in the ticket"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first step is that the user contacts the help desk, and a unique job ticket ID is generated and an agent assigned.","optionWhy":["Correct. Step 1 of the ticket process is the user contacting the help desk. A unique job ticket ID is generated and an agent is assigned at that point.","Escalation happens only when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. No ticket exists yet, so escalation cannot be the first step.","The guide places severity assessment at step 3, after the ticket has been opened and the user has described the issue.","The problem resolution field is completed at the end of the ticket life cycle, after the plan is implemented and tested."],"optionRefs":["p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 345, Escalation Levels","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 347, Clear Written Communication"],"ref":"p. 344, The general process of ticket management","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-05","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Which device details should a help-desk ticket capture when a laptop is involved?","context":"Which device details should a help-desk ticket capture when a laptop is involved?","options":["A link to the inventory record by service tag or asset ID","The user's password, so the agent can sign in on their behalf","The user's home address and personal phone number for shipping","The vendor's marketing contact and sales details for the account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Device information can be linked to an inventory record through a service tag or asset ID.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the ticket should record device information and might link to the relevant inventory record via a service tag or asset ID.","The guide lists user information as name, contact details, and department or job role. A password is not a ticket detail in the guide.","The guide lists user information as name, contact details, and relevant information such as department or job role. A home address is not mentioned.","Vendor marketing contacts are not part of the ticket details described in the guide. Device details are linked to the inventory record."],"optionRefs":["p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 344, The general process of ticket management"],"ref":"p. 344, The general process of ticket management","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-06","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A help-desk supervisor wants to group related tickets so that they can be assigned to the right team and reported on. What does a category do?","context":"A help-desk supervisor wants to group related tickets so that they can be assigned to the right team and reported on. What does a category do?","options":["It groups related tickets for assignment and reporting","It sets the monetary value of each repair job in the ticket","It replaces the need for a severity level for each ticket","It encrypts the ticket content to protect private customer data"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Categories and subcategories group related tickets together for assignment, reporting, and analysis.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says categories and subcategories group related tickets together, which is useful for assigning tickets to the right section and for reporting and analysis.","The guide does not describe categories as cost settings. Categories group related tickets for assignment and reporting.","Severity is a separate way of ranking tickets by priority. Categories group tickets; they do not replace severity levels.","The guide does not describe encryption for ticket categories. Categories are used to group tickets for assignment and reporting."],"optionRefs":["p. 344, Categories","p. 344, Categories","p. 345, Severity","p. 344, Categories"],"ref":"p. 344, Categories","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-07","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A user asks for a new laptop to be bought, using a purchasing procedure that the IT department already has. Which ticket type fits?","context":"A user asks for a new laptop to be bought, using a purchasing procedure that the IT department already has. Which ticket type fits?","options":["Request, for provisioning something covered by an existing SOP","Incident, for an unexpected error faced by a user","Problem, for the root cause of repeated incidents","Project, for a complex request with no existing procedure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Requests are for provisioning things that IT has an SOP for, such as purchasing new hardware.","optionWhy":["Correct. Requests provision things that the IT department already has an SOP for, and purchasing new hardware is one of the guide's examples.","Incidents are errors or unexpected situations faced by users. Buying a new laptop is not an error, so it is not an incident.","Problems are causes of incidents and need analysis. A planned purchase is not a cause of incidents.","Complex requests that are not covered by existing procedures are treated as projects. This request already has a procedure, so it is not a project."],"optionRefs":["p. 344, Requests","p. 345, Incidents","p. 345, Problems","p. 344, Requests"],"ref":"p. 344, Requests","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-08","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"The help desk receives many tickets each morning about the same printer crashing. What ticket type should be raised to find the root cause?","context":"The help desk receives many tickets each morning about the same printer crashing. What ticket type should be raised to find the root cause?","options":["Problem, to analyze the root cause of repeated incidents","Incident, for each individual crash reported by the users","Request, for provisioning an item that has an existing SOP","Escalation, to move the ticket to a higher support tier now"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Problems are the causes of incidents and need analysis. They are often generated when many incidents of the same type arrive.","optionWhy":["Correct. A problem ticket is the cause of incidents, usually raised internally when many incidents of the same type arrive. Finding the root cause needs analysis.","An incident is an error faced by a user. Each crash is an incident, but the guide says a problem is what should investigate the repeated cause.","Requests provision things that already have an SOP, such as new user accounts. They are not used to find the root cause of printer crashes.","Escalation is a route for unresolved tickets, not a ticket type. Escalation may later move a ticket to a higher tier, but it does not record a root-cause analysis."],"optionRefs":["p. 345, Problems","p. 345, Incidents","p. 344, Requests","p. 345, Escalation Levels"],"ref":"p. 345, Problems","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-09","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"An outage stops a service for all customers at the data center. Using the guide's three-level impact scale, which severity applies?","context":"An outage stops a service for all customers at the data center. Using the guide's three-level impact scale, which severity applies?","options":["Critical, because the outage affects all customers at the data center","Major, because it affects a limited group of customers in one team","Minor, because it has no significant effect on customer groups at all","Low-priority request, because it is not urgent for the business today"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Critical incidents have a widespread effect on customers or involve a potential or actual data breach.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide defines critical incidents as having a widespread effect on customers or involving a potential or actual data breach. An outage for all customers matches this.","Major incidents affect a limited group of customers or involve a suspected security violation. An outage for all customers is wider than that.","Minor incidents do not have a significant effect on customer groups. A data-center outage affects all customers, so it is not minor.","Low-priority request is not a severity level in the guide. Severity levels are minor, major, and critical, and requests are a ticket type."],"optionRefs":["p. 345, Severity","p. 345, Severity","p. 345, Severity","p. 344, Requests"],"ref":"p. 345, Severity","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-10","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A fault affects a limited group of customers. There is no data breach, but the group is unable to work. Using the guide's three levels, which severity should the ticket have?","context":"A fault affects a limited group of customers. There is no data breach, but the group is unable to work. Using the guide's three levels, which severity should the ticket have?","options":["Major, because it affects a limited group of customers","Critical, because it has a widespread effect on customers","Minor, because it has no significant effect on customer groups","Informational, because it only needs to be logged for records"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Major incidents affect a limited group of customers. Critical incidents have a widespread effect.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide defines major incidents as affecting a limited group of customers or involving a suspected security violation. This fault fits that description.","Critical incidents have a widespread effect or involve a potential or actual data breach. This fault affects only a limited group and has no breach.","Minor incidents are not having a significant effect on customer groups. A group that cannot work is affected significantly.","The guide's three severity levels are minor, major, and critical. There is no informational level in the guide's impact scale."],"optionRefs":["p. 345, Severity","p. 345, Severity","p. 345, Severity","p. 345, Severity"],"ref":"p. 345, Severity","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-11","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A ticket needs a senior technician inside the company, but it has not become a problem for engineering yet. Which support tier is this?","context":"A ticket needs a senior technician inside the company, but it has not become a problem for engineering yet. Which support tier is this?","options":["Tier 2 – Internal, for senior technicians inside the company","Tier 0, for self-service knowledge base or help bot options","Tier 1, for the agent's initial diagnosis of the reported issue","Tier 3, for escalation as a problem to engineers or senior managers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tier 2 lets the agent escalate to senior technicians (internal) or to a third-party group (external).","optionWhy":["Correct. Tier 2 – Internal is the escalation to senior technicians inside the company. Tier 2 – External goes to a third-party support group.","Tier 0 offers self-service options such as a knowledge base or help bot. It does not involve a senior technician.","Tier 1 connects the customer to an agent for initial diagnosis. A senior technician is not involved at this stage.","Tier 3 escalates the ticket as a problem to engineers or senior managers. It is a higher level than a senior technician in the same company."],"optionRefs":["p. 346, Tier 2 – Internal","p. 346, Tier 0","p. 346, Tier 1","p. 346, Tier 3"],"ref":"p. 346, Tier 2 – Internal","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-12","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A customer wants to fix a problem on their own first, using a help bot and FAQs. Which support tier is this?","context":"A customer wants to fix a problem on their own first, using a help bot and FAQs. Which support tier is this?","options":["Tier 0, for self-service options such as a help bot","Tier 1, for the agent who connects the customer to a person","Tier 2 – External, for a third-party support group to handle","Tier 3, for a problem sent to engineering teams for analysis"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tier 0 presents self-service options, such as a knowledge base or help bot.","optionWhy":["Correct. Tier 0 presents self-service options so the customer can try to resolve an incident using a knowledge base or help bot.","Tier 1 connects the customer to an agent for initial diagnosis. The customer in this case is still working alone.","Tier 2 – External means escalation to a third-party support group, which happens after the ticket has been worked on by staff.","Tier 3 escalates the ticket as a problem to development or senior managers. The customer here has not reached that stage."],"optionRefs":["p. 346, Tier 0","p. 346, Tier 1","p. 346, Tier 2 – External","p. 346, Tier 3"],"ref":"p. 346, Tier 0","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-13","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A technician cannot resolve a ticket and must hand it to someone else. Which situation is a valid reason to escalate?","context":"A technician cannot resolve a ticket and must hand it to someone else. Which situation is a valid reason to escalate?","options":["A sales or marketing team must handle a refund request","The user has described the issue in their own words","The agent has categorized the support case for reporting","The incident is minor and no troubleshooting was tried yet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Escalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket, for example when sales must handle a refund request.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists the need for sales or marketing involvement to deal with service complaints or refund requests as a reason for escalation.","Describing the issue is step 2 of the normal process. It is not a reason to escalate, because the agent can still work on the ticket.","Categorizing the case is step 3 of the normal process. It is not a reason to escalate the ticket.","The guide expects the agent to take the user through initial troubleshooting before escalating. Not trying steps does not justify escalation."],"optionRefs":["p. 345, Escalation Levels","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 344, The general process of ticket management"],"ref":"p. 345, Escalation Levels","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-14","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Before filing a trouble ticket, a user looks up common troubleshooting steps on the company's internal site. What is this resource called?","context":"Before filing a trouble ticket, a user looks up common troubleshooting steps on the company's internal site. What is this resource called?","options":["Knowledge base, a self-serve store of troubleshooting articles","Service level agreement, which sets service metrics and uptime","Acceptable use policy, which sets what the service may be used for","Lessons learned report, written after a major outage has ended"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository of troubleshooting articles and FAQs.","optionWhy":["Correct. A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information, such as troubleshooting articles and FAQs that end users refer to before filing a ticket.","An SLA defines the level of service requirements and metrics with a provider. It is not a store of troubleshooting articles.","An AUP sets out what someone is allowed to use a service for. It does not contain troubleshooting steps.","A lessons learned report is written after a critical or major incident to find causes. It is not the self-service resource users consult before filing a ticket."],"optionRefs":["p. 347, Knowledge Base","p. 343, Service Level Agreements","p. 347, Policy Documentation","p. 346, Lessons Learned"],"ref":"p. 347, Knowledge Base","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-15","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"After a major outage, a manager wants a document that finds the underlying causes and recommends steps to prevent a repeat. Which document fits?","context":"After a major outage, a manager wants a document that finds the underlying causes and recommends steps to prevent a repeat. Which document fits?","options":["A lessons learned (after-action) report for critical incidents","A severity rating that ranks the ticket by its impact on users","A service tag that links the ticket to the device record","An acceptable use policy that states what usage is allowed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An incident report, also called an after-action report or lessons learned report, identifies underlying causes and recommends preventive measures.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a lessons learned report, also called an after-action report, is made for critical and major incidents to identify underlying causes and recommend preventive measures.","An AUP sets out what someone is allowed to use a service or resource for. It does not investigate the causes of an outage.","A service tag is an identifier that links a ticket to an inventory record. It is not a report about the causes of an outage.","A severity level classifies a ticket by impact. It does not record underlying causes or recommend preventive measures."],"optionRefs":["p. 346, Lessons Learned","p. 347, Policy Documentation","p. 344, The general process of ticket management","p. 345, Severity"],"ref":"p. 346, Lessons Learned","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-16","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A technician has found the probable cause of a fault and recorded the diagnostic steps. Which ticket field should hold this information?","context":"A technician has found the probable cause of a fault and recorded the diagnostic steps. Which ticket field should hold this information?","options":["Progress notes, with diagnostic results and the probable cause","Issue description, with the initial request and its details","Problem resolution, with the plan, testing, and customer acceptance","Ticket category, used to group related tickets for reporting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The progress notes field records what diagnostic tools and processes have discovered, and the probable cause.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says progress notes record what diagnostic tools and processes have discovered, including the identification and confirmation of a probable cause.","The issue description records the initial request, with details collected at the time. The probable cause is found later.","Problem resolution records the plan of action, testing, and customer acceptance. That comes after the cause has been identified.","A category groups related tickets for assignment and reporting. It does not record the diagnostic steps or the probable cause."],"optionRefs":["p. 346, Clear Written Communication","p. 346, Clear Written Communication","p. 347, Clear Written Communication","p. 344, Categories"],"ref":"p. 346, Clear Written Communication","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-17","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Before a ticket is closed, which field should show that the fix was tested and that the customer accepted it?","context":"Before a ticket is closed, which field should show that the fix was tested and that the customer accepted it?","options":["Problem resolution, with the testing and the customer's acceptance noted","Issue description, recorded at the start of the ticket by the agent","Progress notes, with diagnostic steps and the probable cause found","Severity, which classifies the ticket by its impact on customers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Problem resolution records the action plan, the successful implementation and testing, and the customer's acceptance.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the problem resolution field documents the successful implementation and testing of the plan and records end-user or customer acceptance that the ticket can be closed.","The issue description records the initial request. It is written at the start and does not record testing or acceptance.","Progress notes record diagnostic steps and the probable cause. They are not where customer acceptance is recorded.","Severity classifies the ticket by impact. It does not record testing or customer acceptance."],"optionRefs":["p. 346–347, Clear Written Communication","p. 346, Clear Written Communication","p. 346, Clear Written Communication","p. 345, Severity"],"ref":"p. 347, Clear Written Communication","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-18","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"A technician writes a ticket note for a colleague. Which writing style does the guide recommend for ticket notes?","context":"A technician writes a ticket note for a colleague. Which writing style does the guide recommend for ticket notes?","options":["Plain language and short sentences with only the needed facts","Long sentences that explain every background detail for context","Heavy use of acronyms and jargon to save the reader time here","Informal slang to sound friendly to the reader and be relaxed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Clear means plain language rather than jargon. Concise means as few words as possible in short sentences.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide defines clear as plain language rather than jargon, and concise as using as few words as possible in short sentences.","The guide says concise means using as few words as possible. Long background detail goes against that definition.","Acronyms and jargon make notes harder to understand. The guide asks for plain language rather than jargon.","The guide says ticket notes need clear and professional writing. Informal slang does not meet the guide's standard of clarity."],"optionRefs":["p. 347, Clear Written Communication","p. 347, Clear Written Communication","p. 347, Clear Written Communication; p. 353, Using Proper Language","p. 347, Clear Written Communication; p. 353, Using Proper Language"],"ref":"p. 347, Clear Written Communication","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-19","module":11,"lesson":"11A Documentation","question":"Users must see a reminder about data-handling rules every time they log in to a workstation. What is the purpose of this login notice?","context":"Users must see a reminder about data-handling rules every time they log in to a workstation. What is the purpose of this login notice?","options":["To remind users of data-handling and regulated-use rules","To make logins faster by skipping the password prompt entirely","To install updates automatically when users log in each time","To record every keystroke a user types at the desk for logs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A splash screen can remind users of data-handling requirements or other regulated use of a workstation or app.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a splash screen at login can remind users of data-handling requirements or other regulated use of the workstation or network app.","The guide describes the splash screen as a reminder of rules, not as a way to make logins faster.","The guide does not describe the splash screen as an update tool. Its purpose is to remind users about regulated use.","The guide does not describe the splash screen as a monitoring tool. It is a reminder of data-handling requirements."],"optionRefs":["p. 348, Policy Documentation","p. 348, Policy Documentation","p. 348, Policy Documentation","p. 348, Policy Documentation"],"ref":"p. 348, Regulatory compliance","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-20","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer's PC was left unfixed after a technician missed an appointment, and the customer is upset. Which response best follows the guide's advice?","context":"A customer's PC was left unfixed after a technician missed an appointment, and the customer is upset. Which response best follows the guide's advice?","options":["Admit the missed visit, apologize, and give a timed next step","Explain that the schedule and traffic were not your fault","Tell the customer they should have booked earlier next time","Say nothing about the missed visit and move straight to the fix"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Be accurate and honest, empathize, and identify a positive action to resolve the situation.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide's example says you are right, you are sorry, you guarantee a technician will be there by a set time, and you will call back to check.","The guide says not to defend every action of your company. Blaming schedules or others does not resolve the complaint.","Telling a customer they should have booked earlier is judgmental. The guide warns against this and against defending the failure with excuses.","Ignoring a complaint about a missed appointment dismisses the customer's concern. The guide says to acknowledge the complaint first."],"optionRefs":["p. 350, Meet Expectations and Timeline","p. 350, Meet Expectations and Timeline","p. 350, Meet Expectations and Timeline; p. 356, Avoid being judgmental","p. 356, Avoid denying that a problem exists"],"ref":"p. 350, Meet Expectations and Timeline","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-21","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer asks when their repair will be finished. Which action best follows the guide's advice on setting expectations?","context":"A customer asks when their repair will be finished. Which action best follows the guide's advice on setting expectations?","options":["Repeat the request, state next steps, and agree a timeline","Say you will call back when you have a free moment later today","Promise a fix today before checking the parts list in stock","Wait until the customer calls again for an update on progress"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Acknowledge the request, repeat it back, state the next steps, and establish a timeline.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to acknowledge the request, repeat it back, state the next steps, and establish a timeline so the customer knows what to expect.","A vague promise of a call with no timeline does not set expectations. The customer should know when to expect the next update.","The guide says to state constraints such as parts and time. Promising a fix before checking the parts sets an unrealistic expectation.","The guide says customers should not need to call to find out what is happening. Waiting for the customer breaks that rule."],"optionRefs":["p. 350, Set Expectations and Timeline","p. 350, Set Expectations and Timeline","p. 350, Set Expectations and Timeline","p. 350, Meet Expectations and Timeline"],"ref":"p. 350, Set Expectations and Timeline","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-22","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer's printer must be sent away for repair. Which items must the customer be given?","context":"A customer's printer must be sent away for repair. Which items must the customer be given?","options":["Packing and return instructions with a tracking number and RMA","A delivery order form for a new replacement printer model","A list of other customers' repair records to compare against","A software license key for the printer's driver package download"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a repair, the customer needs clear packing and return instructions, a ticket-tracking number, and a returned-merchandise authorization (RMA).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a repair needs clear instructions on how to pack and return the item, a ticket-tracking number, and a returned-merchandise authorization (RMA).","A new delivery order is part of the replace option, not the repair option. The customer here is sending the printer away for repair.","The guide says to respect the customer's confidential information. Other customers' records have no place in a repair handover.","The guide's repair advice covers packing, a tracking number, and an RMA. A license key is not one of the required items for a repair."],"optionRefs":["p. 350, Repair and Replace Options","p. 350, Repair and Replace Options","p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","p. 350, Repair and Replace Options"],"ref":"p. 350, Repair and Replace Options","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-23","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A technician has fixed a customer's PC. Which step should happen before the ticket is logged as closed?","context":"A technician has fixed a customer's PC. Which step should happen before the ticket is logged as closed?","options":["Test that the system works and confirm customer satisfaction","Close the ticket right after the repair, without testing","Delete the progress notes so that earlier mistakes are not seen","Wait a full month before telling the customer what the cause was"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The fix must be tested, the customer must be given a general indication of the cause, and the customer's satisfaction must be recorded.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to test that the system operates normally, explain what caused the issue, and close the ticket only when the solution is verified and the customer is satisfied.","The guide requires testing and verification before the ticket is closed. Closing immediately skips the verification step.","The guide says tickets are likely to be reviewed and forwarded to customers. Deleting notes goes against the record-keeping the guide asks for.","The guide says to give the customer a general indication of the cause and fix when you resolve the ticket. Waiting a month is not described."],"optionRefs":["p. 351, Follow Up","p. 351, Follow Up","p. 347, Clear Written Communication","p. 351, Follow Up"],"ref":"p. 351, Follow Up","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-24","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"During a site visit, a bank statement is lying on the desk next to the PC you are fixing. What should you do?","context":"During a site visit, a bank statement is lying on the desk next to the PC you are fixing. What should you do?","options":["Do not read it; make the customer aware and let them put it away","Read it quickly to check the account belongs to the right customer","Photograph it for the ticket record as evidence of the visit","Move it into a drawer yourself while the customer is away now"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Do not look at printed confidential materials. Make the customer aware of them and allow time for them to be put away.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says not to look at printed confidential materials, to make the customer aware of them, and to allow time for them to be put away.","The guide says not to look at printed confidential materials such as bank statements. Reading it to check ownership is not allowed.","Photographing confidential material is not in the guide, and it would break the rule against looking at private materials.","The guide says to make the customer aware and let them put the material away. Moving it yourself is not the described approach."],"optionRefs":["p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials"],"ref":"p. 351, Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-25","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"While working at a customer's site, you receive a non-urgent social media notification and a colleague's personal text. What should you do?","context":"While working at a customer's site, you receive a non-urgent social media notification and a colleague's personal text. What should you do?","options":["Ignore the notifications and keep working on the ticket","Reply briefly, since a quick reply only takes a moment to send","Call the colleague back on speaker while you work on the ticket","Post a quick update about your visit on social media channels"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Other than a genuinely critical incident, do not allow interruptions. Do not take personal calls or texts, or use social media, at a customer's site.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a distraction is anything that interrupts the task, and that personal calls, texts, and social media should not be used at the customer's site.","The guide says to avoid distractions, including personal texts, when at a customer's site. A quick reply is still a distraction.","The guide says not to take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent. A personal call on speaker is not allowed.","The guide says not to respond to social media posts or post about experiences on social media. Posting an update breaks this rule."],"optionRefs":["p. 351, Avoid Distractions","p. 351, Avoid Distractions","p. 351, Avoid Distractions","p. 351, Avoid Distractions; p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media"],"ref":"p. 351, Avoid Distractions","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-26","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"You greet a customer at the door of their office. Which greeting follows the guide's advice on professional language?","context":"You greet a customer at the door of their office. Which greeting follows the guide's advice on professional language?","options":["Good morning, with eye contact and a polite introduction","Hey! Nice to see you, so what's going on with the PC then?","Yo, what's up with the PC today, then? Let's get going now.","Hi mate, so what's broken on this one, exactly, today?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Greet the customer with Hello or Good morning. Avoid slang such as Hey.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to greet a customer with Hello or Good morning rather than Hey, and to avoid over-familiar or slang phrases.","The guide gives Hey! as an example of over-familiar language that should not be used with a customer.","Slang phrases should not be used with customers. The guide says to avoid over-familiar language and slang.","Informal terms like mate are over-familiar for a professional greeting. The guide asks for a respectful greeting such as Good morning."],"optionRefs":["p. 353, Using Proper Language","p. 353, Using Proper Language","p. 353, Using Proper Language","p. 353, Using Proper Language"],"ref":"p. 353, Using Proper Language","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-27","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer's title is unknown to you. Which approach does the guide recommend for addressing them?","context":"A customer's title is unknown to you. Which approach does the guide recommend for addressing them?","options":["Start formally and move to informal terms only if signalled","Use the first name straight away to be friendly with them","Pick the title based on the customer's nationality and background","Ask the customer to pick a title from a list in the contract"],"answer":0,"explanation":"It is safer to start on a formal basis and use more informal terms only if the customer signals they are happier with that.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says it is safer to start on a formal basis and use informal terms of address if the customer signals they are happier that way.","The guide says it is safer to start formally. Using first names straight away can be inappropriate for some customers.","The guide warns against stereotyped assumptions about people's cultural background. Nationality should not decide the form of address.","The guide does not describe a contract list of titles. It recommends starting formally and following the customer's signals."],"optionRefs":["p. 353, Cultural Sensitivity","p. 353, Cultural Sensitivity","p. 353, Cultural Sensitivity","p. 353, Cultural Sensitivity"],"ref":"p. 353, Cultural Sensitivity","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-28","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer says, My printer is broken. Which question is an open-ended starting question?","context":"A customer says, My printer is broken. Which question is an open-ended starting question?","options":["What seems to be the problem with the printer right now?","What error number is displayed on the panel?","Is the power LED on the printer lit right now?","Did you restart the printer before it failed?"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An open-ended question invites the other person to compose a response, such as What seems to be the problem?","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide's example of an open-ended question is What seems to be the problem? It invites the customer to describe what they think is wrong.","This is a closed question with one fixed answer. The guide gives it as an example of a closed question.","This is a closed question that can be answered yes or no. The guide lists it as an example of a closed question.","This question can be answered with yes or no, which makes it a closed question. The guide says to start with open-ended questions."],"optionRefs":["p. 354, Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","p. 354, Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","p. 354, Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","p. 354, Clarifying and Questioning Techniques"],"ref":"p. 354, Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-29","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"While a customer describes a fault, which action is part of active listening?","context":"While a customer describes a fault, which action is part of active listening?","options":["Restate the issue and summarize what the customer said","Interrupt with your own diagnosis to save time on the call","Argue if you think the customer's explanation is wrong and say so","Check your phone for messages while the customer is speaking"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Active listening includes reflecting the customer's phrases and restating the issue, and taking notes of what was said.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says active listening includes reflecting phrases, restating the issue, summarizing what was said, and taking notes for an accurate record.","Active listening means not being distracted by your reply. Interrupting with a diagnosis goes against the guide's definition.","The guide defines active listening as listening without arguing with or commenting on what the customer says.","The guide says active listening requires full attention. Checking a phone is a distraction that the guide warns against."],"optionRefs":["p. 354, Active Listening","p. 354, Active Listening","p. 354, Active Listening","p. 354, Active Listening; p. 351, Avoid Distractions"],"ref":"p. 354, Active Listening","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-30","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"An angry customer is shouting about a faulty product. Which action follows the guide's advice for defusing the situation?","context":"An angry customer is shouting about a faulty product. Which action follows the guide's advice for defusing the situation?","options":["Keep your voice low, stay calm, and focus on positive actions","Raise your voice so the customer knows you are serious about it","Tell the customer they are being difficult to work with today","End the call immediately without any warning at all to them"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keep your voice low, use soothing language, and focus on positive actions. Do not take complaints personally.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to try to calm the situation with a low voice, soothing language, and a focus on positive actions. It also says not to take the complaint personally.","The guide says to stay calm and not argue. Raising your voice escalates tension rather than defusing it.","The guide says to think of the situation as difficult, not of the customer as difficult. Labeling the customer goes against this.","The guide says to issue a caution first. Ending the call is used only if the abuse continues after the caution."],"optionRefs":["p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","p. 355, Difficult Situations","p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers"],"ref":"p. 355–356, Maintain a Positive Attitude","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-31","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer insists that a problem with their PC is trivial, but they want it fixed today. What is the best approach?","context":"A customer insists that a problem with their PC is trivial, but they want it fixed today. What is the best approach?","options":["Acknowledge the problem and show how it will be resolved","Explain that the problem is small and not worth the time","Tell the customer the fault is in how they use the PC","Argue that the PC is working normally, so nothing is wrong"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Acknowledge the customer's statement about the problem, and demonstrate how it can be resolved.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that whether you think the matter is trivial is not the issue. Acknowledge the statement and show how the problem can be resolved.","The guide says to avoid dismissing the importance of a problem the customer has complained about. Calling it small is dismissive.","The guide warns against being judgmental and against assuming the customer is causing the problem. Blaming the customer is not recommended.","The guide says to avoid arguing with the customer. Insisting that the PC works denies the problem the customer has raised."],"optionRefs":["p. 356, Avoid denying that a problem exists","p. 356, Avoid denying that a problem exists","p. 356, Avoid being judgmental","p. 356, Arguing with the customer"],"ref":"p. 356, Avoid denying a problem","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-32","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer becomes abusive on the phone, even after you issued a caution. What does the guide recommend?","context":"A customer becomes abusive on the phone, even after you issued a caution. What does the guide recommend?","options":["End the call or escalate to a manager, and document the reasons","Keep working on the call until the customer stops shouting at you","Call the customer back straight away to continue the job today","Post the call on social media to ask others for their advice"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the abuse continues, end the call or escalate it to a manager, and explain and document your reasons.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that if the abuse continues after a caution, you should end the call or escalate it to a manager, and explain and document your reasons.","The guide says that if the abuse continues, the call should end or be escalated. Continuing indefinitely is not recommended.","The guide does not recommend an immediate callback after abuse. The call should end or be escalated, with the reasons documented.","The guide says technicians should never disclose these experiences on social media. Posting about the call goes against that advice."],"optionRefs":["p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media"],"ref":"p. 356, Dealing with Difficult Customers","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-33","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A technician had a very bad visit and wants to talk about it. What does the guide recommend?","context":"A technician had a very bad visit and wants to talk about it. What does the guide recommend?","options":["Talk privately with a colleague face to face, not online","Post about it anonymously so no customer is named","Post after removing the customer's name from the story","Describe it in an online forum to get advice from others"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Find a colleague for a private face-to-face chat, and never disclose these experiences on social media.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to find a colleague for a private face-to-face chat and never to disclose these experiences on social media.","The guide says anything posted publicly is very hard to withdraw. Posting anonymously still breaks the rule against social media disclosure.","Removing the name does not stop the public disclosure the guide prohibits. Anything posted publicly is hard to withdraw.","The guide says technicians should exercise discretion when referencing cases. An online forum is a public outlet, which the guide advises against."],"optionRefs":["p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media"],"ref":"p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-34","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A business needs a computer to run a server that manages user accounts for the whole office network. Which OS type is designed for this?","context":"A business needs a computer to run a server that manages user accounts for the whole office network. Which OS type is designed for this?","options":["Network Operating System (NOS) for servers in business networks today","Business client, a PC in a centrally managed domain network","Home client, for standalone use or a home or small-office workgroup","Cell phone or tablet OS, designed for touch-only handheld devices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Network Operating System (NOS) is designed to run servers in business networks.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes a NOS as designed to run servers in business networks, such as Windows Server 2019, 2022, and 2025.","A business client is designed as a client in a centrally managed domain network, not as a server running the network.","A home client is designed for standalone use or a workgroup in a home or small office. It is not the server OS for a business network.","Cell phone and tablet OSs are designed for handheld devices with touch interfaces, not for running a business server."],"optionRefs":["p. 358, Windows and macOS; p. 359, Microsoft Windows","p. 358, Windows and macOS","p. 358, Windows and macOS","p. 358, Windows and macOS"],"ref":"p. 358, Windows and macOS","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-35","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A customer wants to install macOS on a laptop they bought from a non-Apple manufacturer. What should you tell them?","context":"A customer wants to install macOS on a laptop they bought from a non-Apple manufacturer. What should you tell them?","options":["macOS is only available on Apple-built devices","macOS installs on any PC with enough memory","macOS installs on any PC that has UEFI firmware","macOS is a free download for any laptop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"macOS is exclusively available on Apple-built devices and cannot be purchased or installed on non-Apple PCs.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says macOS is exclusively available on Apple-built devices and cannot be purchased or installed on non-Apple PCs.","The guide says macOS cannot be installed on non-Apple PCs, regardless of memory. Hardware compatibility is not the only limit.","The guide does not say UEFI firmware allows macOS installation. It states that macOS cannot be installed on non-Apple PCs.","The guide says macOS cannot be purchased or installed on non-Apple PCs. It is not a free download for any laptop."],"optionRefs":["p. 359, Apple macOS","p. 359, Apple macOS","p. 359, Apple macOS","p. 359, Apple macOS"],"ref":"p. 359, macOS","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-36","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A school wants low-cost laptops mainly for web applications and online testing. Which OS fits this purpose?","context":"A school wants low-cost laptops mainly for web applications and online testing. Which OS fits this purpose?","options":["Chrome OS, built for web apps on budget Chromebooks","macOS, which only runs on Apple-built devices and Macs","Windows Server 2025, a network OS for servers in companies","iOS, which runs only on iPhone and early iPad models"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Chrome OS is built for specific hardware such as Chromebooks, targets budget and education markets, and is primarily built for web applications.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Chrome OS is designed for Chromebooks, targets budget and education markets, and is primarily built for web applications accessed through a browser.","The guide says macOS is exclusively available on Apple-built devices. It is not an option for generic school laptops.","Windows Server 2025 is a network operating system for servers. It is not designed for classroom laptops.","iOS is for Apple's iPhone and early iPad models only. It does not run on laptops for schools."],"optionRefs":["p. 361, Chrome OS","p. 359, Apple macOS","p. 359, Microsoft Windows","p. 361, Apple iOS"],"ref":"p. 361, Chrome OS","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-37","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A company wants a Linux system with stable versions and a long-term support release. Which Linux release model fits?","context":"A company wants a Linux system with stable versions and a long-term support release. Which Linux release model fits?","options":["Standard release with long-term support (LTS) versions","Rolling release, with updates once they are stable and tested","Community-supported desktop release for home users and students","Subscription-based server release from a commercial vendor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard releases use versioning, and some versions offer long-term support (LTS).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says standard releases use versioning for updates, with some versions offering long-term support (LTS).","A rolling release provides updates as they become stable, without version distinctions. It does not offer LTS versions.","Community support describes who supports some distributions, not a release model. The guide's two release models are standard and rolling.","Subscription support describes how some distributions are supported, not how their releases are versioned. The guide lists two release models."],"optionRefs":["p. 361, Linux release models","p. 361, Linux release models","p. 360, Linux","p. 360, Linux"],"ref":"p. 361, Linux release models","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-38","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A school has a recent iPad and asks which Apple OS it runs. Which answer is correct?","context":"A school has a recent iPad and asks which Apple OS it runs. Which answer is correct?","options":["iPadOS, built from iOS for the latest iPad models","iOS, for the iPhone and early iPad models","macOS, for Mac desktops, iMacs, and MacBooks","Android, an open-source OS from other manufacturers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"iPadOS is developed from iOS and supports the latest iPad models (2019 and later).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says iPadOS is developed from iOS and supports the latest iPad models, from 2019 onwards, with better multitasking and Apple Pencil support.","The guide says iOS runs on the iPhone and early iPad models. Recent iPads run iPadOS instead.","macOS is for Mac desktops, iMac all-in-ones, and MacBooks. It is not the OS for iPads.","Android is open source and used on smartphones and tablets from other manufacturers. It does not run on Apple iPads."],"optionRefs":["p. 362, Apple iPadOS","p. 361, Apple iOS; p. 362, Apple iPadOS","p. 359, Apple macOS","p. 362, Android"],"ref":"p. 362, Apple iPadOS","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-39","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A technician is preparing a new disk partition to install Windows. Which file system must the partition use?","context":"A technician is preparing a new disk partition to install Windows. Which file system must the partition use?","options":["NTFS, the only file system that Windows can be installed to today","FAT32, used for the system partition and removable drives","exFAT, used for removable hard drives and flash media","ReFS, Microsoft's newest file system for data protection"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows can only be installed to an NTFS-formatted partition.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Windows can only be installed to an NTFS-formatted partition, and NTFS is usually the best choice for additional Windows partitions.","FAT32 is typically used for the system partition holding the boot loader and for removable drives. The guide does not allow Windows installation on FAT32.","exFAT is designed for removable hard drives and flash media. The guide does not list it as a Windows installation partition.","ReFS is Microsoft's newest file system for keeping data protected. The guide only states that Windows must be installed to NTFS."],"optionRefs":["p. 364, New Technology File System","p. 365, FAT32","p. 365, exFAT","p. 364, Resilient File System"],"ref":"p. 364, New Technology File System","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-40","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"After a power cut, an NTFS volume must recover quickly and reliably. Which NTFS feature helps with this?","context":"After a power cut, an NTFS volume must recover quickly and reliably. Which NTFS feature helps with this?","options":["Journaling, which makes recovery faster after power cuts","Snapshots, which give read-only copies of files over time","Indexing, which builds a catalog of file locations","Dynamic disks, which combine space across several disks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Journaling makes recovery after power outages and crashes faster and more reliable.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says journaling makes recovery after power outages and crashes faster and more reliable, because data writes are verified and logged.","Snapshots provide read-only copies of files at points in time, which help revert changes and support backups. They are not the recovery feature described for power cuts.","The Indexing Service creates a catalog of file locations to speed up searches. It does not help recovery after a power cut.","Dynamic disks let space on several physical disks be combined into volumes. They are not described as a power-cut recovery feature."],"optionRefs":["p. 364, Journaling","p. 364, Snapshots","p. 364, Indexing","p. 364, Dynamic Disks"],"ref":"p. 364, New Technology File System","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-41","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A user needs a flash drive that stores files larger than 4 GB and works with both Windows and macOS. Which file system is the best choice?","context":"A user needs a flash drive that stores files larger than 4 GB and works with both Windows and macOS. Which file system is the best choice?","options":["exFAT, which supports large files on removable flash drives today","FAT32, which limits file size to 4 GB minus 1 byte on drives","NTFS, which macOS can read but not write to on removable drives","ext4, the typical Linux file system for fixed disks on servers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"exFAT is designed for removable flash media, supports large volumes, and supports access permissions but not encryption.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes exFAT as designed for removable hard drives and flash media, supporting large volumes. Standard FAT32 limits file size to 4 GB minus 1 byte.","FAT32 has a maximum file size of 4 GB minus 1 byte. It cannot store the larger files in this scenario.","macOS can read NTFS drives but cannot write to them. NTFS does not meet the requirement to work well with both Windows and macOS.","ext4 is the typical Linux file system for fixed disks. The guide does not describe it as a removable flash format for Windows and macOS."],"optionRefs":["p. 365, exFAT; p. 365, FAT32","p. 365, FAT32","p. 364, New Technology File System","p. 365, Linux File Systems"],"ref":"p. 365, exFAT","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-42","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A technician is installing Red Hat Enterprise Linux. Which file system is the default for those installations?","context":"A technician is installing Red Hat Enterprise Linux. Which file system is the default for those installations?","options":["XFS, the default file system for RHEL installations","ext4, the most common Linux file system for fixed disks","FAT32, typically used for the system partition and removable media","APFS, the proprietary file system used on Mac computers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"XFS is the default file system for Red Hat Enterprise Linux (RHEL) installations.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says XFS is the default file system for Red Hat Enterprise Linux installations and is supported by other distributions.","The guide says most Linux distributions use some version of the ext file system, and ext4 offers good performance. It is not the RHEL default in this guide.","FAT32 is typically used for the system partition and removable media. It is not the default for Red Hat Enterprise Linux.","APFS is the proprietary file system used on Apple Mac workstations and laptops. It is not used on Red Hat Enterprise Linux."],"optionRefs":["p. 365, Linux File Systems","p. 365, Linux File Systems","p. 365, FAT32","p. 366, Apple File System"],"ref":"p. 365, Linux File Systems","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-43","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A PC does not have TPM 2.0, so the Windows 11 upgrade will not install. Which compatibility category does this belong to?","context":"A PC does not have TPM 2.0, so the Windows 11 upgrade will not install. Which compatibility category does this belong to?","options":["OS compatibility with device hardware, such as TPM 2.0","Software app compatibility, meaning the app is coded for the OS","Network compatibility, meaning protocols must match","User training requirements for the new desktop style"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide lists OS compatibility with device hardware, and gives Windows 11's TPM 2.0 requirement as an example.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes update limitations where the OS needs hardware features the device lacks, and gives Windows 11's need for TPM version 2 as an example.","Software app compatibility is about whether an app is coded to run on a particular OS. A missing TPM chip is a hardware requirement.","Network compatibility is about devices exchanging data using common protocols. The TPM requirement concerns the hardware of the PC.","User training concerns the new desktop style and file system. It does not explain a hardware requirement that blocks the upgrade."],"optionRefs":["p. 366, Hardware Compatibility and Update Limitations","p. 367, Software Compatibility","p. 368, Network Compatibility","p. 368, User Training and Support"],"ref":"p. 366, Hardware Compatibility and Update Limitations","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-44","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A company's PCs still run Windows 10 after Microsoft's end-of-life date of October 14, 2025. What does the guide say about these systems?","context":"A company's PCs still run Windows 10 after Microsoft's end-of-life date of October 14, 2025. What does the guide say about these systems?","options":["They no longer receive free support or updates from Microsoft","They are in extended support, with critical patches still issued","They are in the public beta phase used for feedback","They are in the supported phase with regular patches"],"answer":0,"explanation":"After the Windows 10 end-of-life date, Microsoft provides no free support or updates.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Microsoft designated October 14, 2025 as the end-of-life date for Windows 10, and no free support or updates are provided after that date.","Extended support is a phase where the vendor still issues critical patches. The guide gives the Windows 10 end-of-life date as the point when free updates stop.","A public beta phase is used to gather user feedback before a release. It does not apply to a Windows 10 system that is past its end-of-life date.","The supported phase is when the product is actively marketed and receives regular patches. Windows 10 has passed its end-of-life date, so this phase has ended."],"optionRefs":["p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations"],"ref":"p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-45","module":11,"lesson":"11C Operating Systems","question":"A server in a company's network is end-of-life and no longer receives updates. Why does the guide say this is a risk?","context":"A server in a company's network is end-of-life and no longer receives updates. Why does the guide say this is a risk?","options":["It no longer gets security updates, a critical vulnerability","It runs more slowly than newer systems, which hurts productivity","It draws too much electrical power for the server room floor","It cannot connect to any network, so it is fully isolated"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An end-of-life system no longer receives security updates and therefore represents a critical vulnerability if it remains in use.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says an end-of-life system no longer receives security updates and therefore represents a critical vulnerability if any remain in active use.","The guide's concern is security. It does not describe end-of-life systems as a risk because they run slowly.","The guide does not discuss power use for end-of-life systems. Its concern is that security updates stop.","An end-of-life system can still be connected. The risk the guide names is the absence of security updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations","p. 368, Vendor Life-Cycle Limitations"],"ref":"p. 368, End-of-life","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-46","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"You are visiting a customer's office for a troubleshooting appointment. Which clothing does the guide recommend?","context":"You are visiting a customer's office for a troubleshooting appointment. Which clothing does the guide recommend?","options":["Business casual, which means smart clothes","Jeans and a T-shirt, which are comfortable for work","Shorts or a short skirt, which are cool for the site","Coveralls for every visit, whatever the task"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide recommends business casual for troubleshooting appointments and says jeans, shorts, and T-shirts are not smart workwear.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says business casual is typically sufficient for troubleshooting appointments, and that it means smart clothes.","The guide says jeans and T-shirts or vests are not smart workwear and should not be worn for an appointment with a customer.","The guide says shorts and short skirts are not smart workwear. They are not an appropriate choice for a customer visit.","The guide only suggests a dust suit or coveralls when planned maintenance may dirty clothing, and only with company approval."],"optionRefs":["p. 352, Professional Appearance","p. 352, Professional Appearance","p. 352, Professional Appearance","p. 352, Professional Appearance"],"ref":"p. 352, Professional Appearance","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-47","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"You know you will be twenty minutes late for an on-site appointment because of traffic. What does the guide recommend?","context":"You know you will be twenty minutes late for an on-site appointment because of traffic. What does the guide recommend?","options":["Tell the customer as soon as possible, and stay positive","Arrive without saying anything about the delay","Cancel the appointment without telling anyone","Wait until you arrive before calling the customer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says to be honest about delays, inform the customer as soon as possible, and do it in a positive manner.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says if it becomes obvious you will not be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible, be honest, and keep the message positive.","The guide says to be accountable and honest about delays. Arriving without mentioning the delay goes against this.","The guide does not suggest cancelling without notice. It asks you to tell the customer about the delay as soon as possible.","The guide says to inform the customer as soon as it becomes obvious you will be late. Waiting until arrival is too late."],"optionRefs":["p. 351, Be On Time","p. 351, Be On Time","p. 351, Be On Time","p. 351, Be On Time"],"ref":"p. 351, Be On Time","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-48","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer asks how to open a support ticket and how long repairs usually take. Which kind of documentation should the service provide?","context":"A customer asks how to open a support ticket and how long repairs usually take. Which kind of documentation should the service provide?","options":["Advertised contact details, hours, and what to expect for repairs","A personal blog post with the technician's own experiences","A verbal promise made by the first technician on site","Private notes kept by the technician for internal use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Support contact information and hours should be well advertised, and the service should document what customers can expect, including repair times.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says contact information and hours of operation should be well advertised, and the service should document what to expect, including how long incidents may take to resolve.","The guide says technicians should not disclose experiences publicly, and it does not suggest personal blogs as support documentation.","The guide asks for proper written documentation so the customer knows what to expect. A verbal promise alone is not described as enough.","Private technician notes are not the customer-facing documentation the guide describes. The guide asks for documentation that tells the customer what to expect."],"optionRefs":["p. 350, Professional Documentation","p. 357, Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","p. 350, Professional Documentation","p. 350, Professional Documentation"],"ref":"p. 350, Professional Documentation","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M11-49","module":11,"lesson":"11B Professional Communication","question":"A customer's first contact with your company is a support call that is handled badly. What does the guide say about the support process?","context":"A customer's first contact with your company is a support call that is handled badly. What does the guide say about the support process?","options":["The process must reassure customers from the first contact onwards","Only the repair itself matters, not the first contact","A poor first contact is the customer's own responsibility","First contact is handled only by the sales department"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the support process must reassure customers from the point of first contact that their inquiry will be handled efficiently.","The guide says a faulty support process at first contact doubles a poor impression. The first contact matters as much as the repair.","The guide makes the support process responsible for reassuring the customer. It does not put the blame on the customer.","The guide does not say that first contact is handled only by sales. It describes the support process as a whole."],"optionRefs":["p. 349, Professional Support Processes","p. 349, Professional Support Processes","p. 349, Professional Support Processes","p. 349, Professional Support Processes"],"ref":"p. 349, Professional Support Processes","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-01","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Where is all Windows configuration data ultimately stored?","context":"Where is all Windows configuration data ultimately stored?","options":["Within the registry database","In the Control Panel applets","In the user's AppData folder","In the root of the C: drive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in a database called the registry. Windows Settings and Control Panel provide graphical pages and applets for changing these settings, and Registry Editor can edit the registry directly.","optionWhy":["Correct. The registry is the database that holds all Windows configuration data.","Control Panel applets are interfaces for changing configuration settings. The settings themselves are held in the registry.","AppData is where user-context applications can be installed. It isn't the store for all Windows configuration.","The root directory (C:\\) holds top-level folders such as Windows, Program Files, and Users. Configuration data lives in the registry."],"optionRefs":["p. 372","pp. 372, 374","p. 395","p. 382"],"ref":"p. 372, Windows Settings and Control Panel","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-02","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A technician can't find a legacy configuration option anywhere in the Windows Settings app. Which statement about Settings and Control Panel explains this?","context":"A technician can't find a legacy configuration option anywhere in the Windows Settings app. Which statement about Settings and Control Panel explains this?","options":["Settings is preferred, but some options still need Control Panel.","Control Panel is preferred, and Settings is only used for touch.","Settings has replaced Control Panel completely in Windows 11.","Only Microsoft can add applets to Control Panel or Settings."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Settings app is the touch-enabled, preferred administrative interface. Most standard Windows 10/11 settings are in Settings, but not all of them; some options are still configured through the legacy Control Panel. Some applications, such as antivirus software, add their own Control Panel applets.","optionWhy":["Correct. Settings is the preferred interface, but some configuration is still done in the legacy Control Panel.","This reverses them. The guide calls Settings the preferred administrative interface and Control Panel the legacy one.","The guide shows a Windows 11 Control Panel with many applets and says some options are still configured there.","The guide notes that some software applications, such as antivirus software, add their own Control Panel applets."],"optionRefs":["p. 374","pp. 372, 374","pp. 374–375","p. 374"],"ref":"pp. 372–374, Windows Settings and Control Panel","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-03","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A technician sets up a new Windows PC and then adds accounts for three office staff. With default behavior and good practice, how should the accounts be configured?","context":"A technician sets up a new Windows PC and then adds accounts for three office staff. With default behavior and good practice, how should the accounts be configured?","options":["The first account is the administrator; staff get standard user accounts.","All four accounts become administrators so that staff can install apps.","Staff accounts get administrator rights, and the first account is standard.","Every account shares one profile so staff can see each other's documents."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The first user of the computer is configured as the default administrator account, with privileges to change any aspect of the system. Additional accounts are usually configured as standard users, with privileges on their own profile only.","optionWhy":["Correct. The first user becomes the default administrator, and additional accounts are usually standard users.","The guide says additional accounts are usually standard users. Store apps and user-context apps can be installed without admin rights anyway.","This reverses the default. The first user is the default administrator.","Each user account is associated with its own profile containing personal folders. Profiles aren't shared like this."],"optionRefs":["p. 375","pp. 375, 395","p. 375","p. 375"],"ref":"p. 375, Accounts Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-04","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user wants to sign in to his work laptop and home desktop with the same account, and have his desktop settings and profile data synced between them. Which account type does he need?","context":"A user wants to sign in to his work laptop and home desktop with the same account, and have his desktop settings and profile data synced between them. Which account type does he need?","options":["A Microsoft account","A local-only account","A standard user account","The default admin account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in, and syncs desktop settings and user profile data across multiple devices.","optionWhy":["Correct. A Microsoft account allows sign-in on multiple devices and syncs settings and profile data.","A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only, so nothing would sync.","Standard user is a privilege level (rights on the user's own profile only), not a sync-capable account type.","The default administrator is the first account created on a PC and has full system privileges. Admin rights don't provide syncing between devices."],"optionRefs":["p. 376","p. 376","p. 375","p. 375"],"ref":"p. 376, Windows Account Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-05","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A technician needs to join a new laptop to the company's centrally managed domain network. Which page of Account settings is used for this?","context":"A technician needs to join a new laptop to the company's centrally managed domain network. Which page of Account settings is used for this?","options":["Access work or school","Family and other users","Sign-in options","Sync settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Account settings, Access work or school joins the computer to a centrally managed domain network. Sign-in options configure a fingerprint reader or PIN, Family and other users lets other accounts log on, and Sync settings applies the same preferences across devices.","optionWhy":["Correct. Access work or school joins the computer to a centrally managed domain.","This page lets other local or Microsoft accounts log on to the computer. It doesn't join a domain.","Sign-in options configure a fingerprint reader or PIN instead of a password, and automatic locking.","Sync settings use the cloud to apply the same personalization on each device you sign in to with a Microsoft account."],"optionRefs":["p. 376","p. 376","p. 376","p. 376"],"ref":"p. 376, Windows Account Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-06","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"With User Account Control (UAC) at its default level, what happens when a user tries to change an administrative setting?","context":"With User Account Control (UAC) at its default level, what happens when a user tries to change an administrative setting?","options":["They must confirm a prompt or else enter administrator credentials.","The change is blocked unless the PC is joined to a domain network.","Windows applies the change silently and logs it in Event Viewer.","The account is converted to a standard user until the next sign-in."],"answer":0,"explanation":"UAC is a system to prevent unauthorized use of administrator privileges. At the default level, changing an administrative setting requires the user to confirm a prompt or enter the credentials of an administrator account. UAC settings can be changed through the User Accounts applet.","optionWhy":["Correct. At the default level, UAC requires confirming a prompt or entering admin credentials.","UAC isn't tied to domain membership. It prompts for confirmation or admin credentials.","The point of UAC is to prevent silent, unauthorized use of admin privileges, so a prompt appears.","UAC doesn't change account types. The User Accounts applet is where the privilege level can be changed manually."],"optionRefs":["p. 376","p. 376","p. 376","p. 376"],"ref":"p. 376, User Accounts Control Panel Applet","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-07","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user wants to stop a newly installed app from using the laptop's camera and microphone. Which group of Windows privacy settings controls this?","context":"A user wants to stop a newly installed app from using the laptop's camera and microphone. Which group of Windows privacy settings controls this?","options":["App permissions","Data collection","Ease of Access","Sync settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Privacy settings include data collection toggles, which let Microsoft process usage telemetry (speech, input personalization, diagnostics, activity history), and app permissions, which allow or deny apps access to devices such as location, camera, and microphone, and to user data such as contacts and files.","optionWhy":["Correct. App permissions allow or deny apps access to devices such as the camera and microphone.","Data collection settings control what usage telemetry Microsoft can process, not which apps may use the camera.","Ease of Access (Accessibility) configures input and output options such as Magnifier, captions, and speech input.","Sync settings apply the same personalization across devices that use a Microsoft account. They don't control app device access."],"optionRefs":["p. 377","p. 377","pp. 379–380","p. 376"],"ref":"p. 377, Privacy and Security Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-08","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A PC's clock has drifted several minutes from the correct time. Why should you fix this promptly?","context":"A PC's clock has drifted several minutes from the correct time. Why should you fix this promptly?","options":["Processes such as authentication and backup depend on it.","Windows Update refuses all patches if the clock is wrong.","The display's refresh rate is calculated from the clock.","Product activation fails if the time zone isn't local."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Time and Language settings set the correct date, time, and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide names authentication and backup as processes that depend on accurate time.","The guide doesn't say this. It links accurate time to authentication and backup.","Refresh rate is a display setting tied to the screen's native specifications, not to the system clock.","Activation verifies that Windows is legitimately licensed using a product key or digital license. The guide doesn't link it to the time zone."],"optionRefs":["p. 378","p. 378","pp. 391–392","p. 389"],"ref":"p. 378, Time and Language Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-09","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user with impaired vision wants Windows to read out descriptions of what is currently selected on screen. Which tool provides this?","context":"A user with impaired vision wants Windows to read out descriptions of what is currently selected on screen. Which tool provides this?","options":["Narrator","Magnifier","Closed captions","High contrast"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Ease of Access (Accessibility in Windows 11) has three groups. Vision covers cursor indicators, high-contrast and color-filter modes, Magnifier, and Narrator, which gives audio descriptions of the current selection. Hearing covers mono audio, visual notifications, and captions. Interaction covers keyboard and mouse usability and speech or eye-controlled input.","optionWhy":["Correct. Narrator provides audio descriptions of the current selection.","Magnifier is a Vision zoom tool that enlarges part of the screen. It doesn't speak.","Closed captioning is in the Hearing group and shows text for audio content.","High-contrast mode is a Vision option that changes colors to improve visibility. It doesn't produce audio."],"optionRefs":["p. 379","p. 379","p. 380","p. 379"],"ref":"pp. 379–380, Ease of Access","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-10","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"On a 64-bit installation of Windows, where are 32-bit applications installed by default?","context":"On a 64-bit installation of Windows, where are 32-bit applications installed by default?","options":["Program Files (x86)","Program Files","Windows\\System32","Users\\AppData"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Program Files holds installed applications. In 64-bit Windows, a Program Files (x86) folder is created for 32-bit applications. The Windows folder is the system root, and Users holds profile data, including NTUSER.DAT.","optionWhy":["Correct. 64-bit Windows creates Program Files (x86) to store 32-bit applications.","On 64-bit Windows, Program Files is used for 64-bit applications. 32-bit apps go to Program Files (x86).","System32 is the subdirectory of the system root that holds 64-bit system files, such as DLLs and EXEs, not installed applications.","User-context applications can be installed to a user's AppData folder, but that isn't the default location for 32-bit desktop apps."],"optionRefs":["pp. 382, 406","p. 406","pp. 382, 406","p. 395"],"ref":"p. 382, System Files","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-11","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user can't find a configuration file that she knows exists in her profile folder. A technician suspects it has the Hidden attribute. What File Explorer setting will reveal it?","context":"A user can't find a configuration file that she knows exists in her profile folder. A technician suspects it has the Hidden attribute. What File Explorer setting will reveal it?","options":["Turn on \"Show hidden files, folders, and drives\"","Turn off \"Hide extensions for known file types\"","Rebuild the search index using Indexing Options","Switch to single-click to open items on General"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On the View tab of File Explorer Options, the \"Show hidden files, folders, and drives\" option reveals files marked as Hidden. The \"Hide protected operating system files\" option controls files with the System attribute, and \"Hide extensions for known file types\" affects only file name extensions.","optionWhy":["Correct. This option reveals files marked with the Hidden attribute.","This shows file extensions such as .docx. It doesn't reveal hidden files.","A corrupted index can cause search problems, but rebuilding it doesn't make hidden files visible in Explorer.","This General-tab option only changes how items are opened. It doesn't affect hidden files."],"optionRefs":["p. 383","p. 383","p. 383","p. 382"],"ref":"p. 383, File Explorer Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-12","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Why are file extensions for known file types hidden by default in File Explorer?","context":"Why are file extensions for known file types hidden by default in File Explorer?","options":["Overtyping an extension when renaming can make a file hard to open.","Extensions show the Hidden attribute and must be kept out of view.","Hiding extensions stops users from deleting protected system files.","Extensions slow down the index, which then causes search problems."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The file extension associates a file type with a software application. Overtyping the extension when renaming a file can make it difficult to open, so extensions are normally hidden from view.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hiding extensions protects users from accidentally changing them when renaming files.","The Hidden attribute is a separate file attribute. It's revealed by the \"Show hidden files, folders, and drives\" option.","System files are protected by Windows File/Resource Protection, which stops even administrators deleting them.","The guide names a corrupted index as a common cause of search problems, not visible extensions."],"optionRefs":["p. 383","p. 383","p. 383","p. 383"],"ref":"p. 383, File Explorer Options","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-13","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"Users report that Windows search no longer finds files that definitely exist in indexed folders. What is a common cause, and which tool fixes it?","context":"Users report that Windows search no longer finds files that definitely exist in indexed folders. What is a common cause, and which tool fixes it?","options":["A corrupted index; rebuild it in the Indexing Options applet.","Hidden extensions; show them in File Explorer Options instead.","Fragmented files; run Defragment and Optimize Drives on them.","Too little space; run Disk Cleanup to remove unwanted files."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Search behavior is governed by the Search tab in File Explorer Options and by the Indexing Options applet, which defines indexed locations (folders and email data stores) and can rebuild the index. A corrupted index is a common cause of search problems.","optionWhy":["Correct. A corrupted index commonly causes search problems, and Indexing Options can rebuild it.","Hiding extensions affects how file names are displayed, not whether search can find the files.","Defragment and Optimize Drives maintains disk performance by optimizing file storage. It isn't the guide's fix for search problems.","Disk Cleanup regains disk capacity. It doesn't repair the search index."],"optionRefs":["p. 383","p. 383","p. 400","p. 400"],"ref":"p. 383, Indexing Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-14","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"In the advanced system settings, what does the environment variable %SYSTEMROOT% expand to by default?","context":"In the advanced system settings, what does the environment variable %SYSTEMROOT% expand to by default?","options":["C:\\Windows","C:\\Users","C:\\Program Files","C:\\"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Environment variables set useful file paths. %SYSTEMROOT% expands to the location of the Windows folder, which is C:\\Windows by default.","optionWhy":["Correct. %SYSTEMROOT% points to the Windows folder, C:\\Windows by default.","C:\\Users stores user profile settings and data. It isn't the system root.","Program Files holds installed applications. It isn't what %SYSTEMROOT% points to.","C:\\ is the root directory of the C: drive. The system root is the Windows folder beneath it."],"optionRefs":["p. 387","p. 382","p. 382","p. 382"],"ref":"pp. 386–387, System Settings","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-15","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A home user asks how to turn Windows Update off permanently using the Settings app. What should the technician tell her?","context":"A home user asks how to turn Windows Update off permanently using the Settings app. What should the technician tell her?","options":["In Settings it can only be paused for a while, not disabled.","Use the Disable Windows Update switch in the Settings app.","Turning on game mode will disable Windows Update for good.","Remove the Activation key so that updates no longer apply."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Update detection and scheduling are configured in Settings. In the basic interface, Windows Update can only be paused temporarily; it can't be completely disabled. You can check for updates manually and choose which optional updates to apply.","optionWhy":["Correct. The basic interface allows updates to be paused temporarily but not disabled completely.","The guide says Windows Update cannot be completely disabled in the basic interface.","Game mode suspends Windows Update while a game is active. It isn't a permanent off switch.","Activation verifies that Windows is legitimately licensed. Removing it disables certain features but isn't a way to control updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 388","p. 388","p. 398","p. 389"],"ref":"p. 388, Windows Update","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-16","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A cumulative update keeps failing to install on a workstation. Where should you look to find the cause?","context":"A cumulative update keeps failing to install on a workstation. Where should you look to find the cause?","options":["The WindowsUpdateClient Operational log in Event Viewer","The Performance tab in the advanced system settings","The Activation page under Update & Security Settings","The Indexing Options applet's troubleshooting section"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Update activity is recorded by Event Viewer in Applications and Service Logs > Microsoft\\Windows > WindowsUpdateClient > Operational. If an update fails, check that log; the update fails with an error code that can be looked up on the Microsoft Knowledge Base.","optionWhy":["Correct. Event Viewer's WindowsUpdateClient log records update activity and error codes.","Performance options control visual effects, virtual memory, and process priority. They don't record update failures.","The Activation page shows licensing status and lets you enter a product key. It isn't an update log.","Indexing Options manages the search index. It has nothing to do with Windows Update failures."],"optionRefs":["p. 388","p. 386","p. 389","p. 383"],"ref":"p. 388, Windows Update (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-17","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"What happens if Windows isn't activated before the end of its grace period?","context":"What happens if Windows isn't activated before the end of its grace period?","options":["Certain features are disabled until it's activated.","Windows deletes user data and reinstalls itself.","Windows Update stops until a key is entered.","All user accounts are converted to standard users."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Microsoft Product Activation is anti-piracy technology that verifies software was legitimately purchased. Windows must be activated within a given number of days. After the grace period, certain features are disabled until the system is activated using a valid product key or digital license.","optionWhy":["Correct. Features are disabled after the grace period until valid activation.","The guide describes only features being disabled, not data deletion or reinstallation.","The guide doesn't link activation to Windows Update. It says certain features are disabled after the grace period.","Account privilege levels are set in the User Accounts settings, not by activation status."],"optionRefs":["p. 389","p. 389","p. 389","p. 376"],"ref":"p. 389, Activation","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-18","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"An old scanner's vendor only offers a 32-bit driver, and the office PCs run 64-bit Windows 11. What will happen if the technician tries to use that driver?","context":"An old scanner's vendor only offers a 32-bit driver, and the office PCs run 64-bit Windows 11. What will happen if the technician tries to use that driver?","options":["It won't work, because 64-bit Windows needs 64-bit drivers.","It will run under WOW64, which translates 32-bit requests.","Plug and Play converts the driver to 64-bit automatically.","It will work if it's installed in the Program Files (x86)."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a 64-bit edition of Windows, you must obtain 64-bit device drivers; 32-bit drivers won't work. (32-bit applications can run on 64-bit Windows through WOW64, but that applies to applications, not drivers.)","optionWhy":["Correct. With a 64-bit edition of Windows, you must obtain 64-bit device drivers; 32-bit drivers won't work.","WOW64 lets 32-bit applications run on 64-bit Windows. The guide states that 32-bit drivers won't work.","Plug and Play detects devices and locates and installs drivers. It doesn't convert driver architecture.","Program Files (x86) is where 32-bit applications are installed. It doesn't make 32-bit drivers work."],"optionRefs":["p. 389","pp. 389, 406","p. 389","pp. 382, 389"],"ref":"p. 389, Device Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-19","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A user has a large high-resolution monitor and complains that the text and icons are far too small to read comfortably. Which display setting should be adjusted?","context":"A user has a large high-resolution monitor and complains that the text and icons are far too small to read comfortably. Which display setting should be adjusted?","options":["Scale","Color calibration","Refresh rate","Display arrangement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A large high-resolution screen can use very small font sizes. Scaling makes the system use proportionally larger fonts. TFT and OLED screens are designed for their native resolution, so lowering the resolution isn't the recommended fix.","optionWhy":["Correct. Scaling makes Windows use proportionally larger fonts on high-resolution screens.","Color calibration makes sure colors match a designer's intentions. It doesn't change text size.","Screens are designed to run at their native refresh rate. Changing it doesn't make text larger.","Arranging multiple displays sets their relative positions so the cursor moves predictably between them."],"optionRefs":["p. 391","p. 391","pp. 391–392","p. 391"],"ref":"p. 391, Display and Sound Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-20","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which ACPI power-saving mode saves the contents of memory to a file on disk and then turns the computer off?","context":"Which ACPI power-saving mode saves the contents of memory to a file on disk and then turns the computer off?","options":["Hibernate (S4)","Standby (S1–S3)","Powered on (S0)","Soft off (S5)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standby/Suspend to RAM (ACPI S1–S3) cuts power to most devices but keeps memory powered. Hibernate/Suspend to Disk (ACPI S4) saves open data from memory to hiberfil.sys in the root of the boot volume and then turns the computer off. S5 is soft power off.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hibernate saves memory contents to hiberfil.sys and powers the computer off.","Standby keeps memory powered and cuts power to other devices. It doesn't write memory to disk.","S0 is the powered-on state. Modern Standby uses an S0 low-power idle mode but doesn't save memory to disk and power off.","S5 is soft power off. Nothing in memory is preserved for resuming."],"optionRefs":["p. 393","p. 393","pp. 392–393","p. 392"],"ref":"pp. 392–393, Power Options","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-21","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A user's laptop runs its battery flat because it doesn't turn off when he closes the lid. Where can a technician set what closing the lid does?","context":"A user's laptop runs its battery flat because it doesn't turn off when he closes the lid. Where can a technician set what closing the lid does?","options":["The Power Options applet in Control Panel","The Ease of Access Interaction settings","The Devices and Printers Control Panel","The Personalization lock screen settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The System Power settings configure timers for turning off the screen and sleeping. The Control Panel Power Options applet exposes more options, including what pressing the power button or closing the laptop lid does, such as shut down, sleep, or hibernate.","optionWhy":["Correct. Power Options lets you define what closing the lid or pressing the power button does.","Interaction settings configure keyboard and mouse usability and speech or eye-controlled input, not power behavior.","Devices and Printers is for adding devices manually and reaching their configuration pages. It doesn't set lid actions.","Personalization sets themes, wallpaper, screen saver, and similar appearance options, not power actions."],"optionRefs":["p. 393","p. 380","p. 390","p. 379"],"ref":"p. 393, Power Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-22","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A user's desktop PC is configured to use hybrid sleep. What happens when it goes to sleep?","context":"A user's desktop PC is configured to use hybrid sleep. What happens when it goes to sleep?","options":["It creates a hibernation file and then goes into standby.","It sleeps but switches to hibernate if the battery runs low.","It stays in S0 low-power idle to keep network connectivity.","It restores RAM from the hibernation file for a quick boot."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On a desktop, Windows creates a hibernation file and then goes into the standby state. This is hybrid sleep. It can also be set to switch to full hibernation after a defined period. A laptop goes into standby and switches to hibernate on battery before the battery runs down.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hybrid sleep writes a hibernation file and then enters standby.","That describes how a laptop's sleep works on battery power, not desktop hybrid sleep.","That describes Modern Standby, which uses an S0 low-power idle mode.","That describes Fast Startup, configured in the Power Options applet."],"optionRefs":["p. 393","p. 393","p. 393","p. 394"],"ref":"p. 393, Power Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-23","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A user with only a standard account wants to install a note-taking app. Which type of installation does not require administrator privileges or a UAC confirmation?","context":"A user with only a standard account wants to install a note-taking app. Which type of installation does not require administrator privileges or a UAC confirmation?","options":["A Microsoft Store app","An MSI desktop installer","A setup.exe desktop app","An optional Windows Feature"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Store apps run in a restrictive sandbox that prevents system-wide changes, so standard users can install them without UAC or administrator privileges. Desktop apps installed with a setup program or MSI installer require administrator privileges. Windows Features are OS components enabled through the Settings app or Programs and Features.","optionWhy":["Correct. Store apps run in a sandbox, so standard users can install them without UAC or admin privileges.","Desktop apps installed by a setup program or MSI installer require administrator privileges.","Running a setup program to install a desktop app requires administrator privileges.","Windows Features, such as Hyper-V, are OS components that are enabled or disabled through Apps settings or Programs and Features. The guide describes Store apps, not Features, as installable by standard users."],"optionRefs":["p. 395","p. 395","p. 395","p. 395"],"ref":"pp. 394–395, Apps, Programs, and Features","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-24","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A developer wants to install Ubuntu and run Linux command-line tools on his Windows PC without creating a virtual machine or setting up dual boot. What should he use?","context":"A developer wants to install Ubuntu and run Linux command-line tools on his Windows PC without creating a virtual machine or setting up dual boot. What should he use?","options":["Windows Subsystem for Linux","Hyper-V virtualization role","The WOW64 environment layer","A user-context AppData install"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Subsystem for Linux (WSL) allows a Linux distribution to be installed and Linux applications to be used without a virtual machine or dual boot. Hyper-V is a virtualization platform that can be installed as an optional Windows Feature.","optionWhy":["Correct. WSL installs a Linux distribution and runs Linux apps without a VM or dual boot.","Hyper-V is a virtualization platform, so using it would mean creating the virtual machine he wants to avoid.","WOW64 lets 32-bit Windows applications run on 64-bit Windows. It doesn't run Linux.","User-context applications install in a user's AppData folder without admin rights. That's an install location, not a way to run Linux."],"optionRefs":["p. 395","p. 395","p. 406","p. 395"],"ref":"pp. 394–395, Apps, Programs, and Features","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-25","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A technician needs to add a second email profile and check which .PST and .OST data files Microsoft Outlook is using on a PC. Which Control Panel applet provides these basic settings?","context":"A technician needs to add a second email profile and check which .PST and .OST data files Microsoft Outlook is using on a PC. Which Control Panel applet provides these basic settings?","options":["Mail","Internet Options","Programs and Features","Credential Manager"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Mail applet is added to Control Panel when the Microsoft Outlook client is installed. It can add email accounts and profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration is done in Outlook's own settings.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Mail applet manages Outlook profiles, accounts, and .OST/.PST data files.","Internet Options configures web browser settings, such as which types of active content may run.","Programs and Features is the legacy interface for installing and modifying desktop apps and Windows Features.","Credential Manager appears in the Control Panel list, but the guide names the Mail applet as the place to manage Outlook profiles and data files."],"optionRefs":["p. 397","p. 398","p. 397","pp. 375, 397"],"ref":"p. 397, Mail","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-26","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"What does enabling game mode in Windows do?","context":"What does enabling game mode in Windows do?","options":["It suspends Windows Update and gives resources to the active game.","It turns off Windows Defender Firewall to lower network latency.","It favors background services over the app in the foreground.","It switches the display to a lower resolution to raise frame rate."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game, rather than to other software or background services. The Gaming page also manages captures, recording, and in-game chat or broadcast features.","optionWhy":["Correct. Game mode suspends updates and prioritizes the game's 3-D performance and frame rate.","The guide doesn't say game mode changes the firewall. It suspends Windows Update and prioritizes the game.","This is the opposite. Game mode dedicates resources to the active game rather than background services.","Screens are designed to run at their native resolution, and the guide doesn't say game mode changes it."],"optionRefs":["p. 398","p. 398","p. 398","pp. 391, 398"],"ref":"p. 398, Gaming","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-27","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A laptop connects to the Wi-Fi in a coffee shop. Which network profile should it be assigned, and what is the effect?","context":"A laptop connects to the Wi-Fi in a coffee shop. Which network profile should it be assigned, and what is the effect?","options":["Public, which applies more restrictive firewall policies","Private, which applies more restrictive firewall policies","Domain, which joins the laptop to the shop's own network","Public, which turns off the firewall to allow guest access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each network an adapter connects to is assigned a trust profile: public, private, or domain. The profile type determines firewall settings, and a public network has more restrictive firewall policies than a private or domain network.","optionWhy":["Correct. Public networks get more restrictive firewall policies than private or domain networks.","Private is for trusted networks and is less restrictive than public. A coffee shop network should be public.","The domain profile is for the organization's own domain network. It doesn't join you to a shop's network.","A public profile makes the firewall more restrictive, not disabled."],"optionRefs":["p. 398","p. 398","pp. 376, 398","p. 398"],"ref":"p. 398, Network Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-28","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Users on a small office network can't see each other's PCs or shared printers in File Explorer's Network view. Which Control Panel applet turns on network discovery and file and printer sharing?","context":"Users on a small office network can't see each other's PCs or shared printers in File Explorer's Network view. Which Control Panel applet turns on network discovery and file and printer sharing?","options":["Advanced sharing settings","Network Connections (ncpa.cpl)","Network and Sharing Center","Internet Options Security tab"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Advanced sharing settings configures network discovery (detecting other hosts on the network) and enables or disables file and printer sharing. Network Connections (ncpa.cpl) manages adapters and IP addresses, and Network and Sharing Center shows status information.","optionWhy":["Correct. This applet configures network discovery and file and printer sharing.","Network Connections manages adapter devices and their IP address information, not discovery and sharing.","Despite its name, the guide describes Network and Sharing Center as showing status information.","The Internet Options Security tab restricts risky active content in web browsers."],"optionRefs":["p. 398","p. 398","p. 398","p. 398"],"ref":"p. 398, Network Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-29","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A technician wants a backup script to run automatically every night at 02:00 on a Windows workstation. Which management console should be used?","context":"A technician wants a backup script to run automatically every night at 02:00 on a Windows workstation. Which management console should be used?","options":["Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc)","Services console (services.msc)","Event Viewer (eventvwr.msc)","Local Security Policy (secpol.msc)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc) runs software and scripts according to calendar or event triggers. The Services console starts, stops, and pauses background processes, and Event Viewer reviews logs.","optionWhy":["Correct. Task Scheduler runs programs and scripts on calendar or event triggers.","The Services console starts, stops, and pauses background processes. It doesn't schedule scripts by time.","Event Viewer reviews system, security, and application logs.","Local Security Policy (secpol.msc) views and edits security settings. It has no scheduling function."],"optionRefs":["p. 400","p. 400","p. 400","p. 400"],"ref":"pp. 399–400, Administrative Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-30","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A PC's system drive is nearly full of temporary and other unwanted files. Which built-in tool is designed to regain disk capacity by deleting them?","context":"A PC's system drive is nearly full of temporary and other unwanted files. Which built-in tool is designed to regain disk capacity by deleting them?","options":["Disk Cleanup (cleanmgr.exe)","Defragment and Optimize (dfrgui.exe)","Resource Monitor (resmon.exe)","Registry Editor (regedit.exe)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk Cleanup (cleanmgr.exe) regains disk capacity by deleting unwanted files. Defragment and Optimize Drives (dfrgui.exe) maintains performance by optimizing file storage patterns, and Resource Monitor views performance statistics.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disk Cleanup deletes unwanted files to regain capacity.","This tool optimizes file storage patterns for performance. It doesn't free space by deleting files.","Resource Monitor (resmon.exe) views and logs performance statistics. It doesn't delete files to free space.","Registry Editor makes manual edits to the configuration database. It isn't for removing files."],"optionRefs":["p. 400","p. 400","p. 400","p. 400"],"ref":"pp. 399–400, Administrative Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-31","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"Which keyboard shortcut opens the Run dialog, which can launch a program with switches such as perfmon /comp?","context":"Which keyboard shortcut opens the Run dialog, which can launch a program with switches such as perfmon /comp?","options":["WINDOWS + R","WINDOWS + X","START + SPACE","WINDOWS, then type"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WINDOWS+R opens the Run dialog, which can run programs with switches, Settings pages through URIs such as ms-settings:system, and Control Panel applets with commands such as control ncpa.cpl. WINDOWS+X opens the shortcut menu of management utilities, and START+SPACE toggles the input language.","optionWhy":["Correct. WINDOWS+R opens the Run dialog, which can execute a program with switches that modify how it runs.","WINDOWS+X shows the shortcut menu with links to utilities such as Device Manager and Computer Management.","START+SPACE toggles the active input language when multiple languages are enabled.","Pressing WINDOWS and typing a name uses the Instant Search box on the Start menu to run programs by simple name. It doesn't open the Run dialog, which is what accepts switches."],"optionRefs":["p. 403","p. 401","p. 378","p. 403"],"ref":"pp. 400–403, Management Shortcuts","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-32","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"A user wants to install the 64-bit version of a statistics package on an older PC that runs a 32-bit edition of Windows on a 64-bit CPU. What will happen?","context":"A user wants to install the 64-bit version of a statistics package on an older PC that runs a 32-bit edition of Windows on a 64-bit CPU. What will happen?","options":["It can't be installed, because the OS is 32-bit as well.","It will install, because the CPU itself is 64-bit capable.","It will run under WOW64, which translates for the 32-bit OS.","It will install to Program Files (x86) and then run normally."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A 64-bit application requires both a 64-bit CPU and a 64-bit OS, so it can't be installed on a 32-bit platform. 32-bit applications can usually be installed on 64-bit platforms, where they run under WOW64.","optionWhy":["Correct. A 64-bit app needs a 64-bit CPU and a 64-bit OS; a 32-bit OS isn't enough.","Both the CPU and the OS must be 64-bit. A 64-bit CPU running a 32-bit OS can't run the app.","WOW64 works the other way: it lets 32-bit apps run on 64-bit Windows.","Program Files (x86) is where 64-bit Windows stores 32-bit apps. It doesn't let 64-bit apps run on a 32-bit OS."],"optionRefs":["pp. 404, 406","p. 406","p. 406","p. 406"],"ref":"pp. 404, 406, System Requirements for Applications","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-33","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"An application's installer says the PC lacks hardware-assisted virtualization, but the CPU's specification sheet says it supports this feature. What should you check first?","context":"An application's installer says the PC lacks hardware-assisted virtualization, but the CPU's specification sheet says it supports this feature. What should you check first?","options":["The system setup program, in case the feature is disabled","The Compatibility tab of the installer's Properties dialog","Whether Windows Subsystem for Linux has been installed yet","Whether the PC has a dedicated graphics card with video RAM"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An application may require a particular CPU feature, such as hardware-assisted virtualization or a TPM. If a required feature isn't detected, check the system setup program to make sure it hasn't simply been disabled.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to check that the feature hasn't been disabled in system setup.","The Compatibility tab shows an executable's application compatibility settings. It can't enable a CPU feature.","WSL runs Linux apps without a VM. It isn't a prerequisite for the CPU's virtualization feature.","Dedicated graphics is a separate requirement for demanding apps such as games and design software, not for CPU virtualization support."],"optionRefs":["p. 405","p. 406","p. 395","p. 405"],"ref":"pp. 404–405, Central Processing Unit, System Memory, and Storage Requirements","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-34","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"A design team's new 3-D rendering software refuses to install on office PCs that use graphics built into the CPU. What does the software most likely require?","context":"A design team's new 3-D rendering software refuses to install on office PCs that use graphics built into the CPU. What does the software most likely require?","options":["A dedicated graphics card with its own video RAM","More integrated graphics memory from the chipset","An external hardware token for the design users","A monitor running below its native resolution"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Graphics built into the CPU or motherboard chipset are called integrated graphics. Demanding applications, such as graphic design software or games, are likely to need a dedicated graphics card with its own video RAM, separate from system RAM.","optionWhy":["Correct. Demanding graphics apps typically need a dedicated card with its own video RAM.","Integrated graphics are what the PCs already have. The guide says demanding apps need a dedicated card instead.","A hardware token is a smart card or USB device for stronger authentication, not graphics performance.","TFT and OLED screens are designed for their native resolution. Lowering it doesn't meet a graphics card requirement."],"optionRefs":["p. 405","p. 405","pp. 405–406","pp. 391–392"],"ref":"p. 405, Dedicated Graphics Card Requirements","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-35","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"An application requires users to authenticate with an external hardware token. How does a user sign in with it?","context":"An application requires users to authenticate with an external hardware token. How does a user sign in with it?","options":["The user presents the token and gives a PIN, password, or fingerprint.","The token replaces all other credentials, so no PIN is ever needed.","The token holds the app's license so it can be activated offline.","The token adds a dedicated processor to speed up the app's work."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An external hardware token is a smart card or USB device that stores cryptographic user identification data. The user must present the token and also supply a password, PIN, or fingerprint scan to authenticate.","optionWhy":["Correct. The token is presented together with a password, PIN, or fingerprint.","The guide says the user must present the token and also supply a password, PIN, or fingerprint.","The token stores cryptographic user identification data. Licensing and activation are separate.","A token is an authentication device, not a performance upgrade."],"optionRefs":["p. 406","p. 406","pp. 389, 405–406","pp. 405–406"],"ref":"pp. 405–406, External Hardware Token Requirements","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-36","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"A designer sends you an installer for a macOS application. Which pair of file formats would you expect it to use?","context":"A designer sends you an installer for a macOS application. Which pair of file formats would you expect it to use?","options":["DMG and PKG","EXE and MSI","DEB and RPM","ISO and APK"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows desktop apps use .EXE or .MSI setup files. macOS apps can use DMG or PKG formats. Linux packages use DEB with the APT package manager, or RPM with YUM.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists DMG and PKG as the formats that macOS apps can use.","EXE and MSI are the extensions used by Windows desktop application setup files.","DEB (used with APT) and RPM (used with YUM) are Linux package formats.","An ISO holds the contents of an optical disc in one file. APK is the Android app format from Module 20."],"optionRefs":["p. 406","p. 406","p. 406","p. 407"],"ref":"p. 406, Distribution Methods","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-37","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"You download a Linux software package from the internet. How should you verify that it's authentic and hasn't been altered?","context":"You download a Linux software package from the internet. How should you verify that it's authentic and hasn't been altered?","options":["Make her own hash of it, then compare it to the maintainer's.","Check for a UAC prompt naming the publisher when it installs.","Mount it as an ISO and check that it gets a new drive letter.","Install it on a single PC and see if any errors are reported."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When downloading an installer, verify its authenticity and integrity and scan it for malware. Linux software is verified by publishing a hash value of the package: generate your own hash of the download and compare it to the value published by the package maintainer. Windows uses digital signatures to identify valid developers.","optionWhy":["Correct. Comparing your hash of the download with the published value verifies a Linux package.","Publisher information and digital signatures are how Windows identifies valid developers. The guide's method for Linux packages is hash comparison.","Mounting an ISO just makes its contents appear as a drive. It doesn't verify integrity.","Installing first defeats the purpose. Integrity should be verified before installation."],"optionRefs":["p. 406","pp. 406–407","p. 407","p. 406"],"ref":"p. 406, Distribution Methods","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-38","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"A large database application is supplied as a single ISO file on a Windows PC. What is the simplest way to start installing it?","context":"A large database application is supplied as a single ISO file on a Windows PC. What is the simplest way to start installing it?","options":["Right-click the ISO and choose Mount, then use its new drive letter.","Rename the file extension from .iso to .msi and then run the file.","Enable Windows Subsystem for Linux so it can read the ISO format.","Install it through the Microsoft Store using the sandboxed method."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An ISO file contains the contents of an optical disc in a single file. A mountable ISO is often used for complex apps such as databases. In Windows, right-click the ISO and select Mount; it appears in File Explorer with the next available drive letter.","optionWhy":["Correct. Mounting the ISO makes it appear in File Explorer as a drive.","Changing an extension doesn't convert a file. The guide warns that overtyping extensions can make files hard to open.","WSL is for running Linux distributions and apps. Windows can mount ISOs directly.","Store apps are installed from the Microsoft Store, not from a local ISO file."],"optionRefs":["p. 407","pp. 383, 407","pp. 395, 407","p. 395"],"ref":"pp. 406–407, Distribution Methods","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-39","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"What does the term \"shadow IT\" refer to?","context":"What does the term \"shadow IT\" refer to?","options":["Software and devices used without IT's approval","Apps that run hidden inside the Store app sandbox","Files that are marked with the Hidden attribute","Installs that are pushed to clients overnight"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IT should maintain control and oversight of all third-party software installed on network hosts. Unsanctioned software and devices, known as shadow IT, raise substantial operational and business risks.","optionWhy":["Correct. Shadow IT is unsanctioned software and devices, which raise operational and business risks.","The Store app sandbox stops apps from making system-wide changes. It has nothing to do with shadow IT.","Hidden files are a file attribute that File Explorer can be set to show. They aren't shadow IT.","Scheduling network installs outside working hours is a sanctioned way to avoid congestion, not shadow IT."],"optionRefs":["p. 408","p. 395","p. 383","p. 408"],"ref":"pp. 407–408, Other Considerations","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-40","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"IT plans to push a large application to 300 desktops over the network using Group Policy. Why should this be scheduled outside normal working hours?","context":"IT plans to push a large application to 300 desktops over the network using Group Policy. Why should this be scheduled outside normal working hours?","options":["Network pushes cause congestion and add latency for other users.","GPOs only apply when no users are signed in to the client PCs.","Users must be at their desks to approve the UAC admin prompts.","Licenses can only be activated at night when servers are quiet."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pushing an installation or system image over the network causes congestion and increases latency. Many organizations limit over-the-network deployments to non-critical times, such as overnight or weekends, to avoid disrupting normal operations.","optionWhy":["Correct. Deployment traffic congests the network, so it's scheduled for non-critical times.","The guide doesn't say this. It recommends off-hours scheduling because of network congestion.","GPO deployment means users don't need to log on with admin privileges. Setup can run under a service account.","Activation and licensing aren't tied to time of day in the guide. Congestion is the reason given."],"optionRefs":["p. 408","p. 408","p. 408","pp. 389, 408"],"ref":"p. 408, Impact to Operation","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-41","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"After an application is deployed with Group Policy, where should users' own files and personal settings for the app be saved?","context":"After an application is deployed with Group Policy, where should users' own files and personal settings for the app be saved?","options":["In the user's home folder or profile","In the app's own installation folder","In the Windows\\System32 directory","In the network share it installed from"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To run an application, users need read/execute permission on its installation directory. Write and modify permissions there are restricted to administrators. Files created with the app and user-specific settings or preferences should be saved to the user's home folder or profile, not to the application directory.","optionWhy":["Correct. User-created files and personal settings belong in the user's home folder or profile.","Users get only read/execute permission there, and the guide says not to save user files to the application directory.","System32 holds system files required by the OS, not user data.","The shared folder holds the setup file that clients run during deployment. It isn't for users' own files."],"optionRefs":["p. 408","p. 408","pp. 382, 406","p. 408"],"ref":"p. 408, Impact to Operation","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-42","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"A user finds a free \"PDF converter\" on an unfamiliar website and asks you to install it. What is the principal threat, and how do you mitigate it?","context":"A user finds a free \"PDF converter\" on an unfamiliar website and asks you to install it. What is the principal threat, and how do you mitigate it?","options":["A Trojan Horse; use only trusted, digitally signed sources.","Shadow IT; ask the user to install it from AppData instead.","Licensing; buy a single-user license for every PC using it.","Congestion; schedule the installation for late in the night."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The principal threat is a Trojan Horse: software whose true, malicious purpose is concealed, often to steal data or provide covert remote access. Setup files can also carry viruses. Mitigate by installing only from trusted sources, with installer code digitally signed by a reputable publisher, and ideally by testing in a lab first.","optionWhy":["Correct. Trojans are the principal threat, mitigated by trusted sources and signed code.","Installing it without IT approval would itself be shadow IT. A user-context AppData install does nothing to reduce the malware risk.","Licensing is a business consideration. The guide names Trojans as the principal security threat when selecting apps.","Off-hours scheduling addresses network congestion from mass deployments, not malicious software."],"optionRefs":["pp. 408–409","pp. 395, 408","pp. 408, 412","p. 408"],"ref":"pp. 408–409, Impact to Device and to Network","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-43","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"A sales rep is working from a hotel on a metered connection that charges per gigabyte. She uses a cloud storage app that syncs her work folders. What should she do?","context":"A sales rep is working from a hotel on a metered connection that charges per gigabyte. She uses a cloud storage app that syncs her work folders. What should she do?","options":["Pause synchronization while she's on the metered connection.","Uninstall the cloud storage app until she's back at the office.","Set the hotel network to the private profile to lower data use.","Enable game mode so Windows suspends background data transfers."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cloud storage can sync selected files and folders. You can pause or suspend synchronization, which helps on a metered connection with limited bandwidth or higher data costs.","optionWhy":["Correct. Pausing sync avoids data costs on a metered connection.","Uninstalling is unnecessary. The guide notes sync can simply be paused or suspended.","Network profiles determine firewall settings. They don't reduce cloud sync traffic.","Game mode suspends Windows Update and prioritizes a game. It isn't a cloud sync control."],"optionRefs":["p. 411","p. 411","p. 398","p. 398"],"ref":"p. 411, Storage","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-44","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"A user editing a spreadsheet in the Office 365 web portal needs a feature that isn't available in the browser version. What should she do?","context":"A user editing a spreadsheet in the Office 365 web portal needs a feature that isn't available in the browser version. What should she do?","options":["Switch to the installed desktop version of the app.","Ask IT to assign a different license in the portal.","Clear the browser's settings under Internet Options.","Re-sync the file using the OneDrive folder's status."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some cloud-based collaboration tools run in the web browser and offer an option to switch to the desktop version. In Office 365, a user can switch from the web portal to the installed desktop app, which is useful when a feature is available only in the desktop version.","optionWhy":["Correct. The web portal can switch to the desktop app, which may have extra features.","Licenses are assigned through an admin portal such as Microsoft Entra, but the guide's answer to a missing web feature is the desktop app.","Internet Options configures browser security settings. It won't add application features.","OneDrive status icons show whether files are synchronized. They don't change which app features are available."],"optionRefs":["p. 412","pp. 412–413","p. 398","p. 411"],"ref":"p. 412, Collaboration Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-45","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"A small business buys one single-installation, single-user license for an application and installs it on five office PCs used by different staff. Is this allowed?","context":"A small business buys one single-installation, single-user license for an application and installs it on five office PCs used by different staff. Is this allowed?","options":["It's likely a violation of the license terms.","It's allowed as long as one person uses it.","It's allowed if the PCs share a domain.","It's fine for cloud apps but not for local apps."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A single-installation, single-user license normally authorizes installation on one system for use by one user. Installing it on several systems or letting multiple users use it could be considered a violation of the license terms.","optionWhy":["Correct. A single-user license covers one installation for one user.","The license limits installations as well as users. Five installations exceed one system.","Domain membership doesn't change the license terms. The guide says to use the appropriate license type.","The guide says cloud licensing works much like local licensing, with similar terms and limits."],"optionRefs":["p. 412","p. 412","pp. 412–413","p. 412"],"ref":"p. 412, User Licensing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-46","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"You need to assign Microsoft 365 cloud application licenses to a group of new employees. Which tool should you use?","context":"You need to assign Microsoft 365 cloud application licenses to a group of new employees. Which tool should you use?","options":["Microsoft Entra admin center","Programs and Features applet","Local Security Policy console","Windows Activation page"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Licenses for cloud applications can be assigned to employees through an administrative portal, such as the Microsoft Entra admin center. If the wrong license is assigned, change the license or uninstall and reinstall with the correct one.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide shows license assignment in the Microsoft Entra admin center.","Programs and Features is the legacy interface for installing and modifying desktop applications and Windows Features.","Local Security Policy (secpol.msc) views and edits a computer's security settings.","The Activation page shows Windows licensing status and accepts a product key for that PC only."],"optionRefs":["p. 413","p. 397","p. 400","p. 389"],"ref":"pp. 412–413, User Licensing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-47","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"What is a key benefit of synchronizing identities between a cloud identity provider and on-premises systems?","context":"What is a key benefit of synchronizing identities between a cloud identity provider and on-premises systems?","options":["Users sign in to cloud and on-premises resources with one set of credentials.","Users can copy cloud files to on-premises PCs without needing any network.","Each cloud provider gets its own separate set of credentials for security.","Permissions are managed separately so each environment stays independent."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With identity synchronization, users have a single set of credentials for both cloud resources and on-premises resources such as their workstation. Fewer credentials need remembering, and the same access-control policies and permissions apply across the whole environment, which also eases management and troubleshooting.","optionWhy":["Correct. One set of credentials, with permissions kept consistent across environments.","Identity synchronization concerns credentials and permissions, not file transfer.","The guide describes synchronization as removing the need for a separate set of credentials for each provider.","Synchronization applies the same control policies across cloud and on-premises systems, keeping them consistent."],"optionRefs":["p. 413","p. 413","p. 413","p. 413"],"ref":"p. 413, Identity Synchronization","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-48","module":12,"lesson":"12D Cloud-Based Applications","question":"After installing a videoconferencing desktop app, a user can join meetings but others can't see or hear her. What is the most likely configuration issue?","context":"After installing a videoconferencing desktop app, a user can join meetings but others can't see or hear her. What is the most likely configuration issue?","options":["The app hasn't been given permission to use the camera and microphone.","The app was installed to Program Files instead of the user's profile.","The cloud sync of her files was paused while she was on a metered link.","Her account is a local account rather than a Microsoft account login."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Collaboration and videoconferencing apps may need permissions adjusted to access local files for synchronization, the web camera and microphone for video and audio calls, or other hardware. In Windows, app permissions in privacy settings allow or deny access to the camera and microphone.","optionWhy":["Correct. Collaboration apps may need camera and microphone permissions, which Windows app permissions control.","Installation location doesn't block camera or microphone access. App permissions control that.","Pausing file sync affects cloud storage, not live audio and video in a meeting.","Account type affects sign-in and settings sync across devices, not device permissions for an app."],"optionRefs":["pp. 377, 412","pp. 377, 382","p. 411","p. 376"],"ref":"p. 412, Collaboration Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-49","module":12,"lesson":"12B Windows System Settings","question":"A laptop user wants to switch quickly between a battery-saving setup and a full-speed setup, each a preset group of display, sleep, and other power settings. What should you show them?","context":"A laptop user wants to switch quickly between a battery-saving setup and a full-speed setup, each a preset group of display, sleep, and other power settings. What should you show them?","options":["Power plans, in the Power Options applet","Fast Startup, also set in Power Options","USB selective suspend for peripheral ports","Hybrid sleep mode for the laptop's standby"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A power plan is a collection of hardware and system settings (display, brightness, sleep, and so on) that manages how the computer uses power. Plans such as Balanced, Power saver, and High performance let users switch between preconfigured option sets in Power Options.","optionWhy":["Correct. A power plan lets the user switch easily between different sets of preconfigured power options, such as Balanced and Power saver.","Fast Startup uses the hibernation file to restore system RAM so the PC is ready sooner. It doesn't switch between sets of settings.","USB selective suspend is one advanced power setting that turns off power to peripheral devices, not a preset group of settings.","Hybrid sleep is how a desktop sleeps: it creates a hibernation file and then enters standby. It isn't a way to switch power setups."],"optionRefs":["p. 394","p. 394","p. 394","p. 393"],"ref":"p. 394, Power Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-50","module":12,"lesson":"12C Install and Configure Applications","question":"An office has used version 1 of an accounting app on Windows 7 for years. Since the PCs were upgraded to Windows 10, the app no longer works fully. What is the most likely fix?","context":"An office has used version 1 of an accounting app on Windows 7 for years. Since the PCs were upgraded to Windows 10, the app no longer works fully. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Upgrade the app to a version that supports Windows 10","Install the macOS version of the app instead","Compile the app from its source code for Windows","Reinstall version 1 again from its original install media"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Every software application is designed to run under a specific operating system, and an application might not be supported for use under newer operating systems. After an OS upgrade, an app may need to be upgraded to a newer version for full compatibility.","optionWhy":["Correct. An application might not be supported under newer operating systems, and after an OS upgrade it may need upgrading to a newer version for full compatibility.","Every application is designed to run under a specific operating system. If you buy the macOS version of an application, it will not run on Windows.","The guide does not describe compiling from source as a fix for an app that lost support after an OS upgrade. The likely fix is upgrading the app to a version that supports the newer OS.","It's version 1 itself that might not be supported under the newer OS. For full compatibility, the app might need to be upgraded to a newer version."],"optionRefs":["p. 406","p. 406","p. 406","p. 406"],"ref":"p. 406, OS Requirements for Applications","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-01","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A newly connected USB barcode scanner shows in Device Manager as \"Unknown Device\" with a yellow exclamation mark. What does this indicate?","context":"A newly connected USB barcode scanner shows in Device Manager as \"Unknown Device\" with a yellow exclamation mark. What does this indicate?","options":["Windows couldn't find a suitable driver for it.","The device was disabled by an administrator.","The device was safely removed but not unplugged.","The device's driver was rolled back to an old one."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows may identify a device's type but fail to find a driver, so it shows as \"Unknown Device\" or a generic type with a yellow exclamation mark. If the device never worked, check that the device and driver are compatible with the OS. Manufacturers often provide updated drivers, and some come through Windows Update as optional updates.","optionWhy":["Correct. A yellow exclamation mark with Unknown Device means no suitable driver was found.","Disabled devices are shown with a down arrow, not a yellow exclamation mark.","Safely Remove Hardware stops or ejects a storage device. It doesn't produce an Unknown Device entry.","Roll Back Driver reverts to a previous working driver. It doesn't leave the device without one."],"optionRefs":["p. 416","p. 419","p. 417","p. 417"],"ref":"p. 416, Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-02","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A laptop's built-in webcam is faulty and keeps crashing video apps, but it can't easily be removed physically, and a replacement won't arrive for two weeks. What is the best interim step in Device Manager?","context":"A laptop's built-in webcam is faulty and keeps crashing video apps, but it can't easily be removed physically, and a replacement won't arrive for two weeks. What is the best interim step in Device Manager?","options":["Disable the device","Update the driver","Scan for changes","Use Safely Remove"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Device Manager you can disable a device that is malfunctioning, or to restrict access while you seek a replacement. Disabling is also useful for devices that are hard to uninstall physically. Disabled devices are shown with a down arrow.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disabling stops a malfunctioning device from being used while you wait for a replacement.","Updating the driver helps when the driver is at fault. Here the hardware is faulty, and the guide recommends disabling it.","Scan for Hardware Changes looks for newly connected devices. It doesn't stop a faulty device from being used.","Safely Remove Hardware is for ejecting removable storage before unplugging it. It isn't for internal devices."],"optionRefs":["p. 419","pp. 417, 419","p. 419","p. 417"],"ref":"pp. 417–419, Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-03","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"On a typical Windows installation, which volume on Disk 0 contains the boot files (usually EFI) and is not assigned a drive letter?","context":"On a typical Windows installation, which volume on Disk 0 contains the boot files (usually EFI) and is not assigned a drive letter?","options":["The system volume","The boot volume","The recovery partition","The data volume"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk 0 typically has at least three volumes. The system volume contains boot files (usually EFI) and has no drive letter. The boot volume contains the OS files and is typically C:. Recovery partitions contain repair or factory-reset tools such as WinRE and have no drive letters.","optionWhy":["Correct. The system volume holds the boot files and has no drive letter.","Despite its name, the boot volume contains the operating system files and is typically C:.","Recovery partitions contain repair or factory-reset tools such as WinRE, not the boot files.","A data volume is an ordinary lettered volume for files. The guide lists the system, boot, and recovery volumes on Disk 0."],"optionRefs":["p. 420","p. 420","p. 420","pp. 419–420"],"ref":"p. 420, Disk Management Console","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-04","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A technician adds a brand-new, unformatted SSD to a workstation. What must she do in Disk Management before she can create partitions on it?","context":"A technician adds a brand-new, unformatted SSD to a workstation. What must she do in Disk Management before she can create partitions on it?","options":["Initialize it with the MBR or GPT partition style","Format it as FAT32 first so that Windows detects it","Convert it to a dynamic disk for better speed","Run chkdsk /r on it to recover any bad sectors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When adding an unformatted disk, you must first initialize it with the MBR or GPT partition style, which defines how partition information is stored. Then create at least one partition and format it with a file system such as NTFS.","optionWhy":["Correct. A new disk must be initialized with MBR or GPT before partitioning.","Formatting is done to a partition after the disk is initialized and partitioned, and NTFS is the usual choice.","The dynamic disks feature is deprecated. Windows now offers Storage Spaces for redundant configurations.","chkdsk scans an existing file system for errors. A blank, uninitialized disk has no file system to check."],"optionRefs":["pp. 420–421","p. 421","p. 421","p. 437"],"ref":"pp. 420–421, Disk Management Console","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-05","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"When formatting a volume, what is the trade-off between using smaller and larger cluster (allocation unit) sizes?","context":"When formatting a volume, what is the trade-off between using smaller and larger cluster (allocation unit) sizes?","options":["Smaller clusters save capacity; larger ones speed large-file I/O.","Smaller clusters speed up large files; larger ones save capacity.","Larger clusters are needed for FAT32; NTFS only uses small ones.","Cluster size only matters on SSDs, as HDDs use fixed 4K sectors."],"answer":0,"explanation":"File systems group 512-byte sectors into clusters of 2, 4, or 8 sectors. Smaller clusters use disk capacity more efficiently, while larger clusters improve I/O performance for large files.","optionWhy":["Correct. Smaller clusters use capacity efficiently, while larger clusters improve I/O performance for large files.","This reverses the trade-off. Larger clusters improve performance for large files.","The guide doesn't tie cluster size to the file system like this. You can choose the allocation unit size when formatting.","Advanced Format 4K sectors are a disk feature. The capacity versus performance trade-off applies to file systems generally."],"optionRefs":["p. 421","p. 421","p. 421","p. 421"],"ref":"pp. 420–421, Disk Management Console","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-06","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A user's D: data partition is almost full, and there is unallocated space directly after it on the same disk. What can be done in Disk Management?","context":"A user's D: data partition is almost full, and there is unallocated space directly after it on the same disk. What can be done in Disk Management?","options":["Extend the D: partition into the unallocated space.","Reformat D: with larger clusters to make it bigger.","Initialize the disk again with GPT to add the space.","Run Disk Cleanup to merge the space into the volume."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Repartitioning lets you expand an existing partition when there is unpartitioned space, or shrink or remove partitions to free space. Reformatting an existing partition deletes all of its files.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disk Management can expand a partition into unpartitioned space.","Reformatting deletes all files and doesn't add capacity. Cluster size affects efficiency and performance, not partition size.","Initialization is for new, unformatted disks. Doing it on a disk in use isn't how you add space to a partition.","Disk Cleanup frees space by deleting unwanted files. It can't change the partition layout."],"optionRefs":["p. 421","p. 421","pp. 420–421","p. 422"],"ref":"pp. 420–421, Disk Management Console","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-07","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"Users complain that a PC has become sluggish, and its C: drive is filling up. At what point does a Windows boot volume start to suffer performance problems from lack of space?","context":"Users complain that a PC has become sluggish, and its C: drive is filling up. At what point does a Windows boot volume start to suffer performance problems from lack of space?","options":["When it has less than 20% free space","When it has less than 200 MB free","When it has less than 50% free space","When its clusters are 4K in size"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Performance suffers if the boot volume has less than 20% free space. A Low Disk Space warning appears below 200 MB. The guide recommends running disk maintenance tools at least monthly and before software installations.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says performance suffers below 20% free space on the boot volume.","200 MB is the point at which the Low Disk Space warning appears. Performance has already suffered well before that.","The guide gives 20% free space as the threshold at which boot volume performance suffers, not 50%.","Cluster size affects capacity efficiency and I/O performance, not the free-space threshold."],"optionRefs":["p. 421","p. 421","p. 421","p. 421"],"ref":"p. 421, Disk Maintenance Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-08","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"What does the Defragment and Optimize Drives tool mainly do when it runs on an SSD?","context":"What does the Defragment and Optimize Drives tool mainly do when it runs on an SSD?","options":["It runs TRIM so the controller can mark deleted data as writable.","It moves all file data into contiguous clusters to cut seek time.","It replaces the controller's wear-leveling routine with its own.","It deletes temporary files and caches to recover free capacity."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters to reduce seek time. On an SSD, the drive controller manages blocks and uses wear leveling, so the optimizer runs TRIM, which lets the controller mark data the OS has deleted as writable. It may also defragment if the SSD holds the OS and Volume Shadow Copy is enabled.","optionWhy":["Correct. On an SSD the optimizer's main job is running TRIM so the controller can mark deleted data as writable.","That describes defragmenting an HDD, where the controller has to seek across the disk.","The SSD's drive controller manages blocks and wear leveling, not the OS tool.","Deleting temporary files and caches to free space is what Disk Clean-up does."],"optionRefs":["p. 421","p. 421","p. 421","p. 422"],"ref":"p. 421, Disk Defragmenter","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-09","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"Disk Clean-up has removed the Recycle Bin and temp files, but the technician also wants to reclaim space used by Windows Update and Defender caches. What should she do?","context":"Disk Clean-up has removed the Recycle Bin and temp files, but the technician also wants to reclaim space used by Windows Update and Defender caches. What should she do?","options":["Run it as administrator and choose \"Clean up system files.\"","Run Defragment and Optimize Drives so it compacts the caches.","Use the format command with /q to clear the system caches.","Run sfc /scannow so it removes the old update cache files."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk Clean-up identifies files that are safe to delete, including Recycle Bin contents and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the \"Clean up system files\" option also reclaims space from caches such as Windows Update and Defender.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Clean up system files option, run as administrator, reclaims space from these caches.","Defragmenting reorganizes file storage for performance. It doesn't delete cache files.","format creates a new file system and deletes all existing data on the drive, which isn't the intent here.","sfc verifies and restores protected system files. It isn't a space-reclaiming tool."],"optionRefs":["p. 422","p. 421","p. 436","p. 438"],"ref":"p. 422, Disk Clean-up","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-10","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"You create a scheduled task to run a maintenance script every night, but the task never runs. The trigger and script path are correct. What is a likely cause?","context":"You create a scheduled task to run a maintenance script every night, but the task never runs. The trigger and script path are correct. What is a likely cause?","options":["The task's account doesn't have enough permissions.","Task Scheduler can only run tasks once, not daily.","Tasks can't be triggered unless a user signs in.","Scripts can only run as one action, not batches."],"answer":0,"explanation":"As well as the file or script path and the trigger, you must enter the credentials the task runs under. If that account lacks sufficient permissions, the task won't execute. All task activity is logged, so failures can be investigated.","optionWhy":["Correct. If the account the task runs under lacks permissions, the task won't execute.","Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule, so a daily schedule is supported.","Sign-in is one possible trigger, but calendar times and other events can trigger tasks too.","Tasks can run applications, batch files, or scripts, and each task can include multiple actions."],"optionRefs":["p. 424","p. 424","p. 424","p. 424"],"ref":"p. 424, Task Scheduler","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-11","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A user's browser trusts a fake banking website because a rogue certification authority's certificate was installed on the PC. Which certificate store holds trusted issuers, and which console manages the computer's certificate store?","context":"A user's browser trusts a fake banking website because a rogue certification authority's certificate was installed on the PC. Which certificate store holds trusted issuers, and which console manages the computer's certificate store?","options":["Trusted Root CAs, managed with certlm.msc","Personal store, managed with secpol.msc","Trusted Root CAs, managed with gpedit.msc","Personal store, managed with certmgr.msc"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Trusted Root Certification Authorities contains certificates from trusted issuers. Trusting an unsafe CA lets a website masquerade as a legitimate service, so compromised certificates should be removed. certmgr.msc manages the current user's certificates, and certlm.msc manages the computer store.","optionWhy":["Correct. Rogue roots sit in the trusted root store, and certlm.msc manages the computer store.","The Personal folder holds certificates issued to the user, not trusted CAs. secpol.msc edits local security settings.","The store is right, but Group Policy Editor configures policy settings rather than managing certificate stores.","certmgr.msc manages the current user's store, and the Personal folder isn't where trusted issuers are kept."],"optionRefs":["pp. 425–426","pp. 425, 427","pp. 425–426","pp. 425–426"],"ref":"pp. 425–426, Certificate Manager","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-12","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"Why might an administrator use the Group Policy Editor rather than Settings or Control Panel?","context":"Why might an administrator use the Group Policy Editor rather than Settings or Control Panel?","options":["It reaches registry settings the GUIs don't expose, without editing the registry.","It is the only tool that can make any change to the registry database at all.","It replaces Device Manager for installing and rolling back device drivers.","Its policies take free-form values only, unlike enabled or disabled toggles."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Settings and Control Panel modify configuration stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't available through them. Group Policy Editor configures these settings without editing the registry directly, applies settings efficiently across many computers, and supports vendor administrative templates. Policies usually use enabled, disabled, or not defined.","optionWhy":["Correct. Group Policy reaches settings that Settings and Control Panel can't, without direct registry edits.","Registry Editor can view and edit the registry directly, and Settings and Control Panel also modify registry-stored settings.","Driver updates and rollbacks are done in Device Manager, not Group Policy.","Most policies use an enabled, disabled, or not defined toggle, though some take discrete values."],"optionRefs":["p. 426","pp. 426–427","pp. 416–417","p. 427"],"ref":"pp. 426–427, Group Policy Editor","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-13","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A technician needs to change which application opens .csv files system-wide. Which registry root key contains file associations and registered application information?","context":"A technician needs to change which application opens .csv files system-wide. Which registry root key contains file associations and registered application information?","options":["HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT","HKEY_CURRENT_CONFIG","HKEY_CURRENT_USER","HKEY_USERS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"HKEY_CLASSES_ROOT (HKCR) contains information about registered applications, file associations, and OLE object classes, and determines which application opens a file type. HKLM manages system-wide settings, HKCU holds the signed-in user's settings, and HKCC reflects the current hardware profile.","optionWhy":["Correct. HKCR holds file associations and determines which app opens a file type.","HKCC reflects the hardware profile used at startup, built dynamically at boot.","HKCU is a subset of HKEY_USERS holding settings for the signed-in user, such as desktop personalization.","HKU contains settings for individual user profiles. It isn't the key the guide links to file associations."],"optionRefs":["p. 428","p. 428","p. 428","p. 428"],"ref":"pp. 427–428, Registry Keys","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-14","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"Where is each user's own registry hive file (NTUSER.DAT) stored?","context":"Where is each user's own registry hive file (NTUSER.DAT) stored?","options":["In that user's profile folder","In C:\\Windows\\System32\\Config","In the Program Files directory","In the hidden system volume"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The registry is stored in binary files called hives. Most hive files are in the C:\\Windows\\System32\\Config folder, but each user profile's hive (NTUSER.DAT) is in that user's profile folder.","optionWhy":["Correct. Each user's NTUSER.DAT hive is in their profile folder.","Most hive files are stored there, but the user hives (NTUSER.DAT) are kept in each profile folder.","Program Files holds installed applications, not registry hives.","The system volume holds boot files (usually EFI) and has no drive letter. It doesn't hold user hives."],"optionRefs":["p. 428","p. 428","p. 428","p. 420"],"ref":"pp. 427–428, Registry Keys","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-15","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"You've fixed a registry setting on one PC and need to apply exactly the same change on 20 other PCs. How should you do this?","context":"You've fixed a registry setting on one PC and need to apply exactly the same change on 20 other PCs. How should you do this?","options":["Export a registry file and merge it on the other PCs.","Copy the System32\\Config hive files to the other PCs.","Use the Find tool to push the value out to other PCs.","Create a scheduled task that reboots each of the PCs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"To copy part of the registry to other computers, select File > Export Registry File in Registry Editor. The exported file can be merged into another registry by double-clicking it or calling it from a script.","optionWhy":["Correct. Exported registry files can be merged on other computers by double-clicking or from a script.","Hive files are the registry's binary store. The guide's method for copying settings is export and merge, not copying hives.","Find searches for keys or values in the local registry. It doesn't copy settings to other computers.","Rebooting doesn't transfer registry settings between computers."],"optionRefs":["p. 429","pp. 428–429","p. 428","p. 424"],"ref":"p. 429, Editing the Registry","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-16","module":13,"lesson":"13A Use Management Consoles","question":"A technician wants a single console window containing Device Manager, Disk Management, and Certificate Manager snap-ins. How can she build it?","context":"A technician wants a single console window containing Device Manager, Disk Management, and Certificate Manager snap-ins. How can she build it?","options":["Run mmc, add the snap-ins, and save it as an .msc file.","Pin all three tools to the taskbar and group them there.","Use Group Policy Editor to merge the tools into a policy.","Export the three tools as a registry file and then merge it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins such as Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Run mmc to create a custom console with your chosen snap-ins and save it as an .msc file in the Administrative Tools folder. Most snap-ins can manage local and remote computers.","optionWhy":["Correct. The mmc command creates a custom console that is saved as an .msc file.","Pinning makes apps quick to launch but doesn't combine snap-ins into one console.","Group Policy Editor is itself a snap-in for configuring policy. It doesn't build consoles.","Exporting registry files copies settings between registries. It doesn't create management consoles."],"optionRefs":["p. 429","p. 429","pp. 426, 429","p. 429"],"ref":"pp. 429–430, Custom Microsoft Management Consoles","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-17","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician types a command and gets the message \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" What should he do?","context":"A technician types a command and gets the message \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" What should he do?","options":["Open Command Prompt with Run as administrator and confirm the UAC prompt.","Change to the C:\\Windows\\System32 folder first and run the command again.","Add the /? switch to the command so it runs with full permissions this time.","Type cls to clear the screen, then re-enter the command with quotes around it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some commands need elevated privileges. To get them, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, choose Run as administrator, and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively, type cmd in Instant Search and press CTRL+SHIFT+ENTER. The title bar then shows \"Administrator: Command Prompt\", and the default folder is C:\\Windows\\System32.","optionWhy":["Correct. Elevation means running the prompt as administrator, for example with right-click or CTRL+SHIFT+ENTER.","An elevated prompt starts in System32, but changing folder in a normal prompt doesn't give it admin privileges.","/? displays help for a command. It doesn't elevate privileges.","cls only clears the screen, and quotes are for arguments containing spaces. Neither gives elevation."],"optionRefs":["p. 431","p. 431","p. 432","p. 432"],"ref":"p. 431, Administrative Command Prompt","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-18","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"How can you display the syntax and available switches for the robocopy command at a Command Prompt?","context":"How can you display the syntax and available switches for the robocopy command at a Command Prompt?","options":["robocopy /?","robocopy /L","robocopy /S","cls robocopy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Type help to list available commands, or help followed by a command name for its syntax. Use the /? switch for command-specific help; for example, netstat /? displays help on netstat.","optionWhy":["Correct. The /? switch shows command-specific help, including syntax and switches.","With robocopy, /L lists the files and directories that would be copied without copying them. It isn't a help switch.","With robocopy, /S copies subdirectories but excludes empty ones. It isn't a help switch.","cls clears the screen. It doesn't show help for another command."],"optionRefs":["p. 432","p. 435","p. 435","p. 432"],"ref":"p. 432, Getting Help","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-19","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician wants the dir command to list only the files that have the Hidden attribute in the current folder. Which command does this?","context":"A technician wants the dir command to list only the files that have the Hidden attribute in the current folder. Which command does this?","options":["dir /a:h","dir /o:h","dir /t:h","dir /a:s"],"answer":0,"explanation":"dir lists files and subdirectories. /o:x sorts the output (n name, s size, e extension, d date), /t:x picks the date field (c created, a accessed, w modified), and /a:x shows files with an attribute (r read-only, h hidden, s system, a archive).","optionWhy":["Correct. The /a switch filters by attribute, and h selects files with the Hidden attribute.","/o sorts the listing and takes n, s, e, or d. It doesn't filter by attribute.","/t chooses which date field is shown: c, a, or w. It doesn't filter by attribute.","/a:s shows files with the System attribute, not the Hidden attribute."],"optionRefs":["p. 433","pp. 432–433","p. 433","p. 433"],"ref":"pp. 432–433, Listing Files and Directories","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-20","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"The command prompt shows C:\\Users\\David\\Documents>. Which command moves the working directory up one level to C:\\Users\\David?","context":"The command prompt shows C:\\Users\\David\\Documents>. Which command moves the working directory up one level to C:\\Users\\David?","options":["cd ..","cd \\","cd David","cd \\Users"],"answer":0,"explanation":"cd changes the working directory. cd .. moves up to the parent directory, cd \\ goes to the root of the drive, cd Documents moves into a subfolder of the current directory, and cd \\Windows goes to a folder off the root.","optionWhy":["Correct. cd .. moves up one level to the parent directory, here C:\\Users\\David.","cd \\ goes straight to the root directory of the drive (C:\\), not one level up.","cd with a folder name looks for that subfolder inside the current directory, and there's no David folder inside Documents.","cd \\Users goes to C:\\Users, two levels up, not to C:\\Users\\David."],"optionRefs":["p. 433","p. 433","p. 433","p. 433"],"ref":"p. 433, Changing the Current Directory","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-21","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"The prompt shows C:\\Reports>, and the technician needs to work on files on the D: drive. Which command makes D: the current drive?","context":"The prompt shows C:\\Reports>, and the technician needs to work on files on the D: drive. Which command makes D: the current drive?","options":["D:","cd D:\\","dir D:","md D:"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The active drive is managed separately from the current directory. To switch drives, type the drive letter and a colon, such as D:, and press ENTER. The prompt then changes to D:\\.","optionWhy":["Correct. Typing the drive letter followed by a colon switches the active drive.","cd changes directories. The guide says the drive is managed separately, and you switch it by typing the letter and a colon.","dir lists the files on a path. Listing D: doesn't make it the current drive.","md creates a new directory, here a folder called D:. It doesn't change the active drive."],"optionRefs":["p. 434","pp. 433–434","p. 432","p. 435"],"ref":"p. 434, Changing the Current Drive","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-22","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician must copy a project folder to a new server, keeping the whole directory structure, including subfolders that are currently empty. Which robocopy switch does this?","context":"A technician must copy a project folder to a new server, keeping the whole directory structure, including subfolders that are currently empty. Which robocopy switch does this?","options":["/E","/S","/L","/xd"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Microsoft recommends robocopy over xcopy because it handles long file names and NTFS attributes better. /E copies all subdirectories including empty ones, /S copies subdirectories but skips empty ones, /L lists what would be copied without copying, and /xd excludes matching directories.","optionWhy":["Correct. /E copies all subdirectories, including empty ones.","/S copies subdirectories but excludes empty ones, so the empty folders would be lost.","/L only lists the files and directories that would be copied. Nothing is actually copied.","/xd excludes directories that match the given names or paths."],"optionRefs":["p. 435","p. 435","p. 435","p. 435"],"ref":"p. 435, Copying Directory Structures","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-23","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician needs to delete a folder named Temp that still contains files and subfolders, without being asked to confirm. Which command does this?","context":"A technician needs to delete a folder named Temp that still contains files and subfolders, without being asked to confirm. Which command does this?","options":["rd /s /q Temp","rd /q Temp","rd Temp","md /s Temp"],"answer":0,"explanation":"rd (or rmdir) deletes an empty directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them as well. The /q switch suppresses confirmation messages (quiet mode).","optionWhy":["Correct. /s removes the contents, and /q suppresses the confirmation prompts.","/q only makes it quiet. Without /s, rd can't remove a directory that has contents.","Plain rd removes only empty directories, and Temp still has files in it.","md (mkdir) creates directories. It doesn't remove them, and /s isn't one of its switches in the guide."],"optionRefs":["p. 435","p. 435","p. 435","p. 435"],"ref":"p. 435, Removing a Directory","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-24","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"In diskpart, a technician has run select volume 3 and wants to change that volume's drive letter. Which diskpart command does this?","context":"In diskpart, a technician has run select volume 3 and wants to change that volume's drive letter. Which diskpart command does this?","options":["assign","extend","detail volume","delete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"diskpart is the command-line interface for Disk Management. After selecting a disk, partition, or volume and viewing details, you can use assign to change the drive letter, delete to remove the volume, or extend to enlarge it. Type exit to quit.","optionWhy":["Correct. After selecting a volume, assign changes its drive letter.","extend enlarges the selected volume. It doesn't change its letter.","detail volume displays more information about the selected volume without changing anything.","delete removes the selected volume, which would be destructive."],"optionRefs":["p. 435","p. 435","p. 435","pp. 435–436"],"ref":"p. 435, The diskpart Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-25","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"Before donating an old USB drive, a user runs a standard format on it and believes the data is gone for good. Is she right?","context":"Before donating an old USB drive, a user runs a standard format on it and believes the data is gone for good. Is she right?","options":["The data may still be recoverable; a secure format tool is needed.","A standard format overwrites every sector, so recovery is impossible.","Only a quick format leaves data behind; a standard one removes it.","The data is safe as long as the drive was formatted as exFAT first."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard and quick formats both remove the file references in the volume boot record but don't erase the data. New files can overwrite it, but data recovery is still possible with third-party tools. A secure format utility prevents recovery by overwriting each sector with zeros, often in multiple passes.","optionWhy":["Correct. Standard formats leave the data recoverable, and secure formats overwrite sectors.","Overwriting every sector is what secure format utilities do. A standard format only removes file references.","The guide says both standard and quick formats leave the data in place.","The file system type doesn't change whether formatted data can be recovered."],"optionRefs":["p. 437","p. 437","p. 437","pp. 436–437"],"ref":"pp. 436–437, The format Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-26","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A data drive (E:) is reporting read errors. The technician wants chkdsk to fix file system errors and also try to recover bad sectors. Which command should she run?","context":"A data drive (E:) is reporting read errors. The technician wants chkdsk to fix file system errors and also try to recover bad sectors. Which command should she run?","options":["chkdsk E: /r","chkdsk E: /f","chkdsk E:","sfc /scannow"],"answer":0,"explanation":"chkdsk E: with no switch runs read-only and reports errors. chkdsk E: /f tries to fix file system errors. chkdsk E: /r fixes file system errors and tries to recover bad sectors, saving recoverable data as filennnn.chk files in the root directory.","optionWhy":["Correct. /r fixes file system errors and attempts to recover bad sectors.","/f fixes file system errors but doesn't try to recover bad sectors.","With no switch, chkdsk runs read-only and only reports errors.","sfc verifies and restores protected Windows system files. It doesn't scan a data drive's sectors."],"optionRefs":["p. 437","p. 437","p. 437","p. 438"],"ref":"p. 437, The chkdsk Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-27","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician runs chkdsk C: /f, but the check can't run because files on the system drive are in use. What is the appropriate course of action?","context":"A technician runs chkdsk C: /f, but the check can't run because files on the system drive are in use. What is the appropriate course of action?","options":["Schedule the scan to run at the next system restart.","Run chkdsk C: /r, which is able to repair open files.","Run format C: /q to clear the open files and try again.","Cancel any running scans and use diskpart to repair it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Check Disk can't fix open files, so you may need to schedule the scan for the next system restart. chkdsk /f and /r can take a long time, and the guide advises against cancelling a scan; consider a read-only scan first. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot.","optionWhy":["Correct. Open files can't be fixed, so the scan is scheduled for the next restart.","/r adds bad-sector recovery, but Check Disk still can't fix open files.","format creates a new file system and deletes all existing data. That would destroy the installation.","The guide advises against cancelling a scan, and diskpart manages partitions and volumes rather than repairing file systems."],"optionRefs":["p. 437","p. 437","p. 436","pp. 435, 437"],"ref":"p. 437, The chkdsk Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-28","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician wants a remote user's PC to restart in 60 seconds, giving the user time to save their work. Which command does this?","context":"A technician wants a remote user's PC to restart in 60 seconds, giving the user time to save their work. Which command does this?","options":["shutdown /r /t 60","shutdown /s /t 60","shutdown /h /t 60","shutdown /a /t 60"],"answer":0,"explanation":"shutdown /r restarts (a soft reset), /s shuts down, /h hibernates, and /l logs off. /t nn delays the action by nn seconds (default 30), and shutdown /a aborts a pending shutdown if run quickly.","optionWhy":["Correct. /r restarts the computer, and /t 60 delays the action by 60 seconds (the default is 30).","/s shuts the computer down, closing all programs, rather than restarting it.","/h hibernates the computer, saving the session to disk before powering off.","/a aborts a pending shutdown if run quickly. It doesn't start a restart."],"optionRefs":["p. 437","p. 437","p. 437","p. 437"],"ref":"p. 437, System Management Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-29","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician suspects protected Windows system files are corrupted and wants System File Checker to run once at the next restart, not now. Which command should she use?","context":"A technician suspects protected Windows system files are corrupted and wants System File Checker to run once at the next restart, not now. Which command should she use?","options":["sfc /scanonce","sfc /scannow","sfc /scanboot","chkdsk /r"],"answer":0,"explanation":"sfc verifies and restores system files protected by Windows Resource Protection, using cached copies. Run it from an admin prompt: /scannow scans immediately, /scanonce schedules a scan for the next restart, and /scanboot schedules a scan at every boot.","optionWhy":["Correct. /scanonce schedules a single scan for the next restart.","/scannow runs the scan immediately, which isn't what she wants here.","/scanboot schedules a scan at every boot, not just the next one.","chkdsk checks the file system and disk sectors. It doesn't restore protected system files from cache."],"optionRefs":["p. 438","p. 438","p. 438","pp. 437–438"],"ref":"p. 438, System File Checker utility","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-30","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"In the output of winver, which part identifies the quality updates (patches) that have been applied, and can be used to look up known issues in the Microsoft Knowledge Base?","context":"In the output of winver, which part identifies the quality updates (patches) that have been applied, and can be used to look up known issues in the Microsoft Knowledge Base?","options":["The rev number in the second part of the OS Build","The Version code, such as 21H1 or a year/month value","The first part of the OS Build before the decimal point","The line saying whether it's Windows 10 or Windows 11"],"answer":0,"explanation":"winver shows that the OS is a Windows client version, the Version (a year/month code for the feature update, such as 21H1), and the OS Build. The OS Build has two parts: the first shows the brand and feature update, and the second (rev) shows quality updates and can be used to find changes and known issues.","optionWhy":["Correct. The rev part of the OS Build reflects quality updates or patches.","The Version code identifies the feature update by its release time, not individual quality updates.","The first part shows the brand and feature update. The second part (rev) shows patches.","That identifies the OS as a client version, distinct from Windows Server, not its patch level."],"optionRefs":["p. 439","p. 439","p. 439","p. 438"],"ref":"pp. 438–439, Reporting the Windows Version","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-31","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A user is denied access to a shared folder that her department's security group should be able to open. Which command quickly shows which security groups her signed-in account belongs to?","context":"A user is denied access to a shared folder that her department's security group should be able to open. Which command quickly shows which security groups her signed-in account belongs to?","options":["whoami /groups","whoami /priv","whoami","winver"],"answer":0,"explanation":"whoami displays the current user's name in DOMAIN\\Username format. whoami /groups lists the security groups the user belongs to, and whoami /priv displays the user's privileges. Both help when troubleshooting permissions.","optionWhy":["Correct. /groups lists the security groups of the signed-in user.","/priv displays the user's privileges, not group memberships.","Plain whoami shows only the username and domain or computer name.","winver reports Windows version information, not user identity."],"optionRefs":["p. 439","p. 439","p. 439","pp. 438–439"],"ref":"p. 439, Verifying User Identity","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-32","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A warehouse access point has SSID broadcast suppressed, so the network doesn't appear in the list of available Wi-Fi networks on a new laptop. How should the technician connect?","context":"A warehouse access point has SSID broadcast suppressed, so the network doesn't appear in the list of available Wi-Fi networks on a new laptop. How should the technician connect?","options":["Add it under Wi-Fi > Manage known networks > Add a new network.","Pick the strongest unnamed network in the list and select Connect.","Change the adapter's transmit power in Device Manager to reveal it.","Set the network profile to Private so hidden networks are shown."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the access point broadcasts its SSID, the network appears in the list. If SSID broadcast is suppressed, enter the WLAN settings manually: from the Network & Internet page, select Wi-Fi > Manage known networks > Add a new network.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hidden SSIDs are added manually through Manage known networks.","With broadcast suppressed, the network isn't in the list, so you need to enter its settings manually.","Transmit power and roaming settings address connection quality. They don't reveal a hidden SSID.","The network profile controls discoverability and firewall behavior after connecting. It doesn't show hidden SSIDs."],"optionRefs":["p. 442","p. 442","p. 442","p. 447"],"ref":"p. 442, Establish a Wireless Network Connection","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-33","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A laptop keeps dropping its Wi-Fi connection as the user moves between access points in a large office. Where can a technician adjust settings such as roaming aggressiveness and transmit power?","context":"A laptop keeps dropping its Wi-Fi connection as the user moves between access points in a large office. Where can a technician adjust settings such as roaming aggressiveness and transmit power?","options":["In the wireless adapter's properties in Device Manager","On the Proxy page of the Network & Internet settings","In Windows Defender Firewall's list of allowed apps","In the Internet Protocol Version 4 Properties dialog"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Wi-Fi adapter properties are configured through Device Manager. The most important setting is the 802.11 standard supported by the access point, and you can also adjust parameters such as roaming aggressiveness and transmit power to address connection issues.","optionWhy":["Correct. Device Manager exposes adapter parameters such as roaming aggressiveness and transmit power.","Proxy settings tell clients how to forward traffic through a proxy server. They don't tune the radio.","The allowed apps list configures firewall exceptions, not wireless adapter behavior.","The IPv4 properties set the IP address, mask, gateway, and DNS servers, not radio parameters."],"optionRefs":["p. 442","p. 454","p. 449","p. 445"],"ref":"p. 442, Establish a Wireless Network Connection","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-34","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A host has the IPv4 address 192.168.1.100 with the subnet mask 255.255.255.0. What does the mask tell you?","context":"A host has the IPv4 address 192.168.1.100 with the subnet mask 255.255.255.0. What does the mask tell you?","options":["The host is .100 on the logical network 192.168.1.0.","The host is .1 on the logical network 192.168.100.0.","The address is IPv6, since the mask is written in decimal.","The host can reach any network without using a router."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In IPv4, the 32-bit subnet mask distinguishes the network portion of the address from the host portion. With 192.168.1.100 and 255.255.255.0, the host uses the address portion .100 on the logical network 192.168.1.0. Devices on the same network communicate directly, and other networks are reached through a router.","optionWhy":["Correct. The mask separates the network (192.168.1.0) from the host portion (.100).","This mixes up the portions. With a 255.255.255.0 mask, the first three octets are the network.","IPv4 addresses and masks are typically written in dotted decimal. IPv6 addresses are 128 bits and use prefixes.","Devices on different networks need a router to communicate. The mask defines which hosts are local."],"optionRefs":["p. 444","p. 444","p. 444","p. 444"],"ref":"p. 444, Internet Protocol Addressing Scheme","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-35","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A statically configured PC can ping other hosts on its own subnet by IP address, but can't ping any IP address on other networks or the internet. Which setting is most likely wrong or missing?","context":"A statically configured PC can ping other hosts on its own subnet by IP address, but can't ping any IP address on other networks or the internet. Which setting is most likely wrong or missing?","options":["The default gateway","The DNS server","The domain suffix","The network profile"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hosts with addresses in different ranges can only be reached by forwarding packets through a router. Each host must be configured with the IP address of a local router, called the default gateway. DNS resolves names to IP addresses, so it wouldn't affect pings by IP address.","optionWhy":["Correct. Without a valid default gateway, the host can't reach other networks.","DNS resolves names to IP addresses. Pings by IP address don't depend on it.","The domain suffix forms the host's fully qualified domain name. It doesn't affect routing.","The network profile sets firewall and discovery behavior. It doesn't stop the host reaching other networks."],"optionRefs":["p. 444","p. 444","p. 444","p. 447"],"ref":"p. 444, Internet Protocol Addressing Scheme","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-36","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A user can open websites by typing their IP addresses, but not by typing names such as www.example.com. Which part of the IP configuration is most likely at fault?","context":"A user can open websites by typing their IP addresses, but not by typing names such as www.example.com. Which part of the IP configuration is most likely at fault?","options":["The DNS server addresses","The default gateway address","The subnet mask of the PC","The Ethernet speed settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Hosts are typically configured with the addresses of domain name system (DNS) servers, which resolve requests for names (such as www.example.com) to IP addresses. Reaching sites by IP but not by name points to a DNS problem.","optionWhy":["Correct. DNS resolves names to IP addresses, so name failures with working IP access point to DNS.","If the gateway were wrong, the user couldn't reach websites by IP address either.","The mask separates the network and host portions. Access by IP already works, so the mask isn't the issue.","Ethernet settings (usually autonegotiate) govern the physical link, which is clearly working here."],"optionRefs":["p. 444","p. 444","p. 444","p. 440"],"ref":"p. 444, Internet Protocol Addressing Scheme","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-37","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A manager asks why the network team uses DHCP instead of giving every PC a static IP address. What is the main reason?","context":"A manager asks why the network team uses DHCP instead of giving every PC a static IP address. What is the main reason?","options":["Manual setup is time-consuming and error-prone on large networks.","Static addresses can't be used with IPv4 subnet masks in Windows.","DHCP gives administrators more precise control over each host.","Hosts with static addresses can't be given a default gateway."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Static addressing means manually giving each host an IP address, subnet mask, gateway, and DNS server. It gives precise control but is time-consuming and error-prone on large networks. DHCP assigns valid IP parameters automatically, and Windows adapters default to \"Obtain an IP address automatically.\"","optionWhy":["Correct. DHCP removes the work and errors of configuring each host manually.","Static IPv4 addresses with masks can be configured in the IPv4 properties or the Settings app.","The guide credits static addressing with precise control. DHCP's advantage is simpler management.","Static configuration includes a gateway, along with the address, mask, and DNS server."],"optionRefs":["p. 444","pp. 444–446","p. 444","p. 444"],"ref":"p. 444, Static versus Dynamic Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-38","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A technician is setting a static IPv4 address in the Windows Settings app rather than the legacy IPv4 Properties dialog. The network uses the mask 255.255.255.0. What should she enter for the subnet?","context":"A technician is setting a static IPv4 address in the Windows Settings app rather than the legacy IPv4 Properties dialog. The network uses the mask 255.255.255.0. What should she enter for the subnet?","options":["A subnet prefix length of 24","A subnet prefix length of 255","A subnet prefix length of 32","A subnet prefix length of 64"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the Settings app, the mask is entered as a prefix length in bits rather than in dotted decimal. A 255.255.255.0 mask is 24 bits, so the subnet prefix length is 24.","optionWhy":["Correct. Settings uses the prefix length, and 255.255.255.0 is 24 bits.","255 is the value of one octet in dotted decimal, not the prefix length in bits.","32 bits is the full length of an IPv4 address. The 255.255.255.0 mask covers 24 bits.","64 bits relates to IPv6, where the interface portion is the last 64 bits."],"optionRefs":["p. 445","p. 445","pp. 444–445","p. 444"],"ref":"pp. 444–445, Windows Client Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-39","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Why doesn't the Windows network location prompt let a user choose the Domain profile?","context":"Why doesn't the Windows network location prompt let a user choose the Domain profile?","options":["It applies when the PC is domain-joined; Group Policy sets the firewall.","It's identical to the Public profile, so offering both would be redundant.","It can only be chosen in the legacy Network and Sharing Center applet.","It's only offered when the user connects through a cellular WWAN link."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When connecting to a new network, the user is prompted to choose Private (discoverable, sharing allowed; for trusted networks) or Public (firewall blocks access and the host is undiscoverable). The Domain profile can't be chosen; it applies when the computer is joined to a domain, and the firewall policy is then configured via Group Policy.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Domain profile is applied automatically for domain-joined PCs and is managed by Group Policy.","The profiles differ. Public blocks access and hides the host, while Domain firewall policy comes from Group Policy.","The guide says the Domain profile can't be chosen at all. It's applied when the PC is joined to a domain.","WWAN connections can be set as metered, but that has nothing to do with the Domain profile."],"optionRefs":["p. 447","p. 447","p. 447","pp. 447, 452"],"ref":"p. 447, Network Location","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-40","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A user needs to open the Reports shared folder on a server named FS01 by typing its path into File Explorer. Which path uses the correct UNC syntax?","context":"A user needs to open the Reports shared folder on a server named FS01 by typing its path into File Explorer. Which path uses the correct UNC syntax?","options":["\\\\FS01\\Reports","FS01:\\Reports","C:\\FS01\\Reports","\\FS01\\Reports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows uses universal naming convention (UNC) syntax to address network hosts and resources: \\\\Host\\Path. Host is the server's hostname, FQDN, or IP address, and Path is the shared folder or file path.","optionWhy":["Correct. UNC paths take the form \\\\Host\\Path, where Host is the server and Path is the share.","A letter or name followed by a colon is drive notation. UNC paths start with two backslashes and the host.","This points to a local folder on the C: drive, not to a network host.","A single leading backslash means the root of the current drive. UNC needs two backslashes before the host."],"optionRefs":["p. 448","pp. 434, 448","p. 448","pp. 433, 448"],"ref":"pp. 447–448, Network Location","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-41","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A newly installed inventory app can't receive connections because Windows Defender Firewall is blocking it. How should the technician create an exception for it?","context":"A newly installed inventory app can't receive connections because Windows Defender Firewall is blocking it. How should the technician create an exception for it?","options":["Use \"Allow an app through the firewall\" and tick the profiles.","Tick \"Block all incoming connections\" so the app is listed first.","Set the network to Public so the firewall lets the app through.","Disable network discovery so the firewall ignores the app's traffic."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On the Windows Firewall status page, select Allow an app through the firewall. Check the box for one or both network profile types, or use Allow another program to locate the executable and add it to the list. You can also block all incoming connections from the firewall settings.","optionWhy":["Correct. Allowing the app for the relevant profiles creates the exception.","Blocking all incoming connections includes apps on the allowed list, so it would make things worse.","Public makes the firewall block all access and hides the host, so it's more restrictive.","Network discovery controls whether other hosts can see this PC. It doesn't create firewall exceptions."],"optionRefs":["p. 449","pp. 448–449","p. 447","p. 448"],"ref":"pp. 448–449, Windows Defender Firewall Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-42","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A home worker needs secure access to the office file server over her home internet connection, as if her laptop were on the office network. What should be configured?","context":"A home worker needs secure access to the office file server over her home internet connection, as if her laptop were on the office network. What should be configured?","options":["A VPN connection to the office network","A proxy server address and TCP port","A WWAN cellular adapter with a SIM","A Private network profile at home"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A virtual private network (VPN) connects two private networks over a public network such as the internet. It is a tunnel that uses special protocols and encryption to keep traffic secure and authenticate the user. Once connected, the remote computer becomes part of the local network, though it's limited by the WAN link's bandwidth.","optionWhy":["Correct. A VPN creates a secure, authenticated tunnel, so the laptop becomes part of the office network.","A proxy forwards internet requests and can cache content. It doesn't join a remote PC to the office network.","A WWAN adapter provides internet access over a cellular network. It doesn't by itself give secure access to office resources.","The Private profile makes the PC discoverable on the home network. It doesn't connect to the office."],"optionRefs":["p. 450","pp. 453–454","p. 452","p. 447"],"ref":"p. 450, Establish a Virtual Private Network Connection","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-43","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"Besides the adapter itself, what does a WWAN adapter need in order to use GSM, 4G, or 5G cellular services?","context":"Besides the adapter itself, what does a WWAN adapter need in order to use GSM, 4G, or 5G cellular services?","options":["A SIM card from the network provider","A VPN client from the network provider","A proxy address from the network provider","A Domain profile set up by Group Policy"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WWAN means using a cellular adapter to connect to the internet through a provider's network. The adapter can be USB or internal. For GSM and 4G or 5G services, it must also be fitted with a subscriber identity module (SIM) card issued by the network provider.","optionWhy":["Correct. GSM and 4G/5G WWAN adapters need a provider-issued SIM card.","A VPN connects to a private network over a public one. It isn't needed to use cellular service.","Proxy settings are for networks that forward traffic through a proxy server, not for cellular access.","The Domain profile applies to domain-joined PCs. It has nothing to do with cellular connections."],"optionRefs":["p. 452","pp. 450, 452","pp. 452, 454","pp. 447, 452"],"ref":"p. 452, Establish a Wireless Wide Area Network Connection","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-44","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A field engineer's laptop uses a cellular data plan with a 4 GB monthly cap, and the provider charges heavily for any overage. What can be configured in Windows to help avoid this?","context":"A field engineer's laptop uses a cellular data plan with a 4 GB monthly cap, and the provider charges heavily for any overage. What can be configured in Windows to help avoid this?","options":["Set it as a metered connection with a monthly data limit.","Set the network profile to Public to block all data use.","Configure a proxy server so pages are cached and reused.","Lower the adapter's transmit power in Device Manager."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cellular providers can charge heavily when the data allowance is exceeded. In Windows you can define the network as a metered connection and set a data limit to avoid exceeding the cap. You can also monitor data usage by each app.","optionWhy":["Correct. A metered connection with a data limit helps avoid exceeding the provider's cap.","Public profile affects firewall behavior and discoverability. It doesn't track or cap data usage.","Proxy caching can reduce bandwidth on networks that use a proxy, but the guide's cellular solution is a metered connection and data limit.","Transmit power adjustments address Wi-Fi connection issues. They don't limit data usage."],"optionRefs":["p. 452","pp. 447, 452","pp. 452–454","pp. 442, 452"],"ref":"p. 452, Establish a Wireless Wide Area Network Connection","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-45","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A company's network forwards web traffic through a proxy server that is neither transparent nor autoconfiguring. What must be set on each Windows client?","context":"A company's network forwards web traffic through a proxy server that is neither transparent nor autoconfiguring. What must be set on each Windows client?","options":["The proxy's IP address and TCP port, in Network & Internet settings","Nothing, because all proxy servers intercept traffic without any setup","The proxy's address as the default gateway in the IPv4 properties","The proxy's name as the DNS server in each adapter's IP settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A proxy server can improve performance and security, and may cache content. An intercepting (transparent) proxy needs no client configuration, and some proxies configure themselves automatically. Otherwise each client must be set with the proxy's IP address and TCP port, in Network & Internet settings.","optionWhy":["Correct. Without a transparent or autoconfiguring proxy, clients need its address and port.","Only intercepting (transparent) proxies need no client configuration.","The default gateway is the local router's address. Proxy settings are configured separately.","DNS servers resolve names to IP addresses. They aren't how a client is pointed at a proxy."],"optionRefs":["p. 454","p. 454","pp. 444, 454","pp. 444, 454"],"ref":"pp. 453–454, Proxy Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-01","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A laptop set to obtain an address automatically shows \"Limited connectivity\", and ipconfig reports the address 169.254.37.12. What is the most likely cause?","context":"A laptop set to obtain an address automatically shows \"Limited connectivity\", and ipconfig reports the address 169.254.37.12. What is the most likely cause?","options":["No DHCP server could be contacted, so APIPA assigned this address.","The DNS server failed to resolve www.msftconnecttest.com properly.","The network cable is unplugged, so the adapter has no link at all.","The adapter was given a static address that duplicates another host."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Limited connectivity means the link is up, but the adapter is set to obtain an address automatically and no DHCP server can be contacted. The adapter then uses an automatic private IP address (APIPA) from the 169.254.x.y range, or an alternate configuration set in the IPv4 properties.","optionWhy":["Correct. A 169.254.x.y address with limited connectivity means DHCP couldn't be reached.","A failed DNS check against msftconnecttest.com produces the \"No Internet access\" state, not a 169.254 address.","An unplugged cable shows as unplugged or disconnected. Limited connectivity means the link is available.","The adapter is set to obtain an address automatically, and 169.254.x.y comes from APIPA, not a static entry."],"optionRefs":["p. 456","p. 457","p. 456","p. 456"],"ref":"p. 456, Troubleshoot IP Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-02","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A PC has a valid IP configuration for the local network and can reach other local hosts, but Windows reports \"No Internet access.\" What could this state indicate?","context":"A PC has a valid IP configuration for the local network and can reach other local hosts, but Windows reports \"No Internet access.\" What could this state indicate?","options":["A problem with the router, DNS, or both","No DHCP server could give it an address","The network adapter's driver is missing","The Ethernet cable has been disconnected"],"answer":0,"explanation":"No Internet access means the IP configuration is valid for the local network, but Windows can't identify a working internet connection. Windows tests by connecting to www.msftconnecttest.com and checking that DNS resolves it correctly, so the state could point to a problem with the router, DNS, or both.","optionWhy":["Correct. The internet test depends on a working router and correct DNS resolution.","That causes Limited connectivity with an APIPA address. Here the local IP configuration is valid.","Without a driver there would be no working link at all. This PC can reach local hosts.","A disconnected cable shows as unplugged or disconnected, not as \"No Internet access.\""],"optionRefs":["p. 457","p. 456","p. 457","p. 456"],"ref":"pp. 456–457, Troubleshoot IP Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-03","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A technician needs to see a PC's DHCP and DNS server addresses and its adapter's MAC address from the command line. Which command shows all of these?","context":"A technician needs to see a PC's DHCP and DNS server addresses and its adapter's MAC address from the command line. Which command shows all of these?","options":["ipconfig /all","ipconfig","ipconfig /displaydns","hostname"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without switches, ipconfig shows the IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway for each adapter. The /all switch shows the detailed configuration, including DHCP and DNS servers, the MAC address, and NetBIOS status.","optionWhy":["Correct. /all shows the detailed configuration, including DHCP and DNS servers and the MAC address.","Plain ipconfig shows only the IP address, subnet mask, and default gateway.","/displaydns shows the DNS resolver cache of recently queried names, not adapter details.","hostname returns only the name configured on the local machine."],"optionRefs":["p. 457","p. 457","p. 458","p. 458"],"ref":"p. 457, ipconfig Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-04","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"After the DHCP server's scope was corrected, a PC still holds an old, incorrect lease. Which sequence gets it a fresh lease from the command line?","context":"After the DHCP server's scope was corrected, a PC still holds an old, incorrect lease. Which sequence gets it a fresh lease from the command line?","options":["ipconfig /release, then ipconfig /renew","ipconfig /flushdns, then ipconfig /all","ipconfig /displaydns, then ipconfig /renew","ping 127.0.0.1, then ipconfig /release"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ipconfig /release releases the address obtained from DHCP, so the adapter no longer has an IP address. ipconfig /renew forces the DHCP client to renew its lease. ipconfig /flushdns clears the DNS resolver cache, which is a separate name-resolution fix.","optionWhy":["Correct. Releasing then renewing makes the client obtain a new DHCP lease.","/flushdns clears cached DNS records and /all displays settings. Neither requests a new lease.","/displaydns only lists cached DNS names. The guide's sequence starts with /release.","Pinging the loopback tests the TCP/IP stack, and ending with /release would leave the adapter with no address."],"optionRefs":["p. 458","pp. 457–458","p. 458","pp. 458–459"],"ref":"pp. 457–458, ipconfig Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-05","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A web server was moved to a new IP address. Most users can reach it, but one PC keeps trying the old address. Which command is most likely to fix this?","context":"A web server was moved to a new IP address. Most users can reach it, but one PC keeps trying the old address. Which command is most likely to fix this?","options":["ipconfig /flushdns","ipconfig /displaydns","ipconfig /release","netstat -n"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The DNS resolver cache holds recently queried host and domain names to reduce network traffic. If cached records are out of date, they can cause problems reaching hosts and services. ipconfig /flushdns clears the cache, and ipconfig /displaydns shows it.","optionWhy":["Correct. Flushing the cache removes the out-of-date record so the name is looked up again.","/displaydns shows the cache, including the stale entry, but doesn't clear it.","/release gives up the DHCP lease. It doesn't affect cached DNS records.","netstat -n lists ports and connections in numeric form. It doesn't change name resolution."],"optionRefs":["p. 458","p. 458","p. 458","p. 465"],"ref":"pp. 457–458, ipconfig Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-06","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"After persistent network problems, a technician uses Network reset on a user's laptop and the problem is solved. What else should the technician expect to do?","context":"After persistent network problems, a technician uses Network reset on a user's laptop and the problem is solved. What else should the technician expect to do?","options":["Reconfigure custom adapter settings and VPNs.","Restore the user's documents from a backup.","Reinstall Windows, since the reset removed it.","Restart every server so clients can reconnect."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Network reset (Settings > Network & Internet > Status) resets the device's network stack. This clears custom adapter configurations and network connections, including VPN connections, which then have to be reconfigured.","optionWhy":["Correct. Network reset clears custom adapter configurations and connections, including VPNs.","Network reset affects network configuration only, not user data files.","Network reset only resets the network stack. It doesn't remove Windows.","The guide warns against restarting servers without considering the impact on other users."],"optionRefs":["p. 458","p. 458","p. 458","p. 458"],"ref":"p. 458, Network Reset","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-07","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"You start troubleshooting a PC's network connectivity by running ping 127.0.0.1. What does this first step test?","context":"You start troubleshooting a PC's network connectivity by running ping 127.0.0.1. What does this first step test?","options":["To verify that TCP/IP is installed and loaded correctly","To check for duplicate addresses on the local network","To verify that the default gateway is up and responding","To confirm traffic can pass through the router to others"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The steps are: (1) ping the loopback address 127.0.0.1 to check that TCP/IP is installed and loaded correctly; (2) ping your own IP to check it was added correctly and look for duplicates; (3) ping the default gateway; (4) ping a remote host to check communication through the router.","optionWhy":["Correct. Pinging the loopback address 127.0.0.1 verifies that TCP/IP is installed and loaded correctly.","Checking for duplicates is the purpose of step 2, pinging the workstation's own IP address.","That's step 3: pinging the default gateway to verify it's up and that you can reach a local host.","That's step 4: pinging a remote host's IP address to verify communication through the router."],"optionRefs":["p. 459","p. 459","p. 459","p. 459"],"ref":"pp. 458–459, Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-08","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A technician pings a printer on the same local network segment and gets \"Reply from [own IP]: Destination unreachable.\" What does this most likely mean?","context":"A technician pings a printer on the same local network segment and gets \"Reply from [own IP]: Destination unreachable.\" What does this most likely mean?","options":["The printer didn't answer ARP; it's probably disconnected.","The gateway router has no route for the printer's network.","The printer is on a remote network that didn't reply in time.","The DNS server couldn't resolve the printer's host name."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When both hosts should be on the same segment, this message means the sending host gets no response to ARP probes. ARP locates the MAC address of the interface that owns an IP. The most likely cause is that the destination is disconnected or non-discoverable; if it's definitely up, suspect duplicate addresses or a wrong subnet mask.","optionWhy":["Correct. No ARP response on the local segment usually means the destination host is down or disconnected.","A missing route produces \"Reply from GatewayIP: Destination unreachable\", not a reply from your own IP.","No reply from a remote host via the gateway produces \"Request timed out.\"","This ping was to an IP address, and a DNS failure doesn't produce \"Destination unreachable\" from your own IP."],"optionRefs":["p. 461","p. 461","p. 461","pp. 461–462"],"ref":"pp. 458–461, Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-09","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"A technician wants to keep pinging a flaky switch continuously while she reseats its cables, rather than sending only the default four packets. Which command does this, and how is it stopped?","context":"A technician wants to keep pinging a flaky switch continuously while she reseats its cables, rather than sending only the default four packets. Which command does this, and how is it stopped?","options":["ping -t, stopped with CTRL+C","ping -4, stopped by typing exit","pathping, stopped by typing quit","tracert, stopped by pressing ESC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By default, ping in Windows sends four packets. ping -t pings continuously, and CTRL+C stops the test. The -4 and -6 switches force IPv4 or IPv6.","optionWhy":["Correct. ping -t sends packets continuously instead of the default four, until you press CTRL+C.","The -4 switch forces ping to use IPv4. It doesn't make ping run continuously.","pathping traces the route and then pings each hop for a set period. It isn't the continuous-ping option.","tracert traces the route a packet takes to its target. It doesn't ping one host repeatedly."],"optionRefs":["p. 462","p. 462","p. 463","p. 462"],"ref":"p. 462, Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-10","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"Users report a slow connection to a remote branch office. The technician wants to see the path and measure round-trip time and packet loss at each hop. Which tool gives this best?","context":"Users report a slow connection to a remote branch office. The technician wants to see the path and measure round-trip time and packet loss at each hop. Which tool gives this best?","options":["pathping","tracert","nslookup","netstat"],"answer":0,"explanation":"tracert traces the path to a target, listing each hop, the router's ingress interface, and response times, with an asterisk for probes that time out. pathping performs a trace and then pings each hop router repeatedly to measure RTT and link latency more accurately, and also shows packet loss at each hop.","optionWhy":["Correct. pathping measures RTT and packet loss at each hop after tracing the route.","tracert shows the hops and response times, but pathping is the tool that measures latency and packet loss per hop more accurately.","nslookup queries DNS records. It doesn't measure path latency.","netstat shows open ports and connections on the local host, not the path to a remote network."],"optionRefs":["p. 463","pp. 462–463","p. 464","p. 465"],"ref":"pp. 462–463, Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-11","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"What does the command nslookup -type=mx comptia.org 8.8.8.8 do?","context":"What does the command nslookup -type=mx comptia.org 8.8.8.8 do?","options":["Asks Google's DNS server 8.8.8.8 for comptia.org's mail records","Asks the default DNS server for the IP address of host 8.8.8.8","Traces the route to comptia.org through the router at 8.8.8.8","Lists open mail ports on comptia.org that are listening for 8.8.8.8"],"answer":0,"explanation":"nslookup troubleshoots DNS, using the syntax nslookup -Option Host Server. Here -type=mx asks for mail (MX) records, comptia.org is the host, and 8.8.8.8 (Google's public DNS) is the server to query instead of the default. Comparing results with your own DNS server can reveal configuration problems.","optionWhy":["Correct. -type=mx requests mail records, and 8.8.8.8 is the DNS server queried.","In this syntax the last argument is the DNS server to query, not the host being looked up.","Tracing routes is done with tracert or pathping, not nslookup.","Checking listening ports is what netstat does on the local host. nslookup queries DNS records."],"optionRefs":["p. 464","p. 464","pp. 462–464","pp. 464–465"],"ref":"p. 464, Troubleshoot Name Resolution","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-13","module":14,"lesson":"14A Troubleshoot Windows Networking","question":"One user can't open the shared network printer's web page or print to it. Other users on the same network can print to it without problems. Where does the problem most likely lie?","context":"One user can't open the shared network printer's web page or print to it. Other users on the same network can print to it without problems. Where does the problem most likely lie?","options":["With the first user's own client PC","With the print device itself","With the network between all clients","With the ISP's internet service"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When troubleshooting unavailable resources, establish the scope of the problem. If the service can be used from a different host, the problem should lie with the first client. If other hosts also can't connect, it's with the server or print device, or the network infrastructure between them.","optionWhy":["Correct. Other hosts can connect, so the problem is isolated to this client.","If the printer were at fault, the other users couldn't print either.","A shared infrastructure fault would affect the other users too.","A local network printer doesn't depend on the ISP, and other users can reach it."],"optionRefs":["p. 464","p. 464","p. 464","pp. 463–464"],"ref":"p. 464, Troubleshoot Name Resolution","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-14","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A technician wants to use Remote Desktop to connect from his PC to an office workstation. Which client program does he run, and what TCP port does the RDP server listen on by default?","context":"A technician wants to use Remote Desktop to connect from his PC to an office workstation. Which client program does he run, and what TCP port does the RDP server listen on by default?","options":["mstsc.exe; TCP port 3389","msra.exe; TCP port 3389","mstsc.exe; TCP port 5900","msinfo32.exe; TCP port 22"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP). To connect, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe, then enter the server's IP address or FQDN. The RDP server runs on TCP port 3389 by default, although this can be changed.","optionWhy":["Correct. mstsc.exe is the Remote Desktop client, and RDP uses TCP 3389 by default.","msra.exe is Remote Assistance, which uses invitation files and dynamic ports, not the standard RDP client.","mstsc.exe is right, but TCP 5900 is the port VNC uses. RDP defaults to TCP 3389.","msinfo32 is System Information, and TCP 22 is the default SSH port."],"optionRefs":["pp. 468, 472","pp. 472–473","p. 470","pp. 474, 478"],"ref":"pp. 468, 472, Remote Desktop Protocol","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-15","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"When connecting with Remote Desktop Connection, a technician wants to sign in with the local account \"support\" on the remote PC, not a domain account. Which credential format should he use?","context":"When connecting with Remote Desktop Connection, a technician wants to sign in with the local account \"support\" on the remote PC, not a domain account. Which credential format should he use?","options":[".\\support","support@local","Domain\\support","\\\\support"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To specify a domain account, use Domain\\Username. To use a local account on the remote host, use either .\\Username or Host\\Username. RDP authentication and session data are always encrypted.","optionWhy":["Correct. .\\Username (or Host\\Username) specifies a local account on the remote host.","The guide gives .\\Username or Host\\Username for local accounts, not this format.","Domain\\Username specifies a domain account, not a local one.","Two leading backslashes begin a UNC network path, not an account name."],"optionRefs":["p. 469","p. 469","p. 469","pp. 469, 533"],"ref":"pp. 468–469, Remote Desktop Protocol","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-16","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A user is working at her office PC when you start an RDP session to that same PC. What happens?","context":"A user is working at her office PC when you start an RDP session to that same PC. What happens?","options":["The local desktop locks; only one person can be signed in.","Both users share the desktop and can see and control it at the same time.","The RDP connection is refused until the local user signs out by herself.","Each user gets a separate desktop session running side by side on the PC."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A limitation of RDP on Windows is that only one person can be signed in at a time. Starting an RDP session locks the local desktop, and if a local user then signs in, the remote user is disconnected.","optionWhy":["Correct. RDP on Windows allows one signed-in user, so the local session is locked.","Shared viewing and control of a user's session is what Remote Assistance and Quick Assist do.","The guide says starting an RDP session locks the local desktop rather than being refused.","Windows RDP allows only one signed-in person at a time, so separate side-by-side sessions aren't possible."],"optionRefs":["p. 470","pp. 470, 472","p. 470","p. 470"],"ref":"p. 470, Remote Desktop Protocol (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-17","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A home user wants to connect by Remote Desktop to his PC running Windows 11 Home, but the connection always fails. What is the reason?","context":"A home user wants to connect by Remote Desktop to his PC running Windows 11 Home, but the connection always fails. What is the reason?","options":["Home editions lack the RDP server and have only the client.","Home editions only accept RDP on TCP port 5900, not 3389.","Home editions need NLA turned off before RDP can connect.","Home editions only allow RDP over a VPN to the local LAN."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Remote Desktop server isn't enabled by default and is configured on the Remote Desktop page of Settings. Windows Home editions don't include the Remote Desktop server, so you can't connect to them, but they do include the client for connecting to other computers.","optionWhy":["Correct. You can't connect to Home editions by RDP, only from them.","Port 5900 is used by VNC. Home editions don't include an RDP server on any port.","NLA is an option on editions that have an RDP server. Home editions don't have one.","A VPN can add a layer of security, but Home editions can't host RDP sessions at all."],"optionRefs":["p. 472","pp. 470, 472","p. 472","pp. 472, 477"],"ref":"pp. 470–472, RDP Server and Security Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-18","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"How does requiring Network Level Authentication (NLA) protect a Remote Desktop server?","context":"How does requiring Network Level Authentication (NLA) protect a Remote Desktop server?","options":["It authenticates users before resources are given to the session.","It moves the RDP server from TCP port 3389 to a random high port.","It encrypts the RDP session data, which is otherwise sent in clear.","It lets several users sign in remotely to the same PC at once."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Without NLA, the system configures a desktop before the user logs on. NLA protects the RDP server against denial-of-service attacks (Student Guide p. 470–472).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says NLA protects the RDP server against denial-of-service attacks, because the user is authenticated before a desktop is configured.","The guide says NLA authenticates the user before a desktop is configured. It does not describe NLA as moving the RDP port.","RDP authentication and session data are always encrypted, with or without NLA.","RDP on Windows allows only one signed-in user at a time. NLA doesn't change that."],"optionRefs":["p. 472","p. 472","pp. 469, 472","pp. 470, 472"],"ref":"pp. 470–472, RDP Server and Security Settings","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-19","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"Which protocol is macOS Screen Sharing based on, and which TCP port does that protocol use?","context":"Which protocol is macOS Screen Sharing based on, and which TCP port does that protocol use?","options":["VNC, over TCP port 5900","RDP, over TCP port 3389","SSH, over TCP port 22","HTTPS, over TCP port 443"],"answer":0,"explanation":"macOS Screen Sharing provides remote desktop functionality and is based on Virtual Network Computing (VNC), so any VNC client can connect to it. VNC works over TCP port 5900. Not all VNC versions support connection security, but macOS Screen Sharing is encrypted.","optionWhy":["Correct. Screen Sharing is VNC-based, and VNC uses TCP 5900.","RDP is Windows' remote desktop protocol. Screen Sharing is based on VNC.","SSH connects to a command interpreter, not a desktop, and uses TCP 22.","Quick Assist and third-party screen-sharing tools use HTTPS on 443. macOS Screen Sharing uses VNC."],"optionRefs":["p. 470","pp. 468, 470, 472","p. 474","pp. 470, 473, 476"],"ref":"p. 470, Virtual Network Computing","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-20","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A help desk wants a Windows tool to let technicians view and control users' desktops, but the firewall team objects to opening a dynamic range of ports. Which built-in option fits best?","context":"A help desk wants a Windows tool to let technicians view and control users' desktops, but the firewall team objects to opening a dynamic range of ports. Which built-in option fits best?","options":["Quick Assist, which works over HTTPS on TCP port 443","Remote Assistance, which uses the ephemeral port range","Remote Desktop, which shares the user's live session","WinRM, which streams the desktop using SOAP messages"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote Assistance (msra.exe) uses a passcode-protected invitation file and assigns a port dynamically from the ephemeral range (49152 to 65535), which makes the firewall hard to configure securely. Quick Assist (CTRL+WINDOWS+Q) works over encrypted HTTPS on TCP 443. The helper must be signed in with a Microsoft account and generates the passcode for the sharer.","optionWhy":["Correct. Quick Assist uses TCP 443, avoiding the dynamic port problem.","Remote Assistance's dynamic ephemeral ports are exactly what make it hard to firewall.","RDP locks the local desktop when a remote user connects, so it doesn't share the user's live session.","WinRM runs management commands remotely over HTTP/HTTPS. It doesn't share desktops."],"optionRefs":["p. 473","p. 473","p. 470","p. 473"],"ref":"pp. 472–473, Microsoft Remote Assistance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-21","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"During a Quick Assist session, a technician tries to change a setting that triggers a UAC prompt, but he can't interact with it. Why?","context":"During a Quick Assist session, a technician tries to change a setting that triggers a UAC prompt, but he can't interact with it. Why?","options":["By default the helper can't perform tasks that need UAC consent.","The helper isn't signed in to Windows with a Microsoft account.","Quick Assist only lets the helper view, never take any control.","The session needs NLA turned on before any UAC prompts appear."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the default configuration, neither Remote Assistance nor Quick Assist lets the helper perform tasks that need UAC consent. Allowing it would mean disabling UAC's Secure Desktop feature, or turning UAC notifications off or lower, which weakens security.","optionWhy":["Correct. UAC consent tasks are blocked for the helper in the default configuration.","The guide does not say Quick Assist requires a Microsoft account. The UAC limitation applies to tasks that need consent.","Helpers can take control in these tools; the specific limitation is tasks requiring UAC consent.","NLA is a Remote Desktop server setting. It's unrelated to UAC prompts in Quick Assist."],"optionRefs":["p. 473","p. 473","pp. 472–473","p. 472"],"ref":"p. 473, Microsoft Remote Assistance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-22","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"The first time an administrator connects to a Linux server by SSH, the client warns that the server's host key isn't in its cache and shows a key fingerprint. Why should she check that fingerprint before accepting it?","context":"The first time an administrator connects to a Linux server by SSH, the client warns that the server's host key isn't in its cache and shows a key fingerprint. Why should she check that fingerprint before accepting it?","options":["To confirm it's the trusted server, not an on-path attacker.","To generate the private key she'll use for public key authentication.","To move the SSH connection from TCP port 22 to an encrypted port.","To turn on Network Level Authentication for the SSH session."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each SSH server has a public/private key pair identified by a host key fingerprint. Clients use the fingerprint to verify they're connecting to a trusted server, which mitigates the risk of on-path attacks. The host key pair is then used to set up an encrypted channel for submitting credentials.","optionWhy":["Correct. Verifying the fingerprint confirms the server's identity and mitigates on-path attacks.","The fingerprint identifies the server's host key. User key pairs are a separate client-side matter.","SSH already encrypts each session on TCP 22 by default. The fingerprint doesn't change the port.","NLA is a Remote Desktop server feature that authenticates users before creating a session. It isn't part of SSH."],"optionRefs":["p. 474","pp. 474–475","p. 474","pp. 472, 474"],"ref":"p. 474, Secure Shell","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-23","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"An administrator sets up SSH public key authentication on a Linux server. How does the client prove its identity at sign-in?","context":"An administrator sets up SSH public key authentication on a Linux server. How does the client prove its identity at sign-in?","options":["The client uses its private key to decrypt the server's challenge.","The client sends its private key to the server to be checked there.","The server sends its private key so the client can trust the host.","The client submits a username and password checked by the server."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The server holds a list of the public keys of authorized user accounts. The client asks to authenticate with one of these keys, and the server generates a challenge using that public key. The client must decrypt the challenge with the matching private key it holds. Monitoring for and removing compromised client public keys is a critical security task.","optionWhy":["Correct. Only the holder of the matching private key can decrypt the challenge.","The guide says the private key is kept secret and known only to the server, while clients receive the public key.","Server identity is checked with the host key fingerprint. Private keys are never sent.","That describes password authentication, the other common SSH method."],"optionRefs":["p. 475","p. 475","pp. 474–475","p. 474"],"ref":"pp. 474–475, Secure Shell","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-24","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"Your company is choosing between a remote monitoring and management (RMM) tool and a desktop management/MDM suite. What distinguishes RMM tools?","context":"Your company is choosing between a remote monitoring and management (RMM) tool and a desktop management/MDM suite. What distinguishes RMM tools?","options":["RMM is built for MSPs, with client accounts and billable reporting.","RMM can only manage mobile devices, not Windows or macOS desktops.","RMM is for a single organization and focuses on access control.","RMM needs a hardware vPro controller to communicate with hosts."],"answer":0,"explanation":"RMM tools are principally designed for managed service providers (MSPs), outsourcing companies that handle IT support for clients. They can distinguish client accounts and record and report billable support activity. Desktop management and MDM suites are deployed by a single organization and focus mainly on access control and authorization.","optionWhy":["Correct. RMM tools distinguish client accounts and track billable work for MSPs.","Most suites support both desktop and mobile hosts, and this isn't what defines RMM.","That describes desktop management/MDM suites. RMM is designed for MSPs serving multiple clients.","Agents use the running OS; vPro or AMD PRO can optionally add out-of-band management."],"optionRefs":["p. 475","p. 475","p. 475","p. 475"],"ref":"p. 475, Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-25","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"An employee gets a phone call from someone claiming to be IT support, asking her to install a screen-sharing app and read out the PIN code it shows. What control should the company have in place for this?","context":"An employee gets a phone call from someone claiming to be IT support, asking her to install a screen-sharing app and read out the PIN code it shows. What control should the company have in place for this?","options":["An out-of-band way to verify the caller is an authorized technician","Blocking TCP port 443 so screen-sharing apps can't make any connections","Requiring NLA on all PCs so screen sharing must authenticate users first","Requiring users to share only one app window instead of the full desktop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Third-party screen-sharing tools such as TeamViewer and LogMeIn work over HTTPS (TCP 443), and the user grants access by giving a technician a PIN code generated by the software. Users must be made aware that threat actors may use social engineering to get access, and there should be a specific out-of-band verification method for confirming a contact is an authorized technician.","optionWhy":["Correct. Out-of-band verification counters social engineering by fake technicians.","These tools use HTTPS on 443, which ordinary web traffic also uses. The guide's control is user awareness and verification.","NLA protects Remote Desktop servers from denial-of-service. It doesn't verify a caller's identity.","Limiting what is shared doesn't stop an attacker who has been granted access."],"optionRefs":["p. 476","p. 476","pp. 472, 476","p. 476"],"ref":"p. 476, Screen-sharing Software","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-26","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A technician uses a Microsoft Teams screen share, with control granted, to help a standard user. Why can't the technician use this session to change administrator-level settings?","context":"A technician uses a Microsoft Teams screen share, with control granted, to help a standard user. Why can't the technician use this session to change administrator-level settings?","options":["The share runs with the signed-in user's privileges.","Teams only allows a single window to be shared at once.","Video conferencing apps never grant remote control.","Teams sessions need the RDP server to be enabled first."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Video-conferencing software such as Teams or Zoom can share a window or the whole desktop, and some allow participants to be granted control. The share has the privileges of the signed-in user, so it can't be used for administrator-level configuration. It's useful for demonstrating tasks or observing a support issue.","optionWhy":["Correct. The share inherits the user's privileges, so a standard user can't make admin changes.","The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop.","Some video-conferencing apps do allow participants to be granted control of the share.","Teams screen sharing doesn't depend on the Windows RDP server."],"optionRefs":["p. 476","p. 476","p. 476","pp. 470, 476"],"ref":"p. 476, Video-conferencing Software","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-27","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access Technologies","question":"A company wants home workers to use Remote Desktop to reach their office PCs, without exposing RDP ports through the corporate firewall to the internet. What should you set up?","context":"A company wants home workers to use Remote Desktop to reach their office PCs, without exposing RDP ports through the corporate firewall to the internet. What should you set up?","options":["Connect with a VPN first, then use Remote Desktop over the tunnel.","Move the RDP server to TCP port 22 so it looks like normal traffic.","Use Remote Assistance instead, as it needs no firewall configuration.","Allow RDP only from local accounts in the format .\\Username form."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote desktop and SSH connect to a single host, while a VPN creates a tunneled link that joins your computer to a remote network. You can set up a VPN link first and then use Remote Desktop to connect to a host on the private network, which avoids opening Remote Desktop ports on the firewall.","optionWhy":["Correct. The VPN adds a layer of security and avoids exposing RDP ports.","Port 22 is SSH's default. Changing RDP's port still exposes an RDP server to the internet.","Remote Assistance uses dynamic ephemeral ports, which make it harder to firewall securely.","The account format doesn't change which firewall ports must be open."],"optionRefs":["p. 477","pp. 472, 474","p. 473","pp. 469, 477"],"ref":"p. 477, Virtual Private Networks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-28","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"A technician needs one comprehensive report of a PC's hardware resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status. Which tool provides this?","context":"A technician needs one comprehensive report of a PC's hardware resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status. Which tool provides this?","options":["System Information (msinfo32)","Resource Monitor (resmon.exe)","Event Viewer (eventvwr.msc)","System Configuration (msconfig)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on hardware and software components, including an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status.","optionWhy":["Correct. msinfo32 produces a comprehensive hardware and software report.","Resource Monitor shows detailed snapshot performance statistics, not a full inventory report.","Event Viewer displays and manages logs of events such as errors and warnings.","msconfig changes how the computer boots and loads Windows, mainly for diagnostic testing."],"optionRefs":["p. 478","p. 486","p. 479","p. 489"],"ref":"p. 478, System Information","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-29","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"A network adapter's driver keeps failing to load at startup. In which Windows log would you expect to find these events?","context":"A network adapter's driver keeps failing to load at startup. In which Windows log would you expect to find these events?","options":["System log","Application log","Security log","Setup log"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Windows Logs folder has four main logs. The System log records events affecting the core OS, such as service and driver load failures, hardware conflicts, and network issues. The Application log covers non-core processes and some third-party apps, such as installers. The Security log holds audit data, and the Setup log records installation events.","optionWhy":["Correct. Driver load failures affect the core OS and are recorded in the System log.","The Application log records non-core processes and some third-party apps, such as installers.","The Security log holds audit data, such as sign-in successes and failures.","The Setup log records installation events, not routine driver load failures."],"optionRefs":["p. 480","p. 480","pp. 480–481","p. 480"],"ref":"p. 480, Default Log Files","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-30","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"In Event Viewer, you see an event warning that disk space is running low, but nothing has failed yet. What severity level does this event have?","context":"In Event Viewer, you see an event warning that disk space is running low, but nothing has failed yet. What severity level does this event have?","options":["Warning","Critical","Information","Audit Failure"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Critical events are the highest priority, often a halted or unresponsive process. Error events are less severe and investigated after critical ones. Warnings are conditions that could lead to errors if not addressed, such as low disk space. Information events record noteworthy operations, and Audit Success/Failure are Security log events.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide gives low disk space as an example of a Warning.","Critical is for the highest-priority issues, often a halted or unresponsive process.","Information events record noteworthy operations or states that need no action.","Audit Failure is a Security log event, such as an incorrect password entry."],"optionRefs":["p. 481","p. 481","p. 481","p. 481"],"ref":"pp. 480–481, Event Sources and Severity Levels","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-31","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"Which Event Viewer log overwrite option closes the current log file when it's full and starts a new one?","context":"Which Event Viewer log overwrite option closes the current log file when it's full and starts a new one?","options":["Archive","Overwrite","Do not overwrite","Export"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each log has a default maximum size (usually about 20 MB), adjustable in Properties. The options are overwrite, do not overwrite, or archive, which closes the current file and starts a new one. Archived logs can grow large if not monitored.","optionWhy":["Correct. Archive closes the current file and starts a new one.","Overwrite replaces old events when the log is full, rather than keeping a closed copy.","Do not overwrite keeps existing events and doesn't start a new file.","Export isn't one of the overwrite options the guide lists for Event Viewer logs."],"optionRefs":["p. 480","p. 480","p. 480","p. 480"],"ref":"p. 480, Default Log Files","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-32","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"A user's VoIP calls break up whenever other heavy applications are busy. What could a technician try in Task Manager to improve call quality?","context":"A user's VoIP calls break up whenever other heavy applications are busy. What could a technician try in Task Manager to improve call quality?","options":["Raise the VoIP app's priority to Above normal","Disable the VoIP app on the Startup tab","Sign the user out from the Users tab","End the VoIP process from the Details tab now"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Task Manager, right-click a process and choose an option from the Set Priority submenu. For example, setting a Voice over IP application's priority to Above normal may improve call quality by giving it more CPU resources.","optionWhy":["Correct. Raising its priority gives the VoIP app more CPU resources.","The Startup tab controls what launches at sign-in. It doesn't help during a call.","The Users tab can sign users out, which ends their session. It doesn't give the VoIP app more resources.","Ending the process stops the call. The guide's fix is changing its priority."],"optionRefs":["p. 482","p. 485","p. 484","pp. 481–482"],"ref":"pp. 481–482, Task Manager Process Monitoring","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-33","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"You're checking the memory figures in Task Manager on a slow PC. Which observation is the more concerning sign of a performance problem?","context":"You're checking the memory figures in Task Manager on a slow PC. Which observation is the more concerning sign of a performance problem?","options":["High pagefile utilization","High physical RAM use","A large Cached memory value","Short CPU usage peaks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"High physical memory use, up to the amount of system RAM, isn't necessarily a sign of poor performance, because it's good to make full use of the resource. High pagefile use is more problematic, because paged memory is written to disk.","optionWhy":["Correct. Heavy pagefile use is the more problematic sign, as paging relies on the disk.","The guide says high RAM use isn't necessarily a problem, as it's good to use the resource fully.","Cached memory holds frequently used files ahead of time to speed up access. That helps performance.","The guide says high peak CPU values are nothing to worry about. Sustained high use matters."],"optionRefs":["p. 483","p. 483","p. 483","p. 483"],"ref":"p. 483, Memory Monitoring","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-34","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"On a shared office PC, a technician needs to sign out a colleague who left a session running, and see how much CPU and memory each signed-in user is using. Which Task Manager tab should he use?","context":"On a shared office PC, a technician needs to sign out a colleague who left a session running, and see how much CPU and memory each signed-in user is using. Which Task Manager tab should he use?","options":["Users","Services","Startup","App History"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Users tab in Task Manager shows all signed-in users. From it you can send messages to users, sign them out, see each user's running processes, and monitor resource usage (CPU, memory, disk) per user.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Users tab shows signed-in users, lets you sign them out, and shows per-user resource use.","The Services tab tracks background services that run without user interaction.","The Startup tab lets you disable programs that run at startup.","The App History tab shows usage information for Windows Store apps. It doesn't sign users out."],"optionRefs":["p. 484","p. 485","p. 485","p. 482"],"ref":"p. 484, Task Manager User Monitoring","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-35","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"A technician wants to stop a nonessential service from starting automatically, but still let it be started when needed. What startup type should she set in the Services console?","context":"A technician wants to stop a nonessential service from starting automatically, but still let it be started when needed. What startup type should she set in the Services console?","options":["Manual","Disabled","Automatic","Above normal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From Task Manager's Services tab, Open Services opens the Services console. Setting a service to Manual prevents it starting automatically, and Disabled stops it from running at all. Be careful with dependencies, and consider restarting a service as a first troubleshooting step.","optionWhy":["Correct. Manual stops the service starting automatically but still allows it to be started.","Disabled stops the service from running entirely, so it couldn't be started when needed.","Automatic would keep it starting at boot, which is what she wants to stop.","Above normal is a process priority setting in Task Manager, not a service startup type."],"optionRefs":["p. 486","p. 486","p. 486","p. 482"],"ref":"pp. 485–486, Startup Processes and Services Console","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-36","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"A technician wants to record a server's memory, disk, and processor statistics over several weeks to set a baseline for long-term analysis. Which tool and feature should be used?","context":"A technician wants to record a server's memory, disk, and processor statistics over several weeks to set a baseline for long-term analysis. Which tool and feature should be used?","options":["Performance Monitor and a Data Collector Set","Task Manager's Performance tab left running","Resource Monitor's snapshot statistics","System Information exported to a report"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Performance Monitor (perfmon.exe) offers real-time charts and logs data for long-term analysis, in more detail than Task Manager. Data Collector Sets record performance data over time to set a system baseline. Counter logs collect resource statistics, and trace logs gather detailed service statistics.","optionWhy":["Correct. Data Collector Sets record data over time to build a baseline.","Task Manager is for quick checks. Performance Monitor is the tool for detailed long-term logging.","Resource Monitor gives advanced snapshot monitoring, not long-term logged baselines.","msinfo32 reports configuration and inventory, not performance statistics over time."],"optionRefs":["p. 487","pp. 486–487","p. 486","p. 478"],"ref":"p. 487, Performance Monitor","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-37","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"In Performance Monitor, what is the difference between counter logs and trace logs?","context":"In Performance Monitor, what is the difference between counter logs and trace logs?","options":["Counter logs track resource stats; trace logs record detailed service data.","Counter logs record service details; trace logs track resource statistics.","Counter logs show in Event Viewer; trace logs can't be saved or exported.","Counter logs are real-time only; trace logs run over a longer period."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Counter logs collect statistics on resources such as memory, disk, and processor to assess system health and performance. Trace logs gather detailed service statistics and extend Event Viewer by logging data that would otherwise be inaccessible.","optionWhy":["Correct. That matches the guide's definitions of the two log types.","This reverses the definitions. Counter logs collect resource statistics.","Saved logs can be analyzed in the Reports folder or exported to other programs.","Both are types of Data Collector Set logs that record data over time."],"optionRefs":["p. 487","p. 487","p. 487","p. 487"],"ref":"p. 487, Performance Monitor","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-38","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"Performance Monitor shows % Processor Time above 85% for long periods, and % Privileged Time is much higher than % User Time. What does this suggest?","context":"Performance Monitor shows % Processor Time above 85% for long periods, and % Privileged Time is much higher than % User Time. What does this suggest?","options":["The CPU may be underpowered for the core OS workload.","The CPU is healthy, as high processor time is expected.","The disk is failing, as privileged time measures disk I/O.","A user app has a memory leak that needs fixing by the vendor."],"answer":0,"explanation":"% Processor Time should generally be low, and sustained values over 85% may indicate a bottleneck. Comparing % Privileged Time and % User Time helps analyze it: significantly higher privileged time suggests the CPU may be underpowered, barely able to run Windows core processes efficiently.","optionWhy":["Correct. Much higher privileged time suggests the CPU can barely run core Windows processes.","Sustained values over 85% may indicate a bottleneck, so it isn't a sign of health.","Disk activity is measured with the Physical Disk counters, such as % Disk Time.","Memory leaks show up as a continuous decline in Available Bytes, not in processor time counters."],"optionRefs":["p. 488","p. 488","pp. 488–489","p. 489"],"ref":"pp. 487–488, Performance Counters","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-39","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"Over several days, the Memory > Available Bytes counter on a PC falls steadily, even though the same programs are running. What does this pattern most likely indicate?","context":"Over several days, the Memory > Available Bytes counter on a PC falls steadily, even though the same programs are running. What does this pattern most likely indicate?","options":["A memory leak in a process that doesn't release memory","Normal caching of frequently used files into the memory","A disk bottleneck shown by a long average disk queue length","A slow processor shown by high processor privileged time"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Available memory shouldn't fall below 10% of total RAM. A continuous decline may indicate a memory leak: a process that allocates memory but doesn't release it. Low memory can also slow the disk through excessive paging, so counters should be analyzed together.","optionWhy":["Correct. A continuous decline in Available Bytes may indicate a memory leak.","Caching speeds up access and isn't described as causing a continuous decline in Available Bytes.","Disk bottlenecks are measured by Physical Disk counters, not by Available Bytes.","Processor time counters measure CPU load. A steady fall in available memory points to a leak."],"optionRefs":["p. 489","pp. 483, 489","p. 489","pp. 488–489"],"ref":"pp. 487–489, Performance Counters","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-40","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"Physical Disk > % Disk Time averages over 85% for long periods, and Average Disk Queue Length is also high. What does this suggest?","context":"Physical Disk > % Disk Time averages over 85% for long periods, and Average Disk Queue Length is also high. What does this suggest?","options":["A disk problem or bottleneck","A memory leak in a process","Normal disk caching activity","An underpowered processor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"% Disk Time is the percentage of time the disk is busy with read or write requests, and an average above 85% for an extended period may indicate a disk problem. Average Disk Queue Length shows outstanding requests, and high values alongside high disk time suggest disk problems.","optionWhy":["Correct. High disk time together with a long queue suggests disk problems.","Memory leaks show as a continuous decline in Available Bytes.","Sustained values over 85% with a long queue go beyond normal activity.","CPU problems show in the Processor counters, such as % Privileged Time."],"optionRefs":["p. 489","p. 489","p. 489","p. 488"],"ref":"pp. 487–489, Performance Counters","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-41","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"While troubleshooting, you use msconfig to set a PC to start in Safe Boot. What must you remember once you've finished?","context":"While troubleshooting, you use msconfig to set a PC to start in Safe Boot. What must you remember once you've finished?","options":["Make sure Safe Boot isn't left set permanently afterwards.","msconfig is for permanent changes, so leave Safe Boot set.","Safe Boot can only be set with bcdedit, not with msconfig.","Safe Boot removes all services, so they must be reinstalled."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The System Configuration Utility (msconfig.exe) changes how the computer boots and loads Windows, and is mainly for diagnostic testing rather than permanent changes. The Boot tab can add options such as Safe Mode, but you should make sure Safe Boot or command prompt options aren't left set permanently.","optionWhy":["Correct. msconfig is for diagnostics, so Safe Boot shouldn't be left on.","The guide says msconfig is mainly for diagnostic testing, and permanent changes are made with tools like Services.","The msconfig Boot tab can add boot options such as Safe Mode directly.","Safe Mode loads only basic drivers and services. It doesn't remove the others."],"optionRefs":["p. 490","p. 489","p. 490","p. 495"],"ref":"pp. 489–490, System Configuration Utility","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-42","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance and Troubleshooting Tools","question":"When boot logging is enabled in msconfig, where are the boot events written?","context":"When boot logging is enabled in msconfig, where are the boot events written?","options":["To a log file in %SystemRoot%, not shown in Event Viewer","To the System log in Event Viewer's Windows Logs folder","To a Data Collector Set in Performance Monitor's Reports","To the Setup log in Event Viewer for installation events"],"answer":0,"explanation":"You can log boot events to a file saved in the %SystemRoot% folder. This log isn't displayed in Event Viewer.","optionWhy":["Correct. Boot logging writes boot events to a file in %SystemRoot%, which Event Viewer doesn't display.","The guide notes that the boot log file isn't displayed in Event Viewer.","Data Collector Sets record performance data, not boot logging from msconfig.","The Setup log records installation events. Boot logging writes to a separate file that Event Viewer doesn't display."],"optionRefs":["p. 491","pp. 480, 491","p. 487","pp. 480, 491"],"ref":"p. 491, System Configuration Utility","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-43","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A legacy BIOS PC with one Windows installation is starting up. In which order are the boot components used?","context":"A legacy BIOS PC with one Windows installation is starting up. In which order are the boot components used?","options":["MBR, boot sector, BOOTMGR, BCD, WINLOAD, kernel","GPT, EFI partition, BOOTMGFW.EFI, BCD, WINLOAD","BCD, MBR, WINLOAD, BOOTMGR, kernel, boot sector","WINLOGON, kernel, WINLOAD, BOOTMGR, MBR, POST"],"answer":0,"explanation":"After POST, the BIOS reads the MBR in the first sector of the boot disk. The MBR identifies the boot sector of the active partition, which loads BOOTMGR.EXE. BOOTMGR reads the BCD and loads WINLOAD.EXE, which loads the kernel (NTOSKRNL.EXE), HAL.DLL, and boot drivers. WINLOGON then waits for the user to sign in.","optionWhy":["Correct. This matches the guide's legacy BIOS sequence: MBR, then the boot sector, BOOTMGR, the BCD, WINLOAD, and the kernel.","That's the UEFI boot sequence, where the firmware reads the GPT. It isn't the legacy BIOS process.","The order is wrong. The MBR is read first, and BOOTMGR reads the BCD before WINLOAD.","This reverses the sequence. POST happens first and WINLOGON comes last."],"optionRefs":["pp. 492–493","p. 493","pp. 492–493","pp. 492–493"],"ref":"pp. 492–493, Boot Process","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-44","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"In UEFI boot mode, after POST, what does the firmware read on the boot device to find the EFI System Partition?","context":"In UEFI boot mode, after POST, what does the firmware read on the boot device to find the EFI System Partition?","options":["The GUID partition table (GPT)","The master boot record (MBR)","The BOOTMGR.EXE file on C:","The msconfig boot log file"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In UEFI mode, the firmware reads the GUID partition table (GPT) on the boot device. The GPT identifies the EFI System Partition, which holds the EFI boot manager and the BCD. BOOTMGFW.EFI reads the BCD to find WINLOAD.EFI.","optionWhy":["Correct. UEFI firmware reads the GPT to find the EFI System Partition.","Reading the MBR in the first sector is the legacy BIOS process.","BOOTMGR.EXE is the BIOS-mode boot manager, normally on the hidden System Reserved partition.","The msconfig boot log is an optional record of boot events. The firmware doesn't use it to find partitions."],"optionRefs":["p. 493","p. 492","p. 492","p. 491"],"ref":"p. 493, Boot Process","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-45","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A UEFI-based Windows PC reaches the lock screen but behaves erratically, and the technician wants to open the startup options menu without signing in. How can this be done?","context":"A UEFI-based Windows PC reaches the lock screen but behaves erratically, and the technician wants to open the startup options menu without signing in. How can this be done?","options":["Hold SHIFT and choose Restart from the lock screen's Power menu.","Press F8 repeatedly during startup, before the OS begins to load.","Press CTRL+SHIFT+ESC on the lock screen and choose Safe Mode there.","Run msinfo32 from the lock screen and pick Advanced boot options."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With BIOS boot, startup options appear if you press F8 before the OS loads. With UEFI, you need to reboot to show them: hold SHIFT while selecting Restart from the Power menu on the lock screen (you don't have to sign in). In Windows 11 you can also use Settings > System > Recovery > Advanced startup > Restart Now.","optionWhy":["Correct. SHIFT+Restart from the lock screen opens startup options without signing in.","F8 works with legacy BIOS boot. With UEFI you need to reboot into the options.","CTRL+SHIFT+ESC opens Task Manager, not the startup options menu.","msinfo32 generates a system information report. It doesn't open boot options."],"optionRefs":["p. 493","p. 493","pp. 481, 493","pp. 478, 493"],"ref":"p. 493, Advanced Boot Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-46","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"After installing a new scanner driver, a PC becomes unstable in normal startup. The technician wants to start Windows with only the basic drivers and services. Which option should he pick on the Startup Settings screen?","context":"After installing a new scanner driver, a PC becomes unstable in normal startup. The technician wants to start Windows with only the basic drivers and services. Which option should he pick on the Startup Settings screen?","options":["Safe Mode","Reset this PC","Startup Repair","Memory diagnostic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Safe Mode (F4 on the Startup Settings screen) loads only the basic drivers and services needed to start the system. This isolates problems to add-in drivers or application services, avoids a full reinstall, and gives a way to run tools such as chkdsk, System Restore, or antivirus.","optionWhy":["Correct. Safe Mode loads only basic drivers and services, isolating add-in driver problems.","Reset reinstalls Windows. It's a last resort, not a diagnostic startup mode.","Startup Repair tries to fix damaged boot files. This PC boots but is unstable.","The memory diagnostic tests RAM chips. It doesn't start Windows with minimal drivers."],"optionRefs":["p. 495","p. 501","p. 495","pp. 495, 505"],"ref":"pp. 493–495, Advanced Boot Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-47","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A PC won't boot, and the recovery environment shows that the boot files are damaged. Which recovery option is designed to try to fix this automatically?","context":"A PC won't boot, and the recovery environment shows that the boot files are damaged. Which recovery option is designed to try to fix this automatically?","options":["Startup Repair","System Restore","Reset this PC","Memory diagnostic"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the recovery environment, choose Troubleshoot > Advanced options. If the boot files are damaged, Startup Repair tries to fix them. Other options include System Restore, image recovery, refresh or reset, the memory diagnostic, and a command prompt for diskpart, sfc, chkdsk, bootrec, bcdedit, or regedit.","optionWhy":["Correct. Startup Repair is the option for damaged boot files.","System Restore rolls back configuration changes, such as registry changes and installs. It isn't the boot-file fix.","Reset reinstalls Windows. Try Startup Repair before something that drastic.","The memory diagnostic tests RAM for errors. It doesn't repair boot files."],"optionRefs":["p. 495","pp. 495–496","p. 501","p. 505"],"ref":"p. 495, WinRE and Startup Repair","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-48","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A user asks whether running System Restore to undo yesterday's faulty software installation will also delete the documents she saved today. What should the technician tell her?","context":"A user asks whether running System Restore to undo yesterday's faulty software installation will also delete the documents she saved today. What should the technician tell her?","options":["No. System Restore does not restore or delete user data files.","Yes. Every file is returned to the state of the restore point.","Yes. Only files saved in the Documents folder are rolled back.","No, but the program's install can't be undone with System Restore."],"answer":0,"explanation":"System Restore rolls back system configuration changes. It keeps multiple restore points, some created automatically, and can roll back changes to the whole registry and reverse program installations and updates. It doesn't restore or delete user data files.","optionWhy":["Correct. System Restore rolls back the registry and program installs but leaves user data files alone.","System Restore affects the registry and program installs, not user data files.","No user data files are restored or deleted, wherever they're saved.","System Restore can reverse program installations and updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 496","p. 496","p. 496","p. 496"],"ref":"p. 496, System Restore","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-49","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"Which requirements must a disk meet to be enabled for System Protection (System Restore)?","context":"Which requirements must a disk meet to be enabled for System Protection (System Restore)?","options":["NTFS, at least 300 MB free, and larger than 1 GB","FAT32, at least 300 MB free, and larger than 1 GB","NTFS, at least 1 GB free, and larger than 300 MB","Any file system, as long as it is the boot volume"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On the System Protection tab, you choose which disks to enable and how much capacity to use. The disk must be formatted with NTFS, have at least 300 MB of free space, and be over 1 GB in size.","optionWhy":["Correct. The disk must use NTFS, have at least 300 MB free, and be larger than 1 GB.","The free-space and size figures are right, but the disk must be formatted with NTFS, not FAT32.","This swaps the figures. It needs at least 300 MB free on a disk over 1 GB.","The guide requires NTFS and doesn't limit protection to the boot volume."],"optionRefs":["p. 496","p. 496","p. 496","p. 496"],"ref":"p. 496, Configuring System Protection","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-50","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A Windows update installed last night is causing an application to crash, and System Restore isn't an option. How can the technician remove that update?","context":"A Windows update installed last night is causing an application to crash, and System Restore isn't an option. How can the technician remove that update?","options":["Programs and Features > View installed updates > Uninstall","Device Manager > Driver tab > Roll Back Driver for the update","Event Viewer > Setup log > delete the update's install events","Disk Clean-up > Clean up system files > Windows Update cache"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If an update causes problems, you can try uninstalling it, possibly with System Restore. Otherwise, open the Programs and Features applet, select View installed updates, select the update, and select Uninstall. For a problem driver, use Roll Back Driver in Device Manager.","optionWhy":["Correct. View installed updates in Programs and Features lets you uninstall an update.","Roll Back Driver reverts device drivers, not Windows updates in general.","The Setup log records installation events. Deleting log entries doesn't remove the update itself.","Clearing the update cache frees space. It doesn't uninstall applied updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 498","p. 499","pp. 480, 498","p. 498"],"ref":"p. 498, Update and Driver Roll Back","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-51","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"With no recent system image, a technician uses Reset this PC with \"Keep my files\" to repair a badly broken Windows installation. What should she warn the user will be lost?","context":"With no recent system image, a technician uses Reset this PC with \"Keep my files\" to repair a badly broken Windows installation. What should she warn the user will be lost?","options":["Desktop applications, which have to be reinstalled","All of the user's personal data files and folders","Apps installed from the Microsoft Store by the user","Nothing at all, as the reset only repairs system files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reset this PC recopies the system files and reverts PC settings to the default. With Keep my files, it can keep user personalization settings, data files, and apps installed from the Windows Store, but desktop applications are removed. Remove everything also deletes personal files.","optionWhy":["Correct. Keep my files keeps data and Store apps but removes desktop applications.","Keep my files preserves personal files. That's the Remove everything option.","Apps installed from the Windows Store can be preserved by a reset with Keep my files.","A reset reverts PC settings to the default and removes desktop applications."],"optionRefs":["p. 501","p. 501","p. 501","p. 501"],"ref":"p. 501, Reinstalling Windows","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-52","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A desktop shows \"Invalid boot disk\" when restarted. A USB flash drive is plugged into the front panel. What should the technician check first?","context":"A desktop shows \"Invalid boot disk\" when restarted. A USB flash drive is plugged into the front panel. What should the technician check first?","options":["Remove the USB drive and check the firmware boot order.","Run bootrec /rebuildbcd from the recovery command prompt.","Reset the PC with Remove everything to rebuild the system.","Run Windows Memory Diagnostics to test the RAM modules."],"answer":0,"explanation":"This message means the system completely failed to boot. A modern cause is firmware set to boot from USB. Check for removable disks and change the boot order if necessary. If it happens when booting from a hard disk or SSD, check the drive connections; transitory errors can be a sign of a failing disk.","optionWhy":["Correct. A USB drive with USB boot set first is a common modern cause.","bootrec repairs boot information when the disk is found but Windows won't boot. Check simple causes like removable media first.","Reinstalling is drastic and unnecessary when a removable disk may be the cause.","Memory diagnostics address instability. An invalid boot disk points to boot device problems."],"optionRefs":["pp. 501–502","p. 502","pp. 501–502","pp. 501, 505"],"ref":"pp. 501–502, Failure to Boot/Invalid Boot Disk","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-53","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A GPT-partitioned SSD is detected by the firmware, but Windows won't boot, and the technician suspects the BCD is missing the Windows installation. Which bootrec command fits, and which should he avoid on this disk?","context":"A GPT-partitioned SSD is detected by the firmware, but Windows won't boot, and the technician suspects the BCD is missing the Windows installation. Which bootrec command fits, and which should he avoid on this disk?","options":["Use /rebuildbcd; avoid /fixmbr on GPT","Use /fixmbr; avoid /rebuildbcd on GPT","Use /fixboot; avoid /rebuildbcd on SSDs","Use /scannow; avoid /fixboot on GPT"],"answer":0,"explanation":"From a recovery command prompt, bootrec repairs boot information. /fixmbr repairs the MBR and shouldn't be used if the disk uses GPT. /fixboot repairs the boot sector. /rebuildbcd adds missing Windows installations to the boot configuration database (BCD).","optionWhy":["Correct. /rebuildbcd adds missing installations, and /fixmbr shouldn't be used on GPT disks.","This reverses the advice. /fixmbr is the option to avoid on GPT disks.","/fixboot repairs the boot sector, but /rebuildbcd addresses missing installations, and the guide doesn't restrict it on SSDs.","/scannow is a switch for sfc (System File Checker), not a bootrec option, so this combination makes no sense."],"optionRefs":["p. 502","p. 502","p. 502","pp. 502, 505"],"ref":"p. 502, No OS Found","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-54","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"After a driver update, Windows boots but never shows the desktop in normal mode. It does reach a working GUI in Safe Mode. What is the most likely fix?","context":"After a driver update, Windows boots but never shows the desktop in normal mode. It does reach a working GUI in Safe Mode. What is the most likely fix?","options":["Replace the graphics adapter driver","Rebuild the user's profile from scratch","Run bootrec /fixmbr to repair the MBR","Restore Windows from a full image backup"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If Windows seems to boot but doesn't show the sign-in screen or desktop, the likely cause is corrupt drivers or system files. If it boots to a GUI in Safe Mode, replace the graphics adapter driver. If it won't reach a GUI at all, the installation probably needs repairing or recovering from backup.","optionWhy":["Correct. A working Safe Mode GUI points to the graphics driver.","A corrupt profile slows the desktop after sign-in. This PC never reaches the desktop in normal mode.","The boot process works, since Safe Mode loads. MBR repair isn't relevant.","Recovery from backup is for when the system won't boot to a GUI at all."],"optionRefs":["p. 502","pp. 502–503","p. 502","p. 502"],"ref":"p. 502, Graphical Interface Fails to Load/Black Screen","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-55","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"One user's desktop takes several minutes to load after sign-in, while other users on the same PC sign in quickly. The technician decides to rebuild her local profile. Which files should NOT be copied from the old profile to the new one?","context":"One user's desktop takes several minutes to load after sign-in, while other users on the same PC sign in quickly. The technician decides to rebuild her local profile. Which files should NOT be copied from the old profile to the new one?","options":["NTUSER.DAT, NTUSER.DAT.LOG, and NTUSER.INI","The Documents, Pictures, and Desktop folders","The pagefile that holds paged memory data","The boot log file and Setup log files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Slow desktop loading after sign-in can mean a corrupt user profile, and NTUSER.DAT is particularly prone to this. To rebuild a local profile, create a new account and copy files from the old profile, excluding NTUSER.DAT, NTUSER.DAT.LOG, and NTUSER.INI.","optionWhy":["Correct. These registry-related profile files are excluded, because they may carry the corruption.","Personal data folders are exactly what should be copied to the new profile.","Paged memory is written to a disk pagefile, a system-level file. It isn't one of the profile files the guide says to exclude.","The boot log is saved in %SystemRoot% and the Setup log is an Event Viewer log. Neither is a profile file."],"optionRefs":["p. 503","p. 503","pp. 483, 503","pp. 491, 503"],"ref":"pp. 502–503, Troubleshoot Profile Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-56","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A PC is very slow. Task Manager shows a svchost.exe process at high utilization with a mix of CPU, memory, and disk activity. What should you do next?","context":"A PC is very slow. Task Manager shows a svchost.exe process at high utilization with a mix of CPU, memory, and disk activity. What should you do next?","options":["Wait for it to complete, as the mixed activity suggests normal work.","End the process at once, since svchost.exe is always malware.","Reset the PC immediately, since high utilization means corruption.","Disable the service permanently in Services without looking further."],"answer":0,"explanation":"First use Task Manager to see whether resources are at 90–100% and which process is most active (often a service in svchost.exe, such as Windows Update or Search). Then wait for these processes to finish. A mix of CPU, memory, and disk activity means it's probably working normally, just slowly. No disk activity, or disk stuck at 100%, could mean it has stalled.","optionWhy":["Correct. Mixed CPU, memory, and disk activity suggests the process is working, just slowly.","svchost.exe hosts genuine Windows services such as Update and Search. Ending the task comes later, only if it stays unresponsive.","High utilization is often a normal service at work. A reset is a last resort, not the next step.","The guide disables a service only if it stays unresponsive after restarting, and then checks with the vendor."],"optionRefs":["p. 503","p. 503","pp. 501, 503","p. 503"],"ref":"p. 503, Troubleshoot Performance Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-57","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A PC shows a blue screen but restarts too quickly for the technician to read the STOP error code. What should he configure?","context":"A PC shows a blue screen but restarts too quickly for the technician to read the STOP error code. What should he configure?","options":["Disable the automatic restart on system failure","Enable boot logging to a file in msconfig","Run Windows Memory Diagnostics from Admin Tools","Set the System log's overwrite option to Archive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A BSoD displays a Windows STOP error, often caused by faulty hardware or drivers. Note the STOP code (Stop: 0x0...) and search the Microsoft Knowledge Base. If the system restarts automatically before you can read it, select Disable automatic restarts in the Advanced Options menu, or set it in Advanced System Properties > Startup and Recovery Settings.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disabling automatic restart keeps the STOP error on screen.","Boot logging writes boot events to a log file. It doesn't keep the blue screen visible.","Memory Diagnostics tests RAM for errors, which helps with instability. It doesn't keep the blue screen visible long enough to read the code.","Log overwrite options control what happens when an Event Viewer log is full. They don't stop the automatic restart after a STOP error."],"optionRefs":["p. 505","p. 491","p. 505","pp. 480, 505"],"ref":"pp. 504–505, Troubleshoot System Fault Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-58","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A workstation freezes and reboots at random without showing any error message. Windows Memory Diagnostics reports errors. What should the technician do next?","context":"A workstation freezes and reboots at random without showing any error message. Windows Memory Diagnostics reports errors. What should the technician do next?","options":["Check the modules are seated, then retest them one at a time.","Replace the motherboard at once, since memory tests failed.","Run sfc /scannow, since memory errors mean corrupt system files.","Roll back the most recent display driver in Device Manager."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Instability with no error message suggests overheating, power problems, CPU/chipset/RAM issues, or corrupt kernel files. If memory errors are found, first check that all modules are seated correctly, then remove all but one module and retest to find the faulty one by elimination. If a known-good module is reported faulty, the problem is likely the motherboard.","optionWhy":["Correct. Reseat first, then isolate the faulty module by elimination.","Suspect the motherboard only if a known-good module is also reported faulty.","sfc checks Windows system files. RAM errors are a hardware matter to isolate first.","Driver rollback addresses driver problems. The memory test has pointed at RAM."],"optionRefs":["p. 505","p. 505","p. 505","pp. 499, 505"],"ref":"p. 505, System Instability and Frequent Shutdowns","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-59","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"A user has connected seven devices to an unpowered USB hub on a USB 3 port and now gets a USB controller resource warning. Updating chipset drivers didn't help. What should be tried next?","context":"A user has connected seven devices to an unpowered USB hub on a USB 3 port and now gets a USB controller resource warning. Updating chipset drivers didn't help. What should be tried next?","options":["Move the hub to a USB 2 port, or reduce the number of devices.","Move the hub to another USB 3 port for more bandwidth.","Enable USB selective suspend so idle devices are switched off.","Run chkdsk /r on each connected device to repair the errors."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A USB controller resource warning means too many devices are connected to one controller, typically through an unpowered hub with more than five devices. Connect the hub to a USB 2 port, because in some chipsets each USB 3 controller supports fewer endpoints. Use USB 2 for low-bandwidth devices and keep USB 3 for disks and network adapters. Reducing the number of devices also helps.","optionWhy":["Correct. USB 2 controllers may support more endpoints, and fewer devices reduces the load.","The issue is endpoints, not bandwidth. Some USB 3 controllers support fewer device connections.","The guide describes selective suspend as a power setting that turns off power to idle peripherals. It does not tie it to this warning.","chkdsk scans file systems. It doesn't address controller resource limits."],"optionRefs":["p. 506","p. 506","p. 506","pp. 505–506"],"ref":"p. 506, USB Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-60","module":14,"lesson":"14D Troubleshoot Windows OS Problems","question":"At startup, Windows reports that one or more services failed to start. In the Services snap-in, the failed service runs under a dedicated account whose password was recently changed. What should be checked?","context":"At startup, Windows reports that one or more services failed to start. In the Services snap-in, the failed service runs under a dedicated account whose password was recently changed. What should be checked?","options":["That the account is valid and its password is correct.","That the service's priority is set to Above normal in Task Manager.","That the user who signed in is a member of the Administrators group.","That the PC's clock is within 30 seconds of the domain controller."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Services depend on account permissions to run. Check that the service is associated with a valid user or system account and that its configured password is correct. Other steps include starting the service manually, checking dependencies, re-registering DLLs with regsvr32, and looking for orphaned entries with msconfig or regedit.","optionWhy":["Correct. An out-of-date password on the service's account will stop it from starting.","Process priority affects CPU share for running processes. It doesn't fix a service that won't start.","Services run under their own configured account, so the signed-in user's group membership isn't the issue.","Time drift affects authentication, but a recently changed password is the more direct cause here."],"optionRefs":["p. 507","pp. 482, 507","p. 507","p. 507"],"ref":"pp. 506–507, Services Not Starting","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-01","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A company runs a security awareness training program for all staff. What type of security control is this?","context":"A company runs a security awareness training program for all staff. What type of security control is this?","options":["Procedural","Logical","Physical","Implicit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"One way to classify security controls is physical, procedural, or logical. Physical controls work in the built environment (fences, doors, locks). Procedural controls are applied and enforced by people (incident response, management oversight, security awareness training). Logical controls are enforced by digital systems and software (authentication, antivirus, firewalls).","optionWhy":["Correct. Security awareness training is applied and enforced by people, so it's procedural.","Logical controls are enforced by digital systems and software, such as authentication, antivirus, and firewalls.","Physical controls work in the built environment, such as fences, doors, and locks.","Implicit deny is an access control principle, not a category of security control."],"optionRefs":["p. 510","p. 510","p. 510","pp. 510–511"],"ref":"p. 510, Logical Security Controls","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-02","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"An attacker alters figures in a company's financial database so that reports can no longer be relied on. Which goal of the CIA triad has been compromised?","context":"An attacker alters figures in a company's financial database so that reports can no longer be relied on. Which goal of the CIA triad has been compromised?","options":["Integrity","Confidentiality","Availability","Authorization"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The CIA triad is Confidentiality (sensitive data is accessible only by authorized users), Integrity (data is accurate and trustworthy), and Availability (resources are available when users need them).","optionWhy":["Correct. Altered data is no longer accurate and trustworthy, which is a loss of integrity.","Confidentiality is about keeping data accessible only to authorized users. The scenario describes altered data, not disclosed data.","Availability means resources can be accessed when needed. The database is still available, but its data is wrong.","Authorization is an IAM component that decides what a user may access. It isn't one of the CIA triad goals."],"optionRefs":["p. 511","p. 511","p. 511","p. 511"],"ref":"pp. 510–511, Logical Security Controls","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-03","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"In Identity and Access Management (IAM), which component determines what resources and actions a user may access, based on their role, responsibilities, and permissions?","context":"In Identity and Access Management (IAM), which component determines what resources and actions a user may access, based on their role, responsibilities, and permissions?","options":["Authorization","Authentication","Identification","Access control"],"answer":0,"explanation":"IAM's core components are identification (defining users, devices, and apps), authentication (proving who a user is with passwords, biometrics, or MFA), authorization (deciding what a user may access based on role and permissions), and access control (enforcing authorization policies with predefined rules).","optionWhy":["Correct. Authorization decides what resources and actions a user may access.","Authentication proves who a user is, for example with a password or biometrics. It doesn't decide what they may access.","Identification defines the users, devices, and applications in the system.","Access control enforces authorization policies. Authorization is what determines the allowed access."],"optionRefs":["p. 511","p. 511","p. 511","p. 511"],"ref":"pp. 510–511, Logical Security Controls","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-04","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"An administrator deletes a user account by mistake and immediately recreates it with exactly the same username. The user can no longer open folders she had permission to before. Why?","context":"An administrator deletes a user account by mistake and immediately recreates it with exactly the same username. The user can no longer open folders she had permission to before. Why?","options":["The new account has a different SID, so old permissions don't apply.","Permissions only take effect after the user changes her password once.","Recreated accounts are added to the Guests group, which has no access.","Usernames are case-sensitive, so the account name no longer matches."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows identifies each user and security group by a unique security ID (SID), and the SID is the only identifier used in permission entries. A recreated account gets a different SID even with the same username, so any permissions must be recreated.","optionWhy":["Correct. Permission entries use SIDs, and the recreated account has a new SID.","Permission entries reference the SID. A password change doesn't restore them.","The guide doesn't describe recreated accounts being put in Guests. The SID change is the cause.","The issue isn't the name. Permissions are tied to the SID, which changes on recreation."],"optionRefs":["p. 511","p. 511","pp. 511, 514","p. 511"],"ref":"p. 511, Access Control Lists (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-05","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A network firewall has rules allowing web and email traffic. A request for a different service doesn't match any rule. What happens, and which principle does this illustrate?","context":"A network firewall has rules allowing web and email traffic. A request for a different service doesn't match any rule. What happens, and which principle does this illustrate?","options":["It's refused by the default rule, illustrating implicit deny.","It's allowed, because no rule explicitly blocks that service.","It's allowed, illustrating the principle of least privilege.","It's queued until an administrator writes a matching rule."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Implicit deny means that unless a rule specifically grants access, the request is denied. Firewall rules are processed from top to bottom, and a request that matches no rule is handled by the last (default) rule, which refuses it.","optionWhy":["Correct. Unmatched requests fall through to the default deny rule.","That's the opposite of implicit deny. Access is denied unless a rule grants it.","Least privilege means granting the minimum access needed. It would never allow unmatched traffic.","Firewalls don't hold unmatched requests. The default rule refuses them."],"optionRefs":["p. 511","p. 511","p. 511","p. 511"],"ref":"p. 511, Implicit Deny","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-06","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A security team is assessing an unpatched server that faces the internet. Which definition describes the risk it poses?","context":"A security team is assessing an unpatched server that faces the internet. Which definition describes the risk it poses?","options":["The likelihood and impact of a threat exploiting a vulnerability","A weakness that could be triggered or exploited to cause a breach","The person or thing that poses the potential to exploit a weakness","The path or tool a malicious actor uses to carry out an attack"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A vulnerability is a weakness that could be triggered or exploited to cause a breach. A threat is the potential for someone or something to exploit a vulnerability; the source is the threat actor, and the path or tool used is the attack (threat) vector. Risk is the likelihood and impact of a threat actor exercising a vulnerability.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide defines risk as the likelihood and impact (consequence) of a threat actor exercising a vulnerability.","That's the definition of a vulnerability, a weakness that could be triggered or exploited. Risk measures likelihood and impact.","That's a threat actor (or threat agent), the source of the threat, not the risk itself.","That's the attack vector (also called the threat vector), the means of attack, not a measure of risk."],"optionRefs":["p. 512","p. 512","p. 512","p. 512"],"ref":"pp. 511–512, Information Security","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-07","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A team plans to use AES to encrypt large nightly backup files. What are the main advantage and the main problem of this symmetric cipher?","context":"A team plans to use AES to encrypt large nightly backup files. What are the main advantage and the main problem of this symmetric cipher?","options":["Fast bulk encryption; distributing the key securely is hard.","Uses a public/private pair; the messages must be very small.","Works as a one-way function; the original can't be recovered.","Its private key can't be derived; its public key is shared."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A symmetric cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt. Its main advantage is speed: it can bulk-encrypt multiple data streams efficiently. Its main problem is securely distributing and storing the key, which gets much harder the more widely the key has to be shared.","optionWhy":["Correct. Speed is the advantage, and secure key distribution is the problem.","That describes asymmetric encryption, which uses a key pair and is limited by key size.","That describes a cryptographic hash, which can't be reversed.","That describes an asymmetric key pair, not a symmetric cipher."],"optionRefs":["p. 512","pp. 512–513","p. 513","p. 513"],"ref":"p. 512, Symmetric Encryption","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-08","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"Alice encrypts a message using Bob's public key. Who can decrypt it?","context":"Alice encrypts a message using Bob's public key. Who can decrypt it?","options":["Only Bob, using his private key","Anyone who has Bob's public key","Alice, using her own private key","Anyone who knows the shared secret"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An asymmetric cipher uses a mathematically linked key pair. Either key can encrypt or decrypt, but only the other key in the pair can reverse the operation. A message encrypted with a public key can only be decrypted by the linked private key; the public key can't decrypt what it has just encrypted.","optionWhy":["Correct. Only the linked private key can reverse encryption done with the public key.","The public key can't decrypt what it has just encrypted. Only the linked private key can reverse it.","Alice's key pair isn't linked to Bob's public key, so it can't reverse that operation.","A shared secret key is used in symmetric encryption, not in this asymmetric exchange."],"optionRefs":["p. 513","p. 513","pp. 512–513","p. 512"],"ref":"pp. 512–513, Asymmetric Encryption","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-09","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A developer is designing how an application stores user passwords so that the original passwords can never be recovered from the stored values. Which cryptographic technology fits?","context":"A developer is designing how an application stores user passwords so that the original passwords can never be recovered from the stored values. Which cryptographic technology fits?","options":["A cryptographic hash such as SHA-256","A symmetric cipher such as AES","An asymmetric public/private key pair","A session key from a key exchange"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A cryptographic hash takes any amount of data and produces a fixed-length value through a one-way function, so the original can't be recovered from the hash. Hashes are used for secure storage of data whose original meaning doesn't need recovering, such as passwords. SHA-256 and SHA-3 are the most widely used.","optionWhy":["Correct. A one-way hash stores passwords without allowing recovery of the original.","AES encryption can be reversed by anyone holding the secret key, so the passwords could be recovered.","Asymmetric encryption can be reversed by the linked key, and messages can't be bigger than the key.","A session key is a symmetric key for encrypting data in a session, which is reversible."],"optionRefs":["p. 513","p. 512","pp. 512–513","pp. 513–514"],"ref":"p. 513, Cryptographic Hashes","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-10","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A developer needs to digitally sign a software update so customers can confirm it hasn't been altered. How is the digital signature created?","context":"A developer needs to digitally sign a software update so customers can confirm it hasn't been altered. How is the digital signature created?","options":["The sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with a private key.","The sender encrypts the whole message with the recipient's public key.","The sender hashes the message and encrypts the hash with their public key.","The sender shares a secret key and encrypts the message symmetrically."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The sender computes a cryptographic hash of the message, encrypts the hash with their own private key, and attaches the result as a digital signature. The recipient decrypts it with the sender's public key to get the sender's hash, computes their own hash of the message, and checks that the two match.","optionWhy":["Correct. A hash encrypted with the sender's private key forms the signature.","That protects confidentiality, as in key exchange. It doesn't prove the sender.","The public key is shared widely, so anyone could create that. Signatures use the private key.","Symmetric encryption protects data, but it doesn't prove the message came from a specific sender."],"optionRefs":["p. 513","pp. 513–514","p. 513","pp. 512–513"],"ref":"p. 513, Digital Signatures","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-11","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"In a key exchange, how does a sender securely deliver a symmetric session key to a recipient?","context":"In a key exchange, how does a sender securely deliver a symmetric session key to a recipient?","options":["Encrypt it with the recipient's public key","Encrypt it with the sender's own private key","Hash it with SHA-256 before sending it over","Send it in plain text over a fast network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Symmetric ciphers are faster, so they protect the actual data, but asymmetric encryption works efficiently only on data smaller than the key, which makes it ideal for exchanging keys. The sender encrypts the secret key with the recipient's public key, and the recipient decrypts it with their private key, then uses it to decrypt the data. A frequently changed session key is an ephemeral key.","optionWhy":["Correct. Only the recipient's private key can then recover the session key.","Anyone with the sender's public key could decrypt that, so the key wouldn't stay secret.","A hash is one-way, so the recipient couldn't recover the key from it.","Sending a secret key in plain text would let anyone intercept it."],"optionRefs":["p. 514","pp. 513–514","p. 513","pp. 512–514"],"ref":"pp. 513–514, Key Exchange","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-12","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A user signs in to PC1 with the local account PC1\\David. Why can't he use that same account to open files on PC2 over the network?","context":"A user signs in to PC1 with the local account PC1\\David. Why can't he use that same account to open files on PC2 over the network?","options":["It exists only in PC1's SAM, and PC2 doesn't recognize it.","Local accounts must be added to the Guests group on PC2 first.","PC2 must have User Account Control disabled to accept it.","The account is a Microsoft account, which can't use networks."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A local account is defined on one computer only and stored in that computer's Security Account Manager (SAM), part of HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE. Each machine keeps its own SAM and SIDs, so a local account can't sign in to a different computer or access files over the network.","optionWhy":["Correct. Local accounts are stored in each machine's own SAM and aren't recognized elsewhere.","The Guests group is one of the default groups in the guide, but PC2 simply has no database entry for the local account.","UAC controls the use of admin privileges. It has nothing to do with recognizing another PC's accounts.","PC1\\David is a local account. Microsoft accounts are identified by an email address."],"optionRefs":["p. 514","pp. 514–515","p. 517","p. 514"],"ref":"p. 514, User and Group Accounts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-13","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"In Windows 10/11, what permissions does the built-in Power Users group have?","context":"In Windows 10/11, what permissions does the built-in Power Users group have?","options":["The same permissions as the standard Users group","Permissions midway between Users and Administrators","The same permissions as the Administrators group","No permissions, as the group is disabled by default"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Power Users exists to support legacy applications. It was originally meant to sit between Administrators and Users, but that created vulnerabilities allowing escalation to Administrators. In Windows 10/11 it has the same permissions as the standard Users group. The Guests group is also legacy, with the same default rights as Users.","optionWhy":["Correct. In Windows 10/11, Power Users has the same permissions as Users.","That was the historic design, but it created escalation vulnerabilities and is no longer used.","Power Users doesn't have admin-level access in Windows 10/11.","It's the Guest user account that's disabled by default. Power Users exists for legacy apps with User-level rights."],"optionRefs":["p. 515","p. 515","p. 515","pp. 514–515"],"ref":"pp. 514–515, Security Groups","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-14","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"From an administrative command prompt, a technician needs to add the existing local account jsmith to the local Administrators group. Which command does this?","context":"From an administrative command prompt, a technician needs to add the existing local account jsmith to the local Administrators group. Which command does this?","options":["net localgroup Administrators jsmith /add","net user jsmith /add /group:Administrators","net user jsmith /active:yes Administrators","net use Administrators jsmith /persistent"],"answer":0,"explanation":"net user manages accounts from an administrative prompt: net user dmartin Pa$$w0rd /add /fullname:\"David Martin\" /logonpasswordchg:yes adds a user who must change the password at first sign-in, net user dmartin /active:no disables the account, and net user dmartin shows its properties. Group membership uses net localgroup Administrators dmartin /add.","optionWhy":["Correct. net localgroup Administrators <user> /add adds an existing account to the local Administrators group.","net user /add creates an account. Group membership is set with net localgroup.","/active controls whether an account is enabled. It doesn't change group membership.","net use maps network drives. It doesn't manage local groups."],"optionRefs":["p. 517","pp. 516–517","p. 517","p. 534"],"ref":"pp. 516–517, net user Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-15","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A user signed in with a standard account tries to install software that needs administrative rights. How does User Account Control handle this?","context":"A user signed in with a standard account tries to install software that needs administrative rights. How does User Account Control handle this?","options":["It prompts for an administrator's credentials to consent.","It shows a Yes/No consent prompt that the user can simply click.","It installs the software silently, but logs it for later review.","It blocks the install with no option to elevate in that session."],"answer":0,"explanation":"UAC is a least privilege control that requires explicit consent for privileged tasks, shown by a Security Shield icon. If the signed-in account is a standard user, an administrator's credentials must be entered in the consent dialog. If the account is already an administrator, the user must still click through the consent dialog.","optionWhy":["Correct. Standard users must enter admin credentials to elevate.","Click-through consent is for accounts that are already administrators.","UAC requires explicit consent for privileged tasks rather than allowing them silently.","UAC lets an administrator elevate within a standard user's session by entering credentials."],"optionRefs":["p. 517","p. 517","p. 517","p. 517"],"ref":"p. 517, User Account Control","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-16","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"You're configuring sign-in for a system that must use multifactor authentication. Which combination qualifies?","context":"You're configuring sign-in for a system that must use multifactor authentication. Which combination qualifies?","options":["A password plus a fingerprint scan","A password plus a separate PIN","A password plus a security question","A longer password changed each month"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Authentication factors are knowledge (something you know, like a password), possession (something you have, like a smart card or smartphone), and inherence (something you are, like a fingerprint). MFA needs at least two different types. Two credentials of the same type, such as a password and a PIN, aren't MFA.","optionWhy":["Correct. Knowledge plus inherence is two different factor types.","A password and a PIN are both knowledge factors. Two of the same type isn't multifactor.","A security question is also something you know, so both are knowledge factors.","However strong or often changed, a password is still a single knowledge factor."],"optionRefs":["p. 520","p. 520","p. 520","p. 520"],"ref":"p. 520, Multifactor Authentication","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-17","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"What is the difference between a TOTP and an HOTP one-time password?","context":"What is the difference between a TOTP and an HOTP one-time password?","options":["TOTP expires after a set time; HOTP uses a counter.","TOTP uses a counter; HOTP expires after a set time.","TOTP needs a server challenge; HOTP doesn't need one.","TOTP can be reused; HOTP is valid for only one login."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A time-based OTP (TOTP) is valid only for a set time, such as 1 minute, before it expires. A hash-based message authentication code OTP (HOTP) uses a counter that increases each time an OTP is requested, so every password is unique and single-use. With challenge-response, the server sends a challenge that the generator uses to make the OTP.","optionWhy":["Correct. A TOTP expires after a set time, while an HOTP uses a counter that increases with each request.","This reverses the definitions. TOTP is the time-based one, and HOTP is counter-based.","Using a server challenge describes the challenge-response OTP method.","Both are one-time passwords, valid for a single login session."],"optionRefs":["p. 521","p. 521","p. 521","pp. 520–521"],"ref":"pp. 520–521, One-Time Passwords","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-18","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"What is the core idea of a zero trust security framework?","context":"What is the core idea of a zero trust security framework?","options":["No user or device is trusted automatically; all are continually validated.","Devices inside the corporate network are trusted once they first authenticate.","Users get access only for as long as a task takes, then it is removed again.","Users get the minimum access they need to do their job and nothing more."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Zero trust means no user or device is ever trusted automatically, regardless of location or previous authentication. All users and devices must be authenticated, authorized, and continuously validated before being granted access. MFA is a core part of zero trust's \"never trust, always verify\" principle.","optionWhy":["Correct. Zero trust requires continuous authentication, authorization, and validation.","Zero trust explicitly rejects trust based on location or previous authentication.","That describes Just-in-Time (JIT) access, a separate practice.","Granting only the minimum access needed is the principle of least privilege, not zero trust."],"optionRefs":["p. 519","p. 519","p. 517","p. 511"],"ref":"p. 519, Authentication Methods","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-19","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"A user signs in to a Windows PC with a Microsoft account. What type of sign-in is this?","context":"A user signs in to a Windows PC with a Microsoft account. What type of sign-in is this?","options":["As a local sign-in, using credentials cached in the registry","As a network sign-in, authenticated by Kerberos on a domain","As a remote sign-in, authenticated over a VPN or web portal","As a SAML sign-in, which an identity provider has asserted"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a Windows local sign-in (interactive logon), the Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the credential with the one stored in the SAM. Signing in with a Microsoft account still counts as local, because its credentials are cached in the registry and compared there. Network sign-in passes credentials to a network service, usually Kerberos on a domain. Remote sign-in uses a VPN or web portal.","optionWhy":["Correct. Microsoft account sign-in is still local, using cached credentials.","Kerberos network sign-in is typical when a device is connected to a domain.","Remote sign-in is for devices not connected to the local network.","SAML passes credentials from an identity provider to a service provider. It isn't how Windows classifies this sign-in."],"optionRefs":["p. 523","p. 523","p. 523","pp. 523, 525"],"ref":"pp. 522–523, Windows Login Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-20","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"A sales rep's laptop has no network connection in a hotel, but she can still sign in with her Windows Hello PIN. Why does this work?","context":"A sales rep's laptop has no network connection in a hotel, but she can still sign in with her Windows Hello PIN. Why does this work?","options":["The PIN is held in the TPM and checked on the device itself.","The PIN is her Microsoft account password, cached by Windows.","The PIN is synced from the cloud and stored in the user profile.","The PIN only works offline because it's numeric and very short."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Windows Hello PIN is configured separately for each device. It uses the TPM and encryption, so the PIN isn't stored in Windows itself, which guards against sniffing and interception. Because the PIN is held in the TPM and checked on the device, it doesn't need verifying over a network, so sign-in works offline. A PIN can contain letters and symbols.","optionWhy":["Correct. Local TPM-based verification means no network check is needed.","A Windows Hello PIN is separate from the Microsoft account password and set per device.","The PIN isn't stored within Windows. It relies on the TPM on that device.","The PIN is a separate sign-in method from the Microsoft account password. The guide says the PIN is held in the TPM, which is why it works on that device."],"optionRefs":["p. 523","p. 523","p. 523","p. 523"],"ref":"pp. 523–524, Windows Hello","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-21","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"A user wants to set up Windows Hello facial recognition, but Windows insists that she set up a PIN first. Why?","context":"A user wants to set up Windows Hello facial recognition, but Windows insists that she set up a PIN first. Why?","options":["The PIN is a required backup if the other methods fail.","The PIN is what encrypts the 3-D facial image in the cloud.","Facial recognition only works when it's combined with a PIN.","The PIN registers the webcam's IR sensor with the TPM chip."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. It acts as a backup if other methods become unavailable, for example if the camera stops working or a hardware token is lost. Facial recognition uses an infrared sensor to record a 3-D image, so it can't be spoofed with a photo.","optionWhy":["Correct. The PIN is required as the fallback for Windows Hello.","The guide doesn't describe storing face images in the cloud. The PIN is a backup sign-in method.","Face sign-in works on its own. The PIN is a backup and a prerequisite, not combined at each sign-in.","The guide describes the PIN as a backup sign-in method, not as the step that registers the facial recognition data."],"optionRefs":["p. 524","p. 524","p. 524","p. 524"],"ref":"p. 524, Windows Hello","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-22","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"A manager wants single sign-on for all company services. What main disadvantage should you warn her about?","context":"A manager wants single sign-on for all company services. What main disadvantage should you warn her about?","options":["One compromised account gives access to many services.","Users must remember a separate password for each service.","It can't work with Kerberos or Active Directory domains.","It always requires a hardware token such as a FIDO2 key."],"answer":0,"explanation":"SSO means authenticating once to get access to multiple applications or services, as Kerberos does for Active Directory. Its advantage is that users don't have to manage multiple identities and passwords. Its disadvantage is that compromising the account compromises all of those services, and password-based SSO has proven very vulnerable to attack.","optionWhy":["Correct. A single compromised account exposes every service it signs in to.","Avoiding many passwords is the advantage of SSO, not its drawback.","Kerberos authentication in Active Directory domains is the guide's main example of SSO.","The guide describes SSO as signing in once to several services with one set of credentials. It does not describe a hardware token as a requirement."],"optionRefs":["p. 525","p. 525","p. 525","pp. 521, 525"],"ref":"p. 525, Single Sign-On","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-23","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"In Windows Hello for Business passwordless SSO, where is the device's private key kept?","context":"In Windows Hello for Business passwordless SSO, where is the device's private key kept?","options":["Only in the TPM of the user's own device","On the network authentication server","In the user's memory as a passphrase","In the SAM database in the registry"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The device is registered on the network with a public/private key pair. The private key is stored only in the device's TPM; it's never sent over the network and the user never knows it. The public key is registered on the server, which uses it to decrypt a secret the device encrypted with its private key, proving the request came from that device.","optionWhy":["Correct. The private key stays in the device's TPM and is never transmitted.","The server stores the device's public key, not its private key.","The user never knows the private key. It's generated and held inside the device's TPM.","The SAM stores local account credentials. The Hello for Business private key is in the TPM."],"optionRefs":["p. 525","p. 525","p. 525","pp. 522, 525"],"ref":"p. 525, Single Sign-On","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-24","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"In a SAML single sign-on process, what does the identity provider (IdP) send to the service provider (SP) after the user authenticates?","context":"In a SAML single sign-on process, what does the identity provider (IdP) send to the service provider (SP) after the user authenticates?","options":["A digitally signed SAML assertion","The user's plain-text password","A Kerberos ticket for the domain","A time-based one-time password"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With SAML, the user tries to sign in to a service provider (such as Google Workspace), which redirects them to the identity provider (such as the company login portal). After authentication, the IdP creates a SAML assertion, a digitally signed document containing the user's credentials, and sends it to the SP. The SP verifies it and grants access.","optionWhy":["Correct. The IdP sends a digitally signed SAML assertion that the SP verifies.","The SP receives a signed assertion, not the user's raw password.","Kerberos provides SSO in Active Directory domains. SAML uses signed assertions.","A TOTP is a soft token typed by the user, not the IdP's message to the SP."],"optionRefs":["p. 525","p. 525","pp. 525–526","pp. 521, 525"],"ref":"p. 525, Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-25","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"Which statement correctly describes a domain controller compared with a member server?","context":"Which statement correctly describes a domain controller compared with a member server?","options":["A DC stores Active Directory; a member server doesn't.","A member server stores AD; a DC only runs email services.","Both store AD, but only member servers authenticate users.","A DC can be any Windows PC; member servers need Server."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A domain needs at least one Windows Server configured as a domain controller (DC), which stores the Active Directory database of user, group, and computer objects and provides authentication. A member server is joined to the domain but doesn't keep a copy of the AD database; it provides file, print, and application services such as Exchange or SQL Server.","optionWhy":["Correct. Only DCs hold the AD database, while member servers provide other services.","This reverses the roles. The DC stores AD, and member servers run services such as Exchange.","The DC provides authentication, and member servers don't keep an AD copy.","The guide describes a domain controller as the server that stores and authenticates domain accounts. Not every Windows PC can take that role."],"optionRefs":["p. 526","p. 526","p. 526","p. 526"],"ref":"p. 526, Domain Controllers","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-26","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"The Sales manager should be able to add and delete Sales user accounts and assign them to the Sales group, but must not be able to change domain account policies. How is this best done in Active Directory?","context":"The Sales manager should be able to add and delete Sales user accounts and assign them to the Sales group, but must not be able to change domain account policies. How is this best done in Active Directory?","options":["Create a Sales OU and delegate control of it.","Add the manager to the Domain Admins security group.","Make the manager's PC a member server in the domain.","Give the manager a local administrator account on DCs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An organizational unit (OU) divides a domain into administrative realms. You might create OUs to delegate administration of departments or locations; for example, a Sales manager can be given rights to add and delete Sales accounts and assign them to a Sales group, but not to change account policies.","optionWhy":["Correct. An OU lets limited administrative control over a department be delegated to its manager.","Domain Admins can manage the whole domain, which goes far beyond what's needed.","A member server provides file, print, or application services. It doesn't delegate account administration.","Domain accounts are managed in AD. Delegating through an OU is the targeted approach."],"optionRefs":["p. 527","pp. 526–527","p. 526","pp. 526–527"],"ref":"p. 527, Organizational Units","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-27","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"An administrator has just changed a GPO and needs it applied to a test workstation now, rather than at the next refresh. Which command should be run on the workstation?","context":"An administrator has just changed a GPO and needs it applied to a test workstation now, rather than at the next refresh. Which command should be run on the workstation?","options":["gpupdate /force","gpresult /r","net user /active:yes","net use * /delete"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Policies are applied at sign-in and refreshed periodically, normally every 90 minutes. gpupdate applies new or changed policy immediately, and /force reapplies all policies, new and old. gpresult displays the resultant set of policy (RSoP), and /r shows the policies for the desktop.","optionWhy":["Correct. gpupdate applies policy immediately, and /force reapplies all policies.","gpresult displays the resultant set of policy. It doesn't apply changes.","net user /active enables or disables an account. It doesn't apply policy.","net use * /delete removes all mapped network drives. It has nothing to do with Group Policy."],"optionRefs":["p. 528","p. 528","pp. 517, 528","p. 535"],"ref":"p. 528, Group Policy Updates","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-28","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"How can a domain GPO's settings apply to every user and computer in the Sales department?","context":"How can a domain GPO's settings apply to every user and computer in the Sales department?","options":["Link the GPO to the Sales OU that holds those accounts.","Copy the GPO onto each Sales PC with secpol.msc tools.","Add the GPO's name to each user's logon script setting.","Run gpresult on every PC to push the policy to the PCs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A domain GPO can apply to multiple users and computers by being linked to a domain or OU object in AD. For example, Sales GPOs linked to the Sales OU apply to every user and computer account in that OU. A domain or OU can be linked to several GPOs, and inheritance determines the resultant set of policy (RSoP).","optionWhy":["Correct. GPOs linked to an OU apply to every user and computer in it.","Domain GPOs are linked centrally, not copied to each PC. secpol.msc edits local security policy.","Login scripts are a separate way to configure the environment. GPOs apply by linking to an OU or domain.","gpresult only displays the resultant set of policy. It doesn't deploy anything."],"optionRefs":["p. 528","p. 528","p. 528","p. 528"],"ref":"pp. 527–528, Group Policy and Login Scripts","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-29","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"When users sign in, an organization wants to map a drive letter to a departmental server folder and to deny sign-in if the client's software is out of date. What could do both?","context":"When users sign in, an organization wants to map a drive letter to a departmental server folder and to deny sign-in if the client's software is out of date. What could do both?","options":["A login script assigned to the accounts","A roaming profile path on each account","An administrative C$ share on each PC","A Windows Hello PIN on each workstation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A login script runs configuration or other processes when a user signs in. It can be defined in the user profile or assigned through Group Policy. It can set environment variables and map drives or printers, and it can check that the client meets security requirements, denying sign-in until out-of-date software is updated. Most of these tasks can also be done with GPOs.","optionWhy":["Correct. Login scripts can map drives and enforce client security requirements at sign-in.","Roaming profiles copy the user's profile from a share at sign-in. They don't enforce software requirements.","Administrative shares give admins access to drive roots. They don't run sign-in tasks.","A Hello PIN is a sign-in method. It doesn't map drives or check software versions."],"optionRefs":["p. 528","pp. 528, 542","p. 532","p. 523"],"ref":"p. 528, Login Scripts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-30","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"In a small office workgroup, a PC's folder shares aren't visible to other computers. The network type is Public. What needs to be done?","context":"In a small office workgroup, a PC's folder shares aren't visible to other computers. The network type is Public. What needs to be done?","options":["Set it to Private and turn on discovery plus file and printer sharing.","Keep it Public and enable discovery, as that's the recommended approach.","Join the PC to the domain, since workgroups can't share folders at all.","Rename the workgroup from WORKGROUP so that sharing gets activated."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a workgroup, the network type normally has to be Private to make the computer discoverable and allow sharing. You can change it from the notification in File Explorer or in Network & Internet settings. In Advanced sharing settings, both Turn on network discovery and Turn on file and printer sharing must be selected.","optionWhy":["Correct. Private allows discovery and sharing, and both settings must be on.","Discovery on public networks applies to all public networks and isn't recommended.","Workgroups can share resources. The problem here is the Public network type.","The workgroup name is almost always left as WORKGROUP. It doesn't control sharing."],"optionRefs":["pp. 530–531","p. 530","pp. 529–530","p. 530"],"ref":"pp. 530–531, Network Discovery and File Sharing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-31","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A small office uses a workgroup with password-protected sharing. Compared with a domain, what management problem will you face?","context":"A small office uses a workgroup with password-protected sharing. Compared with a domain, what management problem will you face?","options":["Each network user needs an account set up on the sharing PC.","Password-protected sharing can't be used in workgroups at all.","Workgroup shares can only be accessed by the Guest account.","Passwords are synced to every PC but can't then be changed."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With password-protected sharing, network users must have an account on the local machine hosting the share. In a workgroup you have to set up accounts for all users on all machines and manage passwords on each manually, use a single shared account, or disable security entirely.","optionWhy":["Correct. Workgroup sharing relies on local accounts on each host, managed manually.","It can be used. The problem is the account management it requires.","The Guest account is disabled by default and used only for sharing without passwords.","Workgroup passwords aren't synced. They have to be managed on each machine manually."],"optionRefs":["p. 531","p. 531","pp. 515, 531","pp. 525, 531"],"ref":"pp. 530–531, Network Discovery and File Sharing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-32","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A technician wants a shared folder on a Windows 11 desktop to have a different share name and accept no more than 10 simultaneous connections. Where are these settings configured?","context":"A technician wants a shared folder on a Windows 11 desktop to have a different share name and accept no more than 10 simultaneous connections. Where are these settings configured?","options":["On the Share tab of the folder's Properties dialog","On the Security tab of the folder's Properties dialog","Under the \"Give access to\" option in the shortcut menu","In Advanced sharing settings, under the All networks section"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Share tab in the folder's Properties dialog can customize permissions, change the share name, and limit simultaneous connections. Windows desktop versions are limited to 20 inbound connections. The quicker Give access to option just sets Read or Read/write for selected accounts.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Share tab handles share names, permissions, and connection limits.","The Security tab configures NTFS permissions (the ACL). It doesn't set share names or connection limits.","Give access to sets basic Read or Read/write access for accounts. It's not where the share name and connection limit are set.","Advanced sharing settings turn discovery and sharing on or off. They don't configure individual shares."],"optionRefs":["p. 532","p. 536","p. 532","pp. 530–532"],"ref":"pp. 531–532, File Share Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-33","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Which statement about Windows administrative shares such as C$ and ADMIN$ is accurate?","context":"Which statement about Windows administrative shares such as C$ and ADMIN$ is accurate?","options":["They're hidden and accessible only to local Administrators.","They're visible to everyone when network discovery is on.","They lose their password when protected sharing is off.","They must be created manually for each drive by the user."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows automatically creates hidden administrative shares, including the root of each local drive (C$) and the system folder (ADMIN$). Only members of the local Administrators group can access them, and they stay password-protected even if password-protected sharing is disabled. Adding $ to any share name hides it from browsing, but it can still be reached by command or drive mapping.","optionWhy":["Correct. Administrative shares are hidden and restricted to the local Administrators group.","The $ at the end of the share name hides them from general network browsing.","The guide says they stay password-protected even if password-protected sharing is disabled.","Windows creates the C$ and ADMIN$ administrative shares automatically."],"optionRefs":["p. 532","p. 532","p. 532","p. 532"],"ref":"pp. 531–532, File Share Configuration","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-34","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A technician wants the DATA share on server FS1 mapped to drive M: and to stay mapped after the PC restarts. Which command does this?","context":"A technician wants the DATA share on server FS1 mapped to drive M: and to stay mapped after the PC restarts. Which command does this?","options":["net use M: \\\\FS1\\DATA /persistent:yes","net use M: \\\\FS1\\DATA /persistent:no","net view \\\\FS1\\DATA /persistent:yes","net use M: /delete /persistent:yes"],"answer":0,"explanation":"net view lists servers, and net view \\\\MYSERVER lists a server's shares. net use M: \\\\MYSERVER\\DATA /persistent:yes maps the share to M: and keeps it mapped after a reboot; without the persistent switch, it has to be remapped. net use M: /delete removes the mapping, and net use * /delete removes all mapped drives.","optionWhy":["Correct. Mapping with /persistent:yes keeps the M: drive mapped after a reboot.","With /persistent:no (or no persistent switch), the mapping isn't kept and must be remapped after a reboot.","net view lists servers or the shares on a server. It doesn't map drives.","net use M: /delete removes the existing M: mapping instead of creating one."],"optionRefs":["p. 534","p. 534","p. 534","p. 534"],"ref":"p. 534, net use Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-35","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A printer shared from a 64-bit Windows 10 PC needs to be used by older 32-bit Windows clients. What can be set on the host to help them connect?","context":"A printer shared from a 64-bit Windows 10 PC needs to be used by older 32-bit Windows clients. What can be set on the host to help them connect?","options":["Additional drivers on the printer's Sharing tab","A mapped drive to the printer using net use","NTFS Full control on the printer's driver folder","The printer's IP address in each client's DNS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A printer is shared from the Sharing tab of its Printer Properties: check Share this printer and give it a name. The Additional drivers button can make drivers available for other client operating systems, such as 32-bit drivers when the print server is 64-bit. A printer shared from a Windows host is available only while that host is on.","optionWhy":["Correct. Additional drivers make the right drivers available to other client OSs.","net use maps file shares to drive letters. It doesn't provide printer drivers.","NTFS permissions control file access, not which printer drivers clients receive.","Network-capable printers can be added by IP address, but 32-bit drivers come from Additional drivers."],"optionRefs":["p. 535","pp. 534–535","pp. 535–537","p. 535"],"ref":"p. 535, Printer Sharing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-36","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"How do share-level permissions differ from NTFS permissions?","context":"How do share-level permissions differ from NTFS permissions?","options":["Share permissions apply only over the network; NTFS applies locally too.","NTFS permissions apply only over the network; share applies locally.","Share permissions can be set on single files; NTFS only on folders.","Both apply identically, so it doesn't matter which one is configured."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Share permissions apply only when a folder is accessed over the network; they give no protection against a user signed in locally on the host. NTFS permissions apply to both network and local access and can be set on folders and individual files. It's better practice to assign NTFS permissions to security groups rather than individual users.","optionWhy":["Correct. NTFS permissions cover local and network access, while share permissions cover only network access.","This reverses the scope. NTFS applies locally and over the network.","NTFS permissions can be applied to individual files. Share permissions are set at the root of the share.","They differ in scope, and the most restrictive applies when both are used over the network."],"optionRefs":["p. 536","p. 536","pp. 536–537","pp. 536–538"],"ref":"p. 536, NTFS versus Share Permissions","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-37","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Through the Sales group, a user has NTFS Read on a folder. Through the Managers group, she has NTFS Modify on the same folder. No deny permissions are set. What is her effective permission?","context":"Through the Sales group, a user has NTFS Read on a folder. Through the Managers group, she has NTFS Modify on the same folder. No deny permissions are set. What is her effective permission?","options":["Modify","Read","Full control","No access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a user gets permissions from several groups, Windows works out the effective permissions. Setting explicit deny aside, the user gets the most effective allow permission from any source. The guide's example: Read from Sales plus Modify from Managers gives Modify. Explicit Deny always takes precedence.","optionWhy":["Correct. Without any deny, the user gets the most effective allow permission, Modify.","With no deny set, the higher allow (Modify) wins, not the more restrictive one.","Full control isn't granted by either group. Combining Read and Modify gives Modify.","Both groups grant allow permissions, and nothing denies access."],"optionRefs":["p. 537","p. 537","p. 537","p. 537"],"ref":"pp. 536–537, NTFS versus Share Permissions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-38","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A parent folder has an inherited Deny Write for a group. An administrator then explicitly grants a user in that group Allow Write directly on one file inside the folder. What is the user's effective permission on that file?","context":"A parent folder has an inherited Deny Write for a group. An administrator then explicitly grants a user in that group Allow Write directly on one file inside the folder. What is the user's effective permission on that file?","options":["Allowed, because explicit permissions override inherited ones.","Write is denied, because any deny always beats any allow.","Write is denied until inheritance is turned off on the folder.","Write is allowed only when the file is opened over the network."],"answer":0,"explanation":"NTFS permissions on a folder are inherited by the files and subfolders created under it, unless inheritance is disabled. Directly assigned (explicit) permissions always override inherited permissions, including inherited denies. So an explicit Allow Write on the child file beats the inherited Deny Write.","optionWhy":["Correct. An explicit permission on the file overrides an inherited deny.","Explicit Deny beats Explicit Allow, but here the deny is inherited, and explicit permissions override inherited ones.","Turning off inheritance isn't needed. The explicit allow already takes precedence.","NTFS permissions apply both locally and over the network, so the location of access doesn't change the result."],"optionRefs":["p. 537","p. 537","p. 537","pp. 536–537"],"ref":"p. 537, Permissions Inheritance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-39","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A folder is shared with the share permission Read for Everyone, and its NTFS permissions give the Users group Modify. What effective permission does a member of Users have when opening the folder over the network?","context":"A folder is shared with the share permission Read for Everyone, and its NTFS permissions give the Users group Modify. What effective permission does a member of Users have when opening the folder over the network?","options":["Read, because the most restrictive applies over the network","Modify, because NTFS permissions always override shares","Full Control, because the two permissions are added together","No access, because Everyone and Users conflict with each other"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When share and NTFS permissions both apply to a resource, the most restrictive applies when it's accessed over the network. In the guide's example, Everyone has Read on the share and Users has Modify through NTFS, so a Users member's effective permission over the network is Read. Many admins set the share to Full Control and manage access with NTFS.","optionWhy":["Correct. Share Read combined with NTFS Modify gives Read over the network.","Over the network, the more restrictive share permission limits access.","Permissions aren't added together. The most restrictive of share and NTFS applies.","There's no conflict. Both grant access, and the most restrictive (Read) applies."],"optionRefs":["p. 538","p. 538","p. 538","p. 538"],"ref":"pp. 536–538, NTFS versus Share Permissions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-40","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A technician can't join a laptop running Windows 11 Home to the company domain. What is the most likely reason?","context":"A technician can't join a laptop running Windows 11 Home to the company domain. What is the most likely reason?","options":["Windows Home edition can't join a domain.","Domains can only be joined during setup.","The user must have a Microsoft account.","The network type must be set to Public."],"answer":0,"explanation":"To join a domain, the computer needs its own account in the domain. Windows Home edition can't join a domain. Joining also requires being on the domain network with DHCP-assigned IP and DNS settings, being able to resolve the domain's FQDN through DNS, and credentials with suitable domain administrative privileges.","optionWhy":["Correct. Windows Home edition can't join a domain, so this is the reason.","Attended setup can't join a domain. Joining is done afterwards through Settings or sysdm.cpl.","Joining needs domain admin credentials, not a Microsoft account.","Network type isn't a join requirement. The PC needs DHCP-configured IP and DNS on the domain network."],"optionRefs":["p. 539","p. 539","p. 539","p. 539"],"ref":"p. 539, Domain Setup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-41","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"On a domain-joined PC, a technician needs to sign in with the computer's local account \"support\" instead of a domain account. What should he type as the username?","context":"On a domain-joined PC, a technician needs to sign in with the computer's local account \"support\" instead of a domain account. What should he type as the username?","options":[".\\support","Domain\\support","support@domain","\\\\support"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To use domain services, sign in with a domain account; the Other user option or the Domain\\Username format specifies a domain sign-in. On a domain-joined machine, .\\Username or hostname\\username authenticates against a local user account instead.","optionWhy":["Correct. .\\Username authenticates against a local account on a domain-joined PC.","Domain\\Username specifies a domain account, not a local one.","The guide gives .\\Username or hostname\\username for local sign-in.","Two leading backslashes start a UNC network path, not an account name."],"optionRefs":["p. 540","p. 540","p. 540","pp. 533, 540"],"ref":"pp. 539–540, Domain Setup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-42","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"You're configuring a user's home folder in Active Directory. What should you enter in the Connect to box?","context":"You're configuring a user's home folder in Active Directory. What should you enter in the Connect to box?","options":["\\\\SERVER\\HOME$\\%USERNAME%","C:\\Users\\%USERNAME%\\Home","\\\\SERVER\\ROAMING$\\%USERNAME%","\\\\SERVER\\C$\\%USERNAME%"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A home folder is a private drive mapped to a network share where users store personal files. It's set on the account's Profile tab in the Connect to box, in the form \\\\SERVER\\HOME$\\%USERNAME%. HOME$ is a hidden share whose permissions let each user read and write only their own subfolder.","optionWhy":["Correct. This is the home folder path format given in the guide.","This is a local path on the PC. Home folders map to a network share.","This form is used for the roaming profile path, not the home folder.","C$ is an administrative share restricted to local Administrators, not a user home share."],"optionRefs":["p. 540","p. 540","p. 542","p. 532"],"ref":"p. 540, Home Folders","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-43","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"Staff who move between PCs want their Documents folders available everywhere, but the network team is worried about slow sign-ins from copying large profiles. Which approach fits best?","context":"Staff who move between PCs want their Documents folders available everywhere, but the network team is worried about slow sign-ins from copying large profiles. Which approach fits best?","options":["Redirect Documents to a file share using a GPO.","Use roaming profiles so everything is copied.","Use local accounts with the same names on each PC.","Share each PC's Documents folder as a hidden C$."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A roaming profile copies the whole profile from a share at sign-in and back at sign-out, which can badly affect bandwidth and sign-in speed if the profile holds large files. Folder redirection changes the target of a personal folder, such as Documents, to a file share. It's available only across the network and is configured through a GPO.","optionWhy":["Correct. Folder redirection makes Documents available everywhere without copying the whole profile.","Copying whole profiles with large files is what causes slow sign-ins and high bandwidth use.","Separate local accounts have separate profiles, and the files wouldn't follow the user.","C$ is an administrative share for drive roots, restricted to Administrators."],"optionRefs":["p. 542","p. 542","pp. 525, 542","p. 532"],"ref":"p. 542, Roaming Profiles and Folder Redirection","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-44","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"A folder on a FAT32-formatted partition is shared on the network. What kind of permissions can protect it?","context":"A folder on a FAT32-formatted partition is shared on the network. What kind of permissions can protect it?","options":["Only share permissions","Only NTFS permissions","Both share and NTFS","Neither type of permission"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Disk partitions using the FAT32 file system can be protected only with share permissions. NTFS permissions need an NTFS volume, and share permissions apply only when the folder is accessed over the network.","optionWhy":["Correct. FAT32 partitions can be protected only with share permissions.","NTFS permissions require an NTFS volume, so they can't protect a FAT32 partition.","FAT32 doesn't support NTFS permissions, so only share permissions are available.","Share permissions can still protect a shared FAT32 folder over the network."],"optionRefs":["p. 538","p. 538","p. 538","p. 538"],"ref":"pp. 536–538, NTFS versus Share Permissions","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-45","module":15,"lesson":"15C Windows Shares","question":"What advantage does mapping a network drive have over accessing the same share by its UNC path?","context":"What advantage does mapping a network drive have over accessing the same share by its UNC path?","options":["It gives a drive letter and can reconnect at every sign-in.","It bypasses the share and NTFS permissions on the server.","It copies the share's files to the local disk for offline use.","It hides the share from other users browsing the network."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A mapped drive assigns a share to a drive letter on the client, and with \"Reconnect at sign-in\" it can be persistent. A UNC path (\\\\server\\share) gives on-demand access only. Mapped drives appear under This PC and are removed with Disconnect.","optionWhy":["Correct. Mapping gives a drive letter and the option of a persistent connection.","Mapping doesn't change permissions. Access is still controlled by share and NTFS permissions.","A mapped drive is a pointer to the share on the server, not a local copy of its files.","Shares are hidden by adding $ to the share name, not by mapping a drive."],"optionRefs":["p. 534","pp. 534, 538","p. 533","p. 532"],"ref":"pp. 533–534, Mapped Drives","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-46","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"You find that the built-in account named \"Administrator\" is enabled on a PC. Why should you disable it?","context":"You find that the built-in account named \"Administrator\" is enabled on a PC. Why should you disable it?","options":["It isn't subject to User Account Control prompts.","It has the same rights as the Guest account.","It's a Microsoft account linked to the cloud.","It can only sign in from the network, not locally."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The default Administrator account is disabled by default to improve security. It isn't subject to UAC, so leaving it enabled bypasses the consent prompts that protect against malware running with elevated privileges.","optionWhy":["Correct. The built-in Administrator account bypasses User Account Control, so it should stay disabled.","The Administrator account has full admin rights. Guests have User-level rights.","\"Administrator\" is a local built-in account, not a Microsoft account managed online.","The guide doesn't describe it this way. The concern is that UAC doesn't apply to it."],"optionRefs":["p. 519","pp. 514, 519","pp. 514, 519","p. 519"],"ref":"p. 519, User Account Control","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-47","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"A company wants staff to authenticate with a USB device that stores credentials in firmware, unlocked by a PIN or fingerprint, and that follows FIDO2 standards. What is this?","context":"A company wants staff to authenticate with a USB device that stores credentials in firmware, unlocked by a PIN or fingerprint, and that follows FIDO2 standards. What is this?","options":["A hard token","An authenticator app","A TOTP soft token","A SAML assertion"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A hard token works like an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than on a smartphone. After registration, the user connects the token and authorizes it with a password, PIN, fingerprint, or voice, and the token sends its credentials to the service. These devices typically comply with FIDO2.","optionWhy":["Correct. A FIDO2 USB device with credentials in firmware is a hard token.","An authenticator app runs on a trusted device such as a smartphone, not as firmware in a USB key.","A soft token is a code sent or generated for the user to type in, not a hardware device.","A SAML assertion is a signed document from an identity provider, not a physical device."],"optionRefs":["p. 521","p. 521","pp. 520–521","p. 525"],"ref":"p. 521, Hard Token Authentication","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-48","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security Concepts","question":"Which practice grants users access to resources only when needed and only for as long as it takes to complete the task?","context":"Which practice grants users access to resources only when needed and only for as long as it takes to complete the task?","options":["Just-in-Time access","Privileged Access Management","Implicit deny rules","Single sign-on (SSO)"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access grants access only when needed and only for as long as the task takes. Privileged Access Management (PAM) focuses on securing, controlling, and monitoring privileged accounts. User Account Control helps implement these practices in Windows.","optionWhy":["Correct. JIT grants access only when needed and only for the duration of the task.","PAM is about securing, controlling, and monitoring privileged accounts generally.","Implicit deny refuses any request not explicitly allowed by a rule.","Single sign-on means authenticating once to get access to multiple services. It isn't time-limited access."],"optionRefs":["p. 517","p. 517","p. 511","p. 525"],"ref":"p. 517, User Account Control","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-01","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A clinic's new receptionist PCs must join the clinic's domain, encrypt their drives with BitLocker, and be managed with Group Policy. Which is the lowest Windows edition you can buy that meets all three needs?","context":"A clinic's new receptionist PCs must join the clinic's domain, encrypt their drives with BitLocker, and be managed with Group Policy. Which is the lowest Windows edition you can buy that meets all three needs?","options":["Windows 11 Pro","Windows Home","Windows Home N","Windows Home retail"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The main difference between Home and the Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions is domain support. Group Policy Editor and BitLocker are also unavailable in Home. Windows Pro, aimed at small and medium-sized businesses, includes all three, and Enterprise is sold only through volume licensing.","optionWhy":["Correct. Pro is the lowest edition that can join a domain and includes BitLocker and Group Policy.","Home can't join a domain and doesn't support BitLocker or Group Policy Editor.","N versions only remove media apps for EU compliance. Home N still lacks domain, BitLocker, and Group Policy support.","A retail license only changes transfer rights and who provides support. It's still Home, without the features needed."],"optionRefs":["p. 548","pp. 547–548","p. 547","p. 548"],"ref":"p. 548, Work and Education Features","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-02","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"An older PC has 8 GB of RAM installed, but Windows only uses about 4 GB of it. The CPU is 64-bit. What should you do so all the RAM can be used?","context":"An older PC has 8 GB of RAM installed, but Windows only uses about 4 GB of it. The CPU is 64-bit. What should you do so all the RAM can be used?","options":["Install a 64-bit edition of Windows, with 64-bit drivers","Switch the existing 32-bit Windows to the Pro edition","Install the Media Feature Pack to unlock extra memory","Replace the CPU, since a 64-bit CPU only runs 32-bit OSs"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All 32-bit Windows editions are limited to 4 GB of RAM, whereas 64-bit editions support more, with limits varying by edition. A 64-bit CPU can run either version. 64-bit Windows also needs 64-bit hardware drivers signed by Microsoft.","optionWhy":["Correct. The 32-bit edition caps RAM at 4 GB, and the 64-bit CPU can run 64-bit Windows.","Every 32-bit edition, including Pro, is limited to 4 GB of RAM.","The Media Feature Pack restores media apps on N versions. It has nothing to do with RAM limits.","This is backwards. A 64-bit CPU can run either 32-bit or 64-bit Windows."],"optionRefs":["p. 547","p. 547","p. 547","p. 547"],"ref":"p. 547, 32-bit Versus 64-bit","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-03","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A user in Sweden bought a PC with Windows 11 Pro N. A training video app she needs fails because Windows Media Player isn't there. How do you fix this?","context":"A user in Sweden bought a PC with Windows 11 Pro N. A training video app she needs fails because Windows Media Player isn't there. How do you fix this?","options":["Download and install Microsoft's Media Feature Pack on the PC.","Reinstall the PC with a 32-bit edition that includes the apps.","Upgrade the PC to Enterprise, which never ships as an N version.","Turn on BitLocker so that the protected media apps get unlocked."],"answer":0,"explanation":"N versions are made to comply with EU rules and leave out some built-in multimedia apps, such as Windows Media Player, the Music and Video apps, Voice Recorder, and Skype. The core OS is unchanged. To restore these features, download the Media Feature Pack from Microsoft.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Media Feature Pack restores the media apps missing from N versions.","Whether Windows is 32-bit or 64-bit has nothing to do with the N version's missing media apps.","Enterprise also comes in N versions. The Media Feature Pack is the fix.","BitLocker encrypts disks. It doesn't hide or unlock media applications."],"optionRefs":["p. 547","p. 547","p. 547","pp. 547–548"],"ref":"p. 547, N Versions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-04","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A user's old laptop came with Windows 11 Home preinstalled. She buys a new self-built PC and wants to move that Windows license to it. What do you tell her?","context":"A user's old laptop came with Windows 11 Home preinstalled. She buys a new self-built PC and wants to move that Windows license to it. What do you tell her?","options":["She can't, because a preinstalled OEM license is tied to the original device.","She can, as long as it's only active on one of the two computers at once.","She can, but only after she upgrades it to Pro through volume licensing.","She can, because every Windows 11 Home license is a Microsoft account license."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Home has two license models. An OEM license comes preinstalled on a PC or laptop, is valid only for that device, and is supported by the computer vendor. A retail license can be moved between computers, but can only be active on one at a time, and Microsoft provides support.","optionWhy":["Correct. OEM licenses are valid only for the device they came with.","That rule applies to retail licenses. Her preinstalled license is OEM.","Upgrading the edition doesn't make an OEM license transferable.","The guide describes OEM and retail licenses for Home, and OEM is tied to the device."],"optionRefs":["p. 548","p. 548","pp. 548–549","p. 548"],"ref":"p. 548, Windows Home Licensing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-05","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A large hospital group needs AppLocker to control which software can run, Windows Defender Credential Guard, and DirectAccess VPN. Which edition do you deploy, and how is it licensed?","context":"A large hospital group needs AppLocker to control which software can run, Windows Defender Credential Guard, and DirectAccess VPN. Which edition do you deploy, and how is it licensed?","options":["Enterprise, bought through a volume licensing agreement","Pro, bought as retail full packaged product copies","Pro for Workstations, as preinstalled OEM licenses","Pro Education, through an academic volume agreement"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows Enterprise is for large organizations, with advanced security, deployment, and management features such as AppLocker, Windows Defender Credential Guard, DirectAccess, and the Microsoft Desktop Optimization Pack. These aren't in Pro. Enterprise and Education are available only through volume licensing.","optionWhy":["Correct. These features are Enterprise-only, and Enterprise is sold only by volume licensing.","Pro doesn't include AppLocker, Credential Guard, or DirectAccess.","Pro for Workstations adds high-end hardware support, not these Enterprise security features.","Pro Education is based on Pro for schools, so it lacks these Enterprise features."],"optionRefs":["pp. 548–549","p. 549","p. 549","p. 548"],"ref":"pp. 548–549, Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-06","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"An engineering workstation has four physical CPUs and 4 TB of RAM. Within the Pro family, which edition supports this hardware?","context":"An engineering workstation has four physical CPUs and 4 TB of RAM. Within the Pro family, which edition supports this hardware?","options":["Pro for Workstations","Windows Pro edition","Pro Education edition","Windows Home edition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pro and Education support 2-way multiprocessing (up to 128 cores) and up to 2 TB of RAM. Pro for Workstations and Enterprise support 4-way multiprocessing (up to 256 cores) and up to 6 TB of RAM, and Pro for Workstations also supports advanced hardware such as NVDIMM. Home doesn't support multiple CPUs and is limited to 128 GB.","optionWhy":["Correct. It supports 4-way multiprocessing and up to 6 TB of RAM.","Pro supports only 2-way multiprocessing and up to 2 TB of RAM.","Pro Education is based on Pro, so it has the same 2-way and 2 TB limits.","Home doesn't support multiple CPUs and is limited to 128 GB of RAM."],"optionRefs":["p. 549","p. 549","pp. 548–549","p. 548"],"ref":"p. 549, Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-07","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"You try to run gpedit.msc on a small-office laptop to enforce a password policy, but Windows says it can't find it. The About page shows Windows 11 Home. What should you do?","context":"You try to run gpedit.msc on a small-office laptop to enforce a password policy, but Windows says it can't find it. The About page shows Windows 11 Home. What should you do?","options":["Upgrade the laptop to Pro, since Home lacks Group Policy Editor.","Run gpedit.msc from an elevated command prompt instead of Run.","Install the Media Feature Pack to add the missing system tools.","Join the laptop to a workgroup so that gpedit becomes available."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc) configures and enforces OS and application settings, but it isn't available in Windows Home. It's included in Pro, Enterprise, and Education, so upgrading the laptop to Pro is the fix.","optionWhy":["Correct. gpedit.msc isn't in Windows Home, but it is in Pro.","Elevation won't help, because the tool isn't included in Home at all.","The Media Feature Pack restores media apps on N versions, not Group Policy.","Workgroup membership doesn't add gpedit, which is missing from Home."],"optionRefs":["p. 548","p. 548","p. 547","p. 548"],"ref":"p. 548, Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-08","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"Which hardware requirements for Windows 11 does the Student Guide list?","context":"PC Health Check reports that a Windows 10 PC can't upgrade to Windows 11. Which hardware feature list must the PC meet?","options":["TPM 2.0 and at least 4 GB of memory","Legacy BIOS, an MBR disk, and at least a 32-bit CPU","A dedicated GPU, 6 TB of RAM, and 4-way processing","An N edition, the Media Feature Pack, and BitLocker"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide lists TPM 2.0 and at least 4 GB of memory for Windows 11, and Windows 11 is 64-bit only.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists TPM 2.0 and at least 4 GB of memory among the requirements for Windows 11, and Windows 11 is 64-bit only.","Windows 11 is 64-bit only, so a legacy 32-bit setup does not meet its requirements. The guide does not list a legacy BIOS or MBR requirement for Windows 11.","These are high-end limits for Pro for Workstations or Enterprise, not upgrade requirements.","N editions, the Media Feature Pack, and BitLocker are software features, not hardware requirements for Windows 11."],"optionRefs":["pp. 550–551","pp. 547, 550–551","p. 549","pp. 547, 550–551"],"ref":"pp. 550–551, Upgrade Paths","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-09","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A company wants to move several Windows 10 Home laptops straight to Windows 11 Enterprise with an in-place upgrade. What should you tell them?","context":"A company wants to move several Windows 10 Home laptops straight to Windows 11 Enterprise with an in-place upgrade. What should you tell them?","options":["Home can't upgrade directly to Enterprise; Home or Pro are the options.","Home can upgrade directly to Enterprise, keeping apps and settings.","Home can only move to Enterprise by downgrading to an N version first.","Home can upgrade to Enterprise, but only on 32-bit laptops with BIOS."],"answer":0,"explanation":"You can upgrade to the same or a higher edition (for example, Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you can't upgrade from Home straight to Enterprise. Downgrades, such as Pro to Home, keep only personal files, and downgrading from Enterprise isn't supported.","optionWhy":["Correct. A direct upgrade from Home to Enterprise isn't a supported path.","The guide states you can't upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly.","N versions only leave out media apps to meet EU rules. They have nothing to do with upgrade paths.","Windows 11 is 64-bit only, and Home can't upgrade directly to Enterprise anyway."],"optionRefs":["p. 551","p. 551","pp. 547, 551","pp. 547, 551"],"ref":"p. 551, Edition Considerations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-10","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A user wants to move her laptop from Windows 11 Pro down to Windows 11 Home. What will be kept after the change?","context":"A user wants to move her laptop from Windows 11 Pro down to Windows 11 Home. What will be kept after the change?","options":["Only her personal data files","Her files, apps, and settings","Her apps but not her files","Nothing, as it erases the disk"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Downgrades, such as Pro to Home, preserve only personal files, not apps or settings. Downgrading from Enterprise isn't supported. Upgrades to the same or a higher edition keep compatible applications, settings, and data.","optionWhy":["Correct. A downgrade keeps personal files but not apps or settings.","Keeping apps and settings is what an in-place upgrade does, not a downgrade.","It's the other way round. Personal files are kept, but apps aren't.","A downgrade keeps personal files. A clean install is what deletes all data."],"optionRefs":["p. 551","pp. 550–551","p. 551","pp. 551, 553"],"ref":"p. 551, Edition Considerations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-11","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"You need to confirm which Windows edition and version are installed on a user's PC before planning an upgrade. Where do you look?","context":"You need to confirm which Windows edition and version are installed on a user's PC before planning an upgrade. Where do you look?","options":["The About page in the Windows Settings app","The Media Feature Pack download page","The firmware boot priority settings","The Windows System Image Manager tool"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The About page in the Settings app reports the installed edition, along with the version, install date, and OS build under Windows specifications. It also lists device specifications such as processor, RAM, and system type.","optionWhy":["Correct. The About page shows the edition, version, and OS build.","That page is for restoring media apps on N versions, not for checking the edition.","Firmware setup controls boot devices. It doesn't report the Windows edition.","System Image Manager is used to configure answer files for unattended installs."],"optionRefs":["p. 550","p. 547","p. 555","p. 554"],"ref":"pp. 549–550, Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-12","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"A home user wants to move to a newer Windows version, keeping her installed applications, settings, and documents without restoring them from backup. Which type of installation should you perform?","context":"A home user wants to move to a newer Windows version, keeping her installed applications, settings, and documents without restoring them from backup. Which type of installation should you perform?","options":["An in-place upgrade from the current OS","A clean install that reformats the disk","A factory reset from the recovery partition","A full reset through Reset Windows"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An in-place upgrade runs setup from the existing OS and keeps third-party apps, user settings, and data files. A clean install repartitions and reformats the disk, deleting all existing data and settings, although it's generally considered more reliable. You can only upgrade within the same type of OS.","optionWhy":["Correct. An in-place upgrade keeps her apps, settings, and data files.","A clean install repartitions and reformats the disk, deleting all existing data, apps, and settings.","A factory recovery erases user data, settings, and third-party apps.","A full reset deletes the OS, apps, settings, and data to prepare for a fresh installation."],"optionRefs":["p. 553","p. 553","p. 558","p. 558"],"ref":"pp. 552–553, Clean Install or In-Place Upgrade","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-13","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"Before an in-place upgrade, the compatibility advisor flags an old accounting app as incompatible with the new Windows version. What should you do?","context":"Before an in-place upgrade, the compatibility advisor flags an old accounting app as incompatible with the new Windows version. What should you do?","options":["Uninstall it first, then get a compatible version from the vendor afterwards.","Leave it installed, as the upgrade will automatically convert it to be compatible.","Perform the upgrade, then roll back to the old OS if the accounting app fails.","Skip the backup, because an in-place upgrade keeps all data files anyway."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Before an in-place upgrade, uninstall any incompatible software or hardware. If an app or driver isn't compatible, check whether the vendor offers a new version that can be installed after the upgrade. Incompatible apps and devices must be replaced. Back up first in case the upgrade needs data recovery.","optionWhy":["Correct. Remove the incompatible software first, then reinstall a compatible version.","Unsupported software can cause problems during an in-place upgrade and should be uninstalled.","The guide's approach is to deal with incompatible software before upgrading.","A backup is crucial before an in-place upgrade in case data recovery is needed."],"optionRefs":["p. 553","p. 553","p. 553","p. 553"],"ref":"p. 553, Upgrade Considerations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-14","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"During a clean install on a server, Windows setup can't see the RAID volume you want to install to. What do you need?","context":"During a clean install on a server, Windows setup can't see the RAID volume you want to install to. What do you need?","options":["The RAID controller's driver from the vendor, loaded during setup","An answer file that lists the RAID volume's drive letter in advance","The Media Feature Pack, which includes extra storage drivers","A firmware change to boot from the network instead of the disk"],"answer":0,"explanation":"OS setup media may not include drivers for some hardware, such as RAID controllers. Without the controller driver, setup can't use the RAID volume. Get the latest drivers from the vendor's website and store them on a USB drive or network location so setup can load them.","optionWhy":["Correct. Setup needs the controller driver to see the RAID volume.","Answer files automate setup choices but can't supply a missing hardware driver.","The Media Feature Pack restores media apps on N versions, not storage drivers.","Network boot changes where setup loads from. Setup still needs the RAID driver."],"optionRefs":["p. 553","pp. 553–554","p. 547","pp. 553, 556"],"ref":"p. 553, Upgrade Considerations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-15","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"You must install Windows with the same settings, updates, and applications on 150 identical clinic PCs. What is the most efficient method?","context":"You must install Windows with the same settings, updates, and applications on 150 identical clinic PCs. What is the most efficient method?","options":["An unattended install using an answer file plus an image","An attended install on each PC, answering every setup prompt","An in-place upgrade on each PC, from whatever OS it has now","A factory reset on each PC from its OEM recovery partition"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Attended installations are labor-intensive. An unattended installation uses an answer file (configured with Windows System Image Manager) to automate setup choices, and often uses image deployment: a clone of an existing installation, including the OS, settings, updates, and apps, stored in one file. Images can be deployed over the network for consistency.","optionWhy":["Correct. Answer files and image deployment automate setup and give a consistent result.","Attended installs are time-consuming and labor-intensive for large deployments.","In-place upgrades keep each PC's existing apps and settings, which doesn't give a consistent configuration.","Recovery partitions restore the original factory state, without your settings or later updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 554","pp. 552–554","p. 553","p. 558"],"ref":"p. 554, Unattended Installations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-16","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"New laptops will be shipped straight from the vendor to remote staff. IT wants each laptop to set itself up with company settings and apps as soon as it connects to the internet, with no IT or user involvement. Which approach fits?","context":"New laptops will be shipped straight from the vendor to remote staff. IT wants each laptop to set itself up with company settings and apps as soon as it connects to the internet, with no IT or user involvement. Which approach fits?","options":["A remote zero-touch deployment","PXE network boot from the LAN","Slipstreamed USB install media","An attended clean installation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote zero-touch deployment sets up and configures devices automatically, without user intervention. It uses cloud-based services to deploy configurations and applications as soon as the device connects to the internet, so devices are ready to use straight out of the box.","optionWhy":["Correct. Zero-touch uses cloud services to configure devices when they first connect.","PXE boot needs the device on the local network with a DHCP and installation server, which remote staff don't have.","Slipstreamed media still needs someone to boot from it and run setup.","An attended install needs someone present to answer the setup prompts, which is the opposite of what IT wants."],"optionRefs":["p. 554","p. 556","p. 555","pp. 552–553"],"ref":"p. 554, Unattended Installations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-17","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"You want PCs with blank disks to boot over the network and start setup from installation files on a server. What do the PCs need for this to work?","context":"You want PCs with blank disks to boot over the network and start setup from installation files on a server. What do the PCs need for this to work?","options":["PXE-compliant firmware and adapters, plus DHCP to find the server","A recovery partition and the F11 key pressed during each startup","An MBR disk set as the highest-priority boot device in firmware","The Media Creation Tool run on each PC to make it network-bootable"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Network booting connects to a shared folder of installation files, which may be slipstreamed or use image deployment. Most computers support it with preboot execution environment (PXE)-compliant firmware and network adapters. The client uses information from a DHCP server to find the installation server. The PC also needs a partition for temporary files.","optionWhy":["Correct. PXE plus DHCP lets the client find the installation server and boot from it.","F11 typically boots an OEM recovery partition, which restores the factory image.","Network boot doesn't need a formatted local disk, and the internal drive should be prioritized only after installation.","The Media Creation Tool makes bootable USB, external drive, or ISO media, not network boot support."],"optionRefs":["p. 556","p. 558","p. 556","p. 555"],"ref":"p. 556, Network Boot","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-19","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"A developer wants to dual-boot Windows and Linux on a new PC. In what order should he install them, and why?","context":"A developer wants to dual-boot Windows and Linux on a new PC. In what order should he install them, and why?","options":["Windows first; it overwrites the boot loader, and GRUB detects it.","Linux first, because Windows setup then detects GRUB and adds itself","Either order, because both use the same Windows Boot Manager menu","Both at once on one partition, so they can share all their files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a multiboot setup, each OS goes on its own partition, and a boot loader such as GRUB or Windows Boot Manager shows a menu at startup. Install Windows first, because it overwrites the boot loader, while Linux's GRUB can detect other operating systems. For multiple Windows versions, install the oldest first.","optionWhy":["Correct. GRUB, installed second, will detect Windows and offer both at startup.","This is the wrong order. Windows setup overwrites the boot loader.","Linux typically uses GRUB, and the order matters because Windows overwrites the boot loader.","Each OS is installed on its own partition. A shared FAT32 partition can be used for data."],"optionRefs":["p. 556","p. 556","p. 556","p. 556"],"ref":"p. 556, Multiboot","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-20","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"You're setting up a 4 TB disk that needs six primary partitions. Which partition style must you use, and what boot method does it require?","context":"You're setting up a 4 TB disk that needs six primary partitions. Which partition style must you use, and what boot method does it require?","options":["GPT, which needs the UEFI firmware boot method","MBR, which requires the UEFI boot method","MBR, which requires the legacy BIOS method","GPT, which requires the legacy BIOS method"],"answer":0,"explanation":"MBR stores the partition table in the first 512-byte sector, allows up to four primary partitions, and requires legacy BIOS boot. GPT supports more than four primary partitions (up to 128 in Windows) and partitions over 2 TB, includes a backup of partition entries, and requires UEFI. BIOS won't recognize a GPT disk as a boot device.","optionWhy":["Correct. GPT supports more than four primary partitions and large disks, and needs UEFI.","MBR allows only four primary partitions and needs legacy BIOS boot.","MBR's boot method is right, but it allows only four primary partitions.","BIOS won't recognize a GPT disk as a boot device. GPT requires UEFI."],"optionRefs":["p. 557","p. 557","p. 557","p. 557"],"ref":"pp. 556–557, Disk Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-21","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"A Windows 10 PC boots from an MBR disk using legacy BIOS. You need to convert it to GPT for a Windows 11 upgrade without losing data. What should you do?","context":"A Windows 10 PC boots from an MBR disk using legacy BIOS. You need to convert it to GPT for a Windows 11 upgrade without losing data. What should you do?","options":["Run mbr2gpt, then switch the PC's firmware to UEFI boot mode.","Reformat the disk as GPT, keeping the firmware set to BIOS.","Create an extended partition with logical drives on the disk.","Mark a different MBR primary partition active, then reboot."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The mbr2gpt utility in Windows 10 version 1703 and later converts an MBR disk to GPT without data loss, although a backup is recommended. After converting, switch the firmware to UEFI boot mode. Windows 11 requires UEFI with Secure Boot.","optionWhy":["Correct. mbr2gpt converts without data loss, then the firmware must be set to UEFI.","Reformatting deletes all data, and a firmware left on legacy BIOS can't boot from a GPT disk.","Logical drives in an extended partition are an MBR workaround for more partitions, not a conversion to GPT.","Changing the active partition affects MBR booting. It doesn't convert the disk."],"optionRefs":["p. 557","pp. 553, 557","p. 557","p. 557"],"ref":"p. 557, GPT-Style Partitioning","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-22","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"A laptop's Windows installation won't boot. You plan to press F11 at startup to use the OEM factory recovery partition. What should you warn the user about first?","context":"A laptop's Windows installation won't boot. You plan to press F11 at startup to use the OEM factory recovery partition. What should you warn the user about first?","options":["It erases her data and apps, and later updates won't be on it.","It keeps her data and apps, but resets the desktop wallpaper only.","It upgrades the laptop to the latest Windows release automatically.","It converts the disk from MBR to GPT, so the firmware must change."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An OEM factory recovery partition restores the OS to its original state, often by pressing F11 or CTRL+F11 at startup. It erases user data, settings, and third-party apps, so back up first. It only works with the original disk, doesn't include updates applied after shipping, and takes up significant disk space.","optionWhy":["Correct. A factory recovery wipes user data and apps and restores the original image.","A factory recovery erases user data, settings, and third-party apps.","It restores the original factory image, without updates applied after shipping.","Disk conversion is done with mbr2gpt, not by the recovery partition."],"optionRefs":["p. 558","p. 558","p. 558","pp. 557–558"],"ref":"p. 558, Recovery Partition","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-23","module":16,"lesson":"16B OS Installations and Upgrades","question":"A technician's old Windows DVD is out of date, and new laptops have no optical drives. How should she create current, bootable installation media?","context":"A technician's old Windows DVD is out of date, and new laptops have no optical drives. How should she create current, bootable installation media?","options":["Use Microsoft's Media Creation Tool to make a bootable USB.","Copy the old DVD to a USB drive and install the updates later.","Boot from the internal drive and run setup from the old Windows.","Use the OEM recovery partition to create new install media."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Fewer computers have optical drives, so USB flash drives and external drives are now common for installations. Disc-based installs quickly go out of date. Microsoft's Media Creation Tool can create installation media on a bootable USB or external drive, or generate an ISO file. Set the USB device as the priority boot option.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Media Creation Tool creates current, bootable USB installation media.","Disc-based installs quickly go out of date. The Media Creation Tool gives current media.","These are new laptops that need fresh installation media, not an upgrade from an existing OS.","A recovery partition restores the factory image. It doesn't create current installation media."],"optionRefs":["p. 555","p. 555","pp. 553, 555","p. 558"],"ref":"p. 555, USB, External Drives, Flash Drives, and Hot-Swappable Drives","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-01","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"The help output for ls scrolls off the screen too fast to read. Which command shows it one page at a time?","context":"The help output for ls scrolls off the screen too fast to read. Which command shows it one page at a time?","options":["ls --help | more","ls --help > more","ls /? ; more","more ls --help"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To see a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Because the output can be long, it's common to pipe it to more so you can view one page at a time, e.g. ls --help | more. Alternatively, man shows the detailed manual page for a command.","optionWhy":["Correct. Piping --help into more shows the output one page at a time.","The > operator redirects output into a file called \"more\" instead of piping it to the pager.","/? is the Windows help switch. The semicolon just runs more as a separate command afterwards.","This tries to page files named \"ls\" and \"--help\". The help output needs to be piped into more."],"optionRefs":["p. 563","pp. 563, 565","pp. 562–563","p. 563"],"ref":"p. 563, Help System","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-02","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You've edited a configuration file in vi and are back in command mode. Which command saves the changes and quits?","context":"You've edited a configuration file in vi and are back in command mode. Which command saves the changes and quits?","options":[":wq",":q!",":w","CTRL+X"],"answer":0,"explanation":"vi/vim has two modes: command and insert. You switch to insert mode with keys such as i, a, A, or o, and press ESC to return to command mode. In command mode, :w saves, :wq saves and quits, :q! quits without saving, and :set number shows line numbers.","optionWhy":["Correct. In vi command mode, :wq writes (saves) the file and then quits the editor.",":q! quits vi without saving, so all the edits you just made would be lost.",":w saves the file but leaves you in vi. It doesn't quit the editor.","CTRL+X is the shortcut that exits the nano editor. It isn't used to save and quit in vi."],"optionRefs":["p. 563","p. 563","p. 563","p. 563"],"ref":"p. 563, Text Editors","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-03","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A user new to Linux is editing a file with nano. Which key combinations does she use to save the file and then exit the editor?","context":"A user new to Linux is editing a file with nano. Which key combinations does she use to save the file and then exit the editor?","options":["CTRL+O to save, then CTRL+X to exit","CTRL+S to save, then CTRL+Q to exit","ESC, then type :wq and press ENTER","Type :set number, then press CTRL+C"],"answer":0,"explanation":"nano is a simple editor for people familiar with Windows. Open or create a file with nano filepath (nano -l shows line numbers). Use the cursor keys to navigate and CTRL key shortcuts for operations: CTRL+O saves changes and CTRL+X exits.","optionWhy":["Correct. In nano, CTRL+O writes (saves) the file and CTRL+X exits the editor.","These aren't the nano shortcuts given in the guide. nano uses CTRL+O and CTRL+X.","ESC then :wq is how you save and quit in vi or vim. nano uses CTRL key shortcuts instead.",":set number shows line numbers in vi. It doesn't save or exit in nano."],"optionRefs":["p. 563","p. 563","p. 563","p. 563"],"ref":"p. 563, Text Editors","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-04","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need a detailed listing of every file in /etc, including hidden files. Which command do you run?","context":"You need a detailed listing of every file in /etc, including hidden files. Which command do you run?","options":["ls -la /etc","ls -l /etc","cat /etc","pwd -a /etc"],"answer":0,"explanation":"ls lists directory contents, like dir in Windows. -l gives a detailed (long) list, and -a shows all files, including hidden ones. They can be combined, as in ls -la /etc.","optionWhy":["Correct. -l gives a detailed list, and -a includes hidden files.","-l gives the detailed (long) list, but without -a it leaves out hidden files.","cat displays file contents, and /etc is a directory, so cat can't list it. Use ls to list a directory.","pwd prints the current working directory. It doesn't list contents."],"optionRefs":["p. 565","p. 565","p. 565","p. 564"],"ref":"p. 565, ls Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-05","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You want to add the contents of today.log to the end of an existing file, all.log, without erasing what is already in all.log. Which command does this?","context":"You want to add the contents of today.log to the end of an existing file, all.log, without erasing what is already in all.log. Which command does this?","options":["cat today.log >> all.log","cat today.log > all.log","cat today.log | all.log","mv today.log all.log"],"answer":0,"explanation":"cat displays and concatenates files. To redirect output to a file, > overwrites the destination, while >> appends to it. These redirection operators work with other commands too.","optionWhy":["Correct. The >> operator appends the output to the end of all.log, keeping its existing contents.","A single > redirects output by overwriting all.log, erasing what was already in it.","A pipe sends output to another command. all.log is a file, not a command.","mv moves or renames a file. It doesn't add one file's contents to the end of another."],"optionRefs":["p. 565","p. 565","pp. 562, 565","p. 568"],"ref":"p. 565, cat Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-06","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need to find every line in the system log /var/log/messages that contains \"uid=1003\", regardless of letter case. Which command does this?","context":"You need to find every line in the system log /var/log/messages that contains \"uid=1003\", regardless of letter case. Which command does this?","options":["grep -i \"uid=1003\" /var/log/messages","find /var/log/messages -name uid=1003","cat \"uid=1003\" /var/log/messages","ls -l /var/log/messages | grep -n"],"answer":0,"explanation":"grep searches and filters file contents, showing lines that match a search string or regular expression. It's especially useful for long files such as system logs. The -i option ignores case: grep -i \"uid=1003\" /var/log/messages.","optionWhy":["Correct. grep shows lines that match the search string, and -i makes the match ignore case.","find searches for files by name, size, and so on. It doesn't search inside a file's contents.","cat displays whole files. It doesn't filter for matching lines, so use grep to find lines containing a string.","This pipes a directory listing, not the log's contents, and gives no search string."],"optionRefs":["p. 566","p. 565","p. 565","p. 566"],"ref":"p. 566, grep Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-07","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need to locate all files owned by the user sam under /home, wherever they are. Which command fits?","context":"You need to locate all files owned by the user sam under /home, wherever they are. Which command fits?","options":["find /home -user sam","grep -i sam /home","ls -la /home/sam","du -user sam /home"],"answer":0,"explanation":"find searches for files with the syntax find path expression. Expressions include -name, -size, -user (owner), -perm (permissions), and -type. grep, by contrast, searches inside file contents for matching lines.","optionWhy":["Correct. find with -user searches for files owned by a given user.","grep searches inside file contents for text. It doesn't search by file owner.","This only lists one directory, and it doesn't filter by owner across /home.","du reports disk usage. It doesn't search for files by owner."],"optionRefs":["p. 565","p. 566","p. 565","p. 568"],"ref":"pp. 565–566, Search Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-08","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"In Linux, which quoting makes the shell treat everything inside it literally, so that $(pwd) isn't expanded?","context":"In Linux, which quoting makes the shell treat everything inside it literally, so that $(pwd) isn't expanded?","options":["Single quotes ' '","Double quotes \" \"","A single backslash","An asterisk *"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Escaping makes the shell treat metacharacters literally. A backslash (\\) escapes only the next character. Single quotes give strong escaping, so everything inside is literal. Double quotes give weak escaping, which still allows variable expansion and command substitution such as $(pwd).","optionWhy":["Correct. Single quotes give strong escaping, treating everything literally.","Double quotes give weak escaping, so $(pwd) would still be expanded.","A backslash escapes only the next character, not the whole string.","The asterisk is itself a metacharacter that matches any number of characters."],"optionRefs":["p. 567","p. 567","p. 567","pp. 566–567"],"ref":"pp. 566–567, Metacharacters and Escaping","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-09","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A new data disk partition, /dev/sdb1, must be attached to /data every time the server boots. Where do you define this?","context":"A new data disk partition, /dev/sdb1, must be attached to /data every time the server boots. Where do you define this?","options":["Add an entry for it in /etc/fstab","Run mount /dev/sdb1 /data once only","Add the partition to /etc/hosts","Run fsck /dev/sdb1 at every boot"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A filesystem must be mounted (attached to a directory in the hierarchy) to be used. mount /dev/sdb1 /data mounts it for now. /etc/fstab holds static filesystem information that defines what is mounted automatically at boot: device, mount point, filesystem type, and options.","optionWhy":["Correct. /etc/fstab defines filesystems mounted automatically at boot.","mount attaches it now, but it won't persist after a reboot without an fstab entry.","/etc/hosts maps hostnames to IP addresses. It has nothing to do with mounting.","fsck checks and repairs filesystems after problems such as improper shutdowns. It doesn't mount them at boot."],"optionRefs":["p. 567","p. 567","p. 575","p. 567"],"ref":"p. 567, Filesystem Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-10","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"After a power cut caused an improper shutdown, you suspect the filesystem on /dev/sda2 is corrupted. Which command checks and repairs it?","context":"After a power cut caused an improper shutdown, you suspect the filesystem on /dev/sda2 is corrupted. Which command checks and repairs it?","options":["fsck /dev/sda2","df /dev/sda2","mount /dev/sda2","chkdsk /dev/sda2"],"answer":0,"explanation":"fsck (filesystem check) checks and repairs filesystems, which is especially useful after an improper shutdown or disk corruption. It's run with the device name, such as fsck /dev/sda1, and often runs automatically at boot if problems are detected.","optionWhy":["Correct. fsck scans the filesystem for errors and attempts to fix them.","df reports free space, filesystem type, and mount point. It doesn't repair anything.","mount attaches a filesystem. It doesn't check it for errors.","chkdsk is the Windows disk-checking command. The Linux equivalent is fsck."],"optionRefs":["p. 567","p. 568","p. 567","p. 567"],"ref":"p. 567, Filesystem Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-11","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need to copy the whole directory /home/david/project, including its contents, into /tmp. Which command does this?","context":"You need to copy the whole directory /home/david/project, including its contents, into /tmp. Which command does this?","options":["cp -r /home/david/project /tmp","cp /home/david/project /tmp","mv /home/david/project /tmp","rm -r /home/david/project /tmp"],"answer":0,"explanation":"cp copies files, and with -r (or --recursive) it copies whole directories, e.g. cp -r /home/david/project /tmp. mv moves or renames files, and rm -r deletes directory trees without prompting.","optionWhy":["Correct. The -r (recursive) option makes cp copy the directory and everything inside it.","Without -r, cp won't copy a directory together with its contents.","mv moves the directory rather than copying it, so the original would be gone.","rm -r deletes a directory tree, without any confirmation prompt. It doesn't copy anything."],"optionRefs":["p. 568","p. 568","p. 568","p. 568"],"ref":"p. 568, mv Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-12","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need to rename the file app1.dat in /var/log to app1.old, without making a copy. Which command does this?","context":"You need to rename the file app1.dat in /var/log to app1.old, without making a copy. Which command does this?","options":["mv /var/log/app1.dat /var/log/app1.old","cp /var/log/app1.dat /var/log/app1.old","cat /var/log/app1.dat > /var/log/app1.old","rm /var/log/app1.dat /var/log/app1.old"],"answer":0,"explanation":"mv either moves files between directories or renames them. Renaming in place: mv /var/log/app1.dat /var/log/app1.old. cp would create a copy and leave the original in place.","optionWhy":["Correct. mv renames a file when the source and destination are in the same directory.","cp creates a second file and leaves the original, which isn't a rename.","This copies the contents into a new file and leaves the original in place.","rm deletes files. It would remove the file rather than rename it."],"optionRefs":["p. 568","p. 567","p. 565","p. 568"],"ref":"p. 568, mv Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-13","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A junior admin is about to run rm -r /home/david/data to clear out an old project. What should you warn him about first?","context":"A junior admin is about to run rm -r /home/david/data to clear out an old project. What should you warn him about first?","options":["It deletes the whole tree at once with no confirmation prompt.","It moves the folder to a recycle bin, so it can be restored.","It only removes empty folders, so the files must go first.","It asks for confirmation of each file before deleting it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"rm deletes files, and with -r it deletes entire directory trees. Use -r with caution: Linux commands don't prompt for confirmation, so there's no chance to cancel.","optionWhy":["Correct. rm -r doesn't ask for confirmation, so there's no chance to cancel.","The guide doesn't describe a recycle bin. rm deletes without confirming.","With -r, rm deletes the whole directory tree, including its contents.","Linux commands don't prompt for confirmation here, which is why care is needed."],"optionRefs":["p. 568","p. 568","p. 568","p. 568"],"ref":"p. 568, rm Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-14","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A Linux server's disk is filling up, and you need to find which directory trees under /var are using the most space. Which command fits?","context":"A Linux server's disk is filling up, and you need to find which directory trees under /var are using the most space. Which command fits?","options":["du, to show how big the directory trees under /var are","df, to show the size of each directory tree in /var","fsck, to report the size of each directory in /var","ls -a, to total the space used by each folder in /var"],"answer":0,"explanation":"df (disk free) shows a device's free space, filesystem, total size, space used, percentage used, and mount point. du (disk usage) shows how a device is used, including the size of directory trees and the files within them.","optionWhy":["Correct. du (disk usage) reports the sizes of directory trees and the files in them.","df shows free and used space for a device as a whole, not per directory tree.","fsck checks and repairs filesystems. It doesn't report usage.","ls lists directory contents. du is the command that reports the size of directory trees."],"optionRefs":["p. 568","p. 568","p. 567","pp. 565, 568"],"ref":"p. 568, df and du Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-15","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Several admins need to run occasional commands with root privileges on a Linux server, but you don't want to share the root password between them. What should you set up?","context":"Several admins need to run occasional commands with root privileges on a Linux server, but you don't want to share the root password between them. What should you set up?","options":["Add them to /etc/sudoers so they can use sudo","Give each admin the root password to use su -","Have every admin sign in as root all the time","Use chown to make each admin the owner of root"],"answer":0,"explanation":"su switches to another account (root if no username is given) and asks for that account's password; su - starts a new shell with root's environment. sudo lets users listed in /etc/sudoers run commands with superuser privileges. Its main advantage over su is that the root password doesn't have to be shared.","optionWhy":["Correct. sudo grants elevated commands without sharing the root password.","su needs the target account's password, which means sharing the root password.","Root should be used only when absolutely necessary, not for daily work.","chown changes file ownership. It doesn't grant administrative privileges."],"optionRefs":["p. 569","pp. 568–569","p. 568","p. 570"],"ref":"pp. 568–569, User Account Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-16","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"On a Linux system, which file stores user passwords as encrypted hashes, along with settings such as password age and expiration?","context":"On a Linux system, which file stores user passwords as encrypted hashes, along with settings such as password age and expiration?","options":["/etc/shadow","/etc/passwd","/etc/group","/etc/sudoers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"User settings are stored in /etc/passwd and group settings in /etc/group. User passwords are usually stored as encrypted hashes in /etc/shadow, with settings such as age and expiration. useradd, usermod, and userdel manage users, and passwd changes passwords.","optionWhy":["Correct. /etc/shadow holds password hashes and password age settings.","/etc/passwd stores user settings, such as the effective group ID, but not the password hashes.","/etc/group stores group settings, not users' password hashes.","/etc/sudoers lists the users allowed to run commands with sudo, not password hashes."],"optionRefs":["p. 569","p. 569","p. 569","p. 569"],"ref":"p. 569, User Management Commands","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-17","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"You need the script backup.sh to give its owner full permissions, its group read and execute, and everyone else no access. Which chmod command does this?","context":"You need the script backup.sh to give its owner full permissions, its group read and execute, and everyone else no access. Which chmod command does this?","options":["chmod 750 backup.sh","chmod 754 backup.sh","chmod 570 backup.sh","chmod 760 backup.sh"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Linux permissions are read (4), write (2), and execute (1), set for the owner, the group, and others. You add the values for each: owner rwx = 4+2+1 = 7, group r-x = 4+0+1 = 5, others none = 0. So chmod 750 backup.sh. Only the owner can change permissions.","optionWhy":["Correct. 7 = rwx for the owner, 5 = r-x for the group, and 0 = nothing for others.","754 gives others read (4), but they're meant to have no access.","This gives the owner r-x and the group rwx, which is the wrong way round.","6 gives the group read and write, but the group needs read and execute (5)."],"optionRefs":["pp. 569–570","pp. 569–570","pp. 569–570","pp. 569–570"],"ref":"pp. 569–570, File Permissions Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-18","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"ls -l shows -rwxr-xr-- 1 bobby admins scan.sh. What can a user who isn't bobby and isn't in the admins group do with scan.sh?","context":"ls -l shows -rwxr-xr-- 1 bobby admins scan.sh. What can a user who isn't bobby and isn't in the admins group do with scan.sh?","options":["Read it only","Read and run it","Read, edit and run it","Nothing at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In symbolic notation, the first character shows the type (- for a file, d for a directory), followed by three sets for owner, group, and others. Here: owner rwx, group admins r-x, others r--. So other users can only read the file.","optionWhy":["Correct. The last three characters, r--, give others read permission only.","Read and execute (r-x) is the group's permission, not the permission for others.","Full read, write, and execute (rwx) permissions belong only to the owner, bobby.","Others have r--, which still allows reading, so they don't have zero access."],"optionRefs":["p. 569","p. 569","p. 569","p. 569"],"ref":"p. 569, File Permissions Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-19","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"Using sudo, you need to make the user anna the owner of the project directory and everything inside it, and set its group to devs. Which command does this?","context":"Using sudo, you need to make the user anna the owner of the project directory and everything inside it, and set its group to devs. Which command does this?","options":["chown -R anna:devs project","chown anna:devs project","chmod -R anna:devs project","chgrp -R anna project"],"answer":0,"explanation":"chown changes the owner of a file or directory, and only the superuser or sudoers can use it. The syntax is chown [OPTIONS] OWNER[:GROUP] FILE, and -R applies the change recursively to a directory and its contents. Regular users can change a file's group with chgrp.","optionWhy":["Correct. -R applies the owner and group change to the directory and all its contents.","Without -R, only the directory itself changes, not its contents.","chmod changes a file's permissions (read, write, execute), not its owner or group.","chgrp changes only the group, and anna is a user, not the target group."],"optionRefs":["p. 570","p. 570","p. 570","p. 570"],"ref":"p. 570, chown Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-21","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"You need to install software on an Ubuntu (Debian-based) machine and on a Red Hat-based server. Which package managers and package formats does each use?","context":"You need to install software on an Ubuntu (Debian-based) machine and on a Red Hat-based server. Which package managers and package formats does each use?","options":["Ubuntu: APT with .deb; Red Hat: DNF with .rpm","Ubuntu: DNF with .rpm; Red Hat: APT with .deb","Ubuntu: APT with .rpm; Red Hat: DNF with .deb","Ubuntu and Red Hat: both use DMG and PKG files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pre-compiled software is installed with a package manager, which varies by distro. The Advanced Packaging Tool (APT) is used by Debian-based distros with .deb packages. DNF (Dandified YUM) is used by Red Hat-based distros with .rpm packages; it succeeds YUM, with better performance and dependency management.","optionWhy":["Correct. Debian-based distros use APT and .deb, while Red Hat-based distros use DNF and .rpm.","This swaps them. APT is for Debian-based distros like Ubuntu.","The package formats are swapped. APT uses .deb, and DNF uses .rpm.","DMG and PKG are macOS installer formats. They aren't used by Linux package managers."],"optionRefs":["p. 571","p. 571","p. 571","pp. 571, 592"],"ref":"p. 571, Package Management Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-22","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"On an Ubuntu workstation, you want to refresh the package information from the repositories and then upgrade all installed packages. Which commands do you run, in order?","context":"On an Ubuntu workstation, you want to refresh the package information from the repositories and then upgrade all installed packages. Which commands do you run, in order?","options":["apt update, then apt upgrade","apt upgrade, then apt update","dnf check-update, then dnf upgrade","apt install, then apt-get update"],"answer":0,"explanation":"apt is the preferred command-line interface for APT. apt update refreshes package information, apt upgrade upgrades all packages, and apt install PackageName installs a new application. Older systems and scripts may use apt-get update and apt-get upgrade.","optionWhy":["Correct. Refresh the package information first, then upgrade all packages.","The order is wrong. Refresh package information before upgrading.","These are DNF commands for Red Hat-based distros, not Ubuntu.","apt install adds one new application. It doesn't upgrade everything."],"optionRefs":["p. 572","p. 572","pp. 572–573","p. 572"],"ref":"p. 572, apt Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-23","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"On a Red Hat-based server, a technician needs to remove the package telnet. Which command should he use?","context":"On a Red Hat-based server, a technician needs to remove the package telnet. Which command should he use?","options":["dnf remove telnet","apt remove telnet","rm -r telnet","dnf check-update telnet"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On Red Hat-based distros, dnf check-update refreshes package information, dnf upgrade (or update) upgrades all packages, dnf install PackageName installs, and dnf remove PackageName removes an application.","optionWhy":["Correct. dnf remove uninstalls a package on Red Hat-based systems.","apt is the package tool for Debian-based distributions, not Red Hat.","rm deletes files and directories. It doesn't uninstall packages through the package manager.","check-update refreshes package information. It doesn't remove anything."],"optionRefs":["p. 573","pp. 571–572","pp. 568, 573","p. 572"],"ref":"pp. 572–573, dnf Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-24","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"Before installing a package downloaded from a vendor's repository, you want to confirm it hasn't been tampered with. How is a package's integrity verified?","context":"Before installing a package downloaded from a vendor's repository, you want to confirm it hasn't been tampered with. How is a package's integrity verified?","options":["Check its hash or signature, e.g. SHA-256 or GPG","Check that its name ends in .deb or the .rpm type","Run it as root to see if it reports any errors","Check the file size with du against the website"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Package managers are configured with the addresses of software repositories, and they verify package integrity before installing or updating, using cryptographic hashes or signatures such as MD5, SHA-256, or GPG. The vendor publishes the hash value and function on its site.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hashes or signatures confirm the package wasn't altered.","The file extension shows the package format. It doesn't prove integrity.","Running an unverified package with full privileges is risky and proves nothing about integrity.","File size alone doesn't verify integrity. The guide uses hashes or signatures."],"optionRefs":["p. 572","pp. 571–572","pp. 568, 572","pp. 568, 572"],"ref":"p. 572, Distributions and Repositories","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-25","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"A Linux server is running slowly. You open top and want to sort the running processes so the biggest memory users are at the top. Which key do you press?","context":"A Linux server is running slowly. You open top and want to sort the running processes so the biggest memory users are at the top. Which key do you press?","options":["M","P","u","q"],"answer":0,"explanation":"top gives a dynamic, real-time view of processes and lets you prioritize, sort, or terminate them. ENTER refreshes, SHIFT+N sorts by decreasing PID, M sorts by memory usage, P sorts by CPU usage, u shows one user's processes, and q exits.","optionWhy":["Correct. In top, pressing M sorts the process list by memory usage.","In top, P sorts the process list by CPU usage, not by memory usage.","u shows only the processes of a user you name at the prompt. It doesn't sort by memory.","q exits top's process list, which ends the session rather than sorting it."],"optionRefs":["p. 574","p. 574","p. 574","p. 574"],"ref":"pp. 573–574, top Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-26","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"You've installed the sshd service. It must start now and also start automatically every time the server boots. Which commands do this?","context":"You've installed the sshd service. It must start now and also start automatically every time the server boots. Which commands do this?","options":["systemctl start sshd and systemctl enable sshd","systemctl enable sshd only, which starts it now","systemctl start sshd only, which persists","systemctl status sshd, then reboot the server"],"answer":0,"explanation":"systemctl controls systemd services. start runs a service immediately, stop stops it, enable makes it start automatically at boot, disable stops it starting at boot, and status shows whether it's active, inactive, or failed. Enabling doesn't start a service immediately.","optionWhy":["Correct. start runs it now, and enable makes it start at every boot.","enable sets it to start at boot. start is what runs it immediately.","start runs the service now, but it won't start again automatically after a reboot unless it's enabled.","status only reports the service's state. It doesn't enable it."],"optionRefs":["p. 574","p. 574","p. 574","p. 574"],"ref":"p. 574, systemd and systemctl Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-27","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"On a modern Linux workstation, you need to see all network interfaces and their IP addresses. The old ifconfig command isn't installed. Which command should you use?","context":"On a modern Linux workstation, you need to see all network interfaces and their IP addresses. The old ifconfig command isn't installed. Which command should you use?","options":["ip addr show","ip route show","dig localhost","ipconfig /all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The ip command replaces older tools such as ifconfig and route. ip addr show displays all interfaces and their IP addresses, ip link set enp0s3 up brings an interface up, and ip route show displays the routing table.","optionWhy":["Correct. ip addr show lists interfaces and their IP addresses.","ip route show displays the routing table, not interface addresses.","dig queries DNS name servers. It doesn't show interface addresses.","ipconfig is the Windows command. On Linux, ip replaces ifconfig."],"optionRefs":["p. 575","p. 575","p. 575","p. 575"],"ref":"p. 575, ip Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-28","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"A developer wants the name myserver.local to resolve to 192.168.1.10 on his own Linux PC, without changing anything on the DNS server. What should he edit?","context":"A developer wants the name myserver.local to resolve to 192.168.1.10 on his own Linux PC, without changing anything on the DNS server. What should he edit?","options":["Add an entry for it to /etc/hosts","Add it as a nameserver in resolv.conf","Add the mapping to /etc/fstab","Run dig myserver.local 192.168.1.10"],"answer":0,"explanation":"/etc/hosts is a text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses for local name resolution, before DNS servers are queried. Its entries can override DNS or define local names. /etc/resolv.conf specifies which DNS servers the system queries.","optionWhy":["Correct. /etc/hosts maps names to IPs locally before DNS is queried.","resolv.conf lists the DNS servers to query, not individual name mappings.","/etc/fstab defines the filesystems mounted at boot. It doesn't map hostnames.","dig only queries DNS servers for records. It doesn't create a local name mapping."],"optionRefs":["p. 575","p. 575","p. 567","p. 575"],"ref":"p. 575, /etc/hosts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-29","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"A Linux PC can reach internet hosts by IP address but not by name. You check the file that defines which DNS servers the system queries. Which file is it?","context":"A Linux PC can reach internet hosts by IP address but not by name. You check the file that defines which DNS servers the system queries. Which file is it?","options":["/etc/resolv.conf","/etc/hosts","/etc/shadow","/etc/network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"/etc/resolv.conf defines how DNS resolution is handled. It lists the DNS servers to query, for example nameserver 8.8.8.8. /etc/hosts holds local name-to-IP mappings that are checked before DNS.","optionWhy":["Correct. resolv.conf lists the nameservers used for DNS resolution.","/etc/hosts holds local name mappings. It doesn't list the DNS servers to query.","/etc/shadow stores user password hashes, not DNS server settings.","The guide describes resolv.conf as the file that lists the DNS servers to query. It does not describe /etc/network as holding that list."],"optionRefs":["p. 575","p. 575","p. 569","p. 574"],"ref":"p. 575, /etc/resolv.conf","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-30","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"A developer needs to test from a Linux terminal whether a web API at https://api.example.com responds correctly. Which command is designed for this?","context":"A developer needs to test from a Linux terminal whether a web API at https://api.example.com responds correctly. Which command is designed for this?","options":["curl https://api.example.com","ping api.example.com once","dig api.example.com A","traceroute api.example.com"],"answer":0,"explanation":"curl transfers data to or from a server using protocols such as HTTP, HTTPS, and FTP. It's commonly used to test and interact with web services, and is especially good for working with APIs. ping only tests reachability, and dig queries DNS.","optionWhy":["Correct. curl makes HTTP/HTTPS requests and is good for testing APIs.","ping tests whether a host is reachable with ICMP. It doesn't test the web service.","dig queries DNS name servers, here for the host's address (A) record. It doesn't make HTTP requests, so it can't test the API's responses.","traceroute shows the path packets take. It doesn't test the web service's responses."],"optionRefs":["p. 575","p. 575","p. 575","p. 576"],"ref":"p. 575, curl Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-31","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"You suspect a DNS problem and want to query the name servers directly for the records of example.com from a Linux terminal. Which command should you use?","context":"You suspect a DNS problem and want to query the name servers directly for the records of example.com from a Linux terminal. Which command should you use?","options":["dig example.com","curl example.com","ip route show","cat /etc/hosts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"dig (Domain Information Groper) is a flexible tool for querying DNS name servers. It performs DNS lookups and displays the answers returned by the server. It's the Linux counterpart to tools such as nslookup.","optionWhy":["Correct. dig queries DNS name servers and shows their answers.","curl transfers data with protocols such as HTTP. It isn't a DNS query tool.","ip route show displays the local routing table. It doesn't query DNS.","This shows local name mappings only. It doesn't query DNS servers."],"optionRefs":["p. 575","p. 575","p. 575","p. 575"],"ref":"p. 575, dig Command","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-32","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"You want a backup script to run at 2:15 a.m. every Friday. Which crontab entry does this?","context":"You want a backup script to run at 2:15 a.m. every Friday. Which crontab entry does this?","options":["15 02 * * 5 /usr/bin/backup.sh","02 15 * * 5 /usr/bin/backup.sh","15 02 5 * * /usr/bin/backup.sh","* 02 * * 5 /usr/bin/backup.sh"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Crontab fields are minutes (0–59), hour (0–23), day of month (1–31), month (1–12), and day of week (0–7), followed by the command with its full path. * means any value. So 15 02 * * 5 runs at 02:15 every Friday (day 5). Edit the crontab with crontab -e, list it with crontab -l, and remove it with crontab -r.","optionWhy":["Correct. Minute 15, hour 02, any day and month, weekday 5 (Friday).","The minute and hour are swapped, so this runs at 15:02 (3:02 p.m.).","5 is in the day-of-month field, so this runs on the 5th of each month.","* in the minute field runs it every minute from 2:00 to 2:59."],"optionRefs":["pp. 576–577","p. 576","p. 576","p. 576"],"ref":"pp. 576–577, Crontab Syntax","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-33","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"You need to add a new scheduled job to your user account's crontab. Which command opens it for editing?","context":"You need to add a new scheduled job to your user account's crontab. Which command opens it for editing?","options":["crontab -e","crontab -l","crontab -r","systemctl enable cron"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each user can schedule tasks in a personal crontab, which cron merges into a system-wide schedule and checks every minute. crontab -e opens the editor (vi by default), crontab -l lists jobs, and crontab -r removes scheduled jobs.","optionWhy":["Correct. crontab -e opens your crontab in the editor (vi by default) so you can add jobs.","crontab -l lists the scheduled jobs without opening an editor.","crontab -r removes scheduled jobs, the opposite of what you want.","This makes the cron service start at boot. It doesn't edit your job list."],"optionRefs":["p. 576","p. 576","p. 576","pp. 574, 576"],"ref":"p. 576, Backup and Scheduling Commands","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-34","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package and Network Management","question":"On many modern Linux distributions, which tool manages persistent network configuration, either through a GUI or the nmcli command?","context":"On many modern Linux distributions, which tool manages persistent network configuration, either through a GUI or the nmcli command?","options":["NetworkManager","/etc/hosts file","crontab entries","The top utility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The persistent configuration is applied after a reboot or when an adapter is reinitialized. Historically it was managed by editing /etc/network/interfaces and using ifup/ifdown. Many distros now use NetworkManager, managed through a GUI or nmcli, or the systemd-networkd configuration manager.","optionWhy":["Correct. NetworkManager is managed through a GUI or the nmcli tool.","/etc/hosts maps hostnames to IPs. It isn't the network configuration manager.","crontab schedules jobs. It doesn't manage network configuration.","top monitors and manages running processes, not network settings."],"optionRefs":["p. 574","p. 575","p. 576","p. 573"],"ref":"p. 574, Network Management Commands","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-35","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac user can't remember where she saved a presentation. What is the quickest way to search the whole Mac for it?","context":"A Mac user can't remember where she saved a presentation. What is the quickest way to search the whole Mac for it?","options":["Press COMMAND+SPACE to open Spotlight Search","Press F3 to open Mission Control and look","Open Disk Utility and verify the disk first","Press COMMAND+OPTION+ESC to show all files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spotlight Search finds almost anything on macOS. Start it by clicking the magnifying glass in the menu bar or pressing COMMAND+SPACE. The Finder is macOS's equivalent of File Explorer, and Mission Control (F3) manages windows and desktops.","optionWhy":["Correct. Spotlight, opened with COMMAND+SPACE, searches the whole Mac.","Mission Control manages windows and multiple desktops. It isn't a file search.","Disk Utility verifies, repairs, and erases disks. It doesn't search for files.","COMMAND+OPTION+ESC opens Force Quit to close unresponsive apps. It doesn't search."],"optionRefs":["p. 579","p. 579","p. 595","p. 596"],"ref":"p. 579, Spotlight Search","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-36","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac user wants one desktop for teaching apps and another for personal apps, and to switch between them quickly. What should you show her?","context":"A Mac user wants one desktop for teaching apps and another for personal apps, and to switch between them quickly. What should you show her?","options":["Mission Control (F3) to create desktops; CONTROL+LEFT/RIGHT to switch","Spotlight (COMMAND+SPACE) to make a new desktop for each group of apps","Two user accounts in Users & Groups, signing in and out to switch","The Dock, pinning her teaching apps on the left and personal on the right"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mission Control manages windows and lets you set up multiple desktops with different apps and backgrounds. Activate it with F3, drag an app's window to the desktop you want at the top, and switch desktops with F3, CONTROL+LEFT/RIGHT, or a 3- or 4-finger swipe.","optionWhy":["Correct. Mission Control creates desktops, and CONTROL+arrows or swipes switch between them.","Spotlight is a search tool for files and apps. It doesn't create desktops.","Separate accounts aren't needed. Mission Control provides multiple desktops in one session.","The Dock gives quick access to apps. It doesn't create separate desktops."],"optionRefs":["p. 579","p. 579","pp. 579, 586","p. 579"],"ref":"p. 579, Mission Control and Multiple Displays","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-37","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"On a Mac, which folder holds system-wide resources and settings used by apps and macOS, such as fonts and application support files, and may be changed as apps are installed?","context":"On a Mac, which folder holds system-wide resources and settings used by apps and macOS, such as fonts and application support files, and may be changed as apps are installed?","options":["/Library","/System","/Applications","/Users"],"answer":0,"explanation":"/Applications holds apps for all users. /Library stores system-wide resources and settings such as fonts, app support files, and preferences. /System holds core OS files managed by macOS and generally not changed by users. /Users holds each user's home folder, and each home has a hidden Library for user-specific data.","optionWhy":["Correct. /Library stores system-wide resources such as fonts and app support files.","/System holds core OS files that macOS manages and users generally don't modify.","/Applications contains the installed apps themselves, not shared resources such as fonts.","/Users holds individual users' home folders and personal data."],"optionRefs":["p. 580","p. 580","p. 580","p. 580"],"ref":"p. 580, System Folders and Finder","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-38","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Windows user moving to a Mac asks which key does the job of CTRL for shortcuts such as copy and paste. What do you tell her?","context":"A Windows user moving to a Mac asks which key does the job of CTRL for shortcuts such as copy and paste. What do you tell her?","options":["COMMAND works like the CTRL key","OPTION works like the CTRL key","CONTROL works the same as CTRL","APPLE/POWER works like CTRL"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mac keyboards have COMMAND, OPTION, and CONTROL keys, plus an APPLE/POWER key. COMMAND works much like CTRL on Windows, and OPTION is often mapped to ALT. Use the Keyboard pane in System Settings to map keys when using a non-Apple keyboard.","optionWhy":["Correct. COMMAND performs the role of CTRL in Windows shortcuts.","The guide does not describe OPTION as the CTRL equivalent. COMMAND is the key used for shortcuts such as copy and paste.","Mac CONTROL is a separate key. COMMAND is the CTRL equivalent for shortcuts.","The APPLE/POWER key isn't the CTRL equivalent. COMMAND handles shortcuts such as copy and paste."],"optionRefs":["p. 583","p. 583","p. 583","p. 583"],"ref":"p. 583, Apple Keyboards","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-39","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac user needs the password of a Wi-Fi network she joined months ago so she can set up her phone. How can you find it?","context":"A Mac user needs the password of a Wi-Fi network she joined months ago so she can set up her phone. How can you find it?","options":["In Keychain Access, open the entry, tick Show password, and authenticate.","In Disk Utility, run First Aid on the disk to reveal saved passwords.","In Spotlight, search for the network name to show its password.","In Terminal, run sudo cat /etc/shadow to read the Wi-Fi password."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keychain manages passwords for internet accounts, websites, and Wi-Fi networks, and iCloud Keychain syncs them across Apple devices. In the Keychain Access app (in Utilities), search for the entry, select it, check Show password, and enter an administrator password. Keychain First Aid can repair problems.","optionWhy":["Correct. Keychain Access shows saved Wi-Fi passwords after admin authentication.","Disk Utility repairs disks. Keychain First Aid is a different tool, and neither reveals passwords that way.","Spotlight finds files and apps. Passwords are viewed in Keychain Access.","/etc/shadow holds Linux user password hashes. macOS keeps Wi-Fi passwords in Keychain."],"optionRefs":["p. 589","pp. 589, 595","pp. 579, 589","pp. 569, 589"],"ref":"p. 589, Internet Accounts and Keychain","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-40","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"You're turning on FileVault for a faculty MacBook. Where should the recovery key NOT be stored?","context":"You're turning on FileVault for a faculty MacBook. Where should the recovery key NOT be stored?","options":["On the same disk that FileVault encrypts","In the user's own iCloud account","Recorded and kept somewhere safe locally","In the institution's IT records"],"answer":0,"explanation":"FileVault encrypts disk data to protect it if the disk is removed, and each user account then needs a password. Set up a recovery method when you first encrypt: the recovery key can be stored in iCloud or recorded locally, but you should avoid saving it on the encrypted disk itself.","optionWhy":["Correct. A key stored on the encrypted disk can't be used to recover that disk.","iCloud is one of the recovery key options the guide mentions.","Recording the key locally (not on the encrypted disk) is an acceptable option.","Keeping a separate local record is acceptable. The rule is to keep it off the encrypted disk."],"optionRefs":["p. 589","p. 589","p. 589","p. 589"],"ref":"p. 589, FileVault","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-42","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A user downloads an app from a vendor's website as a .dmg file. How is it normally installed on the Mac?","context":"A user downloads an app from a vendor's website as a .dmg file. How is it normally installed on the Mac?","options":["Open it and drag the app into the Applications folder","Run it with sudo apt install from the Terminal window","Mount it in Disk Utility and format it as APFS first","Rename it to .pkg so that it runs as a full installer"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some apps aren't in the App Store and are downloaded from the vendor. A DMG is used for simple installs: copy (drag) the app from the disk image to the Applications folder. A PKG is used for installs that need extra actions, such as running services or writing files to several folders. Installed apps are .app directories in Applications.","optionWhy":["Correct. With a DMG, you install by copying (dragging) the app into the Applications folder.","apt is a Debian-based Linux package manager, not a macOS installer.","Formatting would erase the disk image. A DMG install is a drag-and-drop copy.","PKG is a separate format for complex installs. Renaming doesn't convert a file."],"optionRefs":["pp. 591–592","pp. 572, 592","pp. 592, 595","p. 592"],"ref":"pp. 591–592, Installation of Downloaded Apps","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-43","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A user wants to completely remove an app from her Mac. What should you do?","context":"A user wants to completely remove an app from her Mac. What should you do?","options":["Delete the app's .app directory using Finder","Drag the app's Dock icon off the Dock","Use Force Quit (COMMAND+OPTION+ESC)","Use Disk Utility to erase the app's file"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To uninstall an app, use Finder to delete its .app directory. The guide notes that dragging an app to the Trash is unreliable for a complete uninstall, because it often leaves associated or cached files behind.","optionWhy":["Correct. Deleting the .app directory in Finder is the approach given in the guide.","Removing a Dock icon only takes away the shortcut. The app is still installed.","Force Quit only closes an unresponsive app. It doesn't uninstall it.","Disk Utility works on whole disks and volumes, not individual apps."],"optionRefs":["p. 592","pp. 579, 592","p. 596","p. 595"],"ref":"p. 592, App Uninstallation Process","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-44","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac blocks a legitimate app downloaded from a vendor's website because it only allows apps from the App Store and identified developers. Where can an administrator change this?","context":"A Mac blocks a legitimate app downloaded from a vendor's website because it only allows apps from the App Store and identified developers. Where can an administrator change this?","options":["System Settings > Security & Privacy, after unlocking the padlock","System Settings > Users & Groups, after adding a guest account","Mission Control, after creating a new desktop for the new app","Keychain Access, after showing the app's stored password"],"answer":0,"explanation":"By default, macOS allows app installations only from the App Store and identified developers. To change this, go to System Settings > Security & Privacy, click the padlock, and enter the Administrator password. Change it only for trusted sources.","optionWhy":["Correct. The app source setting is in Security & Privacy and needs admin authentication.","Users & Groups adds and manages accounts. It doesn't control app sources.","Mission Control manages windows and desktops, not security settings.","Keychain Access manages saved passwords, not where apps may be installed from."],"optionRefs":["p. 591","pp. 586, 591","p. 579","p. 589"],"ref":"p. 591, Installation of Downloaded Apps","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-45","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"An app on a Mac shows the spinning wait cursor and has stopped responding for several minutes. How do you close it without restarting the Mac?","context":"An app on a Mac shows the spinning wait cursor and has stopped responding for several minutes. How do you close it without restarting the Mac?","options":["Press COMMAND+OPTION+ESC and Force Quit the app.","Hold COMMAND+R while powering the Mac back on.","Press COMMAND+SPACE and search for the app's name.","Press F3 and drag the app to a different desktop."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If the spinning wait cursor stays visible for a long time, the app may be unresponsive. To close and restart it without rebooting, use Force Quit from the Apple menu or press COMMAND+OPTION+ESC.","optionWhy":["Correct. Force Quit closes the unresponsive app without a reboot.","COMMAND+R at startup opens macOS Recovery, which needs a restart.","COMMAND+SPACE opens Spotlight Search, which doesn't close apps.","Mission Control moves windows between desktops. It doesn't stop a hung app."],"optionRefs":["p. 596","p. 597","p. 579","p. 579"],"ref":"p. 596, App Crashes and Force Quit","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-46","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac's system disk is corrupted, and you need to reinstall macOS or repair the disk using the built-in recovery utilities. How do you start macOS Recovery?","context":"A Mac's system disk is corrupted, and you need to reinstall macOS or repair the disk using the built-in recovery utilities. How do you start macOS Recovery?","options":["Hold COMMAND+R while powering up until the Apple logo appears","Press COMMAND+OPTION+ESC once the desktop has finished loading","Press F11 at startup to open the OEM recovery partition","Hold SHIFT and choose Restart from the Apple menu as usual"],"answer":0,"explanation":"macOS includes utilities to restore from Time Machine, reinstall macOS, or reformat and repair the system disk. Hold COMMAND+R while powering up until the Apple logo appears, then choose a language. If the startup drive is unavailable and the Mac is online, it may boot into Internet Recovery, which downloads a minimal recovery system from Apple.","optionWhy":["Correct. Holding COMMAND+R at power-up opens macOS Recovery, with options to repair or reinstall.","COMMAND+OPTION+ESC opens Force Quit for unresponsive apps. It doesn't start Recovery.","F11 is the key many Windows PC vendors use for an OEM recovery partition (see Module 16). Macs use COMMAND+R.","SHIFT+Restart opens the Windows startup options (see Module 14). Macs use COMMAND+R at power-up."],"optionRefs":["p. 597","pp. 596–597","p. 597","p. 597"],"ref":"pp. 597–598, Recovery Menu","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-47","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Mac user deleted a file yesterday and wants it back, but her Time Machine backup drive is at home. The file's tick mark on the Time Machine timeline isn't dimmed. Can she restore it now?","context":"A Mac user deleted a file yesterday and wants it back, but her Time Machine backup drive is at home. The file's tick mark on the Time Machine timeline isn't dimmed. Can she restore it now?","options":["Yes, from a local snapshot, as the tick mark isn't dimmed.","No, because Time Machine only restores from the external drive.","No, because Time Machine only keeps the backups for one hour.","Yes, but only by starting macOS Recovery with COMMAND+R first."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Time Machine backs up to an external APFS or HFS+ drive, keeping hourly backups for 24 hours, daily backups for a month, and weekly backups beyond that, deleting the oldest when full. It also keeps local snapshots on the internal drive, so some files can be restored without the backup drive. A dimmed tick mark means the backup drive must be connected.","optionWhy":["Correct. An undimmed tick means a local snapshot can restore the file without the drive.","Time Machine also keeps local snapshots on the internal drive.","It keeps hourly backups for 24 hours, daily for a month, and weekly after that.","Single files are restored from the Time Machine timeline in Finder, not through Recovery."],"optionRefs":["p. 596","p. 596","pp. 595–596","pp. 596–597"],"ref":"pp. 595–596, Time Machine Backup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-48","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A Linux admin opens Terminal on a new MacBook running a current macOS version and notices the prompt isn't bash. Which shell is macOS using by default?","context":"A Linux admin opens Terminal on a new MacBook running a current macOS version and notices the prompt isn't bash. Which shell is macOS using by default?","options":["zsh (the Z shell)","ksh (the Korn shell)","bash, renamed","PowerShell"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Terminal gives access to the macOS command-line environment. From macOS Catalina onward, the default shell is the Z shell (zsh). Older versions use bash. Common Linux shells include bash, zsh, and ksh.","optionWhy":["Correct. zsh has been the default macOS shell since Catalina.","ksh (the Korn shell) is a Linux shell option, but it isn't macOS's default.","bash was the default only in older macOS versions, before Catalina.","PowerShell is the modern Windows shell. It isn't macOS's default Terminal shell."],"optionRefs":["p. 579","pp. 561, 579","p. 579","p. 579"],"ref":"p. 579, Terminal","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-49","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A company wants to stop staff Macs from installing or removing apps on their own, and to push the company's apps to them centrally. What should IT use?","context":"A company wants to stop staff Macs from installing or removing apps on their own, and to push the company's apps to them centrally. What should IT use?","options":["Enroll the Macs in an MDM/endpoint management suite","Sign every Mac in to one shared company Apple ID","Keep only App Store installs allowed in Security & Privacy","Set the App Store to check for and install updates daily"],"answer":0,"explanation":"macOS can be enrolled in a mobile device/endpoint management suite, which allows restrictions on app installation and uninstallation. Corporate apps can be pushed via the Business Manager portal.","optionWhy":["Correct. Enrollment allows restrictions on app installation and uninstallation, and corporate apps can be pushed via the Business Manager portal.","The App Store needs an Apple ID, but a shared one doesn't restrict installs or push apps. That comes from enrolling the Macs in a management suite.","That setting limits where apps can come from (by default the App Store and identified developers), but it doesn't stop removals or push corporate apps.","Keeping apps and macOS updated is a best practice, but it doesn't restrict what staff install or remove, or deliver the company's apps."],"optionRefs":["p. 592","pp. 591–592","p. 591","p. 592"],"ref":"p. 592, Corporate Restrictions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-01","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"Over several months, staff have changed settings on many workstations, and you suspect some no longer match the hardened configuration baseline. Which tool is designed to find these systems?","context":"Over several months, staff have changed settings on many workstations, and you suspect some no longer match the hardened configuration baseline. Which tool is designed to find these systems?","options":["A vulnerability scanner","A password-cracking tool","A Wi-Fi analyzer","A content filter"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration, which reduces the system's attack surface. A non-compliant system is one that has drifted from that configuration. A vulnerability scanner is the class of software designed to detect non-compliant systems.","optionWhy":["Correct. Vulnerability scanners detect systems that have drifted from their hardened baseline.","Password crackers such as Hashcat try to recover passwords from hashes. They don't check configuration compliance.","Wi-Fi analyzers find nearby networks and congested channels. They don't audit workstation configurations.","Content filters control which internet sites hosts can reach. They don't check workstation baselines."],"optionRefs":["p. 601","pp. 608–609","pp. 621–622","p. 622"],"ref":"p. 601, Non-compliant Systems","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-02","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"Attackers are exploiting a flaw in a popular app that the vendor didn't know about, so no patch exists yet. What is this type of vulnerability called?","context":"Attackers are exploiting a flaw in a popular app that the vendor didn't know about, so no patch exists yet. What is this type of vulnerability called?","options":["A zero-day vulnerability","An end-of-life system","A non-compliant system build","An unprotected system"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A software vulnerability is a fault in design or code that can be exploited, and the malicious code that uses it is an exploit. A vulnerability that is exploited before the developer knows about it or can release a patch is a zero-day. It's an unknown threat with no protection until a patch is released.","optionWhy":["Correct. It's exploited before the vendor knows about it or can patch it.","EOL systems are no longer supported by the vendor. This app is supported but simply unpatched so far.","A non-compliant system has drifted from its hardened baseline. That isn't an unknown code flaw.","An unprotected system is missing a control such as antivirus or a firewall, not affected by an unknown flaw."],"optionRefs":["p. 601","pp. 601–602","p. 601","p. 601"],"ref":"p. 601, Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-03","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A clinic still runs a lab PC on an operating system that its vendor no longer supports with fixes. Why is this a security vulnerability?","context":"A clinic still runs a lab PC on an operating system that its vendor no longer supports with fixes. Why is this a security vulnerability?","options":["New vulnerabilities in it will never be patched by the vendor.","EOL systems can't run antivirus software or host firewalls.","It is automatically a zero-day because the vendor doesn't know.","EOL systems can't be joined to a network for security reasons."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A legacy or end-of-life (EOL) system is one where the vendor no longer provides support or fixes. An unpatched system is one its owner hasn't updated. Zero-days are relatively rare; the guide says the larger number of unpatched or legacy systems is a greater threat, and this also applies to network devices, mobile devices, and IoT.","optionWhy":["Correct. EOL systems no longer get fixes, so newly found flaws stay open.","The guide doesn't say this. The core problem is that there are no vendor fixes.","A zero-day is unknown to the vendor. EOL flaws can be well known but simply unfixed.","They can still be networked. That's exactly why the missing patches are a risk."],"optionRefs":["pp. 601–602","pp. 601–602","p. 601","pp. 601–602"],"ref":"pp. 601–602, Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-04","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A caller claims to be from IT support, says your account needs an urgent remote fix, and asks for your password. What social engineering technique is being used?","context":"A caller claims to be from IT support, says your account needs an urgent remote fix, and asks for your password. What social engineering technique is being used?","options":["Impersonation (pretexting)","Dumpster diving at the office","Shoulder surfing the user","An evil twin access point"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With impersonation, the social engineer creates a pretext to interact with an employee. A classic pretext is phoning a department pretending to be IT support, claiming something must be fixed remotely, and persuading the user to reveal their password. It works through trust, intimidation, or hoaxes. Business Email Compromise is a related attack that uses a hijacked email account.","optionWhy":["Correct. Posing as IT support with a believable story is pretexting by impersonation.","Dumpster diving means searching discarded documents or media for useful information.","Shoulder surfing means watching someone type a password or PIN.","An evil twin is a rogue wireless access point used to harvest credentials."],"optionRefs":["p. 602","p. 603","p. 603","p. 605"],"ref":"p. 602, Impersonation","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-05","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"Someone dressed as a cleaner asks an employee to hold the secure door open while they wheel in a mop bucket, and the employee lets them in. What is this called?","context":"Someone dressed as a cleaner asks an employee to hold the secure door open while they wheel in a mop bucket, and the employee lets them in. What is this called?","options":["Piggybacking","Tailgating","Shoulder surfing","Footprinting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Tailgating means entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind someone who has opened the door. Piggybacking is similar, but the attacker enters with an employee's permission, for example by posing as a cleaner or claiming to have forgotten their badge.","optionWhy":["Correct. The attacker got in with the employee's permission.","Tailgating means following someone in without their permission. Here the employee let them in.","Shoulder surfing means watching someone enter a password or PIN.","Footprinting is gathering information about a network's configuration and security."],"optionRefs":["p. 603","p. 603","p. 603","p. 606"],"ref":"p. 603, Tailgating and Piggybacking","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-06","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"The CEO receives a convincing email, apparently from the board chairman, asking her to open an attached \"merger contract.\" What type of phishing is this?","context":"The CEO receives a convincing email, apparently from the board chairman, asking her to open an attached \"merger contract.\" What type of phishing is this?","options":["Whaling","Vishing","Smishing","Quishing"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spear phishing uses information about the target to make the attack more convincing. Whaling is an attack aimed specifically at upper management, such as CEOs and other \"big catches.\" Vishing uses a voice channel, and smishing uses SMS text messages.","optionWhy":["Correct. Whaling is phishing aimed specifically at upper management, such as the CEO.","Vishing is carried out over a voice channel such as the phone or VoIP, not email.","Smishing is phishing delivered through SMS text messages, not email.","Quishing uses malicious QR codes to lead targets to fake sites."],"optionRefs":["p. 604","p. 604","p. 604","p. 605"],"ref":"p. 604, Phishing and Evil Twins","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-07","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"Posters appear in a car park telling drivers to scan a QR code to pay for parking. The code leads to a fake payment site that asks for card and login details. What attack is this?","context":"Posters appear in a car park telling drivers to scan a QR code to pay for parking. The code leads to a fake payment site that asks for card and login details. What attack is this?","options":["Quishing (QR code phishing)","Smishing (SMS phishing)","Vishing (voice phishing)","An evil twin Wi-Fi access point"],"answer":0,"explanation":"QR code phishing (quishing) uses malicious QR codes to trick targets into visiting a fake website, where they enter credentials and personal information, or into downloading malware. QR codes are essentially shortcuts to websites and downloads.","optionWhy":["Correct. Malicious QR codes leading to fake sites are quishing.","Smishing arrives by SMS text message, not a printed QR code.","Vishing is phishing over a voice channel, such as a phone or VoIP call.","An evil twin is a rogue Wi-Fi access point, not a printed QR code."],"optionRefs":["p. 605","p. 604","p. 604","p. 605"],"ref":"pp. 604–605, Phishing and Evil Twins","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-08","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"At a conference, guests see two Wi-Fi networks with nearly the same name. One lets anyone connect, then shows a login page asking for the network password. What is most likely happening?","context":"At a conference, guests see two Wi-Fi networks with nearly the same name. One lets anyone connect, then shows a login page asking for the network password. What is most likely happening?","options":["An evil twin harvesting passwords via a fake portal","Wi-Fi Enhanced Open is encrypting traffic on the open network","The venue has disabled SSID broadcast on its real network","A replay attack is reusing a stolen session cookie on the AP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An evil twin is a rogue wireless access point used to harvest credentials. It may use an SSID similar to the real one, or use DoS to overcome the legitimate AP. It might let devices connect with open authentication and then redirect browsers to a spoofed captive portal that asks for the network password. It's also an example of an on-path attack.","optionWhy":["Correct. A look-alike SSID with a spoofed login portal is an evil twin.","Enhanced Open encrypts open-network traffic. It doesn't ask for a network password on a web page.","Disabling broadcast hides an SSID. It doesn't create a second, look-alike network.","Replay attacks reuse stolen tokens such as cookies. They don't set up a fake network."],"optionRefs":["p. 605","pp. 605, 612","pp. 605, 621","pp. 605–606"],"ref":"pp. 604–605, Phishing and Evil Twins","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-09","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A technician sets up a personal Minecraft server on one of the company's computers. He means no harm, but it exposes the network to unnecessary risk. How is he classified?","context":"A technician sets up a personal Minecraft server on one of the company's computers. He means no harm, but it exposes the network to unnecessary risk. How is he classified?","options":["A non-malicious insider threat","A malicious external threat","A malicious insider threat","A supply chain attack on the firm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An insider threat actor has been granted permissions on the system, usually an employee, but possibly a contractor or partner. Insider threats can be malicious or non-malicious. The guide's example of a non-malicious insider is a technician setting up a Minecraft server on a company computer.","optionWhy":["Correct. He has authorized access and means no harm, but he still creates risk.","He's an employee with granted access, so he's internal, and he isn't acting maliciously.","Malicious insiders intend damage or theft. This technician has no harmful intent.","Supply chain attacks target vendors or suppliers to reach the company."],"optionRefs":["p. 605","p. 605","p. 605","p. 605"],"ref":"p. 605, External versus Internal Threats","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-10","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"Attackers break into a small IT services provider that manages several clinics' systems, then use that provider's access to get into the clinics' networks. What kind of attack is this?","context":"Attackers break into a small IT services provider that manages several clinics' systems, then use that provider's access to get into the clinics' networks. What kind of attack is this?","options":["A supply chain attack","A replay attack","An evil twin Wi-Fi attack","A whaling attack"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a supply chain (or pipeline) attack, the attacker doesn't target the company directly. Instead, they gain unauthorized access to weaker links in the chain, such as vendors, suppliers, or service providers, and use that access to reach the company's resources. It's carried out by an external threat actor.","optionWhy":["Correct. The attacker reached the clinics through a weaker service provider.","A replay attack reuses a stolen token, such as a cookie, to impersonate a user.","An evil twin uses a rogue Wi-Fi access point to harvest credentials.","Whaling is phishing aimed at senior executives. It isn't an attack through a service provider."],"optionRefs":["p. 605","p. 606","p. 605","p. 604"],"ref":"p. 605, External versus Internal Threats","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-11","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"An attacker steals a user's web session cookie and presents it to the web app, which accepts it and lets him in as that user. What type of attack is this?","context":"An attacker steals a user's web session cookie and presents it to the web app, which accepts it and lets him in as that user. What type of attack is this?","options":["A replay attack","A brute force attack","A denial of service","A dictionary attack"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Spoofing means masquerading as a trusted user or computer. One method is stealing a logical token, such as a web cookie, issued during authentication. If the authorization system isn't well designed, presenting the token again lets the attacker impersonate the original user. This is called a replay attack, and it's usually stealthy.","optionWhy":["Correct. Re-presenting a stolen token to impersonate the user is a replay attack.","Brute force tries every possible password combination against a hash.","DoS makes a service unavailable. It doesn't impersonate a user.","A dictionary attack matches hashes against common words to crack passwords."],"optionRefs":["p. 606","p. 608","p. 606","p. 608"],"ref":"p. 606, Spoofing Threats","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-12","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A company's website is knocked offline by traffic from thousands of compromised home routers and webcams, all controlled from a few attacker servers. What is this?","context":"A company's website is knocked offline by traffic from thousands of compromised home routers and webcams, all controlled from a few attacker servers. What is this?","options":["A DDoS attack from a botnet run by C&C servers","An on-path attack intercepting the site's traffic","A physical DoS caused by cutting a network cable","A footprinting attack scanning the site's ports"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A DoS attack makes a service unavailable to legitimate users. Bandwidth-based DoS is usually carried out as a distributed denial of service (DDoS) from a botnet. The attacker first compromises one or two machines as command & control (C&C) servers, then uses them to install bots on hundreds or thousands of devices and coordinate the attack.","optionWhy":["Correct. Many compromised devices controlled through C&C servers form a botnet carrying out DDoS.","On-path attacks covertly intercept and possibly modify traffic. They don't flood a server.","Physical DoS means cutting power or cables, not a flood of traffic from many devices.","Footprinting gathers information about the target. It doesn't take the site offline."],"optionRefs":["pp. 606–607","p. 606","p. 606","p. 606"],"ref":"pp. 606–607, Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-13","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"An attacker has stolen a file of password hashes and is running software that tries every possible character combination. Which password policy change most increases the time this attack takes?","context":"An attacker has stolen a file of password hashes and is running software that tries every possible character combination. Which password policy change most increases the time this attack takes?","options":["Require longer, more complex passwords","Store passwords in Base64 instead","Allow short passwords of 8 characters","Let users choose their pets' names"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A brute force attack tries every possible combination against a hash. Short (under eight characters), non-complex passwords can be cracked in minutes, and each extra bit of length and complexity raises the computing power needed exponentially. A dictionary attack instead tries common words and personal information, which passphrases and avoiding personal details help counter.","optionWhy":["Correct. Length and complexity raise brute force cracking time exponentially.","Base64 is just an encoding. The password can easily be recovered from it.","Short, non-complex passwords (under eight characters) can be brute-forced in minutes.","Personal words such as pet names make dictionary attacks easier."],"optionRefs":["pp. 608–609","p. 608","p. 608","p. 608"],"ref":"pp. 607–609, Password Attacks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-14","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A web form's login field accepts the input ' or 1=1-- and then lists every user in the database. Which fix most directly prevents this?","context":"A web form's login field accepts the input ' or 1=1-- and then lists every user in the database. Which fix most directly prevents this?","options":["Use parameterized queries so input is treated as data.","Make users enter a stronger password into the login field.","Turn on WPA3 encryption for the web server's Wi-Fi network.","Change the router's admin username from the default setting."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a SQL injection attack, the attacker adds code to app input so the app runs the attacker's own SQL. ' or 1=1-- makes the condition always true and comments out the rest of the statement. It's prevented by input sanitization, parameterized queries (user input is treated as data, not executable code), and stored procedures.","optionWhy":["Correct. Parameterized queries stop input being run as SQL commands.","The attack injects SQL code. Password strength doesn't stop that.","Wi-Fi encryption protects wireless traffic, not a web app's database queries.","That secures router management, not the web app's input handling."],"optionRefs":["p. 610","p. 610","pp. 610, 612","pp. 610, 619"],"ref":"p. 610, SQL Injection Attacks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-15","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A forum post contains a hidden <script> tag. Every user who views the post has the attacker's script run in their browser. What attack is this, and which defence helps?","context":"A forum post contains a hidden <script> tag. Every user who views the post has the attacker's script run in their browser. What attack is this, and which defence helps?","options":["Stored XSS; sanitize and encode input, and use a CSP","SQL injection; switch the site to WPA3-SAE Wi-Fi","Non-persistent XSS; nothing is stored on the server","A replay attack; force users to clear their cookies"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cross-site scripting (XSS) inserts a malicious script that appears to come from a trusted site, so it runs in the user's browser with the site's permissions. A stored/persistent XSS attack puts the code into the site's database, for example in a forum post. Defences include sanitizing user input, output encoding, and a Content Security Policy (CSP).","optionWhy":["Correct. A script saved in a post is stored XSS, countered by sanitizing, encoding, and CSP.","The script runs in browsers, so it's XSS, and Wi-Fi settings don't affect it.","The script is saved in a post on the server, which makes it stored, not non-persistent.","Replay attacks reuse stolen tokens. This attack is script injection."],"optionRefs":["pp. 609–610","pp. 610, 612","p. 610","pp. 606, 610"],"ref":"pp. 609–610, Cross-site Scripting Attacks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-16","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks, Threats, and Vulnerabilities","question":"A staff member enters her PIN at a door keypad that is in clear view of a parking lot. An attacker learns the PIN by watching through binoculars. What is this?","context":"A staff member enters her PIN at a door keypad that is in clear view of a parking lot. An attacker learns the PIN by watching through binoculars. What is this?","options":["Shoulder surfing","Tailgating the user","Dumpster diving","Footprinting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Shoulder surfing means learning a password, PIN, or other secure information by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker doesn't have to be close; they might use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to watch remotely.","optionWhy":["Correct. Watching someone enter a PIN, even from a distance, is shoulder surfing.","Tailgating means following someone through a door without permission.","Dumpster diving means searching discarded documents and media.","Footprinting is gathering information about network configuration and security."],"optionRefs":["p. 603","p. 603","p. 603","p. 606"],"ref":"p. 603, Shoulder Surfing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-17","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"An older access point can't use WPA3. Its security menu offers WPA2-TKIP, WPA2-TKIP+AES, and WPA2-AES. Which should you select?","context":"An older access point can't use WPA3. Its security menu offers WPA2-TKIP, WPA2-TKIP+AES, and WPA2-AES. Which should you select?","options":["WPA2-AES","WPA2-TKIP","WPA2-TKIP+AES","WEP"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2 uses AES within CCMP, which replaces WPA's RC4 and TKIP and makes replay attacks harder. TKIP has its own vulnerabilities and is deprecated. Compatibility modes such as WPA2-TKIP or WPA2-TKIP+AES support legacy clients at the cost of weaker security, so WPA2-AES is the better choice.","optionWhy":["Correct. WPA2-AES uses AES within CCMP and avoids the deprecated TKIP.","TKIP is deprecated and has its own vulnerabilities, so it weakens security.","This compatibility mode supports legacy clients but weakens security.","WEP isn't considered secure enough for continued use, and neither is the original WPA."],"optionRefs":["p. 612","p. 612","p. 612","pp. 611–612"],"ref":"p. 612, WPA2","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-18","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"Which WPA3 feature replaces the WPA2-PSK 4-way handshake, which is vulnerable to attacks that recover the key?","context":"Which WPA3 feature replaces the WPA2-PSK 4-way handshake, which is vulnerable to attacks that recover the key?","options":["Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE)","Galois Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP)","Temporal Key Integrity Protocol (TKIP)","Extensible Authentication Protocol"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2 personal authentication uses a 4-way handshake with a pre-shared key, which can be manipulated to recover the key. WPA3 replaces it with Simultaneous Authentication of Equals (SAE). WPA3 also replaces AES-CCMP with AES-GCMP, mandates protected management frames, and adds Wi-Fi Enhanced Open.","optionWhy":["Correct. In WPA3, SAE replaces the vulnerable WPA2-PSK 4-way handshake mechanism.","GCMP replaces CCMP for encrypting data, not the PSK handshake.","TKIP was added in the original WPA to patch WEP and is now deprecated.","EAP is used for 802.1X enterprise authentication, not personal-mode key agreement."],"optionRefs":["p. 612","p. 612","pp. 611–612","p. 615"],"ref":"p. 612, WPA3","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-19","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"Attackers keep forcing users off the office Wi-Fi by spoofing disconnect messages. Which WPA3 feature protects against this?","context":"Attackers keep forcing users off the office Wi-Fi by spoofing disconnect messages. Which WPA3 feature protects against this?","options":["Protected management frames","Wi-Fi Enhanced Open mode","Disabling SSID broadcast","WPA2-TKIP compatibility"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Management frames handle association, authentication, disassociation, and deauthentication as devices join and leave. Under WPA and WPA2 they can be spoofed and misused. WPA3 requires encryption of these frames (protected management frames), which protects against key recovery attacks and DoS attacks that force stations to disconnect.","optionWhy":["Correct. Encrypted management frames stop spoofed disconnect (deauthentication) messages.","Enhanced Open encrypts traffic on networks with no passphrase. It doesn't target spoofed disconnects.","Hiding the SSID doesn't secure the network, and the SSID can still be detected.","Compatibility modes weaken security and don't protect management frames."],"optionRefs":["p. 612","p. 612","p. 621","pp. 612, 621"],"ref":"p. 612, WPA3","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-20","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"A café wants an open Wi-Fi network with no password, but wants customers' traffic protected from sniffing. What should be enabled?","context":"A café wants an open Wi-Fi network with no password, but wants customers' traffic protected from sniffing. What should be enabled?","options":["WPA3 Wi-Fi Enhanced Open","WPA2 with no passphrase set","A hidden, non-broadcast SSID","WPA2-PSK with a shared key"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An open Wi-Fi network has no passphrase, so any station can join. Under WPA2, all its traffic is unencrypted. WPA3's Wi-Fi Enhanced Open encrypts that traffic, so anyone can still join, but traffic is protected against sniffing.","optionWhy":["Correct. Enhanced Open encrypts traffic on open, passwordless networks.","On WPA2, an open network sends all its traffic unencrypted, so it can be sniffed.","Hiding the SSID doesn't encrypt anything, and it can still be detected.","PSK needs everyone to enter a passphrase, so the network wouldn't be open."],"optionRefs":["p. 612","p. 612","p. 621","p. 614"],"ref":"p. 612, WPA3","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-21","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"You must use WPA2-PSK on a small office access point. How long should the passphrase be to reduce the risk of it being cracked?","context":"You must use WPA2-PSK on a small office access point. How long should the passphrase be to reduce the risk of it being cracked?","options":["At least 14 characters long","Exactly 8 characters long","64 characters or longer","Any length, as it's a hash"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2-PSK uses a passphrase of 8 to 63 characters, converted into the pairwise master key (PMK) used in the 4-way handshake. It's group authentication, since everyone shares the passphrase. All PSK types have been shown to be vulnerable to passphrase recovery attacks, so the passphrase should be at least 14 characters long.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide recommends at least 14 characters to resist cracking.","8 is the minimum WPA2-PSK allows, not a length that resists cracking.","WPA2-PSK passphrases are limited to 8 to 63 characters, so 64+ isn't possible.","Length matters, because PSK is vulnerable to passphrase recovery attacks."],"optionRefs":["p. 614","p. 614","p. 614","p. 614"],"ref":"p. 614, WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-22","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"A company wants each employee to authenticate to Wi-Fi with their own directory credentials, with the access point storing no user accounts, and with logs of who connected. What should you configure on the access point?","context":"A company wants each employee to authenticate to Wi-Fi with their own directory credentials, with the access point storing no user accounts, and with logs of who connected. What should you configure on the access point?","options":["WPA2- or WPA3-Enterprise, using 802.1X and an AAA server","WPA3-SAE with one strong shared passphrase for all staff","WPA2-PSK, with a different passphrase on each laptop","An open network with a hidden SSID and a MAC allow list"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personal modes share one passphrase, which can't be distributed securely and provides no accounting. WPA2/WPA3-Enterprise uses 802.1X with EAP: the AP opens only EAPoW traffic and passes the credentials to an AAA server, which decides whether to accept them. The AP doesn't need to store any user accounts.","optionWhy":["Correct. Enterprise mode passes per-user credentials to an AAA server for validation.","SAE still uses a shared passphrase, so it is not per-user authentication like enterprise mode.","In PSK mode the same secret must be configured on every station.","Hiding the SSID doesn't secure the network, and there's no authentication here."],"optionRefs":["p. 615","p. 614","p. 614","p. 621"],"ref":"pp. 614–615, Enterprise Authentication Protocols","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-23","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"Which EAP method requires digital certificates on both the authentication server and the wireless client, making it one of the strongest forms of multifactor Wi-Fi authentication?","context":"Which EAP method requires digital certificates on both the authentication server and the wireless client, making it one of the strongest forms of multifactor Wi-Fi authentication?","options":["EAP-TLS","WPA2-PSK","WPA3-SAE","TACACS+"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In EAP-TLS, both the server and the supplicant have a key pair and digital certificate. The client's private key is stored in a TPM or USB key and unlocked with a PIN, password, or bio gesture (first factor). The certificate handshake is the second factor. Other EAP methods use a certificate only on the server, to tunnel a username and password.","optionWhy":["Correct. EAP-TLS uses certificates on both server and client.","PSK is personal authentication with a shared passphrase, not EAP.","SAE is WPA3 personal authentication, not a certificate-based EAP method.","TACACS+ is an AAA protocol, often used for admin access to network devices, not an EAP method."],"optionRefs":["p. 615","p. 614","p. 614","p. 617"],"ref":"pp. 614–615, Enterprise Authentication Protocols","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-24","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"You're configuring an access point to send Wi-Fi authentication requests to the company's RADIUS server. What must you enter on the access point?","context":"You're configuring an access point to send Wi-Fi authentication requests to the company's RADIUS server. What must you enter on the access point?","options":["The RADIUS server's IP or hostname and a shared secret","A list of every user's username and password, stored on the AP","A WPA2-PSK passphrase of at least 14 characters for each user","The Kerberos domain controller's address, as the AP talks to it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"With RADIUS as the AAA server, the access point is a RADIUS client. It forwards data between the server and the supplicant without being able to read it. The AP must be configured with the RADIUS server's hostname or IP address and a shared secret, which lets the server and AP trust each other.","optionWhy":["Correct. The AP needs the server address and the shared secret.","In enterprise mode, a RADIUS (AAA) server handles authentication, not a shared passphrase on the access point.","That's personal mode. RADIUS is used for enterprise authentication.","The guide names RADIUS and TACACS+ as AAA protocols for network access. It does not describe Kerberos for this."],"optionRefs":["p. 616","pp. 615–616","pp. 614–616","p. 617"],"ref":"p. 616, RADIUS","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-25","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"The network team wants a centralized AAA service to authenticate administrators when they sign in to manage routers and switches. Which protocol is typically used for this?","context":"The network team wants a centralized AAA service to authenticate administrators when they sign in to manage routers and switches. Which protocol is typically used for this?","options":["TACACS+","WPA3-SAE","Wi-Fi Enhanced Open","Port triggering"],"answer":0,"explanation":"TACACS+ was developed by Cisco and is widely supported. RADIUS is often used to authenticate wireless and VPN users, while TACACS+ is often used to authenticate administrative access to routers, switches, and access points. Kerberos provides SSO on Windows domains but isn't directly supported by access points.","optionWhy":["Correct. TACACS+ is commonly used for administrative access to network devices.","SAE is WPA3 personal Wi-Fi authentication, not a centralized AAA service for device admins.","Enhanced Open encrypts open Wi-Fi traffic. It doesn't authenticate administrators.","Port triggering is a router firewall feature, not an authentication protocol."],"optionRefs":["p. 617","p. 614","p. 612","p. 624"],"ref":"p. 617, TACACS+","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-26","module":18,"lesson":"18B Wireless Security Protocols","question":"In WPA3, which encryption protocol replaces AES-CCMP?","context":"In WPA3, which encryption protocol replaces AES-CCMP?","options":["AES-GCMP","RC4-TKIP","AES-SAE","EAP-TLS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"WPA2 uses AES within CCMP, which replaced RC4 and TKIP. WPA3 replaces AES-CCMP with the stronger AES Galois Counter Mode Protocol (GCMP). AES is a symmetric block cipher that encrypts data in 128-bit blocks.","optionWhy":["Correct. WPA3 uses the stronger AES Galois Counter Mode Protocol.","RC4 with TKIP was used by the original WPA and is deprecated.","SAE is WPA3's authentication method, not its data encryption protocol.","EAP-TLS is an enterprise authentication method, not a data encryption protocol."],"optionRefs":["p. 612","pp. 611–612","p. 612","p. 615"],"ref":"p. 612, Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-27","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"You connect a laptop to a new SOHO router's LAN port, but the router's management page at http://192.168.0.1 won't load. What should you check first?","context":"You connect a laptop to a new SOHO router's LAN port, but the router's management page at http://192.168.0.1 won't load. What should you check first?","options":["That the laptop got an IP address in the router's range","That the laptop is plugged into the router's WAN port","That WPA3 has been set up before trying to log in","That UPnP is on so the router can set up the laptop"],"answer":0,"explanation":"To set up a new router, connect it to the ISP through the WAN port, power it on, and connect a computer to an RJ45 LAN port, set to obtain an IP address automatically. Wait for the router's DHCP server to assign an address, then browse to the management URL. If you can't connect, check that the computer's IP is in the same range as the router's, and configure it manually if needed.","optionWhy":["Correct. The laptop must be in the same IP range as the router to reach it.","The WAN port connects to the ISP. Setup is done from a LAN port.","Wireless security is configured after you can reach the management page.","UPnP lets client devices open firewall ports. It isn't needed for router setup."],"optionRefs":["p. 619","p. 619","pp. 619, 621","p. 625"],"ref":"p. 619, Home Router Setup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-28","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"You've just logged in to a new SOHO router's management interface. Which actions best secure access to it?","context":"You've just logged in to a new SOHO router's management interface. Which actions best secure access to it?","options":["Use HTTPS, set a 12+ character password, change the username","Keep the default password printed on the sticker so it can't be lost","Allow management over HTTP from the WAN so it can be reached anywhere","Hide the SSID, since that also protects the management interface"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Anyone who gets into the management interface can change every setting and lock users out. Use HTTPS instead of unencrypted HTTP, and use secure protocols (such as SSH) for any remote access. Change the default password to a strong one of 12 or more characters, and change the default admin username if possible.","optionWhy":["Correct. These steps protect the router's management access.","Default passwords are documented and widely known, so they must be changed.","HTTP is unencrypted. Any remote access should use a secure protocol.","Hiding the SSID doesn't secure the network or the management interface."],"optionRefs":["p. 619","p. 619","p. 619","p. 621"],"ref":"p. 619, Home Router Setup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-29","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"You're updating a SOHO router's firmware manually. Which practice matters most to avoid problems?","context":"You're updating a SOHO router's firmware manually. Which practice matters most to avoid problems?","options":["Get the right model's file from the official site; keep power on","Use firmware from any download site, as long as the file name matches","Restart the router partway through so the new firmware takes effect","Skip firmware updates, since they only add features and no security"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Keep the router's firmware up to date to fix security holes and support newer standards such as WPA3. Download the update only from the vendor's official website, choosing the right file for your make and model. Install it with the Firmware Upgrade option, and don't interrupt power to the device during the update.","optionWhy":["Correct. Use the correct official firmware, and never interrupt power mid-update.","Firmware should come only from the vendor's official website.","Power must not be interrupted during the update, so restarting mid-way is risky.","Firmware updates fix security holes and add support for standards like WPA3."],"optionRefs":["p. 620","p. 620","p. 620","p. 620"],"ref":"p. 620, Firmware Update","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-30","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A small accountancy firm wants to name its Wi-Fi network \"Accounts\". What advice should you give?","context":"A small accountancy firm wants to name its Wi-Fi network \"Accounts\". What advice should you give?","options":["Avoid it; a meaningful name like that invites evil twin attacks.","Use it, and also hide the SSID so that the network is secure.","Keep the router's default SSID, as it is harder to recognize.","Use the office's street address so that clients can find it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The SSID is a case-sensitive network name. Change the default to something users will recognize, but don't reveal personal information. On a business network, avoid meaningful names: an SSID such as \"Accounts\" could be a tempting target for an evil twin attack. Wi-Fi analyzers help avoid SSID conflicts.","optionWhy":["Correct. Revealing names like \"Accounts\" make the network a tempting target.","Hiding the SSID doesn't secure the network, and it can still be detected.","The guide says to change the default SSID, which is typically based on the brand or model.","On residential networks, avoid personal details such as an address."],"optionRefs":["pp. 620–621","p. 621","p. 620","pp. 620–621"],"ref":"pp. 620–621, Service Set ID","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-31","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"All the office's laptops support WPA3, but one old barcode scanner only supports WPA2. What should you be aware of if you enable WPA3/WPA2 compatibility on the router?","context":"All the office's laptops support WPA3, but one old barcode scanner only supports WPA2. What should you be aware of if you enable WPA3/WPA2 compatibility on the router?","options":["It weakens security, since attackers can request the lower WPA2 mode.","It has no effect on security, since all traffic still uses WPA3.","It disables encryption for all of the devices on the network.","It requires every device to switch to WPA2-TKIP for its traffic."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Set the highest security standard that your client devices support, ideally WPA3. Compatibility support for WPA2 (AES/CCMP) or even WPA2 (TKIP) can be enabled if needed, but it weakens security, because malicious stations can request a downgraded security type.","optionWhy":["Correct. Compatibility allows downgrade requests from malicious stations.","Legacy WPA2 clients can still connect, but transition mode weakens security by supporting them.","Compatibility mode still encrypts traffic, just with weaker options available.","WPA3 devices can still use WPA3. Compatibility only allows legacy devices too."],"optionRefs":["p. 621","p. 621","p. 621","p. 621"],"ref":"p. 621, Encryption Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-32","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Users in a busy office building complain about slow Wi-Fi. The access point's channel is on auto, and there are many neighbouring networks. What should you do?","context":"Users in a busy office building complain about slow Wi-Fi. The access point's channel is on auto, and there are many neighbouring networks. What should you do?","options":["Use a Wi-Fi analyzer to find a less congested channel and set it.","Disable SSID broadcast so neighbouring networks stop interfering.","Turn on UPnP so the access point picks the best channel each time.","Enable WPA2-TKIP compatibility to support more devices at once."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each band (2.4, 5, and 6 GHz) can auto-select or be set to a specific channel. On auto, the AP picks the channel that seems least congested, but as the environment changes it may no longer be the best. Use a Wi-Fi analyzer to find the least congested channel and set it manually.","optionWhy":["Correct. An analyzer shows congestion so you can choose a better channel.","Hiding the SSID doesn't reduce channel congestion from other networks.","UPnP lets devices configure firewall ports. It doesn't select Wi-Fi channels.","Compatibility modes weaken security and don't fix congestion."],"optionRefs":["p. 622","pp. 621–622","p. 625","pp. 621–622"],"ref":"p. 622, Changing Channels","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-33","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"Parents want their home router to block gambling and adult websites on the children's devices. Which router feature does this?","context":"Parents want their home router to block gambling and adult websites on the children's devices. Which router feature does this?","options":["Outbound content filtering","Inbound port forwarding rules","A DMZ host configuration","Disabling guest access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inbound filtering decides whether remote hosts can connect to internal ports; on a home router these are blocked by default, with exceptions made by port forwarding. Outbound filtering controls which internet sites internal hosts can reach; outbound is allowed by default and restricted with a content filter. Content filters use reputation databases and keyword lists, and some can also limit access times.","optionWhy":["Correct. Content filters restrict outbound access to categories of sites.","Port forwarding lets internet hosts reach internal computers. It's the opposite direction.","A home-router DMZ host is exposed to the internet, which reduces protection.","Disabling the guest network doesn't block website categories."],"optionRefs":["p. 622","pp. 622–624","p. 626","p. 622"],"ref":"pp. 622–623, Home Router Firewall Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-34","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A user wants friends on the internet to join a game server running on his home PC, on TCP port 25565. What two things should you configure on the router?","context":"A user wants friends on the internet to join a game server running on his home PC, on TCP port 25565. What two things should you configure on the router?","options":["A DHCP reservation for the PC, and a port forwarding rule for 25565 to it","A content filter rule that allows the game's website for outbound traffic","Disable SSID broadcast and enable WPA3 so friends can connect to it","Turn the router's firewall off completely so all ports are open to it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Internet hosts can only see the router's public WAN address, and the default firewall rule blocks inbound connections. A port forwarding rule sends requests for a service to a specific LAN host, identified by IP address. To keep that IP from changing, create a DHCP reservation using the PC's MAC address.","optionWhy":["Correct. The reservation fixes the PC's IP, and the forwarding rule sends the traffic there.","Content filtering controls outbound access. Friends need inbound access to the server.","Wi-Fi settings affect local wireless clients, not inbound internet connections.","Disabling the firewall exposes everything. A targeted forwarding rule is enough."],"optionRefs":["pp. 623–624","p. 622","pp. 621, 624","pp. 624–625"],"ref":"pp. 623–624, Home Router Port Forwarding Configuration","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-35","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"An application opens an outbound connection on one port, and then needs the remote server to connect back in on a different port for a short time. Which router feature handles this?","context":"An application opens an outbound connection on one port, and then needs the remote server to connect back in on a different port for a short time. Which router feature handles this?","options":["Port triggering","Port mapping","Content filtering","Guest isolation"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Port triggering is used for applications that need more than one port, such as FTP. When the firewall sees activity on outbound port A to a given external IP, it opens inbound access from that IP on port B for a set period. Port forwarding (port mapping) permanently sends a given inbound port to a LAN host, possibly on a different port.","optionWhy":["Correct. Outbound activity on one port temporarily opens an inbound port.","Port mapping permanently forwards an inbound port to a host, possibly on another port. It isn't triggered by outbound traffic.","Content filtering restricts which sites internal hosts can reach.","Guest networks are isolated from local devices. That doesn't open ports."],"optionRefs":["p. 624","p. 624","p. 622","p. 622"],"ref":"p. 624, Configuring Port-Forwarding and Port-Triggering Rules","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-36","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A home router has UPnP enabled, which a game console uses to open ports automatically. What is the recommended security approach?","context":"A home router has UPnP enabled, which a game console uses to open ports automatically. What is the recommended security approach?","options":["Disable it if not needed; never allow UPnP requests from the WAN side.","Leave it on for all devices, since it has no known security problems.","Enable it on printers and webcams too, so they open the ports they need.","Turn the firewall off instead of using UPnP, as that's simpler to manage."],"answer":0,"explanation":"UPnP lets devices such as consoles and VoIP handsets configure the firewall automatically. Because it's associated with many vulnerabilities, it's best disabled if not needed. If you do use it, make sure the router doesn't accept UPnP requests from the external (internet) interface, keep firmware updated, and disable UPnP on client devices unless their implementation is known to be secure.","optionWhy":["Correct. UPnP has many vulnerabilities and must never accept requests from the internet.","The guide says UPnP is associated with many security vulnerabilities.","UPnP vulnerabilities have been found on printers and webcams. Disable it on clients unless secure.","Turning the firewall off is the insecure behaviour UPnP was meant to discourage."],"optionRefs":["pp. 625–626","p. 625","p. 626","p. 625"],"ref":"pp. 625–626, Universal Plug-and-Play","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-37","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A user sets the home router's \"DMZ host\" option to point to his gaming PC so his game always works. What is the risk?","context":"A user sets the home router's \"DMZ host\" option to point to his gaming PC so his game always works. What is the risk?","options":["The PC is exposed to the internet on all unforwarded ports.","The PC moves to a separate, fully firewalled screened subnet.","The PC's outbound web access is restricted by content filters.","The PC can no longer reach the internet through the router."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On many home routers, a \"DMZ host\" is a LAN computer that receives traffic on every port not forwarded to another host. It's effectively unprotected by the firewall and fully reachable from the internet, so it should only be used with robust host security. A true screened subnet is a separate segment with its own firewall rules, used on enterprise networks.","optionWhy":["Correct. A home-router DMZ host isn't protected by the router's firewall.","That describes an enterprise screened subnet. Home-router DMZ means unprotected.","DMZ host settings affect inbound exposure, not outbound content filtering.","The PC can still reach the internet. It's just also reachable from it."],"optionRefs":["p. 626","p. 626","pp. 622, 626","p. 626"],"ref":"p. 626, Screened Subnets","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-38","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"During a monthly review of a SOHO router, you find a port forwarding rule for a remote-access tool that the office stopped using last year. What should you do?","context":"During a monthly review of a SOHO router, you find a port forwarding rule for a remote-access tool that the office stopped using last year. What should you do?","options":["Disable or delete the rule, and record the change","Leave it in place in case the tool is used again","Change it to a port triggering rule for the tool","Enable UPnP so the tool reopens the port if needed"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A basic principle of hardened configuration is to enable only the services that are needed. The home router blocks inbound connections by default, and port forwarding rules are exceptions. A rule that's no longer needed should be disabled or deleted, and the configuration reviewed every month, because forgotten rules cause some of the worst vulnerabilities.","optionWhy":["Correct. Unneeded exceptions should be removed, and changes documented.","Leaving unused rules in place is the kind of oversight that causes vulnerabilities.","Converting it still leaves an unneeded exception for an unused service.","UPnP adds its own security risks and doesn't address an unused port forwarding rule."],"optionRefs":["pp. 624–625","p. 625","pp. 624–625","p. 625"],"ref":"p. 625, Disabling Unused Ports","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-39","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"Staff keep holding the server room door open for each other, and unauthorized people sometimes slip in behind them. Which physical control restricts entry to one person at a time?","context":"Staff keep holding the server room door open for each other, and unauthorized people sometimes slip in behind them. Which physical control restricts entry to one person at a time?","options":["An access control vestibule","Bollards at the entrance","A Kensington cable lock","A circuit-based alarm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A simple door can't accurately record who enters: several people may pass at once, someone may hold it open, or a visitor may tailgate. A turnstile or an access control vestibule reduces this risk. A vestibule is a gateway leading to an enclosed space protected by another barrier, restricting access to one person at a time.","optionWhy":["Correct. A vestibule restricts access to one person at a time.","Bollards stop vehicles getting close to a building. They don't control people at doors.","Kensington locks secure laptops to desks against theft. They don't control door access.","Circuit alarms sound when a door or window is opened. They don't stop tailgating."],"optionRefs":["p. 629","p. 628","p. 630","p. 630"],"ref":"p. 629, Access Control Vestibules","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-40","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A company's magnetic swipe badges are being cloned. Which badge-reader technology is much harder to clone?","context":"A company's magnetic swipe badges are being cloned. Which badge-reader technology is much harder to clone?","options":["Cryptographic contactless smart cards or key fobs","A new set of magnetic swipe cards with longer codes","Electronic keypads where staff enter a shared PIN","Conventional key-operated locks on all of the doors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Badge reader locks work with a hardware token rather than a PIN. The token might be a basic magnetic swipe card. More advanced locks use a cryptographic contactless smart card or key fob, which is much more difficult to clone than an ordinary swipe card.","optionWhy":["Correct. Cryptographic smart cards are much harder to clone than swipe cards.","Magnetic swipe cards remain the easier-to-clone option, whatever code they store.","Keypad PINs can be observed or shared, and they don't solve token cloning.","Physical keys don't address cloned badges and give no electronic control."],"optionRefs":["p. 629","p. 629","pp. 603, 629","p. 629"],"ref":"p. 629, Door Lock Types","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-41","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A data center needs one of the most accurate biometric locks available for a high-security vault, and cost and intrusiveness are acceptable. Which type fits best?","context":"A data center needs one of the most accurate biometric locks available for a high-security vault, and cost and intrusiveness are acceptable. Which type fits best?","options":["A retina scanner","A voice recognition lock","A fingerprint reader","A mobile digital key"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Retina scanners shine infrared light into the eye to identify the pattern of blood vessels, which usually doesn't change from birth to death. This makes retinal scanning one of the most accurate biometrics, but the equipment is expensive and the process is relatively intrusive and complex. Fingerprint readers are simpler but can be affected by moisture or dirt.","optionWhy":["Correct. Retinal scanning is one of the most accurate biometrics, though expensive and intrusive.","Voice recognition can be affected by background noise, illness, and mimicry.","Fingerprint readers are simple, but moisture or dirt can prevent readings.","A mobile digital key is a virtual key on a smartphone, not a biometric."],"optionRefs":["p. 630","p. 630","pp. 629–630","p. 629"],"ref":"pp. 629–630, Biometric Door Locks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-42","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"Laptops in an open-plan office keep going missing. Which equipment lock secures a laptop to a desk?","context":"Laptops in an open-plan office keep going missing. Which equipment lock secures a laptop to a desk?","options":["A Kensington cable lock","A chassis lock and faceplate","A lockable rack cabinet","A duress alarm pendant"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Kensington locks use a cable tie to secure a laptop or other device to a desk or pillar to prevent theft. Chassis locks and faceplates stop server covers being opened, and lockable rack cabinets control access to network equipment in racks.","optionWhy":["Correct. A Kensington lock uses a cable tie to secure a laptop to a desk or pillar.","Chassis locks prevent server covers being opened. They don't tether laptops.","Rack cabinets secure servers, switches, and routers in racks.","Duress alarms are triggered manually by staff under threat. They don't secure laptops."],"optionRefs":["p. 630","p. 630","p. 630","p. 631"],"ref":"p. 630, Equipment Locks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-43","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A lab wants an alarm to go off if someone tries to carry tagged equipment out of the area. Which alarm type uses RFID tags and readers for this?","context":"A lab wants an alarm to go off if someone tries to carry tagged equipment out of the area. Which alarm type uses RFID tags and readers for this?","options":["A proximity alarm","A circuit alarm","A motion sensor alarm","A duress alarm"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Circuit alarms sound when a circuit opens or closes, for example when a door opens. Motion sensors detect movement using radar or passive infrared. Proximity alarms use RFID tags and readers to track tagged objects and detect attempts to remove equipment. Duress alarms are triggered manually by staff under threat.","optionWhy":["Correct. Proximity alarms use RFID tags to detect equipment being removed.","Circuit alarms trigger when a door, window, or fence circuit is broken.","Motion sensors detect movement with radar or passive infrared, not tagged items.","Duress alarms are triggered manually by staff who are under threat."],"optionRefs":["p. 631","p. 630","p. 630","p. 631"],"ref":"pp. 630–631, Alarms and Surveillance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-44","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A night guard wants a way to open the secure door if forced to by an intruder, while silently alerting security. What can the electronic lock be programmed with?","context":"A night guard wants a way to open the secure door if forced to by an intruder, while silently alerting security. What can the electronic lock be programmed with?","options":["A separate duress code that opens the door and alerts security","A circuit alarm that sounds whenever the door is opened at all","A mobile digital key that he opens from his phone's app","A palmprint scanner that needs a deliberate hand gesture"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A duress alarm is triggered manually by staff who come under threat, for example with a wireless pendant or a concealed trigger. Some electronic entry locks can also be programmed with a duress code that differs from the normal access code. It opens the gateway but also alerts security that the lock was operated under threat.","optionWhy":["Correct. A duress code opens the door while silently raising an alarm.","A circuit alarm would sound loudly for every opening, not silently alert under threat.","A mobile key opens the door normally, without signalling duress.","A palmprint scanner authenticates the user but doesn't signal duress."],"optionRefs":["p. 631","pp. 630–631","p. 629","p. 630"],"ref":"pp. 630–631, Alarms and Surveillance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-45","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A government building at risk of vehicle attacks needs to stop cars and trucks from getting close to its entrance. What should be installed?","context":"A government building at risk of vehicle attacks needs to stop cars and trucks from getting close to its entrance. What should be installed?","options":["Bollards and other barricades","An access control vestibule","A walk-through magnetometer gate","Proximity RFID alarm tags"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Perimeter security uses barricades, fences, lighting, and surveillance to control who can approach a site. Sites at risk of terrorist attack use barricades such as bollards (short vertical posts) and security posts to stop vehicles crashing into the building or exploding a bomb near it.","optionWhy":["Correct. Bollards prevent vehicles getting close to the building.","A vestibule controls people entering one at a time. It doesn't stop vehicles.","Magnetometers detect concealed metal items on people. They don't stop vehicles.","RFID proximity alarms track tagged equipment, not approaching vehicles."],"optionRefs":["p. 628","p. 629","p. 629","p. 631"],"ref":"p. 628, Perimeter Security","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-46","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A data center wants staff alerted whenever anyone approaches its perimeter fence at night. Which control, often using motion detection, fits best?","context":"A data center wants staff alerted whenever anyone approaches its perimeter fence at night. Which control, often using motion detection, fits best?","options":["Video surveillance using CCTV or IP cameras","Security lighting all along the fence line","Bollards placed along the perimeter fence","A magnetometer gate at the main entrance"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Video surveillance is typically a second layer of security that improves the resilience of perimeter gateways. It can use older CCTV or IP cameras, and may use motion detection or even facial recognition to alert staff to intrusion attempts.","optionWhy":["Correct. Surveillance can watch perimeter areas, and motion detection or facial recognition can alert staff to intrusion attempts.","Security lighting helps a site feel safe and secure at night, but on its own it doesn't alert staff to intrusion attempts.","Bollards stop vehicles getting close to a building or sensitive area. They don't detect people or alert anyone.","A walk-through magnetometer detects concealed metal items at an entrance. It doesn't watch the perimeter or alert staff."],"optionRefs":["p. 631","p. 631","p. 628","p. 629"],"ref":"p. 631, Video Surveillance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-47","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"A research site wants checkpoints where someone verifies identification, decides on the spot whether to admit each visitor, and logs every entry. What provides all of this?","context":"A research site wants checkpoints where someone verifies identification, decides on the spot whether to admit each visitor, and logs every entry. What provides all of this?","options":["Human security guards at each checkpoint","Key-operated locks on each checkpoint door","A walk-through magnetometer at each one","Video surveillance covering each checkpoint"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Human security guards can monitor critical checkpoints, verify identification, allow or disallow access, and log physical entry occurrences. They also act as a visual deterrent and can apply their own knowledge and intuition to potential breaches.","optionWhy":["Correct. Guards can verify identification, allow or disallow access, log physical entries, and use their own judgment and intuition.","A door lock controls entry without a guard, but it can't check identification, judge each visitor, or log who came in.","A magnetometer detects concealed weapons or other metal items. It doesn't verify identification or log entries.","Cameras can monitor and alert staff, but they can't verify identification or allow and disallow access in person."],"optionRefs":["p. 629","p. 629","p. 629","p. 631"],"ref":"p. 629, Security Guards","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-48","module":18,"lesson":"18D Additional Security Measures","question":"Staff who often carry equipment with both hands want a door lock that opens when they speak a command. What weakness of this lock type should you plan for?","context":"Staff who often carry equipment with both hands want a door lock that opens when they speak a command. What weakness of this lock type should you plan for?","options":["Background noise, a cold, or mimicry can affect it","It must be retrained each time the door is opened","It can't be used by people with limited mobility","It needs a fingerprint as well, as a second factor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Voice recognition unlocks when the spoken command matches the user's voiceprint. It suits users with limited mobility or full hands, but background noise, voice changes such as a cold, and mimicry can affect it, so many systems also require a spoken passcode.","optionWhy":["Correct. Voice recognition can be affected by background noise, voice changes such as a cold, and malicious users mimicking a valid voice.","The command is recorded and analyzed once to create a voiceprint. The lock then compares each spoken command against it.","The opposite: voice recognition works well for users with limited mobility or when their hands are full.","To add security, many voice systems also require the user to speak a passcode with the command, not to scan a fingerprint."],"optionRefs":["p. 630","p. 630","p. 630","p. 630"],"ref":"p. 630, Voice Recognition","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-49","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"In a small office, the router sits on the reception desk where visitors wait. Why is that placement a security concern?","context":"In a small office, the router sits on the reception desk where visitors wait. Why is that placement a security concern?","options":["Visitors could tamper with it, plug in, or reset it","Its Wi-Fi signal would be too strong at reception","A router must always be locked inside a cabinet","Visitors could read the SSID that it broadcasts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Place routers and other network appliances in a secure location. Physical access lets a threat actor tamper with the device, attach unauthorized devices to its network or USB ports, or use the factory reset and log on with the default password. Accidental damage or power-off is also a risk.","optionWhy":["Correct. Physical access lets someone tamper with it, attach unauthorized devices to network or USB ports, or factory reset it and log on with the default password.","Signal strength isn't the concern. A home router can't simply be locked away, because clients would suffer reduced signal strength.","The guide's concern is physical access: someone at the router could tamper with it, attach devices, or reset it. Enterprises keep such appliances in a locked equipment room.","An SSID is broadcast over the air wherever the router sits. The placement risk is physical access to the device itself."],"optionRefs":["pp. 618–619","p. 619","p. 619","pp. 618, 621"],"ref":"pp. 618–619, Physical Placement/Secure Locations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-01","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"A user asks you for advice on choosing a new password for her ordinary work account. Which choice follows good practice?","context":"A user asks you for advice on choosing a new password for her ordinary work account. Which choice follows good practice?","options":["A memorable 12+ character phrase with no personal details","Her dog's name followed by her birth year, e.g. Rex1990","The same strong password she uses for her bank account","A favorite song lyric, since it is long and easy to recall"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Make passwords long enough: 12+ characters suits an ordinary user account, and admin accounts need longer ones. Choose a memorable phrase, but avoid anything a threat actor could discover or guess, such as dates, family or pet names, usernames, job titles, company names, quotations, or song lyrics. Use a unique password for every account.","optionWhy":["Correct. Long and memorable, without guessable personal details.","Pet names and significant dates are guessable and should never be used.","Every account should have a unique password, so one breach doesn't expose the others.","The guide specifically warns against quotations and song lyrics."],"optionRefs":["p. 635","p. 635","p. 635","p. 635"],"ref":"p. 635, Password Rules","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-02","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Staff often leave their PCs unlocked when they go to lunch. What should they be trained to do, and which threat does this prevent?","context":"Staff often leave their PCs unlocked when they go to lunch. What should they be trained to do, and which threat does this prevent?","options":["Press START+L when leaving, to prevent a lunchtime attack","Rely on the screensaver lock, as it prevents shoulder surfing","Log off with CTRL+SHIFT+ESC to stop a replay attack","Turn off the monitor, which locks the desktop as well"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A lunchtime attack is when a threat actor uses a computer that has been left unlocked. Policies can set screensavers to lock the desktop after inactivity, but users shouldn't rely on them. In Windows, START+L locks the desktop, and users should make a habit of pressing it every time they leave the computer.","optionWhy":["Correct. Locking manually stops anyone using an unattended, unlocked PC.","Users shouldn't depend on the screensaver lock, and the threat here is a lunchtime attack.","CTRL+SHIFT+ESC opens Task Manager, which is not a way to lock the screen.","Turning off the display doesn't lock the desktop. The guide says START+L locks the desktop in Windows, and users must lock manually each time they leave a computer unattended."],"optionRefs":["p. 636","p. 636","p. 636","p. 636"],"ref":"p. 636, End User Best Practices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-03","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"During an office walkthrough, you see printed customer records and a sticky note with a password on several desks. Which policy addresses this?","context":"During an office walkthrough, you see printed customer records and a sticky note with a password on several desks. Which policy addresses this?","options":["A clean desk policy","A screen lock timeout","A login time restriction","A concurrent login limit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Paper copies of personal and confidential data must not be left where they could be read or stolen. A clean desk policy makes sure this information isn't left in plain sight. PII and passwords also shouldn't be stored in unprotected files such as plain text documents or spreadsheets; password managers are a safer way to store passwords.","optionWhy":["Correct. A clean desk policy keeps PII and passwords out of plain sight.","A screen lock protects the computer session, not papers left on the desk.","Login time restrictions limit when accounts can sign in, not what's left on desks.","This limits simultaneous sessions for an account. It doesn't address visible papers."],"optionRefs":["p. 636","pp. 636–637","p. 637","p. 637"],"ref":"p. 636, End User Best Practices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-04","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"A network appliance has a built-in administrator account that can't be disabled. How should you handle it?","context":"A network appliance has a built-in administrator account that can't be disabled. How should you handle it?","options":["Change the default password, limit who knows it, and log every use.","Keep the default password so all technicians can use it in emergencies.","Use it for all day-to-day admin tasks so that one account does all the work.","Let the person using it turn off logging to reduce the noise in the logs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Default accounts such as Windows Administrator or Linux root have no practical limits and are the ultimate target for attackers. If one can't be disabled, it must never keep its default password; the new password needs the highest security and should be known by as few people as possible. Any use must be logged, generate an alert, and the person using it must not be able to disable that logging.","optionWhy":["Correct. Secure the password, restrict knowledge of it, and account for every use.","Default passwords must never be left in place, and sharing admin passwords is a risk.","Using the default account should be an unusual event. Named admin accounts should be used instead.","For separation of duties, its operator must not be able to disable accounting."],"optionRefs":["p. 637","p. 637","p. 637","p. 637"],"ref":"p. 637, Change Default Administrator Account and Password","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-05","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"A contractor will work with the company for exactly three months. Which account setting ensures the account can't be used after the contract ends?","context":"A contractor will work with the company for exactly three months. Which account setting ensures the account can't be used after the contract ends?","options":["Account expiration","Restrict login times","Failed attempts lockout","Concurrent logins limit"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Account policies enforce controls through the OS. Account expiration sets a date and time after which the account is automatically disabled. Login time restrictions limit the hours an account can sign in, failed-attempt lockout disables an account after too many wrong passwords, and concurrent login limits restrict simultaneous sessions.","optionWhy":["Correct. Expiration automatically disables the account after the set date.","This limits the hours of the day or days of the week an account can sign in, not an end date.","Lockout disables an account after too many incorrect password attempts.","This limits simultaneous sessions. It doesn't end access on a date."],"optionRefs":["p. 637","p. 637","p. 637","p. 637"],"ref":"p. 637, Account Policies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-06","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Office staff only work weekdays from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Security wants to stop their accounts being used to sign in at night or at weekends. Which policy should you configure?","context":"Office staff only work weekdays from 8 a.m. to 6 p.m. Security wants to stop their accounts being used to sign in at night or at weekends. Which policy should you configure?","options":["Restrict login times","Account expiration","Use timeout/screen lock","Disable the Guest account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Restricting login times stops an account signing in at unusual times of day or night, or at weekends. The server periodically checks whether the user still has the right to use the network and, if not, starts an automatic logout.","optionWhy":["Correct. Login time restrictions block sign-ins outside the allowed hours.","Expiration disables an account so it can't be used beyond the allowed timeframe. It doesn't set daily hours.","A screen lock locks an idle desktop. It doesn't prevent sign-ins at night.","The Guest account is a separate account. Disabling it doesn't limit staff hours."],"optionRefs":["p. 637","p. 637","p. 637","p. 637"],"ref":"p. 637, Account Policies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-07","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"Logs show attackers trying hundreds of passwords against staff accounts. Which account policy directly reduces this risk?","context":"Logs show attackers trying hundreds of passwords against staff accounts. Which account policy directly reduces this risk?","options":["Failed attempts lockout","Concurrent logins limit","Restrict login times","Account expiration"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Failed attempts lockout sets a maximum number of incorrect sign-in attempts within a period. Once it's reached, the account is disabled, which reduces the risk of attackers getting in by trying lists of possible passwords. A locked account can then be unlocked on the Account tab of its Properties dialog.","optionWhy":["Correct. Lockout stops attackers working through lists of passwords.","This limits how many sessions an account can have open at once, not password guessing.","Attackers could still guess passwords during the allowed hours.","Expiration ends an account on a date. It doesn't stop guessing before then."],"optionRefs":["p. 637","p. 637","p. 637","p. 637"],"ref":"p. 637, Account Policies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-08","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"A user mistyped his password several times and is now locked out, although he remembers the correct password. In Local Users and Groups, what should you do?","context":"A user mistyped his password several times and is now locked out, although he remembers the correct password. In Local Users and Groups, what should you do?","options":["Clear the lockout on the Account tab of the account's Properties","Right-click the account and choose Reset Password to a new one","Delete the account and recreate it with the same user name","Disable the failed attempts lockout policy for every account"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When an account breaks a security policy, such as repeated wrong passwords, it can be locked. It stays inaccessible until it's unlocked with the option on the Account tab of the account's Properties dialog. If a user has actually forgotten the password, right-click the account and select Reset Password instead.","optionWhy":["Correct. Unlocking on the Account tab restores access without changing the password.","Reset Password is for forgotten passwords. He still knows his password.","Recreating an account is drastic and unnecessary. It only needs unlocking.","Removing lockout for everyone weakens security instead of fixing one account."],"optionRefs":["p. 638","p. 639","p. 638","pp. 637–638"],"ref":"pp. 637–639, Account Policies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-09","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"A workstation never uses Bluetooth, NFC, or file transfer services such as FTP. What should you do with these services as part of hardening it?","context":"A workstation never uses Bluetooth, NFC, or file transfer services such as FTP. What should you do with these services as part of hardening it?","options":["Disable them until they're actually needed","Leave them all running in case they're needed","Set them to run only for the Guest account","Enable auto updates on them, then leave them on"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some services are essential, but others may not need to run. If you aren't connecting to Bluetooth or NFC devices, disable those services until you need them; likewise disable file transfer services such as FTP or SSH when they aren't in use. Only enable services when they're required.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disabling unused services reduces ways into the system.","Unneeded services widen the attack surface, so they should be disabled until required.","The Guest account should be disabled, and this doesn't remove the exposure.","Updating unused services still leaves them as unnecessary attack paths."],"optionRefs":["p. 639","p. 639","pp. 637, 639","p. 639"],"ref":"p. 639, Unused Services","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-10","module":19,"lesson":"19A Account Security","question":"On a standalone Windows 11 Pro workstation (not in a domain), you need to set a minimum password length and a lockout threshold. Which tool do you use?","context":"On a standalone Windows 11 Pro workstation (not in a domain), you need to set a minimum password length and a lockout threshold. Which tool do you use?","options":["Local Security Policy (secpol.msc)","The Windows Settings Personalization page","Device Manager's account properties tab","Windows Defender Firewall's Advanced pane"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On a standalone workstation, password and account policies are set with the Local Security Policy snap-in (secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc). On a domain, they're defined as GPOs applied to domains and OUs. These tools aren't available in the Home edition.","optionWhy":["Correct. secpol.msc configures local password and account policies.","Personalization controls appearance, such as themes and colors, not password policy.","Device Manager manages hardware devices, not account policies.","The firewall filters network traffic. It doesn't set password rules."],"optionRefs":["p. 637","p. 637","p. 637","pp. 637, 646"],"ref":"p. 637, Account Policies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-11","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"A user signed in with a standard account needs to run a diagnostic utility that requires administrative privileges. How can she do this without signing out?","context":"A user signed in with a standard account needs to run a diagnostic utility that requires administrative privileges. How can she do this without signing out?","options":["Use Run As Administrator and enter an admin's password","Double-click it, since standard users can run anything","Disable User Account Control so the program can run","Copy the utility into System32 and then try running it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Applications run as the signed-in user. The Run As Administrator option lets a standard user run a program with administrative privileges, but they must provide an administrator account's password. This supports execution control: even administrators must confirm certain changes through UAC.","optionWhy":["Correct. Run As Administrator elevates one program after admin credentials are entered.","Programs run with the signed-in user's privileges, so an admin task would fail.","Disabling UAC weakens security. Run As Administrator is the controlled method.","Moving a file into a system folder doesn't give it administrative privileges."],"optionRefs":["p. 640","p. 640","p. 640","p. 640"],"ref":"p. 640, Execution Control","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-12","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Security wants to make sure only an approved set of applications can run on reception PCs, and everything else is denied. What should be configured?","context":"Security wants to make sure only an approved set of applications can run on reception PCs, and everything else is denied. What should be configured?","options":["An application allow list of approved software","An application block list of known bad software","AutoPlay set to always run installers on USB","Antivirus folder exclusions for program files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Third-party network management suites can enforce application control in two ways. A block list denies unapproved software and allows anything else. An allow list permits approved software and denies anything else. Mobile OS vendors use a \"walled garden\", distributing apps only from approved stores.","optionWhy":["Correct. An allow list permits only approved apps and denies everything else.","A block list blocks listed apps but allows anything else, which is the opposite of the goal.","Automatically running code from removable media is a known malware risk.","Exclusions stop scanning of folders. They don't restrict which apps can run."],"optionRefs":["p. 642","p. 642","p. 642","pp. 642, 645"],"ref":"pp. 641–642, Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-13","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Malware on an infected USB stick tried to install itself automatically through an autorun.inf file when the stick was plugged in. What setting change helps prevent this?","context":"Malware on an infected USB stick tried to install itself automatically through an autorun.inf file when the stick was plugged in. What setting change helps prevent this?","options":["Disable AutoRun/AutoPlay for removable media","Add the USB drive's folder to scan exclusions","Encrypt the USB stick with BitLocker To Go","Lower the UAC level so the prompt never appears"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Legacy Windows automatically ran commands defined in an autorun.inf file in the root of an inserted disc or USB drive, which let malware install itself. Disabling AutoRun/AutoPlay prevents this. Modern Windows shows an AutoPlay prompt instead, configurable in a drive's properties or the Settings AutoPlay page, and UAC requires explicit approval for executable code.","optionWhy":["Correct. Disabling AutoRun stops autorun.inf from launching code automatically.","Exclusions stop antivirus scanning that folder, which makes things worse.","Encryption protects data at rest. It doesn't stop code running automatically.","Lowering UAC removes a safeguard that requires approval for executable code."],"optionRefs":["p. 642","pp. 642, 645","pp. 642, 649","p. 642"],"ref":"p. 642, AutoRun and AutoPlay","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-14","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Which two types of update does Windows Defender Antivirus need to keep protection effective, and how are they delivered?","context":"Which two types of update does Windows Defender Antivirus need to keep protection effective, and how are they delivered?","options":["Definition and scan engine updates, through Windows Update","Firmware and BIOS updates, from the motherboard's maker","Definitions only, installed by hand from a USB stick","Feature updates only, delivered twice a year by Microsoft"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Definitions are information about new viruses or malware and may be released daily or even hourly. Scan engine/component updates fix problems in, or improve, the scanning software itself. For Windows Defender Antivirus, both are delivered through Windows Update. Third-party products may use their own updater.","optionWhy":["Correct. Both are needed, and Defender receives them through Windows Update.","Firmware updates are for hardware. Antivirus needs definition and engine updates.","Engine updates are also needed, and Defender updates through Windows Update.","Definitions may be released daily or hourly, far more often than feature updates."],"optionRefs":["p. 644","p. 644","p. 644","p. 644"],"ref":"p. 644, Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-15","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"A developer complains that Windows Defender scanning his virtual machine disk images makes the VMs very slow. What is the best way to deal with this?","context":"A developer complains that Windows Defender scanning his virtual machine disk images makes the VMs very slow. What is the best way to deal with this?","options":["Exclude the folder that holds the VM disk images from scanning.","Turn off Real-time protection for good to stop all the scanning.","Uninstall Windows Defender, since it isn't needed on dev PCs.","Move the VM images into System32 so that Defender skips them."],"answer":0,"explanation":"You may need to exclude folders from scanning. For example, scanning virtual machine disk images can cause performance problems, and some legitimate software or development code can trigger false positives. Turning off Real-time protection only disables Defender briefly; it re-activates itself after a short period.","optionWhy":["Correct. Excluding VM disk image folders avoids the performance hit.","Real-time protection can only be paused briefly. It turns itself back on.","Anti-malware is a critical part of workstation security on every PC.","Defender doesn't skip System32. Use a deliberate folder exclusion instead."],"optionRefs":["p. 645","p. 644","pp. 643–644","p. 645"],"ref":"pp. 644–645, Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-16","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"On a standalone workstation, you need to create a custom outbound rule that blocks connections to TCP port 80. Which tool do you open?","context":"On a standalone workstation, you need to create a custom outbound rule that blocks connections to TCP port 80. Which tool do you open?","options":["Defender Firewall with Advanced Security (wf.msc)","The basic firewall page in the Windows Settings app","The Windows Defender Antivirus real-time protection page","The Encrypting File System option in folder properties"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The basic Settings interface only turns the firewall on or off per profile and adds inbound exceptions for processes. Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security (wf.msc) lets you create custom inbound and outbound rules triggered by port, application, or address. Blocking TCP/80 stops clients connecting to the default web server port. On a domain, it can be configured through Group Policy.","optionWhy":["Correct. wf.msc lets you create custom outbound rules by port.","The basic page only toggles profiles and adds inbound process exceptions.","Antivirus protects the file system. It doesn't filter network ports.","EFS encrypts files. It has nothing to do with network traffic."],"optionRefs":["p. 646","p. 646","pp. 643, 646","p. 647"],"ref":"p. 646, Windows Defender Firewall","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-17","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"In Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security, what does setting a profile's default inbound policy to Block mean?","context":"In Windows Defender Firewall with Advanced Security, what does setting a profile's default inbound policy to Block mean?","options":["Inbound traffic is stopped unless a specific rule allows it.","Inbound traffic is accepted unless a specific rule blocks it.","All inbound connections are refused, even those with rules.","Only outbound traffic is filtered, and all inbound is allowed."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For each profile, the default inbound and outbound policy can be Block or Allow. Block stops traffic unless a specific rule allows it. Allow accepts all traffic unless a specific rule blocks it. Block all connections stops inbound connections regardless of the rules.","optionWhy":["Correct. Under Block, only traffic matching an allow rule gets in.","Accepting all traffic unless a rule blocks it describes the Allow default policy.","Refusing everything regardless of rules is the separate Block all connections option.","The default policy for inbound and outbound is set separately per profile."],"optionRefs":["p. 647","p. 647","p. 647","p. 646"],"ref":"pp. 646–647, Windows Defender Firewall","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-18","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"On a Windows 11 Pro PC, a user wants to encrypt one folder of confidential files so other users of the PC can't open them. What should she do?","context":"On a Windows 11 Pro PC, a user wants to encrypt one folder of confidential files so other users of the PC can't open them. What should she do?","options":["Folder Properties > Advanced, then tick Encrypt contents","Turn on BitLocker To Go for the folder from Control Panel","Set the folder's Hidden attribute in File Explorer Options","Add the folder to Windows Defender's scan exclusion list"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Encrypting File System (EFS) feature of NTFS encrypts individual files and folders. Open the folder's Properties, select Advanced, check Encrypt contents, and confirm. Encrypted items can show in green in Explorer, and any other user (even an administrator) gets \"Access Denied.\" EFS isn't available in Windows Home.","optionWhy":["Correct. Properties > Advanced > Encrypt contents applies EFS encryption to the folder.","BitLocker To Go encrypts removable drives, not a single folder.","Hiding a folder doesn't encrypt it or stop other users opening it.","Exclusions stop antivirus scanning. They don't protect the data."],"optionRefs":["p. 647","p. 649","pp. 647–648","pp. 645, 647"],"ref":"pp. 647–648, Encrypting File System","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-19","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"After an administrator reset a user's forgotten password, the user can no longer open her EFS-encrypted files. Why, and how could this have been prevented?","context":"After an administrator reset a user's forgotten password, the user can no longer open her EFS-encrypted files. Why, and how could this have been prevented?","options":["The reset cut her off from the key; back up keys or use recovery agents.","EFS files expire after 90 days; turn the expiry off in Group Policy.","The files were moved to FAT32; always keep EFS files on exFAT drives.","Defender quarantined the files; restore them from the quarantine list."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With EFS, the user's password gives access to the key used to encrypt and decrypt files. That key can be lost or damaged if the user profile is damaged, the password is reset by an administrator, or Windows is reinstalled. On a Windows domain, the key can be backed up and recovery agents set up to decrypt data.","optionWhy":["Correct. An admin reset can lose access to the EFS key, so keys need a recovery plan.","EFS files don't expire. The problem is losing access to the encryption key.","EFS is an NTFS feature. The guide links this problem to the password reset.","Antivirus quarantine isn't the cause. The reset broke access to the key."],"optionRefs":["p. 649","p. 649","pp. 647, 649","pp. 649, 669"],"ref":"pp. 647–649, Encrypting File System","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-20","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Management wants every laptop's whole system drive encrypted, including swap and temporary files, without relying on users to encrypt files themselves. They also want USB sticks protected. What should you use?","context":"Management wants every laptop's whole system drive encrypted, including swap and temporary files, without relying on users to encrypt files themselves. They also want USB sticks protected. What should you use?","options":["BitLocker for fixed drives, and BitLocker To Go for USB sticks","EFS on each user's Documents folder, and FileVault for the USB","Hidden attributes on the system files, and NTFS on USB sticks","Windows Home on every laptop, with BitLocker turned on there"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Full disk encryption (FDE) with BitLocker encrypts whole volumes on fixed drives, including the swap file, print queues, and temporary files, and doesn't depend on users remembering to encrypt data. BitLocker To Go protects removable drives. BitLocker is available in every edition except Home.","optionWhy":["Correct. BitLocker gives full disk encryption, and BitLocker To Go covers removable drives.","Full disk encryption is what avoids relying on users and also encrypts swap and temporary files. FileVault is macOS disk encryption.","Hiding files and NTFS permissions don't encrypt data at rest.","BitLocker is available in every Windows edition except Home, so this won't work."],"optionRefs":["p. 649","pp. 589, 649","pp. 647, 649","p. 649"],"ref":"p. 649, Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-21","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"During BitLocker setup on a laptop with a TPM, Windows generates a recovery key. Where should this key be kept?","context":"During BitLocker setup on a laptop with a TPM, Windows generates a recovery key. Where should this key be kept?","options":["On removable media or paper, stored securely away from the laptop","In a text file saved on the BitLocker-encrypted drive itself","On a sticky note attached to the underside of the laptop","Nowhere, because the TPM chip can always recover the drive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"BitLocker can use the TPM to store the encryption key and tie the disk to that motherboard, or store the key on a smart card or USB stick. The recovery key generated during setup should be stored on removable media (or written down) and kept securely and separately from the computer. It's used to recover the drive if the startup key is lost.","optionWhy":["Correct. Keep the recovery key secure and separate from the computer.","The recovery key must be stored separately from the computer, because it's needed when the drive can't otherwise be unlocked.","Keeping it with the computer defeats the purpose if the laptop is stolen.","A recovery key is still needed in case the startup key in the TPM is lost."],"optionRefs":["p. 650","p. 650","p. 650","p. 650"],"ref":"pp. 649–650, Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-22","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Before deploying a Windows application installer to staff, you want to confirm it really comes from the stated developer and hasn't been modified. What should you check?","context":"Before deploying a Windows application installer to staff, you want to confirm it really comes from the stated developer and hasn't been modified. What should you check?","options":["That the installer has a valid code-signing digital certificate","That the installer's file name matches the developer's product","That the installer will run without a UAC prompt for any user","That the installer opens automatically using AutoPlay from USB"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Windows application developers use digital certificates for code signing, which proves the authenticity and integrity of an installer package. On Linux, software is signed with a cryptographic key, and the system prompts when you try to install untrusted software. Standard accounts and UAC also restrict running unapproved code.","optionWhy":["Correct. Code signing proves who published the installer and that it's unaltered.","File names are easy to fake. They don't prove authenticity or integrity.","Skipping UAC isn't a sign of trust. UAC is meant to restrict unapproved code.","Automatic execution from removable media is a malware risk, not a check."],"optionRefs":["p. 641","p. 641","p. 641","p. 642"],"ref":"p. 641, Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-23","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"You download a browser installer, and the developer publishes a SHA1 hash for it. How should you verify the download?","context":"You download a browser installer, and the developer publishes a SHA1 hash for it. How should you verify the download?","options":["Compute a SHA1 hash of the file and compare it with the published one","Compute a SHA-256 hash and compare it to the published SHA1 value","Check that the file size matches what the developer's site lists","Install it and check whether antivirus raises any alert at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Because a browser is security-critical, install it from a trusted source and verify the installer, either by checking the vendor's code-signing certificate or by comparing the developer's published hash with one you compute yourself. You must use the same hash function: if the developer gives a SHA1 hash, compute your own with SHA1.","optionWhy":["Correct. The same hash function must be used for a valid comparison.","Different hash functions give different values, so they can't be compared.","File size alone doesn't verify integrity. Compare hashes or check the code signature.","Verify integrity before installing, using the hash or code-signing certificate."],"optionRefs":["p. 652","p. 652","p. 652","p. 652"],"ref":"p. 652, Trusted Sources","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-24","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A new PC from a vendor comes with an extra browser that shows lots of ads and changes the default search engine. How should this be handled during deployment?","context":"A new PC from a vendor comes with an extra browser that shows lots of ads and changes the default search engine. How should this be handled during deployment?","options":["Uninstall it as a potentially unwanted program (PUP)","Keep it but set it as the default browser for all users","Add it to the Windows Defender scan exclusions list","Enable private browsing mode in it to block its adverts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Browsers from untrusted sources, or bundled by vendors, can expose users to unwanted ads, search engines, spyware, and redirection. This bloatware, a potentially unwanted application or program (PUA/PUP), should be uninstalled when deploying a new PC. A PUA is software that can't definitively be called malicious but carries increased privacy risk.","optionWhy":["Correct. Bundled adware browsers are PUPs and should be removed.","Making an adware browser the default exposes users to its risks.","Excluding it from scanning hides the risk instead of removing it.","Private browsing limits locally stored data. It doesn't remove adware behavior."],"optionRefs":["p. 652","p. 652","pp. 645, 652","pp. 652, 659"],"ref":"p. 652, Untrusted Sources","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-25","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"You need to open Mozilla Firefox's settings page directly by typing an internal URL. What do you type in the address bar?","context":"You need to open Mozilla Firefox's settings page directly by typing an internal URL. What do you type in the address bar?","options":["about:preferences","chrome://settings","edge://settings","chrome://flags"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Each browser's settings are opened from its meatball (…) or hamburger menu, or via an internal URL: chrome://settings for Chrome, edge://settings for Edge, or about:preferences for Firefox. Advanced settings use URLs such as chrome://flags or about:config.","optionWhy":["Correct. Typing about:preferences in the address bar opens Firefox's settings.","chrome://settings opens Google Chrome's settings, not Firefox's.","edge://settings opens Microsoft Edge's settings, not Firefox's.","The guide gives chrome://settings for Chrome settings. The flags page is not described in the guide."],"optionRefs":["p. 652","p. 652","p. 652","p. 653"],"ref":"pp. 652–653, Browser Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-26","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A company with an Active Directory domain wants the same browser security settings applied to all 500 PCs without visiting each one. How should this be done?","context":"A company with an Active Directory domain wants the same browser security settings applied to all 500 PCs without visiting each one. How should this be done?","options":["Configure the browser settings centrally with Group Policy (GPMC)","Change each PC's settings by hand through chrome://settings pages","Sign each user into the browser so that sync applies the policy","Install the same theme on every PC to lock the browser settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In enterprise environments, many browser settings can be managed for the whole environment through Group Policy, using the Group Policy Management Console (GPMC) in Active Directory. Individually, settings are changed from the browser's menu or an internal URL.","optionWhy":["Correct. Group Policy pushes browser settings across the domain.","Visiting each PC is exactly the manual work the company wants to avoid.","Browser sync copies a user's own settings between their devices. It doesn't enforce company policy.","Themes only change the browser's appearance. They can't lock or enforce settings."],"optionRefs":["p. 653","pp. 652–653","p. 653","pp. 653–654"],"ref":"p. 653, Browser Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-27","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A user admits she uses the same password on dozens of websites because she can't remember them all. What should you recommend?","context":"A user admits she uses the same password on dozens of websites because she can't remember them all. What should you recommend?","options":["Use a password manager to create and autofill unique passwords","Store all the passwords in a plain spreadsheet on her desktop","Keep reusing one strong password, since strength is what matters","Write each password on a sticky note kept next to the monitor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reusing one password is insecure, because a single site breach can compromise all of a user's identities. Browser password managers (or third-party ones through an extension) can suggest a strong, unique password for each new account and autofill it at sign-in. If the user signs in to the browser, the passwords sync to each device. Where a site isn't recognized, copy and paste is the fallback.","optionWhy":["Correct. A password manager makes unique strong passwords practical.","Passwords shouldn't be stored in unprotected files such as spreadsheets.","One breach would expose every account that shares the password.","A clean desk policy forbids leaving passwords in plain sight."],"optionRefs":["p. 653","pp. 636, 653","p. 653","pp. 636, 653"],"ref":"p. 653, Password Manager","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-28","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A user wants to install a free \"coupon finder\" browser extension that asks for permission to read and change data on every website. What is the main risk, and what should you advise?","context":"A user wants to install a free \"coupon finder\" browser extension that asks for permission to read and change data on every website. What is the main risk, and what should you advise?","options":["Its scripts could read page data; install only trusted ones.","There's no risk, as extensions only change the browser theme.","It's safe if it's from the official store, since none are malicious.","The risk is to Flash only, and Flash is no longer used anyway."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Browser extensions change browser features through its API and must be granted permissions. With enough permissions they can run scripts that interact with the pages you view, which could compromise security or privacy. Only trusted extensions should be installed, even from official stores (which have hosted malicious ones), and extensions should be disabled when not in use.","optionWhy":["Correct. Broad permissions let an extension's scripts access your browsing data.","Themes change appearance. Extensions can run scripts against pages.","Malicious extensions have been found in stores, so trust still matters.","Flash is a plug-in. Extensions are a separate type of add-on with their own risks."],"optionRefs":["p. 654","p. 654","p. 654","p. 654"],"ref":"p. 654, Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-29","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"How does a browser plug-in, such as one for Flash or Silverlight, differ from a browser extension?","context":"How does a browser plug-in, such as one for Flash or Silverlight, differ from a browser extension?","options":["A plug-in plays embedded content; an extension changes browser features.","A plug-in changes browser features; an extension only plays embedded media.","A plug-in sets the default search engine; an extension changes the theme.","A plug-in is always safer, as it has never been linked to vulnerabilities."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Extensions add or change browser features through its API and can run scripts against pages if given enough permissions. Plug-ins play or show content embedded in a page, such as Flash or Silverlight. In theory they're more limited, since they only interact with the multimedia object, but they've had many vulnerabilities and are now rarely used, having been replaced by HTML5.","optionWhy":["Correct. Plug-ins handle embedded media, while extensions change browser functions.","This reverses the definitions. Extensions change features, and plug-ins play embedded media.","Default search provider and themes are separate add-on types from plug-ins and extensions.","Plug-ins have been linked to many vulnerabilities over the years."],"optionRefs":["p. 654","p. 654","p. 654","p. 654"],"ref":"p. 654, Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-30","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"You need to check for and apply updates to Microsoft Edge manually. Where do you go?","context":"You need to check for and apply updates to Microsoft Edge manually. Where do you go?","options":["Edge's settings menu, under Help and Feedback","The Apple App Store's Updates page on the PC","chrome://flags, then set update channel to on","Windows Defender's protection updates button"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Browsers should be routinely updated from trusted sources. Edge is updated from its settings menu under Help and Feedback. Firefox and Chrome check for updates each time they start by default. Safari is updated through the official App Store.","optionWhy":["Correct. Edge updates are applied from Help and Feedback in its menu.","The App Store is how Safari is updated on Apple devices, not how Edge is updated.","The guide gives chrome://settings for Chrome settings. The flags page is not described in the guide.","That updates Defender's antivirus definitions, not the browser itself."],"optionRefs":["p. 655","p. 655","pp. 653, 655","pp. 644, 655"],"ref":"p. 655, Browser Patching","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-31","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"Internal company websites open fine in Edge, but Firefox shows certificate warnings for them. They're signed by the company's internal CA. What should you do?","context":"Internal company websites open fine in Edge, but Firefox shows certificate warnings for them. They're signed by the company's internal CA. What should you do?","options":["Add the internal CA's root certificate to Firefox's own trust store","Tell users to ignore the warnings for any site on the company network","Disable HTTPS on the internal websites so no certificate is needed","Clear Firefox's cache and cookies so it downloads the certificate"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A client trusts a server's certificate if it trusts the CA that signed it, through that CA's root certificate in a trusted store. Edge uses the Windows certificate store, where AD and admins install enterprise roots, but third-party browsers keep a separate store. When using enterprise certificates with a third-party browser, add the internal CA root certificate to that browser.","optionWhy":["Correct. Firefox uses its own store, so the internal root must be added there.","Ignoring certificate warnings trains users into risky habits and could hide a real attack.","That removes encryption and identity checks instead of fixing trust.","Clearing browsing data doesn't add a trusted root certificate."],"optionRefs":["p. 656","pp. 656, 666","p. 655","pp. 656, 659"],"ref":"pp. 655–656, Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-32","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A user is concerned that advertisers track her across different websites using cookies set by domains other than the site she's visiting. Which browser privacy setting addresses this most directly?","context":"A user is concerned that advertisers track her across different websites using cookies set by domains other than the site she's visiting. Which browser privacy setting addresses this most directly?","options":["Block third-party cookies","Turn on the pop-up blocker","Set a new default search engine","Install a theme with dark colors"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cookies are text files that store session data. A modern site uses components from many domains, which can set third-party cookies that create tracking information for a host other than the site owner. Browser privacy settings can block all cookies or just third-party cookies, with exceptions per site. Tracking protection (strict or standard) adds further protection.","optionWhy":["Correct. Blocking third-party cookies stops cross-site tracking cookies.","Pop-up blockers stop extra windows and dialogs, not tracking cookies.","The search provider has no effect on third-party cookies from other sites.","Themes only change the browser's appearance. They don't affect cookies or tracking."],"optionRefs":["p. 658","p. 658","pp. 654, 658","p. 654"],"ref":"pp. 657–658, Browser Privacy Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-33","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A user is annoyed by ads that are built into page layouts rather than opened as pop-up windows. The pop-up blocker doesn't stop them. What would?","context":"A user is annoyed by ads that are built into page layouts rather than opened as pop-up windows. The pop-up blocker doesn't stop them. What would?","options":["An ad blocker installed as a browser extension","Stricter pop-up blocker settings in the browser","Secure DNS turned on in the browser's settings","Private browsing mode for every browsing session"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Pop-up blockers stop sites creating dialogs or extra windows, a technique once used for fake AV warnings and nuisance ads. Few sites use pop-ups now, since the same effect can be built into pages. Ad blockers use more sophisticated techniques to hide anything that isn't the site's main content, often using databases of ad-serving domains. They're usually installed as an extension and can have per-site exceptions.","optionWhy":["Correct. Ad blockers filter in-page ad elements that pop-up blockers miss.","These ads aren't pop-ups, so a pop-up blocker won't catch them.","Secure DNS authenticates DNS responses. It doesn't remove in-page ads.","Private mode limits stored data. It doesn't remove page advertising."],"optionRefs":["p. 658","p. 658","p. 658","p. 659"],"ref":"pp. 657–658, Browser Privacy Settings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-35","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"What does enabling Secure DNS in a browser do?","context":"What does enabling Secure DNS in a browser do?","options":["It authenticates DNS responses using digital signatures.","It sends all web requests through a corporate proxy server.","It blocks ads that come from known ad-serving domains.","It deletes the browser's history when the session ends."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Secure DNS settings let the browser authenticate DNS query responses using digital signatures. This helps protect against DNS spoofing that redirects users to fake sites. Proxy servers, pop-up blockers, and ad blockers are separate controls.","optionWhy":["Correct. Secure DNS uses digital signatures to verify DNS responses.","That describes a proxy server, which can also track and filter web use.","That describes an ad blocker, usually installed as an extension.","Clearing history at the end of a session is a browsing data or private mode feature."],"optionRefs":["p. 658","p. 658","p. 658","p. 659"],"ref":"pp. 657–658, Browser Privacy Settings","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-36","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Malware spreads from server to server across a network by exploiting a vulnerable service, replicating in memory without infecting any files on disk. What type of malware is this?","context":"Malware spreads from server to server across a network by exploiting a vulnerable service, replicating in memory without infecting any files on disk. What type of malware is this?","options":["A worm","A virus","A Trojan","A boot sector virus"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Malware can be classified by vector. A virus is hidden inside an executable file on disk. A boot sector virus infects the boot sector or partition table. A Trojan hides in an apparently legitimate installer. A worm replicates between processes in memory and can exploit vulnerable client/server software to spread between hosts. Fileless malware uses scripting environments such as PowerShell.","optionWhy":["Correct. Worms replicate in memory and spread by exploiting network services.","A virus is hidden inside an executable file stored on disk, rather than spreading in memory.","A Trojan is hidden in an installer for apparently legitimate software.","A boot sector virus infects the disk's boot sector or partition table."],"optionRefs":["p. 661","p. 660","p. 661","p. 661"],"ref":"pp. 660–661, Malware Vectors","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-37","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Your security team finds malicious code that runs entirely through Windows PowerShell scripts, creating processes in memory with no executable file on disk. Why is this type harder to detect?","context":"Your security team finds malicious code that runs entirely through Windows PowerShell scripts, creating processes in memory with no executable file on disk. Why is this type harder to detect?","options":["It's fileless malware with no executable file for scanners to find.","It's a boot sector virus that loads before antivirus can start.","It's a Trojan hidden in a signed installer with valid certificates.","It's adware that only shows ads and runs no other processes."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Fileless malware uses the host's scripting environment, such as PowerShell or PDF JavaScript, to create malicious processes in memory. Because it can be disguised as script instructions or a document rather than an executable file, it's harder to detect. \"Fileless\" means there's no host file containing the malware.","optionWhy":["Correct. With no host executable file, fileless malware is harder to detect.","Boot sector viruses infect the boot sector. This malware runs as PowerShell scripts.","Trojans hide in installer packages. This malware involves no installer.","Adware displays unwanted ads. This malware creates processes in memory."],"optionRefs":["p. 661","p. 661","p. 661","p. 661"],"ref":"pp. 660–661, Malware Vectors","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-38","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A user installs a free PDF editor from an unofficial site. It works, but it also installed hidden malware that now starts every time she signs in. What is this?","context":"A user installs a free PDF editor from an unofficial site. It works, but it also installed hidden malware that now starts every time she signs in. What is this?","options":["A Trojan with persistence","A worm spreading between servers","A boot sector virus in the MBR","A cryptominer stealing CPU time"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A Trojan is malware hidden inside an installer for software that appears legitimate. It's installed alongside the program with the same privileges, and it may add itself to startup locations so it always runs when the computer starts or the user signs in. This is called persistence.","optionWhy":["Correct. Malware hidden in a legitimate-looking installer that runs at every sign-in is a Trojan with persistence.","Worms spread by themselves between hosts. This came bundled with an installer.","Boot sector viruses infect boot code. This was installed with an application.","Cryptomining is a type of payload. The vector here is a disguised installer, which makes it a Trojan."],"optionRefs":["p. 661","p. 661","p. 661","pp. 661, 663"],"ref":"pp. 660–661, Malware Vectors","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-39","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"During a physical inspection, you find a small adapter plugged in between a user's keyboard cable and the PC's USB port. What is it most likely to be?","context":"During a physical inspection, you find a small adapter plugged in between a user's keyboard cable and the PC's USB port. What is it most likely to be?","options":["A hardware keylogger capturing keystrokes","A Kensington lock adapter for the keyboard","A TPM module that stores BitLocker keys","A USB stick holding the BitLocker key"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A keylogger is spyware that records keystrokes to steal information such as passwords or credit card numbers. Keyloggers aren't only software; malicious scripts can send key presses to a website, and hardware devices can capture key presses in a modified USB adapter between the keyboard and the port.","optionWhy":["Correct. A modified USB adapter between the keyboard and port can log key presses.","Kensington locks secure devices to desks with a cable. They don't sit inline on USB.","The TPM is a chip on the motherboard, not a keyboard adapter.","A startup key stick plugs into a port. It doesn't sit between the keyboard and the PC."],"optionRefs":["p. 662","pp. 636, 662","pp. 650, 662","pp. 650, 662"],"ref":"pp. 661–662, Spyware and Keyloggers","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-40","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"You suspect a PC has a rootkit, but Task Manager and netstat on that PC show nothing unusual. Why might this be, and what's a better approach?","context":"You suspect a PC has a rootkit, but Task Manager and netstat on that PC show nothing unusual. Why might this be, and what's a better approach?","options":["A rootkit can hide from the PC's own tools; scan from a clean system.","Nothing unusual means it's clean; return the PC to the user straight away.","Rootkits only exist on Linux, so Windows tools will always reveal them.","Run Task Manager as administrator, as that always reveals rootkits."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Malware running with SYSTEM-level privilege is a rootkit. It can compromise system files and programming interfaces so that local tools such as Explorer, Task Manager, ps, top, or netstat no longer reveal it when run on the infected machine, and it may clean logs. Checking from a clean environment, such as scanning the disk from another system or booting WinRE, avoids relying on the compromised tools.","optionWhy":["Correct. Rootkits can blind local tools, so examine the disk from a clean environment.","A rootkit can make local tools show nothing even when the PC is infected.","Rootkits affect Windows too. The term just comes from UNIX/Linux root.","The rootkit may have compromised the tool itself, whatever privileges you run it with."],"optionRefs":["pp. 662, 670","p. 662","p. 662","p. 662"],"ref":"p. 662, Rootkits","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-41","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Malware encrypts every file on a user's local and network drives and demands payment in cryptocurrency for the key. What is the best defence against data loss from this?","context":"Malware encrypts every file on a user's local and network drives and demands payment in cryptocurrency for the key. What is the best defence against data loss from this?","options":["Keeping up-to-date backups of the files","Paying the ransom quickly to get the key","Turning on the browser's pop-up blocker","Excluding the network drives from scans"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Crypto-ransomware tries to encrypt files on fixed, removable, and network drives, so the user can't open them without the attacker's private key. Once successful, it's extremely difficult to mitigate unless the user has up-to-date backups. Payments use methods such as cryptocurrency or wire transfer, which are hard to trace.","optionWhy":["Correct. Crypto-ransomware is extremely difficult to mitigate unless the user has up-to-date backups.","Paying isn't the mitigation given. This attack is extremely difficult to mitigate unless the user has up-to-date backups.","A pop-up blocker doesn't stop file encryption by malware already running.","Exclusions reduce protection and don't help recover encrypted data."],"optionRefs":["p. 663","p. 663","pp. 658, 663","pp. 645, 663"],"ref":"p. 663, Ransomware and Cryptominers","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-42","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Several office PCs are suddenly slow, with processors at nearly 100% even when idle, but no files are encrypted and no ransom note appears. Which malware payload fits best?","context":"Several office PCs are suddenly slow, with processors at nearly 100% even when idle, but no files are encrypted and no ransom note appears. Which malware payload fits best?","options":["A cryptominer","Crypto-ransomware","A hardware keylogger","A browser plug-in"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A cryptominer hijacks the host's resources to mine cryptocurrency, also called cryptojacking. Mining takes enormous computing resources, so infected hosts show heavy, sustained resource use. Cryptomining is often carried out across botnets.","optionWhy":["Correct. Hijacking CPU resources for mining causes sustained high utilization.","Crypto-ransomware encrypts files and demands payment. No files are encrypted here.","Keyloggers record keystrokes to steal information. They don't max out the CPU.","Plug-ins play embedded media content and aren't malware payloads in themselves."],"optionRefs":["pp. 663–664","p. 663","p. 662","p. 654"],"ref":"pp. 663–664, Ransomware and Cryptominers","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-43","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"On a user's PC you find a running file named scvhost.exe, next to the genuine svchost.exe. Several personal files also now give \"Access Denied.\" What should you do?","context":"On a user's PC you find a running file named scvhost.exe, next to the genuine svchost.exe. Several personal files also now give \"Access Denied.\" What should you do?","options":["Quarantine the PC and investigate it for malware.","Delete scvhost.exe, then return the PC to the user.","Reset the file permissions, as it is only a glitch.","Ignore it, as scvhost.exe is a standard system file."],"answer":0,"explanation":"File system markers of malware include missing or renamed files, extra executables with names similar to genuine system files (such as scvhost.exe or ta5kmgr.exe), altered system files with unexpected dates or sizes, and changed permissions causing Access Denied errors. These are less likely to have other causes, so quarantine the system and investigate closely.","optionWhy":["Correct. Look-alike executables and changed permissions strongly suggest malware.","Deleting one file may not remove the infection. Quarantine and investigate first.","These anomalies are unlikely to have innocent causes and point to malware.","scvhost.exe is the guide's example of a look-alike name for svchost.exe."],"optionRefs":["p. 664","pp. 664, 667","p. 664","p. 664"],"ref":"p. 664, File System Errors and Anomalies","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-44","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"On a user's PC, Windows Update keeps failing, antivirus definitions won't update, and Task Manager crashes when opened. What should you suspect?","context":"On a user's PC, Windows Update keeps failing, antivirus definitions won't update, and Task Manager crashes when opened. What should you suspect?","options":["Malware that is disabling security tools","A wrong time zone setting on the computer","A disk that only needs to be defragmented","A browser extension slowing down the PC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A key sign of malware infection is that security-related apps such as antivirus, firewalls, and Windows Update stop working, with OS and definition updates failing. Windows tools such as Task Manager stopping or crashing often are another sign. If reputable software starts crashing repeatedly, suspect malware and apply quarantine and monitoring procedures.","optionWhy":["Correct. Failing security updates and crashing tools are classic signs of malware.","A time zone mismatch wouldn't cause security tools to crash like this.","Defragmenting maintains disk performance by optimizing file storage. It doesn't explain failing security tools.","Browser extensions don't stop Windows Update and Task Manager working."],"optionRefs":["p. 664","p. 664","pp. 400, 664","pp. 654, 664"],"ref":"p. 664, Application Crashes and Service Problems","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-45","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A user calls in a panic: a pop-up claims her PC is infected and offers a \"scanner\" to download, and someone then phoned claiming to be Microsoft support, asking her to enable a remote desktop tool. What is this?","context":"A user calls in a panic: a pop-up claims her PC is infected and offers a \"scanner\" to download, and someone then phoned claiming to be Microsoft support, asking her to enable a remote desktop tool. What is this?","options":["A false antivirus alert and support scam to install malware or get access","A genuine Windows Defender alert, so she should follow the instructions","A normal browser update prompt, which Microsoft confirms by phone","A certificate warning, caused by a website's certificate having expired"],"answer":0,"explanation":"False antivirus alerts are a popular way to disguise a Trojan. Websites, or misused push notifications, show fake security warnings to frighten users into installing malware. As browsers block this, attackers increasingly cold-call users, claim to be Microsoft support or the ISP, and ask them to enable a remote desktop tool.","optionWhy":["Correct. Fake alerts and fake support calls trick users into installing malware or allowing access.","Defender doesn't phone users or ask them to download scanners from pop-ups.","Browser updates come through trusted channels, not cold calls asking for remote access.","Certificate warnings appear in the address bar. They don't come with phone calls."],"optionRefs":["p. 665","pp. 643, 665","pp. 655, 665","pp. 665–666"],"ref":"p. 665, Desktop Alerts and Notifications","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-46","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A company wants endpoint monitoring that goes beyond its PCs to cover cloud systems and applications, using intelligence-based response strategies. Which solution type fits?","context":"A company wants endpoint monitoring that goes beyond its PCs to cover cloud systems and applications, using intelligence-based response strategies. Which solution type fits?","options":["Extended detection and response (XDR)","Endpoint detection and response (EDR)","Managed detection and response (MDR)","An ad blocker installed as a browser extension"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR) integrates with existing security tools and can automate responses on each endpoint. Managed detection and response (MDR) adds customized dashboards and intelligence-led detection, usually outsourced to an MSSP. Extended detection and response (XDR) extends beyond endpoints to cloud systems and applications, with intelligence-based response.","optionWhy":["Correct. XDR extends monitoring beyond endpoints to cloud systems and apps.","EDR focuses on the endpoints themselves rather than cloud systems and applications.","MDR is usually an outsourced service with dashboards, not defined by reach beyond endpoints.","An ad blocker filters advertising in the browser. It isn't endpoint monitoring."],"optionRefs":["p. 665","p. 665","p. 665","p. 658"],"ref":"p. 665, Endpoint Monitoring Solutions","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-47","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A browser shows a certificate warning because the address typed (www.shop.example) doesn't match the name on the certificate. What should you do?","context":"A browser shows a certificate warning because the address typed (www.shop.example) doesn't match the name on the certificate. What should you do?","options":["Check the certificate and URL, as it could be misconfiguration or a spoof.","Click through the warning, since name mismatches are always harmless.","Clear the cookies, which always fixes certificate name mismatches.","Disable antivirus, because it is causing the certificate mismatch."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Common causes of certificate warnings are a self-signed or untrusted-CA certificate, an FQDN that doesn't match the certificate's subject name, or a certificate that's expired or revoked. Each could mean a misconfigured site or malware redirecting the browser to a spoofed page, so analyze the certificate details and URL to find the likely cause.","optionWhy":["Correct. A name mismatch can mean either, so investigate before trusting the site.","A mismatch may point to a spoofed site. Overriding warnings can enable on-path attacks.","Cookies store session data. They aren't involved in validating a certificate's name.","Turning off protection is risky and doesn't address the certificate problem."],"optionRefs":["p. 666","p. 666","pp. 657, 666","pp. 644, 666"],"ref":"p. 666, Certificate Warnings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-48","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Malware on a PC has added its own root certificate to the trusted root store and is intercepting the user's HTTPS sessions. Why might the user see no browser warning?","context":"Malware on a PC has added its own root certificate to the trusted root store and is intercepting the user's HTTPS sessions. Why might the user see no browser warning?","options":["The spoofed certificate chains to a root the PC now trusts.","HTTPS never shows warnings once a site has been visited.","Private browsing mode hides all of the certificate warnings.","Secure DNS has switched off certificate checking for the site."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a malicious-proxy on-path attack, malware or an evil-twin access point intercepts the request and presents its own spoofed certificate. If the malware can compromise the trusted root certificate store, there's no warning. Once the browser accepts the certificate, the malware proxies the session and can intercept and modify the encrypted traffic.","optionWhy":["Correct. With a compromised root store, the spoofed certificate looks valid.","Browsers check certificates on every connection. Here the trust store itself is compromised.","Private mode controls local data storage, not certificate validation.","Secure DNS authenticates DNS responses. It doesn't disable certificate checks."],"optionRefs":["p. 666","pp. 656, 666","pp. 659, 666","pp. 658, 666"],"ref":"p. 666, Certificate Warnings","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-49","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"You've investigated and verified malware symptoms on a workstation. Using CompTIA's seven-step malware removal procedure, what is the next step?","context":"You've investigated and verified malware symptoms on a workstation. Using CompTIA's seven-step malware removal procedure, what is the next step?","options":["Quarantine the system","Disable System Restore","Update the anti-malware","Educate the end user"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The seven steps are: 1. Investigate and verify symptoms. 2. Quarantine infected systems. 3. Disable System Restore in Windows. 4. Remediate (update anti-malware; scan and remove, e.g. Safe Mode or reimage). 5. Schedule scans and run updates. 6. Enable System Restore and create a restore point. 7. Educate the end user.","optionWhy":["Correct. Quarantining the infected system is step 2, immediately after verifying symptoms.","Disabling System Restore is step 3, which comes after the system is quarantined.","Updating anti-malware is part of step 4, remediation, which comes later.","Educating the end user is the final step, step 7, after the system has been cleaned and returned to service."],"optionRefs":["p. 667","p. 667","p. 667","p. 667"],"ref":"pp. 667–668, Best Practices for Malware Removal","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-50","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Why does the malware removal procedure disable System Restore (and backups such as File History) after quarantining the system?","context":"Why does the malware removal procedure disable System Restore (and backups such as File History) after quarantining the system?","options":["Restore points and backups may be infected and re-infect the PC.","System Restore makes antivirus scans run faster when it's off.","System Restore deletes user files when malware is present.","System Restore must be off before the PC can be disconnected."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Once the system is isolated, disable System Restore and other automatic backups such as File History. If you rely on a backup to recover malware-infected files, you must consider that the backups may be infected too. The safest option is to delete old restore points and backups; if you must keep them, scan them with antivirus software first.","optionWhy":["Correct. Old restore points and backups could be infected and bring the malware back.","The reason is the risk of infected restore points, not scan speed.","System Restore isn't described as deleting user files. Infected restore points are the concern.","Quarantine (disconnecting) happens first and doesn't depend on System Restore."],"optionRefs":["p. 669","p. 669","p. 669","pp. 667–669"],"ref":"p. 669, Disable System Restore","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-51","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"You strongly suspect a workstation has advanced malware with a backdoor. A domain administrator wants to sign in to it remotely to take a look. What should you do?","context":"You strongly suspect a workstation has advanced malware with a backdoor. A domain administrator wants to sign in to it remotely to take a look. What should you do?","options":["Block admin sign-ins and disconnect the PC from the main network.","Let the admin sign in remotely to begin investigating right away.","Leave it connected so the antivirus can download fresh definitions.","Reboot it normally several times to see if the malware goes away."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If a system is under suspicion, don't let users with administrative privileges sign in, locally or remotely, until it's quarantined, because malware could compromise a privileged account. Quarantine means the host can't communicate on the main network, so one of the first actions is disconnecting the network link and moving it to a secure segment or sandbox.","optionWhy":["Correct. Keep admin accounts away and isolate the host first.","An admin signing in to a suspected host risks compromising a privileged account.","The host must be quarantined. Get updates without risking the production network.","Rebooting won't remove a backdoor, and the network risk remains until it's isolated."],"optionRefs":["p. 668","p. 668","pp. 668–669","p. 668"],"ref":"p. 668, Quarantine Infected Systems","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-52","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"Malware keeps relaunching at startup and blocks the antivirus scanner from running in normal Windows. Which approach should you try next?","context":"Malware keeps relaunching at startup and blocks the antivirus scanner from running in normal Windows. Which approach should you try next?","options":["Boot into Safe Mode or WinRE and scan from there","Re-enable System Restore and roll back to yesterday","Turn on AutoPlay so the scanner runs from a USB","Add the malware's folder to Defender's exclusions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Advanced malware may need manual removal of its persistence mechanisms. Tools include Task Manager to end processes, the command prompt and regedit to remove registry items, and msconfig or Safe Mode to stop infected code running at startup. You can also boot WinRE from recovery media, or remove the disk and scan it from another system without letting the infection spread.","optionWhy":["Correct. Safe Mode or WinRE helps stop the malware running at startup.","The guide disables System Restore during remediation (step 3), so re-enabling it now goes against the removal procedure.","AutoPlay running code automatically is a malware risk, not a removal method.","Exclusions stop scanning that folder, which protects the malware."],"optionRefs":["p. 670","pp. 669–670","pp. 642, 670","pp. 645, 670"],"ref":"p. 670, Recovery Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-53","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A PC has been cleaned and passed a scan. Before returning it to service, what should you check in its network settings to help prevent reinfection?","context":"A PC has been cleaned and passed a scan. Before returning it to service, what should you check in its network settings to help prevent reinfection?","options":["Its DNS settings and firewall policy, for unwanted changes","Its display scale and theme, in case malware changed them","Its Bluetooth pairing list, in case the user lost a mouse","Its wallpaper and screen saver, for any hidden scripts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When recommissioning, re-enable System Restore and create a fresh restore point and clean backup. Validate security-critical settings: verify the DNS configuration (DNS spoofing redirects users to fake sites), and inspect the host firewall policy for unauthorized changes such as ports opened for C&C, considering a reset to defaults. Then run a final scan, lift the quarantine, and educate the user.","optionWhy":["Correct. Malware may have changed DNS and opened firewall ports for C&C.","Appearance settings aren't the security-critical settings to validate.","That's unrelated to the reinfection risks the guide highlights.","Wallpaper and screen savers aren't the security-critical settings the guide says to verify."],"optionRefs":["pp. 670–671","p. 671","p. 671","p. 671"],"ref":"pp. 670–671, Malware Infection Prevention","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-54","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"What does on-access scanning do, and what is its trade-off?","context":"What does on-access scanning do, and what is its trade-off?","options":["It scans each file as it's opened, at a small cost to performance.","It scans only at night, so it doesn't affect performance at all.","It scans only email attachments at the company's mail gateway.","It scans only USB drives, the first time each one is inserted."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On-access means the antivirus software intercepts an OS call to open a file and scans the file before allowing or blocking it. This reduces performance somewhat but is essential for effective protection. Scheduled scans are best run when the computer isn't being used.","optionWhy":["Correct. On-access scanning checks files when they're opened, at some performance cost.","Scanning only at night describes scheduled scans, not on-access scanning.","An email security gateway scans mail. On-access scanning checks files on the host.","On-access scanning applies to any file being opened, not just USB media."],"optionRefs":["p. 670","p. 670","pp. 670–671","p. 670"],"ref":"p. 670, Configure On-access Scanning","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-55","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A manager installs monitoring software on staff phones that tracks each person's location and habits, even outside working hours. What is this kind of software classed as?","context":"A manager installs monitoring software on staff phones that tracks each person's location and habits, even outside working hours. What is this kind of software classed as?","options":["Stalkerware","Ransomware","Cryptominer","Keylogger"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Some spyware, and even some legitimate monitoring software, is classed as stalkerware because it can be used to track a user's habits and whereabouts, similar to stalking them. Abuse of employee monitoring software should be monitored and minimized.","optionWhy":["Correct. Monitoring software that tracks a user's habits and whereabouts, similar to stalking them, is classed as stalkerware, even if it's legitimate.","Ransomware tries to extort money, for example by encrypting files or showing threatening messages. Tracking location isn't its purpose.","A cryptominer hijacks the host's resources to perform cryptocurrency mining. It doesn't track a user's habits or whereabouts.","A keylogger records keystrokes to steal information such as passwords or card data, rather than tracking where someone goes."],"optionRefs":["p. 662","p. 663","p. 663","p. 662"],"ref":"pp. 661–662, Spyware and Keyloggers","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-56","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"A user's browser has become very slow on every site, and scans find no malware. Which troubleshooting steps fit best?","context":"A user's browser has become very slow on every site, and scans find no malware. Which troubleshooting steps fit best?","options":["Clear cache and cookies, disable add-ons, or repair it","Reinstall Windows, then restore the user's browser profile","Turn off on-access scanning so pages load faster","Turn off certificate warnings for every website"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If browser performance is below expectations, try repairing or reinstalling the browser, and clear its cache and cookies and disable add-ons or plug-ins to find the root cause. Trying a different browser shows whether the problem lies with the website instead.","optionWhy":["Correct. Repair or reinstall the browser, clear its cache and cookies, and disable add-ons or plug-ins to find the root cause.","For poor browser performance, repair or reinstall the browser application itself. OS reinstallation is for cases such as malware gaining a persistent foothold.","On-access scanning is essential protection against malware. Turning it off weakens security and doesn't address the browser itself.","Certificate warnings protect against misconfigured or spoofed sites. Suppressing them adds risk and doesn't fix performance."],"optionRefs":["p. 666","pp. 666, 670","p. 670","pp. 665–666"],"ref":"pp. 665–666, Troubleshoot Browser Symptoms","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-01","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A technician enables fingerprint unlock on a driver's company smartphone. During setup, the phone insists that a PIN also be configured. Why does the device require the PIN?","context":"A technician enables fingerprint unlock on a driver's company smartphone. During setup, the phone insists that a PIN also be configured. Why does the device require the PIN?","options":["It acts as a fallback method and protects high-privilege tasks such as a factory reset.","It encrypts the fingerprint template before it is uploaded to the vendor's cloud service.","It generates the one-time password codes that are used for two-factor authentication.","It sets how long the device stays locked after each failed fingerprint attempt."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide states that when a biometric method is used, a PIN or password serves as a backup and is required for high-privilege tasks such as a factory reset. The fingerprint is the convenient everyday method; the PIN is the fallback and the gate for sensitive actions.","optionWhy":["Correct. A PIN or password backs up the biometric method and is used for high-privilege tasks like factory resets.","The fingerprint template is created from the initial scan and stored securely on the device itself, not uploaded to the cloud. The PIN's role has nothing to do with uploading a template.","One-time password codes are delivered by email, text, or call to a trusted authenticator app or device during 2FA. They are not generated by the screen-lock PIN.","Escalating lockout times come from the failed-login-attempts restriction setting on the screen lock (for example, 30 seconds, then longer). The PIN itself does not define lockout duration."],"optionRefs":["p. 675","p. 675","pp. 680–681","p. 675"],"ref":"p. 675, Screen Locks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-02","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"During a security audit, an inspector holds a phone at an angle to the light and can see the shape the user draws to unlock it in the grease on the screen. Which screen lock method is in use?","context":"During a security audit, an inspector holds a phone at an angle to the light and can see the shape the user draws to unlock it in the grease on the screen. Which screen lock method is in use?","options":["Pattern lock","PIN lock","Facial recognition","Simple swipe"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A pattern lock is a \"join-the-dots\" swipe. The guide lists its weaknesses: it is easy to observe, it can be traced from screen smudges, and users tend to choose predictable shapes such as C, M, N, O, and S.","optionWhy":["Correct. Pattern locks use a join-the-dots swipe that can be traced from smudges on the screen and is easy to observe.","A PIN is a 4- or 6-digit numeric code typed on a keypad. It doesn't leave a drawn shape behind. The guide says a PIN is adequate if it isn't easily guessable.","Facial recognition authenticates using a 3-D image of the user's face, captured by the camera and infrared technology. The user doesn't draw anything on the screen.","A simple swipe gives unauthenticated access with no secret involved, so it's only suitable for shared or public devices. There's no shape for an attacker to learn, but it provides no real protection either."],"optionRefs":["p. 675","p. 674","p. 675","p. 674"],"ref":"p. 675, Screen Locks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-03","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Company policy states that after a wrong unlock attempt a phone must lock for 30 seconds, and that each further wrong attempt must lock it for longer. Which screen lock setting implements this?","context":"Company policy states that after a wrong unlock attempt a phone must lock for 30 seconds, and that each further wrong attempt must lock it for longer. Which screen lock setting implements this?","options":["Failed login attempts restriction","Erase data after 10 failed attempts","Two-factor authentication (2FA)","iOS Data Protection encryption"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The failed login attempts restriction applies escalating lockout times to deter repeated guessing. The guide's example is a 30-second lock after the first incorrect attempt, with longer lockouts for later attempts.","optionWhy":["Correct. This setting escalates lockout times after incorrect unlock attempts.","This is a separate iOS passcode option shown in the guide's screenshot description (\"erase all data after 10 failed passcode attempts\"). It destroys the data after a set number of failures instead of applying escalating timed lockouts.","2FA requires two different kinds of credentials, such as a fingerprint and a PIN. It strengthens sign-in but doesn't impose escalating lockout timers.","Data Protection is an iOS feature that adds encryption, using a key derived from the user's credentials, to email and participating apps. It protects stored data, not the number of unlock attempts."],"optionRefs":["p. 675","p. 682","p. 680","p. 681"],"ref":"p. 675, Screen Locks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-04","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Why does the availability of OS updates vary more between Android devices than between iOS devices?","context":"Why does the availability of OS updates vary more between Android devices than between iOS devices?","options":["Each device vendor must adapt the patches for its own version of Android.","Android OS updates are only delivered through the Google Play Store app.","Each Android OS update must be installed over a tethered computer connection.","Android devices must be enrolled in MDM before they are offered OS updates."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With Android, updates depend on the device vendor, which must adapt patches for its specific Android version, so support for new OS versions varies. iOS runs on a consistent hardware and software platform, which allows efficient updates.","optionWhy":["Correct. Android updates depend on the device vendor, so update support differs between devices.","The Google Play Store handles app updates. Android OS updates arrive via the notification bar and under Settings > System > Advanced > System updates.","A tethered connection to a computer is mentioned as typical for jailbreaking iOS, not for Android updates. Android updates are delivered over the air via the notification bar or Settings.","MDM applies enterprise security policies to devices. The guide does not make MDM enrollment a requirement for receiving Android OS updates; updates come from the vendor."],"optionRefs":["p. 676","p. 676","pp. 676, 693","pp. 676, 679"],"ref":"p. 676, Patching/OS Updates","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-05","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A company is choosing a mobile antivirus/anti-malware (endpoint security) app for staff phones. Which function can it typically provide?","context":"A company is choosing a mobile antivirus/anti-malware (endpoint security) app for staff phones. Which function can it typically provide?","options":["Filtering content to block known phishing sites and adware or spyware activity","Creating a local VPN interface so that app traffic can be firewalled without root","Removing corporate accounts and files while leaving the user's personal data intact","Encrypting email data with a key that is derived from the user's own credentials"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mobile endpoint security apps often act as content filters that block known phishing sites and adware/spyware activity. They also detect configuration errors and monitor app permissions and usage, and many offer backup and device-location services.","optionWhy":["Correct. These apps commonly act as content filters and also monitor app permissions and configuration errors.","This describes \"no-root\" firewall apps, which create a local VPN interface to manage app access because full firewalls need root permissions.","This is an enterprise wipe, which can be performed on devices enrolled in MDM. It isn't a function of antivirus apps.","This describes iOS Data Protection, which adds encryption to email and participating apps. It's an OS encryption feature, not an antivirus function."],"optionRefs":["p. 676","p. 678","p. 685","p. 681"],"ref":"p. 676, Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-06","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A technician wants to stop certain apps on an unrooted Android phone from connecting to specific IP addresses. A full firewall app would need root permissions. How do \"no-root\" firewall apps solve this?","context":"A technician wants to stop certain apps on an unrooted Android phone from connecting to specific IP addresses. A full firewall app would need root permissions. How do \"no-root\" firewall apps solve this?","options":["They create a local VPN interface through which each app's access is managed.","They boot the phone into Safe Mode so that third-party apps cannot connect.","They enable developer mode to gain control over other apps' network settings.","They install custom firmware that grants the firewall app root-level control."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Firewall apps need higher (root) permissions to control other apps. \"No-root\" firewalls avoid this by creating a local virtual private network (VPN) interface that they use to manage each app's access.","optionWhy":["Correct. A local VPN interface lets the firewall control app traffic without root privileges.","Safe Mode is an Android troubleshooting boot mode that disables all third-party apps while core services keep running. It isn't a way of filtering selected apps' connections.","Developer mode grants advanced configuration settings and diagnostic data for app development. It should not be enabled routinely, and it isn't how no-root firewalls work.","Installing custom firmware (a custom ROM) is a way of rooting the device, which is exactly what a no-root firewall avoids. Rooting can also disable security features."],"optionRefs":["p. 678","p. 687","p. 694","p. 693"],"ref":"p. 678, Firewall Apps","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-07","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A logistics firm buys and owns its drivers' smartphones. Drivers may also use them for personal email and social media, as long as they follow the acceptable use policy. Which deployment model is this?","context":"A logistics firm buys and owns its drivers' smartphones. Drivers may also use them for personal email and social media, as long as they follow the acceptable use policy. Which deployment model is this?","options":["COPE","COBO","BYOD","CYOD"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Corporate Owned, Personally Enabled (COPE) means the company provides and owns the device but lets employees use it for personal activities such as email and social media, following acceptable use policies.","optionWhy":["Correct. The device is corporate owned, and personal use is enabled under acceptable use policies.","Corporate Owned, Business Only: the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes, so personal social media use wouldn't be allowed.","Bring Your Own Device: employees use their own devices, which must meet company specifications. Here the company owns the phones.","Choose Your Own Device is similar to COPE, but employees choose their device from a list of approved models. The scenario doesn't say drivers chose their phones."],"optionRefs":["p. 678","p. 678","p. 678","p. 678"],"ref":"p. 678, Enterprise Mobility Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-08","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"IT needs to push new Wi-Fi and VPN settings to 200 enrolled drivers' phones across the country, and later update or remove those settings without handling the devices. What is the most appropriate tool?","context":"IT needs to push new Wi-Fi and VPN settings to 200 enrolled drivers' phones across the country, and later update or remove those settings without handling the devices. What is the most appropriate tool?","options":["MDM configuration profiles","A remote backup application","A locator (find-my-phone) app","A no-root firewall app"],"answer":0,"explanation":"MDM configuration profiles manage device settings such as Wi-Fi and VPN, and they facilitate remote management by letting administrators update or remove profiles without physical access to the device.","optionWhy":["Correct. Configuration profiles configure Wi-Fi and VPN settings and can be updated or removed remotely.","Remote backup apps (iCloud, Google Sync, OneDrive, Dropbox) back up data, apps, and settings to the cloud. They don't push new network settings to a fleet.","Locator apps track, lock, or message lost or stolen devices. They aren't used to deploy network configuration.","A no-root firewall monitors app activity and blocks connections to specific ports or IP addresses. It doesn't distribute Wi-Fi or VPN settings to other devices."],"optionRefs":["p. 679","p. 682","p. 684","p. 678"],"ref":"p. 679, Mobile Device Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-09","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"Drivers' phones have just been enrolled in the company's MDM. What can IT now do with them?","context":"Drivers' phones have just been enrolled in the company's MDM. What can IT now do with them?","options":["Allowing or restricting apps, corporate data, and functions like the camera","Rolling a device back to its previous OS version after a poorly performing update","Keeping the antivirus pattern databases current against every new mobile threat","Letting GPS calculate the device's position indoors without using Wi-Fi or Bluetooth"],"answer":0,"explanation":"When a device is enrolled, MDM can apply policies that allow or restrict the use of apps, corporate data, and built-in functions such as the video camera or microphone. It also logs device use on the network and decides whether to allow it to connect.","optionWhy":["Correct. MDM policies can allow or restrict apps, corporate data, and built-in functions like the camera or microphone.","The guide notes that rollback options for OS updates are typically unavailable. The usual recourse is to report the issue and wait for a vendor fix.","The guide says the evolving nature of mobile threats makes pattern databases and heuristics hard to maintain effectively. MDM is not described as solving this.","The guide describes location services that combine GPS with cellular towers, Wi-Fi access points, and Bluetooth/RFID beacons, so GPS alone is not the only source."],"optionRefs":["p. 679","p. 688","p. 676","p. 684"],"ref":"p. 679, Mobile Device Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-10","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A dispatcher signs in to the corporate workspace on a new phone. After her fingerprint is accepted, a text message containing a code arrives on her registered phone number, and she must enter it to continue. What is happening?","context":"A dispatcher signs in to the corporate workspace on a new phone. After her fingerprint is accepted, a text message containing a code arrives on her registered phone number, and she must enter it to continue. What is happening?","options":["Two-factor authentication using a one-time code sent to a trusted device","A configuration profile pushing the workspace's VPN settings to the phone","The failed login attempts restriction responding to a mistyped fingerprint","Data Protection deriving a new encryption key from the user's credentials"],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is two-factor authentication. After normal authentication (here a fingerprint), a notification is sent to the trusted authenticator app or device, often containing a one-time password code, which confirms the sign-in attempt is legitimate.","optionWhy":["Correct. 2FA uses a second credential, such as a one-time password sent to a registered phone, after normal authentication.","Configuration profiles configure settings like Wi-Fi and VPN. They don't ask the user to type a code sent by text message.","That restriction locks the device for escalating periods after incorrect unlock attempts. Here, the fingerprint was accepted, and a code was sent rather than a lockout applied.","iOS Data Protection derives an encryption key from the user's credentials automatically in the background. It doesn't send codes to a phone number for the user to type in."],"optionRefs":["pp. 680–681","p. 679","p. 675","p. 681"],"ref":"pp. 680–681, Two-factor Authentication","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-11","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"You find that an Android 13 phone has no screen lock configured. What must you do so that the user data on it is encrypted by default?","context":"You find that an Android 13 phone has no screen lock configured. What must you do so that the user data on it is encrypted by default?","options":["Configure a secure screen lock so that file-level encryption is applied.","Turn on full disk encryption, which is the default from Android 10 onward.","Enable Data Protection under the Touch ID & Passcode settings menu.","Install a no-root firewall app so that its VPN encrypts stored files."],"answer":0,"explanation":"As of Android 10, full disk encryption isn't used because of performance concerns. Instead, user data is encrypted at the file level by default when a secure screen lock is configured, so setting a lock is what enables encryption.","optionWhy":["Correct. On Android 10 and later, user data is encrypted at the file level by default once a secure screen lock is configured.","This is the reverse of the guide: as of Android 10, full disk encryption is not used because of performance concerns. File-level encryption is used instead.","Data Protection and the Touch ID & Passcode menu are iOS features. They don't exist on an Android phone.","A no-root firewall's local VPN interface is used to control apps' network access. It doesn't encrypt data stored on the device."],"optionRefs":["p. 681","p. 681","pp. 681–682","p. 678"],"ref":"p. 681, Device Encryption","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-12","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A manager asks how data on company iPhones is protected. Which statement about iOS encryption is accurate?","context":"A manager asks how data on company iPhones is protected. Which statement about iOS encryption is accurate?","options":["Data Protection encryption is enabled automatically once a passcode lock is set.","Data Protection must be switched on manually after the device enrolls in MDM.","Data Protection adds credential-based encryption to all contacts and SMS messages.","The default encryption key is held in iCloud so the data can be wiped remotely."],"answer":0,"explanation":"On iOS, all user data is encrypted by default with a key stored on the device, so data can be wiped quickly by deleting the key. Data Protection adds extra encryption for email and participating apps and is enabled automatically when a passcode lock is set. Not all data, such as contacts and SMS messages, is covered by Data Protection.","optionWhy":["Correct. Setting a passcode lock automatically enables Data Protection on iOS.","No manual MDM step is described. Data Protection is enabled automatically when a passcode lock is configured.","The guide specifically says not all data, such as contacts and SMS messages, is encrypted with Data Protection. It covers email and apps that use Data Protection.","The default encryption key is stored on the device. Deleting that on-device key is what allows quick data wiping."],"optionRefs":["p. 681","p. 681","p. 681","p. 681"],"ref":"p. 681, Device Encryption","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-13","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A forklift tablet has gone missing inside a large warehouse, and its location can't be found using GPS alone. Why are high-accuracy location services more likely to find it?","context":"A forklift tablet has gone missing inside a large warehouse, and its location can't be found using GPS alone. Why are high-accuracy location services more likely to find it?","options":["They add data from Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth beacons to the GPS data.","They use NFC to locate the device from the nearest contactless card reader.","They use AirDrop to share the device's position with nearby Apple devices.","They move the device's Wi-Fi radio to the 5 GHz band to extend its range."],"answer":0,"explanation":"GPS requires line-of-sight to satellites and doesn't work indoors. High-accuracy location services combine GPS with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth beacons to calculate the device's location.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the location service combines GPS with cellular towers, Wi-Fi access points, and Bluetooth/RFID beacons, so a device can be located in a building where GPS is weak.","NFC is described in the context of contactless payments, which need the device to be held close to a reader. It isn't used by location services.","AirDrop is an iOS feature for transferring files between iOS and macOS devices. It isn't a location service.","The guide mentions 5 GHz only in the context of network compatibility (some devices only support 2.4 GHz). Changing bands isn't how location accuracy is improved."],"optionRefs":["p. 684","p. 692","p. 692","p. 692"],"ref":"p. 684, Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-14","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"A driver believes he left his company phone in a taxi an hour ago, and the taxi firm is checking its cars. What is the best immediate action while recovery is still possible?","context":"A driver believes he left his company phone in a taxi an hour ago, and the taxi firm is checking its cars. What is the best immediate action while recovery is still possible?","options":["Use the locator app to lock the phone and display a return message.","Perform a remote wipe at once to return the phone to factory settings.","Perform an enterprise wipe so only the corporate accounts are removed.","Change the account password and wait 72 hours before taking action."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Locator apps can remotely lock the device and display a \"Please return\" message, among other features. A remote wipe is meant for a device that is irretrievably lost, so it's premature while the phone may still be recovered.","optionWhy":["Correct. Locator apps can remotely lock a device and show a \"Please return\" message while it is tracked.","A remote wipe performs a factory reset and is intended for devices that are irretrievably lost. Here the phone may still be recovered, so locking it is the proportionate step.","An enterprise wipe removes corporate accounts and files from an MDM-enrolled device but leaves personal data. It doesn't secure or help recover the device itself.","The 72-hour note concerns factory resets: avoid resetting within 72 hours of changing the account password. It isn't advice for handling a lost phone."],"optionRefs":["p. 684","p. 684","p. 685","p. 687"],"ref":"p. 684, Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-15","module":20,"lesson":"20A Mobile OS Security","question":"An employee is leaving the company and keeping her personal phone, which was enrolled in MDM under the BYOD policy. IT must remove all corporate data without touching her photos or personal apps. Which action fits?","context":"An employee is leaving the company and keeping her personal phone, which was enrolled in MDM under the BYOD policy. IT must remove all corporate data without touching her photos or personal apps. Which action fits?","options":["Enterprise wipe","Remote wipe","Factory reset","Force-stop work apps"],"answer":0,"explanation":"For MDM-enrolled devices, an enterprise wipe removes corporate accounts and files while leaving personal apps, accounts, settings, and files intact.","optionWhy":["Correct. An enterprise wipe protects corporate data without affecting personal information.","A remote wipe performs a factory reset, clearing all data, apps, and settings, including her personal photos and apps.","A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings. That would destroy her personal data along with corporate data.","Force stopping closes a misbehaving app (Settings > Apps > Force Stop on Android). It stops the app from running but doesn't remove any corporate accounts or files."],"optionRefs":["p. 685","p. 684","p. 687","p. 689"],"ref":"pp. 684–685, Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-16","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"What does powering a mobile device off and on achieve that leaving it in sleep mode does not?","context":"What does powering a mobile device off and on achieve that leaving it in sleep mode does not?","options":["It closes all apps and clears RAM but keeps stored data and settings.","It disables third-party apps while leaving the core services running.","It erases user data, apps, and settings to return to a default state.","It clears each app's cache so that corrupted cached files are removed."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Unlike sleep mode, powering off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for unresponsive systems and should be one of the first steps for malfunctioning apps or slow performance.","optionWhy":["Correct. A reboot clears RAM and closes all apps, but stored data and settings are unaffected.","This describes booting an Android device into Safe Mode, which is a troubleshooting mode rather than a normal restart.","This describes a factory reset. A reboot does not affect stored data or settings.","Clearing an app's cache is a separate step (on Android, Clear Cache under App info). The guide describes a reboot as clearing RAM, not app caches."],"optionRefs":["p. 687","p. 687","p. 687","pp. 687, 690"],"ref":"p. 687, Reboot","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-17","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A driver's Android phone has become unstable since he installed several new apps, but it's unclear which one is responsible. Which step best helps confirm whether a third-party app is the cause?","context":"A driver's Android phone has become unstable since he installed several new apps, but it's unclear which one is responsible. Which step best helps confirm whether a third-party app is the cause?","options":["Boot it into Safe Mode","Enable developer mode","Perform a factory reset","Clear the Play Store cache"],"answer":0,"explanation":"On Android, booting into Safe Mode disables third-party apps while keeping core services running. If the problem disappears in Safe Mode, a third-party app is the likely cause.","optionWhy":["Correct. Safe Mode disables third-party apps while core services run, which isolates whether an app is to blame.","Developer mode exposes advanced configuration and diagnostic data for app development. It shouldn't be enabled routinely because it can be exploited to install unauthorized apps.","A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings. The guide treats it as the step to consider when problems persist after other troubleshooting, not as a diagnostic step.","Clearing the Google Play Store cache is a step for apps that fail to install. It doesn't help identify which installed app causes instability."],"optionRefs":["p. 687","p. 694","pp. 687–688","p. 691"],"ref":"p. 687, Reboot","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-18","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A user changed his Google account password this morning and now wants to factory reset his Android phone this afternoon. What should you advise?","context":"A user changed his Google account password this morning and now wants to factory reset his Android phone this afternoon. What should you advise?","options":["Wait, as resetting within 72 hours of a password change should be avoided.","Reset immediately so that the new password is applied to the fresh install.","Unplug the phone from power so that the reset can't be interrupted midway.","Enable developer mode first so that the sign-in after the reset is skipped."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide notes that you may need to sign in immediately after a factory reset to prevent unauthorized access, so you need your account credentials and should avoid resetting within 72 hours of changing the account password. It also advises making sure the device is fully charged or connected to power.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide advises avoiding a reset within 72 hours of changing the account password.","The guide recommends the opposite: don't reset within 72 hours of a password change.","The guide says to ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before a reset, not unplugged.","Developer mode is for app development and diagnostics. The guide doesn't describe it as a way to skip sign-in, and the sign-in exists to prevent unauthorized access."],"optionRefs":["p. 687","p. 687","p. 687","pp. 687, 694"],"ref":"p. 687, Factory Reset","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-19","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A sales rep says her phone battery drains much faster than it did last month. Which step should you take first?","context":"A sales rep says her phone battery drains much faster than it did last month. Which step should you take first?","options":["Check which apps use the most power and limit unneeded background activity.","Perform a factory reset so that the battery's charge levels are recalibrated.","Switch the phone from Wi-Fi to mobile data to reduce the load on the radios.","Install a no-root firewall app to block all of the apps' network connections."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so the guide advises checking which apps consume the most power and disabling unnecessary background processes. Other measures include adjusting brightness and timeout, limiting location services, and reviewing push notifications and syncing.","optionWhy":["Correct. Identifying power-hungry apps and disabling unnecessary background processes is the guide's first recommendation.","The guide doesn't describe a factory reset as fixing battery life. A reset erases data and is considered only if problems persist after troubleshooting.","The guide advises turning off Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, and mobile data when they aren't in use. Swapping one radio for another doesn't reduce radio use.","Firewall apps are security tools that control app connections to ports or IP addresses. The guide doesn't present them as a battery fix."],"optionRefs":["p. 687","pp. 687–688","p. 688","p. 678"],"ref":"pp. 687–688, Battery Life Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-20","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A tablet in a delivery van has started rebooting at random. What should you check first?","context":"A tablet in a delivery van has started rebooting at random. What should you check first?","options":["Battery health, to check for a hardware fault","Whether the OS and all of the apps are fully up to date","Whether a recently installed app is causing the reboots","Whether the tablet can roll back to the previous OS version"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Random reboots can be caused by overheating, low battery, or faulty hardware. The guide says to first check battery health to identify hardware faults. If hardware isn't the cause, check storage space and whether the OS and apps are up to date, then try to isolate a faulty app.","optionWhy":["Correct. Checking battery health first identifies hardware faults before software causes are investigated.","This is a valid step, but the guide places it after hardware issues have been ruled out.","Isolating and uninstalling a faulty app is also a valid step, but the guide lists it after hardware checks and storage/update checks.","Rollback options for OS updates are typically unavailable, and the guide doesn't list rollback as a step for random reboots."],"optionRefs":["p. 688","p. 688","p. 688","p. 688"],"ref":"p. 688, Device Randomly Reboots","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-21","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A driver's phone shows an OS update but won't install it. The phone is at 15% battery and is connected to a metered mobile hotspot. The model is supported. What should be done next?","context":"A driver's phone shows an OS update but won't install it. The phone is at 15% battery and is connected to a metered mobile hotspot. The model is supported. What should be done next?","options":["Connect it to power and Wi-Fi, since both conditions can block updates.","Clear the app store's cache and then retry the OS update installation.","Boot into Safe Mode so third-party apps cannot interfere with the update.","Perform a factory reset and then install the update on the clean system."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says to make sure the device is connected to a power source and Wi-Fi, because updates may be blocked by low battery or metered networks. Other steps include verifying compatibility and support lifecycle, restarting and retrying, and checking for free space.","optionWhy":["Correct. Low battery and metered networks can block updates, so power and Wi-Fi should be connected.","Clearing the store cache is a fix for apps that fail to install. It doesn't address an OS update blocked by battery level or a metered connection.","Safe Mode disables third-party apps to help isolate app problems. It isn't among the guide's steps for a failed OS update.","A factory reset erases all data. The guide's update steps don't include it, and the likely cause here (battery and metered network) has a simple fix."],"optionRefs":["p. 688","p. 691","pp. 687–688","pp. 687–688"],"ref":"p. 688, OS Fails to Update","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-22","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"After a major OS update, an older phone has become noticeably slower. What can you do about it?","context":"After a major OS update, an older phone has become noticeably slower. What can you do about it?","options":["Rollback is usually unavailable, so report it and wait for a vendor fix.","Roll back the OS using the Software Update page in the Settings app.","Perform a factory reset, which reinstalls the device's original OS.","Sideload the previous OS version onto the device as an .APK file."],"answer":0,"explanation":"OS updates can significantly affect performance on older models. Rollback options are typically unavailable, so reporting the issue and awaiting a vendor fix is often the only recourse.","optionWhy":["Correct. Since rollback is typically unavailable, reporting the problem and waiting for a fix is often the only option.","Software Update is where iOS updates are delivered, not rolled back. The guide states rollback options are typically unavailable.","A factory reset erases user data, apps, and settings. The guide doesn't say it returns the device to an earlier OS version.","The .APK format is used for sideloading Android apps from third-party sources, not OS versions, and enabling unknown sources weakens security."],"optionRefs":["p. 688","pp. 676, 688","p. 687","p. 694"],"ref":"p. 688, Device Is Slow to Respond (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-23","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"Hardware causes such as overheating and low battery have been ruled out, but a phone has felt sluggish since the user added a live-weather widget to the home screen. What is the most useful next step?","context":"Hardware causes such as overheating and low battery have been ruled out, but a phone has felt sluggish since the user added a live-weather widget to the home screen. What is the most useful next step?","options":["Check Battery settings to see which apps are using the most resources.","Enable developer mode so that more memory can be given to the widget.","Move the phone closer to the access point to speed up widget updates.","Clear the app store cache so that the widget's app is reinstalled."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says to consider recently installed apps, especially those running background tasks or showing real-time content in widgets. Battery settings show which apps use the most resources, or a third-party system monitor app can give detailed utilization information.","optionWhy":["Correct. Battery settings reveal resource-heavy apps, and widgets with real-time content are a known cause of slowdowns.","Developer mode offers advanced settings and diagnostics for app development. The guide doesn't describe it as a way to allocate memory, and it shouldn't be enabled routinely.","Moving closer to the access point is a fix for weak wireless signals. It doesn't address a device that's slow because of resource use.","Clearing the store cache helps when apps fail to install. It doesn't reinstall apps or reduce resource use."],"optionRefs":["p. 688","p. 694","p. 691","p. 691"],"ref":"p. 688, Device Is Slow to Respond","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-24","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A user reports that his phone's screen no longer rotates when he turns the phone sideways. What should you check first?","context":"A user reports that his phone's screen no longer rotates when he turns the phone sideways. What should you check first?","options":["Whether rotation lock is on in Control Center or the notification drawer","Whether the device needs to be sent for repair because of a hardware fault","Whether AssistiveTouch must be enabled so that the sensor can be reset","Whether a factory reset is needed to restore the default display settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Although failure to autorotate may be a hardware issue, the guide says to eliminate simple causes first. Step 1 is to check the notification drawer or Control Center to make sure rotation lock isn't enabled. Next, make sure the screen isn't being touched, and close single-orientation apps.","optionWhy":["Correct. Checking rotation lock is the first step in the guide's autorotate troubleshooting.","The guide acknowledges it may be hardware but says to eliminate simple causes, such as rotation lock, before concluding that.","AssistiveTouch, in iOS Accessibility settings, adds manual control of orientation. It doesn't reset or repair automatic rotation.","A factory reset erases all data and is a last resort. Simple checks like rotation lock come first."],"optionRefs":["p. 688","p. 688","p. 689","pp. 687–688"],"ref":"pp. 688–689, Screen Does Not Autorotate","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-25","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A shipment-tracking app on an Android phone keeps crashing. You've already force-stopped it, but it still crashes when reopened. What should you do next?","context":"A shipment-tracking app on an Android phone keeps crashing. You've already force-stopped it, but it still crashes when reopened. What should you do next?","options":["Clear the app's cache, reboot, and check the store for an app update.","Drag the app's icon to Remove on the home screen, then reinstall it.","Enable unknown sources and sideload an older version of the app.","Perform a factory reset, since the app's crashes continue to recur."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If issues persist after force stopping, the guide says to clear the app cache (Clear Cache under App info on Android), reboot the device, and check for app updates. If problems continue, uninstall and reinstall the app.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that if issues persist, clear the app cache, reboot the device, and check for app updates in the store.","The guide's app troubleshooting says to uninstall and reinstall the software or repair it. It does not describe dragging an icon to Remove as the uninstall method.","Sideloading requires enabling unknown sources, which weakens device security. It isn't part of the guide's app troubleshooting steps.","A factory reset erases everything and is considered only when problems persist after the other steps. Cache, reboot, update, and reinstall come first."],"optionRefs":["p. 690","p. 690","p. 694","pp. 688, 690"],"ref":"pp. 689–690, Troubleshoot App Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-26","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"An iPhone won't install an app from the App Store. The user has a stable connection and enough storage, has restarted the phone, and is running the latest iOS. What should you try next?","context":"An iPhone won't install an app from the App Store. The user has a stable connection and enough storage, has restarted the phone, and is running the latest iOS. What should you try next?","options":["Sign out and back into the Apple ID, then check the payment method.","Clear the Google Play Store cache under Settings > Apps > Google Play Store.","Jailbreak the device so that the app can be installed from outside the store.","Connect the iPhone to a PC running iTunes and perform a full factory reset."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For iOS app installation failures, after checking connectivity, storage, restarting, and updating iOS, the guide's next step is to sign out of and back into the Apple ID to refresh the session, and to make sure the payment method is valid.","optionWhy":["Correct. Refreshing the Apple ID session and checking the payment method is step 3 for iOS install failures.","This is an Android step for app installation failures. It doesn't apply to an iPhone.","Jailbreaking gains root privileges and allows sideloading, but it can leave security measures disabled. It's a security risk, not a troubleshooting step.","The guide mentions connecting to a PC with iTunes for iOS devices that don't update wirelessly. It doesn't recommend a factory reset for a single app install failure."],"optionRefs":["p. 691","p. 691","p. 693","p. 691"],"ref":"pp. 689–691, Troubleshoot App Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-27","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A driver's camera app works normally at home, but at one high-security depot the camera is always unavailable. The phone is enrolled in the company's MDM. What is the most likely explanation?","context":"A driver's camera app works normally at home, but at one high-security depot the camera is always unavailable. The phone is enrolled in the company's MDM. What is the most likely explanation?","options":["An MDM security policy disables the camera at that specific location.","The camera app's cache became corrupted when it was used at the depot.","The phone has been booted into Safe Mode while the driver was on site.","The camera app is in the suspended state until the device leaves the depot."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide notes that MDM software may restrict app functionality based on security policies, such as disabling the camera in certain locations. Configuration profiles can also apply selective policies, such as disabling cameras in high-risk areas.","optionWhy":["Correct. MDM can restrict functions like the camera in certain locations.","A corrupted cache would cause problems wherever the app is used, not only at one site. Clearing the cache is a general app fix.","Safe Mode disables third-party apps everywhere until the device restarts normally. It wouldn't selectively affect the camera at one location.","Suspended is a normal app state in which an app uses no resources. Opening the app brings it to the foreground, so this wouldn't block the camera at one site."],"optionRefs":["pp. 678, 691","p. 690","p. 687","p. 689"],"ref":"p. 691, Troubleshoot App Issues (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-28","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"An older handheld scanner with an 802.11n adapter can't join a new access point. The scanner's Wi-Fi is on, airplane mode is off, and the AP is configured as 802.11ac only. What will fix the problem?","context":"An older handheld scanner with an 802.11n adapter can't join a new access point. The scanner's Wi-Fi is on, airplane mode is off, and the AP is configured as 802.11ac only. What will fix the problem?","options":["Set the access point to compatibility mode so that 802.11n devices can join.","Remove the scanner's protective case and move it closer to the access point.","Forget the network on the scanner and then reconnect with the same password.","Install the latest OS update on the scanner and then reboot it to reconnect."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The access point must support the same 802.11 standard as the device. The guide's example is exactly this: a device with an 802.11n adapter can't connect to an AP set to 802.11ac only, so the AP should be put in compatibility mode.","optionWhy":["Correct. Compatibility mode lets the 802.11ac AP accept devices that use the 802.11n standard.","This improves weak signal strength caused by distance or obstruction. It can't fix a mismatch in 802.11 standards.","Forgetting and reconnecting fixes bad saved network settings. The AP would still reject the 802.11n adapter.","An OS update doesn't change the scanner's 802.11n wireless adapter, so it can't solve the standards mismatch."],"optionRefs":["p. 692","p. 691","p. 691","pp. 688, 692"],"ref":"pp. 691–692, Configuration Issues","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-29","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"A driver taps his phone on a fuel-station card reader, but the contactless payment doesn't go through. NFC is enabled for the wallet app and airplane mode is off. What should he try next?","context":"A driver taps his phone on a fuel-station card reader, but the contactless payment doesn't go through. NFC is enabled for the wallet app and airplane mode is off. What should he try next?","options":["Unlock the phone, then hold it closer to the reader for longer.","Pair the phone with the card reader through Bluetooth settings.","Set AirDrop to receive from everyone, then try paying again.","Switch Wi-Fi to the 2.4 GHz band so the reader can detect it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For NFC payment issues, the guide says to make sure the device is unlocked to authorize the payment, that NFC is enabled for the wallet app, and that airplane mode is off. If problems persist, hold the device closer to the reader and keep contact for longer.","optionWhy":["Correct. The device must be unlocked to authorize payment, and holding it closer for longer helps the NFC connection.","Bluetooth pairing is how Bluetooth devices are connected. The guide's NFC steps don't include pairing; they focus on unlocking the device, enabling NFC for the wallet app, turning off airplane mode, and holding the phone close.","AirDrop is an iOS file-transfer feature. Its receiving setting has no effect on NFC payments.","Wi-Fi frequency bands affect connections to access points. NFC payments don't use Wi-Fi."],"optionRefs":["p. 692","pp. 691–692","p. 692","p. 692"],"ref":"p. 692, Troubleshooting NFC","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-30","module":20,"lesson":"20B Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Software","question":"An iPhone user is trying to receive a delivery photo via AirDrop from a coworker who isn't in her contacts list. The two are standing next to each other. What setting should she check?","context":"An iPhone user is trying to receive a delivery photo via AirDrop from a coworker who isn't in her contacts list. The two are standing next to each other. What setting should she check?","options":["Set AirDrop to receive files from everyone.","Turn on Nearby Share in the Google settings.","Enable NFC for the iPhone's wallet app.","Switch off rotation lock in Control Center."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For AirDrop to work, the sender must be in the recipient's contacts, or AirDrop must be set to receive files from everyone. AirDrop is configured under Settings > General > AirDrop, and the devices must be within Bluetooth range.","optionWhy":["Correct. If the sender isn't in her contacts, AirDrop must be set to receive from everyone.","Nearby Share is Android's similar feature, found under Settings > Google > Devices. It doesn't apply to an iPhone.","NFC for the wallet app is needed for contactless payments. AirDrop uses Bluetooth, Wi-Fi, or cellular connections instead.","Rotation lock controls whether the screen autorotates. It has no effect on receiving files."],"optionRefs":["p. 692","p. 692","p. 692","p. 688"],"ref":"p. 692, Troubleshooting AirDrop","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-31","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"How is jailbreaking an iOS device typically carried out?","context":"How is jailbreaking an iOS device typically carried out?","options":["By booting with a patched kernel, often over a tethered connection","By switching on the Install unknown apps toggle for a chosen app","By enabling developer mode from the Settings app on the device","By using a root method that the device vendor has authorized"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Jailbreaking, which gains root privileges on iOS, is typically done by booting the device with a patched kernel, often requiring a tethered connection to a computer. It allows sideloading apps, changing carriers, and customizing the interface, but can leave security measures disabled.","optionWhy":["Correct. A patched kernel boot, often tethered to a computer, is the typical jailbreak method.","The Install unknown apps toggle is an Android setting that allows sideloading from that app. It doesn't exist for jailbreaking iOS.","Developer mode grants access to advanced configuration settings and diagnostic data, and the guide says it doesn't inherently weaken security. Jailbreaking is described separately as gaining root privileges.","Some vendors offer authorized root methods, but the guide says this about Android rooting. iOS is described as more restrictive."],"optionRefs":["p. 693","pp. 694–695","p. 694","p. 693"],"ref":"p. 693, Root Access Security Concerns","difficulty":"Knowledge","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-32","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"Security discovers that a driver has installed an unsigned custom ROM on his company-enrolled Android phone. What can the company's MDM solution do in response?","context":"Security discovers that a driver has installed an unsigned custom ROM on his company-enrolled Android phone. What can the company's MDM solution do in response?","options":["Detect the device and block its access to enterprise apps and networks.","Re-sign the custom firmware so that it passes the signature validation.","Perform an enterprise wipe, which also removes the custom ROM from it.","Switch the phone into developer mode to restore the security controls."],"answer":0,"explanation":"MDM solutions can detect rooted or jailbroken devices and prevent them from accessing enterprise apps, networks, or workspaces. They can also block devices with custom firmware that lacks valid signatures.","optionWhy":["Correct. MDM can detect rooted devices or unsigned custom firmware and block access to enterprise resources.","The guide describes MDM as blocking firmware that lacks valid signatures, not adding signatures to it.","An enterprise wipe removes corporate accounts and files only, leaving everything else intact. It wouldn't remove custom firmware.","Developer mode is for app development and is something MDM can block. It doesn't restore security features disabled by rooting."],"optionRefs":["p. 694","p. 694","p. 685","p. 694"],"ref":"pp. 693–694, Root Access Security Concerns","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-33","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"A trainee developer leaves developer mode switched on permanently on his work phone. What should you tell him?","context":"A trainee developer leaves developer mode switched on permanently on his work phone. What should you tell him?","options":["It doesn't inherently weaken security but should be used only for app development.","It is the same as rooting because it gives the user full administrative control.","It must be turned on before MDM can push apps from Managed Google Play to devices.","It is enabled by default on new devices, and MDM software is unable to detect it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Developer mode grants access to advanced configuration settings and diagnostic data. It doesn't inherently weaken security, but it should be used only for app development and not enabled routinely, because it can be exploited to install unauthorized apps. MDM can often block devices with developer mode enabled.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says developer mode isn't inherently a weakness but shouldn't be enabled routinely.","Rooting gains administrative control and can disable security features. Developer mode only grants advanced settings and diagnostics, and the guide treats them separately.","Managed Google Play lets MDM push private apps directly to devices. The guide doesn't link this to developer mode.","The guide says MDM systems can often be configured to block devices that have developer mode enabled."],"optionRefs":["p. 694","pp. 693–694","p. 694","p. 694"],"ref":"pp. 693–694, Root Access Security Concerns","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-34","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"The company has developed a private shipment-scanning app for its corporate iPhones and doesn't want it listed publicly in the App Store. How should it be distributed?","context":"The company has developed a private shipment-scanning app for its corporate iPhones and doesn't want it listed publicly in the App Store. How should it be distributed?","options":["Via Apple Business Manager, with MDM pushing the app to devices","By sideloading the app's .APK file onto each of the iPhones","By jailbreaking the iPhones so the app can be installed directly","By publishing it through Managed Google Play for the company"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Apple addresses private corporate app distribution with enterprise developer and distribution programs via Apple Business Manager. These let MDM suites push apps from private channels directly to devices.","optionWhy":["Correct. Apple Business Manager supports private enterprise distribution, and MDM can push the app to devices.","The .APK format and sideloading from third-party sources are Android concepts. Unlike Android, iOS doesn't let users install from other stores in this way.","Jailbreaking allows sideloading but can leave security measures disabled, and MDM can block jailbroken devices.","Managed Google Play is Google's private app distribution option for Android devices, not iPhones."],"optionRefs":["p. 694","p. 694","pp. 693–694","p. 694"],"ref":"p. 694, Enterprise Apps and APK Sideloading","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-35","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"A warehouse supervisor wants to enable \"unknown sources\" on drivers' Android phones so they can install a handy third-party tool. What should you advise?","context":"A warehouse supervisor wants to enable \"unknown sources\" on drivers' Android phones so they can install a handy third-party tool. What should you advise?","options":["Enabling unknown sources weakens security, and MDM can block sideloading.","The device must be rooted before an .APK file can be installed manually.","Sideloaded apps are signed by the Play Store, so they're always trusted.","iOS supports .APK sideloading once Apple Business Manager is configured."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Android lets users install apps from third-party sources in .APK format if this is enabled. Enabling unknown sources weakens device security, so only legitimate enterprise apps should be sideloaded, and MDM can be configured to prevent sideloading or third-party stores.","optionWhy":["Correct. Sideloading weakens security and can be prevented by MDM policy.","Android allows installing from third-party sources if enabled, through the Install unknown apps toggle. Rooting isn't described as a requirement.","Trusted stores authenticate developers and sign apps. Sideloaded apps come from outside those stores, which is why they carry a risk.","Apple Business Manager distributes enterprise iOS apps through MDM. The .APK format is used on Android."],"optionRefs":["p. 694","pp. 694–695","p. 694","p. 694"],"ref":"pp. 694–695, Enterprise Apps and APK Sideloading","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-36","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"A user installs a VPN app from the official store. Its name is nearly identical to a well-known VPN, it has hundreds of glowing reviews, and it immediately asks for access to contacts and SMS messages. What is this most likely an example of?","context":"A user installs a VPN app from the official store. Its name is nearly identical to a well-known VPN, it has hundreds of glowing reviews, and it immediately asks for access to contacts and SMS messages. What is this most likely an example of?","options":["App spoofing","A bootleg app store","APK sideloading","Scareware"],"answer":0,"explanation":"App spoofing is when rogue developers publish malicious apps disguised as legitimate ones, often with similar names and fake reviews. VPNs are a common target. Even in an approved store, users should be cautious when an app requests unrelated permissions.","optionWhy":["Correct. A look-alike name, fake reviews, and unrelated permission requests from an official store are signs of app spoofing.","Bootleg app stores are outside the official stores and tempt users to enable unknown sources. This app came from the official store.","Sideloading is installing apps from third-party sources using .APK files. This app was installed from the official store.","Scareware uses fake security warnings to trick users into installing apps or granting permissions. The scenario describes no fake warning."],"optionRefs":["p. 694","p. 695","p. 694","p. 696"],"ref":"p. 694, App Spoofing","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-37","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"Which of these observations is the strongest sign of tracking or spyware activity, rather than ordinary ad-supported software?","context":"Which of these observations is the strongest sign of tracking or spyware activity, rather than ordinary ad-supported software?","options":["Persistent pop-up ads begin opening in the browser on their own","A free puzzle game shows a video advert between each of its levels","The notification bar shows that a system update is ready to install","A map app asks for location permission when its map is first opened"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Free apps are usually supported by advertising, so lots of ads isn't always a sign of a malicious app. However, ads that appear unexpectedly, display in the browser, open persistent pop-ups, or show a high level of unauthorized personalization could suggest tracking or spyware.","optionWhy":["Correct. Unexpected ads in the browser and persistent pop-ups are indicators of tracking or spyware.","Free apps are typically supported by advertising revenue, so in-app ads like this are expected and not a sign of malware by themselves.","Android delivers OS updates through the notification bar. This is normal behavior.","The guide warns about apps requesting unrelated permissions. Location access is related to a map's purpose."],"optionRefs":["p. 696","p. 696","p. 676","p. 694"],"ref":"p. 696, Mobile Security Symptoms","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-38","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"A phone's browser shows frequent certificate warnings, and searches keep landing on look-alike sites instead of the real ones. Wi-Fi signal is strong and the device is connected. What is the most likely cause?","context":"A phone's browser shows frequent certificate warnings, and searches keep landing on look-alike sites instead of the real ones. Wi-Fi signal is strong and the device is connected. What is the most likely cause?","options":["Malware has altered DNS or search settings to redirect traffic","The access point is using an 802.11 standard the phone lacks","The battery is low, which is reducing the radio's signal power","The connection is metered, so the OS is limiting network access"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says malware may corrupt DNS settings or search providers to perform redirection attacks, leading users to spoofed sites. This can disrupt access to legitimate sites, trigger certificate warnings, and slow network performance.","optionWhy":["Correct. Corrupted DNS or search provider settings cause redirection to spoofed sites and certificate warnings.","A standards mismatch prevents the device from connecting at all. Here, the phone is connected but redirected.","Low battery can reduce signal strength, but the signal here is strong, and weak signal wouldn't cause redirects or certificate warnings.","The guide mentions metered networks only as something that can block OS updates. They don't redirect browsing to spoofed sites."],"optionRefs":["p. 696","p. 692","p. 691","p. 688"],"ref":"p. 696, Mobile Security Symptoms","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-39","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"Confidential customer shipment data from the company has turned up for sale on an online forum. Several staff phones had access to that data. What should be done?","context":"Confidential customer shipment data from the company has turned up for sale on an online forum. Several staff phones had access to that data. What should be done?","options":["Quarantine all potential source devices and investigate the breach.","Remotely wipe every staff phone so that no further data can leak.","Disable geotagging on staff phones so that photos reveal no location.","Enable Data Protection on every iPhone so the data becomes encrypted."],"answer":0,"explanation":"If data leakage occurs, all potential source devices must be quarantined and investigated to identify the origin of the breach.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says all potential source devices must be quarantined and investigated.","A remote wipe is intended for devices that are irretrievably lost. For data leakage, the guide's response is to quarantine and investigate all potential source devices, not wipe them.","Removing geotags protects against location exposure from photos posted online. It doesn't address leaked shipment data.","Data Protection is enabled automatically with a passcode and protects data on a device. It doesn't identify where already-leaked data came from."],"optionRefs":["p. 696","pp. 684, 696","p. 697","p. 681"],"ref":"p. 696, Leaked Personal Files/Data","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M20-40","module":20,"lesson":"20C Troubleshoot Mobile OS and App Security","question":"You're writing staff guidance on posting photos to social media. Why should users remove geotagging information from photos first?","context":"You're writing staff guidance on posting photos to social media. Why should users remove geotagging information from photos first?","options":["Criminals can use it to find where people live and when they're away.","Geotagged photos are blocked from uploading to cloud backup services.","Geotagging data allows malware to redirect the phone's DNS requests.","Geotags disable the device's GPS and so prevent locator apps working."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Criminals can obtain location information from geotagged online photos. They can work out where someone lives and identify vacation times from social media posts, so users should strip geotags before posting.","optionWhy":["Correct. Geotagged photos can reveal home locations and vacation times to criminals.","The guide doesn't say this. Remote backup services back up data, apps, and settings, and geotags aren't mentioned as blocking them.","DNS redirection comes from malware corrupting DNS settings or search providers, not from photo metadata.","Geotags are information stored in images. They don't affect GPS hardware or locator apps."],"optionRefs":["p. 697","pp. 682, 697","p. 696","pp. 684, 697"],"ref":"p. 697, Leaked Personal Files/Data (note)","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-01","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A user overwrote an important spreadsheet yesterday. File History has been running on her PC for months. What is the quickest way to get yesterday's version of that one file back?","context":"A user overwrote an important spreadsheet yesterday. File History has been running on her PC for months. What is the quickest way to get yesterday's version of that one file back?","options":["Use the Previous Versions tab in the file's Properties dialog to restore it.","Restore the latest system image through the Backup and Restore Center tool.","Run a copy backup now so the archive attribute exposes the older version.","Point File History at a new network folder target and run the job again."],"answer":0,"explanation":"For a single file or folder, the Previous Versions tab in the object's Properties dialog restores an earlier copy. The File History applet is used when several files need restoring; a system image restores the whole OS, which is far more than this job needs.","optionWhy":["Correct. The Previous Versions tab in an object's Properties dialog restores an earlier copy of that file or folder.","Backup and Restore Center can make image backups of the entire OS. Restoring an image would roll back the whole system just to recover one spreadsheet.","A copy backup is an ad hoc job made outside the chain that does not affect the archive attribute. It saves the current file; it does not bring back an older one.","Changing the target only changes where future backups are stored. Running the job again would copy the current, overwritten file, not restore yesterday's."],"optionRefs":["p. 702","p. 702","p. 703","p. 701"],"ref":"p. 702, Backup Operations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-02","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"Engineers at a design firm say that losing even half a day of CAD edits would be a disaster, because the changes are very hard to redo. The server is backed up once a night. Which backup setting needs to change?","context":"Engineers at a design firm say that losing even half a day of CAD edits would be a disaster, because the changes are very hard to redo. The server is backed up once a night. Which backup setting needs to change?","options":["Raise the backup frequency so that jobs run every hour or more often.","Lengthen the data retention period so each backup job is kept longer.","Switch the chain from incremental jobs to nightly differential jobs.","Replace the nightly job with weekly full backups on separate media."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Frequency is the period between backup jobs and reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. When edits are hard to reconstruct, frequency may need to be measured in hours, minutes, or seconds rather than daily. Retention is how long each job is kept, which does not reduce the work lost between jobs.","optionWhy":["Correct. Frequency reflects how much lost work can be tolerated; hard-to-reconstruct edits call for jobs measured in hours or less.","Retention is how long a given job is kept. Keeping nightly jobs longer still leaves up to a full day of edits unprotected between jobs.","Differential versus incremental changes how much data each job selects and how complex recovery is, not how often jobs run. Nightly differentials still lose up to a day.","Weekly full jobs would make the gap between backups even longer, so far more work could be lost, not less."],"optionRefs":["p. 702","p. 702","p. 702","p. 702"],"ref":"p. 702, Backup Methods","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-03","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A backup ran Monday. On Tuesday a document was infected with a virus, and Tuesday's backup then overwrote Monday's copy of it. Now no clean copy exists. What was missing from the backup policy?","context":"A backup ran Monday. On Tuesday a document was infected with a virus, and Tuesday's backup then overwrote Monday's copy of it. Now no clean copy exists. What was missing from the backup policy?","options":["Short-term retention that keeps earlier versions instead of overwriting.","A higher frequency so that the backup job runs several times each day.","A long-term retention rule that keeps yearly copies for legal reasons.","A copy backup taken outside the chain so the archive attribute is kept."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Short-term retention keeps several versions of backed-up data so an earlier, uninfected copy survives. The guide uses exactly this Monday/Tuesday virus scenario to show why short-term retention matters for version control and malware recovery.","optionWhy":["Correct. Short-term retention supports version control and recovery from malware by keeping older copies instead of overwriting them.","Running jobs more often would back up the infected file sooner and overwrite the clean copy even faster if versions are not retained.","Long-term retention exists to meet legal, policy, or industry requirements. Yearly copies would not provide Monday's version of a file edited this week.","Copy backups are ad hoc jobs that do not affect the archive attribute. They are not a scheduled way to keep each day's version."],"optionRefs":["p. 702","p. 702","p. 702","p. 703"],"ref":"p. 702, Backup Methods","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-04","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A file server uses a full backup every Sunday night and a differential backup on each weeknight. The server's disk fails on Thursday morning. Which backup jobs are needed to restore the most recent data?","context":"A file server uses a full backup every Sunday night and a differential backup on each weeknight. The server's disk fails on Thursday morning. Which backup jobs are needed to restore the most recent data?","options":["Sunday's full backup and the differential job from Wednesday night.","Sunday's full backup plus every differential from Monday to Wednesday.","Only Wednesday night's differential, because it holds every file.","Only Sunday's full backup, since differentials do not hold new files."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A differential job selects everything new or changed since the last full job, so restoring needs at most two jobs: the full backup plus the latest differential. An incremental chain would need the full plus every incremental since then.","optionWhy":["Correct. A differential chain needs a maximum of two jobs: the full backup and the most recent differential.","That is how an incremental chain is restored. Each differential already contains all changes since the full job, so only the latest one is needed.","A differential job only selects files that are new or modified since the full backup. The full backup is still needed for everything else.","Differential jobs do select new files and files modified since the full job. Restoring the full backup alone would lose Monday to Wednesday's changes."],"optionRefs":["pp. 702–703","p. 702","p. 702","p. 702"],"ref":"pp. 702–703, Backup Chains","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-05","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A company has very little backup storage and a short nightly backup window, and it accepts that a restore may need several jobs from different media. Which backup chain fits best?","context":"A company has very little backup storage and a short nightly backup window, and it accepts that a restore may need several jobs from different media. Which backup chain fits best?","options":["Full with incremental, since each job copies changes since the last job.","Full with differential, since each job copies changes since the last full.","Full only, since a single job is all that is needed when restoring data.","Copy backups only, since they run ad hoc and leave the archive bit alone."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An incremental job selects only data that is new or changed since the previous job, giving the lowest time and storage requirement. The trade-off is the highest recovery complexity, since a restore can involve two or more jobs on different media.","optionWhy":["Correct. Incremental jobs have the lowest time and storage requirement but the most recovery complexity.","Differential jobs grow as changes build up since the full job, giving moderate time and storage needs. They are not the lowest.","A full-only chain has the least recovery complexity but the highest storage and time requirement, the opposite of what this company needs.","Copy backups are made outside the chain system on an ad hoc basis. They are not a scheduled scheme for saving storage and time."],"optionRefs":["p. 702","p. 702","p. 702","p. 703"],"ref":"p. 702, Backup Chains","difficulty":"Comparison","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-06","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"Before a risky database upgrade, a technician wants an extra one-off backup of the data. It must not disturb the scheduled incremental chain that relies on the archive attribute. What type of backup should be run?","context":"Before a risky database upgrade, a technician wants an extra one-off backup of the data. It must not disturb the scheduled incremental chain that relies on the archive attribute. What type of backup should be run?","options":["A copy backup, since it runs ad hoc and leaves the archive attribute alone.","An extra full backup, because full jobs leave the archive attribute set.","An extra incremental job, because it only selects files changed today.","A synthetic full, because it is built by scanning the whole system again."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Copy backups are made outside the chain system (ad hoc) and do not affect the archive attribute, so the next scheduled incremental still sees the correct set of changed files. Full and incremental jobs both clear the archive attribute.","optionWhy":["Correct. Copy backups are made outside the chain and leave the archive attribute untouched.","A full backup clears the archive attribute, so the next incremental would miss files changed before the upgrade.","An incremental job also clears the archive attribute, so running one ad hoc would alter what the scheduled chain backs up next.","A synthetic full is built from the original full backup plus the incrementals, not by rescanning the system. It is not a one-off copy outside the chain."],"optionRefs":["p. 703","p. 703","p. 703","p. 703"],"ref":"pp. 702–703, Backup Chains","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-07","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A server takes too long to rescan for a weekly full backup. The admin wants a new up-to-date full backup built from the last full backup plus the incrementals already taken. What is the main risk of this approach?","context":"A server takes too long to rescan for a weekly full backup. The admin wants a new up-to-date full backup built from the last full backup plus the incrementals already taken. What is the main risk of this approach?","options":["A corrupt incremental job will also affect the new full backup built from it.","It needs more storage and more time to create than a normal full backup.","Restoring from it is slower than restoring from a long chain of incrementals.","It clears the archive attribute on every file, so the chain must restart."],"answer":0,"explanation":"This is a synthetic full backup. It is quicker to create and needs less storage than a real full backup, but if any of the incremental backups it is built from are corrupted, the synthetic full will be affected.","optionWhy":["Correct. A synthetic full is assembled from the original full and the incrementals, so corruption in any of them carries over.","The opposite is true: a synthetic full is typically quicker to create and needs less storage than a normal full backup.","Restoring from a synthetic full is quicker than restoring from incremental backups, though it can take longer than a normal full backup.","The guide describes synthetic fulls as being built from existing backups. It does not describe any archive attribute problem with them."],"optionRefs":["p. 703","p. 703","p. 703","p. 703"],"ref":"p. 703, Synthetic Full Backup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-08","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"You are setting up a GFS tape rotation with a full backup every Friday night on a father tape. Why should five father tapes be labeled instead of four?","context":"You are setting up a GFS tape rotation with a full backup every Friday night on a father tape. Why should five father tapes be labeled instead of four?","options":["Some months have five Fridays, so each one needs its own father tape.","One father tape must always be kept spare in case the son tapes fail.","The fifth father tape holds the month-end full backup for that month.","Each father tape stores one weekday's incremental backup for the week."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In the guide's GFS example, a weekly full backup goes to a father tape each Friday. Because some months have five Fridays, five tapes are dedicated to the father role. Son tapes take the daily incrementals and twelve grandfather tapes hold month-end fulls.","optionWhy":["Correct. Some months have five Fridays, so five father tapes are needed for the weekly full backups.","The guide gives no spare-tape rule. Son tapes hold the daily incrementals and are reused weekly in the same order.","The month-end full backup goes to a grandfather tape, of which twelve are needed. It is not stored on a father tape.","Daily incremental backups are written to son tapes. Father tapes hold the weekly full backup made on Friday night."],"optionRefs":["p. 703","p. 703","p. 703","p. 703"],"ref":"pp. 703–704, Grandfather-Father-Son (GFS) Scheme","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-09","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A small office keeps its data on a server and one backup copy on a NAS in the same rack, both on hard disks. What should be added to meet the 3-2-1 backup rule?","context":"A small office keeps its data on a server and one backup copy on a NAS in the same rack, both on hard disks. What should be added to meet the 3-2-1 backup rule?","options":["A third copy on a different media type, kept offline and off site.","A second NAS in the same rack that mirrors the first NAS every night.","A nightly job that verifies each backup on the NAS with a hash value.","A cloud sync of the server that replaces the local NAS backup copy."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The 3-2-1 rule calls for three copies of the data (including the production copy), across two media types, with one copy held offline and off site. The office has two copies on one media type, both on site.","optionWhy":["Correct. That gives three copies, two media types, and one copy offline and off site.","That adds a third copy, but it is on the same media type and in the same location, so a disaster at the site could still destroy every copy.","Hash verification is a good testing practice, but it does not add the extra copy, media type, or offline off-site copy that the rule requires.","Replacing the NAS keeps only two copies. The guide also advises keeping local backups in addition to cloud backups."],"optionRefs":["p. 704","p. 704","p. 704","p. 704"],"ref":"p. 704, 3-2-1 Backup Rule","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-10","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"A fire has destroyed a branch office's file server, and the hardware cannot be used. A recent backup is available. Which recovery method should be used?","context":"A fire has destroyed a branch office's file server, and the hardware cannot be used. A recent backup is available. Which recovery method should be used?","options":["Restore to an alternate location, such as another server or a cloud host.","Perform an in-place recovery that overwrites the original server's data.","Restore the backup into a test directory to confirm that the data is valid.","Run chkdsk on the backup media and then restore over the original server."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Restoring to an alternate location puts the data on a different computer or an off-site cloud environment, and is preferred when catastrophic hardware failure or a major attack leaves the original hardware inaccessible. In-place recovery overwrites the original system and suits minor issues such as a corrupted file.","optionWhy":["Correct. An alternate-location recovery is preferred when the original hardware is inaccessible.","In-place recovery restores to the original system and location. That is not possible when the server's hardware has been destroyed.","Restoring to a test directory is a way to test a backup scheme. It does not put the branch's data back into service.","Running chkdsk checks backup media integrity, but restoring over the original server is impossible because its hardware is destroyed."],"optionRefs":["p. 705","p. 705","p. 704","p. 704"],"ref":"pp. 704–705, Recovery Options","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-11","module":21,"lesson":"21A Data Backup and Recovery","question":"You have just designed a new backup scheme for a production server and need to prove that restores work. How should you test it without risking the live data?","context":"You have just designed a new backup scheme for a production server and need to prove that restores work. How should you test it without risking the live data?","options":["Restore some data to a test directory or VM, not over the live host.","Perform an in-place recovery over the live server during business hours.","Check that the job finished with no errors and then skip any restore test.","Test the backups only once, since retesting adds risk to the backup chain."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide recommends restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory without overwriting anything, or using a virtual machine to test recovery procedures without affecting the production host. Recovery should also be re-tested whenever the schedule or requirements change.","optionWhy":["Correct. A test directory or a virtual machine lets you test recovery without affecting production data.","An in-place recovery overwrites the current system and will likely need downtime. That puts the live data at risk instead of testing safely.","A clean job log does not prove recovery works. The guide says to test by restoring data and to verify that the backup contains all required files.","Backups should be tested, not assumed to work, so one test is not enough."],"optionRefs":["p. 704","p. 705","p. 704","p. 704"],"ref":"p. 704, Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-12","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"A hospital shares patient test results with a research partner. The names are replaced with codes, but the hospital can use those codes to match each record back to a patient. What kind of data set is this?","context":"A hospital shares patient test results with a research partner. The names are replaced with codes, but the hospital can use those codes to match each record back to a patient. What kind of data set is this?","options":["A de-identified data set, since the provider can rebuild identities.","An anonymized data set, because the patient names have been removed.","Government-issued PII, because a patient code acts like an SSN number.","Cardholder data, because test results are linked to insurance billing."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A de-identified data set contains codes that allow the subject information to be reconstructed by the data provider. An anonymized data set has the identifying data removed completely, so it cannot be linked back.","optionWhy":["Correct. De-identified data contains codes that let the data provider reconstruct subject information.","In an anonymized data set the identifying data is removed completely, so no one can reconstruct the identities. Here the hospital still can.","Government-issued information is issued by federal or state governments, such as an SSN or passport. A hospital's research code is not.","Cardholder data covers card names, addresses, account and card numbers, and expiry dates under PCI DSS. Test results are protected health information."],"optionRefs":["p. 707","p. 707","p. 707","p. 707"],"ref":"p. 707, Healthcare Data","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-13","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"An online store plans to save customers' card numbers and expiry dates so they can check out faster next time. Which standard sets out how that stored data must be protected?","context":"An online store plans to save customers' card numbers and expiry dates so they can check out faster next time. Which standard sets out how that stored data must be protected?","options":["The Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS).","The US Privacy Act, which covers personal data held by companies.","HIPAA, which sets the rules for storing any customer billing records.","A NIST framework, which is the regulation that licenses card payments."],"answer":0,"explanation":"PCI DSS is an industry-enforced standard that governs processing of credit card and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must be provided if cardholder data such as card numbers and expiry dates is stored.","optionWhy":["Correct. PCI DSS governs card transactions and sets protections for stored cardholder data.","The US Privacy Act covers data collected and held by the US federal government, not a private online store.","HIPAA is mentioned in the guide in connection with healthcare data destruction requirements. It does not govern card payment data.","The guide mentions NIST only for password recommendations. It does not describe NIST as the standard that governs card payment data."],"optionRefs":["p. 707","p. 707","p. 713","p. 707"],"ref":"p. 707, Credit Card Transactions","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-14","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Management wants a tool that finds where sensitive data is stored, classifies it by sensitivity, and blocks it from being emailed or copied outside the company. What kind of solution is this?","context":"Management wants a tool that finds where sensitive data is stored, classifies it by sensitivity, and blocks it from being emailed or copied outside the company. What kind of solution is this?","options":["A Data Loss Prevention (DLP) solution that monitors and blocks the data.","A splash screen at sign-in that reminds users of the data policy rules.","A backup rotation scheme that moves the sensitive files to off-site tape.","A chain-of-custody form that records everyone who handles the data."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Data Loss Prevention (DLP) covers the policies and tools used to handle data and stop it from leaving the organization's control. DLP software identifies and classifies sensitive data, then monitors it and can block it from being removed or accessed.","optionWhy":["Correct. DLP software finds and classifies sensitive data and can block it from leaving the organization.","Splash screens remind users of policies when they start a program or log in. They do not find, classify, or block data.","Rotation schemes such as GFS manage backup media. They do not stop sensitive data from being emailed or copied out.","A chain-of-custody form tracks the handling of collected evidence in an investigation. It is not a tool that blocks data loss."],"optionRefs":["p. 708","p. 708","p. 703","p. 711"],"ref":"pp. 707–708, Data Handling Best Practices","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-15","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"An employee installs a utility on his company laptop. The vendor's license says the program is free for personal use only. What is the problem?","context":"An employee installs a utility on his company laptop. The vendor's license says the program is free for personal use only. What is the problem?","options":["The company is now infringing the license, since the use is not personal.","There is no problem, since freeware can legally be used on any device.","The utility is now covered by a corporate license for every employee.","The program becomes open source once it runs on company-owned devices."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A EULA may distinguish between personal and corporate/business use. If an employee installs a freeware-for-personal-use product on a company-owned device, the company is infringing the license. Installing unauthorized software also counts as prohibited content.","optionWhy":["Correct. Installing personal-use-only freeware on a company-owned device infringes the license.","A EULA can restrict freeware to personal use. Using it on a company device breaks that license, so it is not automatically allowed.","A corporate-use license must be issued by the vendor for an agreed number of computers. Installing personal freeware does not create one.","Open-source status depends on the license the software is released under. Where it is installed does not change that."],"optionRefs":["p. 708","p. 708","p. 709","p. 709"],"ref":"p. 708, End-User License Agreements","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-17","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Two companies are about to work together on a joint product, and each will share trade secrets with the other. Which kind of agreement suits this partnership?","context":"Two companies are about to work together on a joint product, and each will share trade secrets with the other. Which kind of agreement suits this partnership?","options":["A mutual NDA, so that both companies must protect each other's secrets.","A unilateral NDA, so that both companies must protect each other's secrets.","An end-user license agreement that limits how the shared data is copied.","An acceptable use policy that both companies' employees must sign first."],"answer":0,"explanation":"With a mutual NDA, both parties agree to protect each other's secrets, which is common when two organizations partner on a project. In a unilateral NDA only the receiving party is obligated, such as employees signing before joining a sensitive project.","optionWhy":["Correct. A mutual NDA binds both parties and is common when organizations partner on a project.","In a unilateral NDA only the receiving party must keep the information secret, so it would not protect both companies.","A EULA governs the use of installed software, such as how many computers it may run on. It does not protect shared trade secrets.","An AUP sets out how employees may use an organization's resources. The legally binding contract to protect shared secrets is an NDA."],"optionRefs":["p. 710","p. 709","p. 708","p. 708"],"ref":"pp. 709–710, Non-Disclosure Agreements","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-18","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"While fixing a printer driver on a user's PC, a technician finds confidential documents the user should not have access to. The company has a computer incident response team (CIRT). What should the technician do first?","context":"While fixing a printer driver on a user's PC, a technician finds confidential documents the user should not have access to. The company has a computer incident response team (CIRT). What should the technician do first?","options":["Notify the right CIRT member so they can take charge as first responder.","Delete the documents at once so that the user can no longer open them.","Call the police directly, since every incident must involve the police.","Copy the documents to a USB stick as proof and then finish the repair."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Finding prohibited material, including confidential documents the user should not have, is a security incident. When an incident is detected, it is critical that the appropriate person on the CIRT is notified so they can act as first responder and formulate the response.","optionWhy":["Correct. The appropriate CIRT member should be notified so they can take charge and plan the response.","The guide says evidence should be preserved and handled by the CIRT. Deleting documents is not the first step.","Informing law enforcement directly may be appropriate if there is no formal CIRT, and is usually the business owner's decision.","Copying material yourself risks compromising the investigation and could itself be a breach. The CIRT should handle evidence."],"optionRefs":["p. 710","p. 710","p. 710","p. 710"],"ref":"p. 710, Incident Response","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-19","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"An investigator has used a forensic tool with a write blocker to image a suspect's hard drive. How can they later prove that the evidence has not been modified since it was collected?","context":"An investigator has used a forensic tool with a write blocker to image a suspect's hard drive. How can they later prove that the evidence has not been modified since it was collected?","options":["Make cryptographic hashes of the source disk and image and compare them.","Run chkdsk on the image to confirm that the file system has no errors.","Store the image on archival tape, since tape is the least volatile media.","Boot the suspect's PC from the disk to show the files open as normal."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide's procedure says to make a cryptographic hash of each source disk and its forensic image. Matching hashes prove the digital evidence has not been modified after collection. The write blocker prevents changes to the source during imaging.","optionWhy":["Correct. A cryptographic hash of the source and its image proves the evidence has not been changed since collection.","chkdsk checks media integrity for backup drives. It cannot prove that evidence is unchanged since it was collected.","Archival media is the least volatile in the order of volatility, but storing evidence there does not prove it is unmodified.","Booting from the source disk would change it. A forensic imaging tool uses a write blocker precisely to prevent changes to the source disk."],"optionRefs":["p. 711","p. 704","p. 711","p. 711"],"ref":"p. 711, Documentation of Incident and Recovery of Evidence","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-20","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"A compromised workstation is still powered on. From the sources below, which evidence should be collected first?","context":"A compromised workstation is still powered on. From the sources below, which evidence should be collected first?","options":["The contents of system memory (RAM), which are lost at power-off.","The temporary file system and the swap space on the system drive.","The files stored on the workstation's internal solid-state drive.","The USB backup drive that the user plugs in each Friday afternoon."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The order of volatility from most to least volatile is CPU cache and registers, memory (RAM), temporary file system/swap space, disk storage, and archival media. Of the options listed, RAM is the most volatile, and its contents are lost when the power is turned off.","optionWhy":["Correct. Of these, RAM is the most volatile and must be captured before the device is powered off.","Swap space comes after RAM in the order of volatility. It is less volatile than memory.","Disk storage is persistent, so it is fourth in the order of volatility, after cache, RAM, and swap.","Archival media such as backup USB drives is the least volatile and should be collected last."],"optionRefs":["p. 711","p. 711","p. 711","p. 711"],"ref":"p. 711, Order of Volatility","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-21","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"A laptop seized as evidence passes from the technician to a manager and then to an outside investigator. What must the chain of custody form show?","context":"A laptop seized as evidence passes from the technician to a manager and then to an outside investigator. What must the chain of custody form show?","options":["Who collected it, when and where, and everyone who handled or stored it.","Only the name of the investigator who will present the evidence in court.","The hash values of the disk image, which replace any handover records.","The software licenses installed on the laptop at the time of seizure."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A chain of custody form tracks where, when, and who collected the evidence, who has handled it since, and where it was stored. Everyone who handles the evidence must sign it and indicate what they were doing with it, from the scene to the courtroom.","optionWhy":["Correct. The form tracks collection, every later handler, and storage at every point until court.","The chain of custody records every person who handles the evidence and where it is stored, not just the investigator's name.","Hashing proves the evidence is unmodified, but it does not replace records of who handled and stored the evidence.","License details may be relevant to an incident about unlicensed software, but they are not what a chain of custody form records."],"optionRefs":["p. 711","p. 711","p. 711","p. 711"],"ref":"p. 711, Chain of Custody","difficulty":"Definition","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-22","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"A batch of SSDs from self-encrypting laptops must be sanitized quickly and reliably before reuse. Which method fits best?","context":"A batch of SSDs from self-encrypting laptops must be sanitized quickly and reliably before reuse. Which method fits best?","options":["Crypto Erase, which destroys the drive's media encryption key (MEK).","Degaussing, which disrupts the magnetic pattern that stores the data.","Disk wiping software that overwrites every location with zeroes.","A standard OS format that deletes the partitions and writes a new one."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Instant secure erase (Crypto Erase) uses the capabilities of self-encrypting drives. The SED encrypts its contents with a media encryption key (MEK), and Crypto Erase destroys that key, making the data unrecoverable. Overwrite tools do not work reliably with SSDs, and degaussing does not work on SSDs at all.","optionWhy":["Correct. Crypto Erase destroys the MEK on a self-encrypting drive, so the encrypted data cannot be recovered.","Degaussing works on the magnetic surface of hard disks. It does not work with SSDs or optical media.","Overwriting is time-consuming and does not work reliably with SSDs.","Standard formatting only removes references to files and marks space as usable. The data could still be recovered."],"optionRefs":["p. 712","p. 713","p. 712","p. 712"],"ref":"p. 712, Low Level Format","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-23","module":21,"lesson":"21B Data Handling Best Practices","question":"Your company sends old hard drives that held patient records to a third-party disposal vendor. What should you get back from the vendor and keep on file?","context":"Your company sends old hard drives that held patient records to a third-party disposal vendor. What should you get back from the vendor and keep on file?","options":["A certificate listing each drive's serial number and how it was destroyed.","A signed NDA in which the vendor promises not to read any of the drives.","A chain-of-custody form signed by every courier who moved the drives.","A software license key that proves the vendor's wiping tool is genuine."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Third-party disposal vendors should provide a certificate of destruction or recycling listing the make, model, and serial number of each drive, the date of destruction, and how it was destroyed. These certificates are important to retain, especially for regulated data such as that covered by HIPAA.","optionWhy":["Correct. A certificate of destruction/recycling records make, model, serial, date, and method, and should be kept.","An NDA protects shared information, but the record the guide says to retain is the certificate of destruction or recycling.","Chain-of-custody forms are for forensic evidence in an investigation. The disposal vendor should provide a certificate of destruction.","Product keys activate software. They are not the record the guide says to retain after outsourced disposal."],"optionRefs":["p. 713","p. 713","p. 711","p. 708"],"ref":"p. 713, Disposal and Recycling Outsourcing Concepts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-24","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Your company is rolling out AI tools to staff and wants a document that sets clear rules on how the tools may and may not be used. Which policy should be written?","context":"Your company is rolling out AI tools to staff and wants a document that sets clear rules on how the tools may and may not be used. Which policy should be written?","options":["An acceptable use policy (AUP) for AI that sets usage rules for staff.","A mutual NDA between the company and each of the AI tool's developers.","An end-user license agreement that each employee writes for the tool.","A data retention policy that limits how long AI chat logs can be kept."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An Acceptable Use Policy (AUP) for AI outlines guidelines and restrictions on how AI tools and systems can be used. It sets clear expectations for users and helps keep the company compliant with laws and regulations.","optionWhy":["Correct. An AUP for AI sets the guidelines and restrictions for how staff may use AI tools.","An NDA protects sensitive information shared between parties. It does not set rules for how employees use AI tools.","A EULA is the vendor's license that you accept when installing software. It is not an internal usage policy.","Retention policies govern how long data is kept. The policy that sets rules for using AI tools is the AUP."],"optionRefs":["p. 715","pp. 709–710","p. 708","p. 708"],"ref":"p. 715, Policy","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-25","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"An AI tool screens job applicants. Trained on years of past hiring records, it keeps ranking one group of applicants lower. Which AI limitation explains this?","context":"An AI tool screens job applicants. Trained on years of past hiring records, it keeps ranking one group of applicants lower. Which AI limitation explains this?","options":["Bias, since the AI learned the same flaws found in its training data.","Hallucination, since the AI is inventing facts not found in its data.","Prompt injection, since an attacker crafted inputs to change the AI.","Computer vision, since the AI cannot recognize the applicants' photos."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Bias arises when the data used to train the AI is flawed or biased, so the AI carries the same biases. Algorithm design and developers can also introduce bias.","optionWhy":["Correct. If training data is biased, the AI will be flawed and carry the same biases.","A hallucination is wrong information not based on facts or training data. Here the AI is faithfully reproducing patterns in its data.","Prompt injection is crafting a prompt to trick an AI into revealing sensitive data. Nothing suggests an attack here.","Computer vision is a feature that lets applications recognize images. It is not a limitation that causes unfair rankings."],"optionRefs":["p. 715","p. 715","p. 716","p. 715"],"ref":"p. 715, Limitations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-26","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"A colleague used an AI chatbot to draft a report. It quoted a research study and a statistic that, when checked, do not exist. What is this called, and what should be done?","context":"A colleague used an AI chatbot to draft a report. It quoted a research study and a statistic that, when checked, do not exist. What is this called, and what should be done?","options":["A hallucination, so all AI output should be fact-checked before use.","A bias, so the chatbot needs to be retrained with a bigger data set.","A prompt injection, so the chatbot's account password must be reset.","A plagiarism breach, so the report must credit the chatbot as author."],"answer":0,"explanation":"AI hallucinations occur when the AI generates wrong information not based on facts or its training data, including completely made-up information. This is why it is important to fact-check any AI output.","optionWhy":["Correct. Made-up information is a hallucination, which is why AI output must be fact-checked.","Bias comes from flawed or biased training data and shows up as skewed results. Inventing a study that doesn't exist is a hallucination.","Prompt injection is a malicious prompt designed to make an AI reveal sensitive data. It does not describe invented citations.","Plagiarism policies define how AI may assist with writing and require attribution, but fabricated facts are a hallucination."],"optionRefs":["p. 715","p. 715","p. 716","p. 715"],"ref":"p. 715, Limitations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-27","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"A law firm wants an AI assistant trained on its own case files that only its staff can use. Which deployment fits, and what must be protected most carefully?","context":"A law firm wants an AI assistant trained on its own case files that only its staff can use. Which deployment fits, and what must be protected most carefully?","options":["A private AI model, with secure access so the case data cannot leak.","A public AI model, since it is trained only on the firm's own case files.","A public AI model, since connecting over HTTPS makes the files private.","A private AI model, so no access controls are needed for its users."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Private AI models are developed for a specific organization and are only accessible to users within it. They can be trained on public data and the organization's private data, so access must be kept secure to prevent sensitive data from leaking.","optionWhy":["Correct. A private model is built for one organization; since it may be trained on sensitive data, access must be secured.","Public models are built by larger organizations, are open to anyone, and are trained on public data and user interactions.","The guide's concern is what happens to data entered into AI tools, not only the network link.","Because private models may be trained on sensitive data, access must be kept secure, including from employees or vendors who could reach that data."],"optionRefs":["p. 715","pp. 715–716","p. 716","pp. 715–716"],"ref":"p. 715, Private vs. Public","difficulty":"Comparison","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-28","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"A sales rep pastes a full customer list, with names and phone numbers, into a public AI chatbot to get help writing emails. Why is this a privacy concern?","context":"A sales rep pastes a full customer list, with names and phone numbers, into a public AI chatbot to get help writing emails. Why is this a privacy concern?","options":["The data can be stored and used to train the model, so it could leak.","The chatbot connects over plain HTTP, so the list is sent unencrypted.","The chatbot will hallucinate new phone numbers into the customer list.","Phone numbers are not PII, but sharing names breaks the vendor's EULA."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When users interact with AI, they often enter personal or confidential information, and that data can be stored and used to train the AI model. This can lead to an accidental leak of private information, which is why user privacy should be a primary concern.","optionWhy":["Correct. Input to an AI may be stored and used for training, which can lead to an accidental leak.","The guide does not describe plain HTTP for this chatbot. The privacy problem is what happens to the data that was entered.","Hallucinations are wrong output generated by the AI. The privacy problem is what happens to the data that was put in.","A cell phone number is a good example of PII, as are names, so the list is personal data that should not be shared."],"optionRefs":["p. 716","p. 716","p. 715","p. 707"],"ref":"pp. 715–716, Private vs. Public","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-29","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"An attacker types a carefully worded request into a company's internal AI assistant, trying to trick it into revealing confidential salary data it was trained on. What type of attack is this?","context":"An attacker types a carefully worded request into a company's internal AI assistant, trying to trick it into revealing confidential salary data it was trained on. What type of attack is this?","options":["A prompt injection attack, crafting a prompt to make the AI reveal data.","An AI hallucination, where the assistant makes up the salary figures.","A denial of service attack, flooding the assistant until it shuts down.","A bias attack, changing the training data so results favor the attacker."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A prompt injection attack involves crafting a prompt to trick the AI into revealing sensitive data. With private models trained on internal data, appropriate security protocols are needed to reduce the chance of sensitive information being exposed.","optionWhy":["Correct. Prompt injection uses a crafted prompt to trick an AI into revealing sensitive data.","Hallucinations are false information the AI generates on its own. Here an attacker is deliberately trying to extract real data.","A DoS attack attempts to damage a network or service. A single crafted request aimed at extracting data is prompt injection.","Bias comes from flawed training data, algorithms, or developers. The guide does not describe it as an attack using crafted prompts."],"optionRefs":["p. 716","p. 715","p. 710","p. 715"],"ref":"pp. 715–716, Private vs. Public","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-01","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"The company's main order-processing server has crashed, and a configuration change is needed right now to restore service. How should this change request be classified?","context":"The company's main order-processing server has crashed, and a configuration change is needed right now to restore service. How should this change request be classified?","options":["As an emergency change, which uses an expedited CM process and approval.","As a standard change that can be repeated without any CM process at all.","As a normal change that waits for the next scheduled review.","As a proactive change, since the need was anticipated and planned internally."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An emergency change is high impact but urgent, such as correcting a major outage, and goes through an expedited change management (CM) process and approval. Documenting the type and priority of the change helps speed up approval when necessary.","optionWhy":["Correct. Emergency changes are high impact and urgent, such as fixing a major outage, and use an expedited process.","Standard changes are low impact and repeatable. A fix for a major outage is high impact, so it is not a standard change.","A normal change goes through the full CM process. Waiting would prolong a major outage.","Proactive changes are anticipated and initiated internally. A crash that forces a change on the organization is reactive."],"optionRefs":["p. 719","p. 719","p. 719","p. 718"],"ref":"pp. 718–719, Change Requests","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-02","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"Your team regularly replaces failed keyboards with the same approved model. The change process for this was set up once and has been repeated many times with no issues. What category of change is this?","context":"Your team regularly replaces failed keyboards with the same approved model. The change process for this was set up once and has been repeated many times with no issues. What category of change is this?","options":["A standard change, since it is low impact and repeatable without full CM.","A normal change, since every hardware swap needs the full CM process.","An emergency change, since a broken keyboard stops the user from working.","A major change, since it must be run as a project approved by the CAB."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A standard change is low impact and repeatable. It may require the change management process to be set up once, but can then be repeated as needed without triggering the full CM process.","optionWhy":["Correct. Standard changes are low impact and can be repeated without triggering the full CM process.","A normal change is a one-time change that triggers the full CM process. A routine, repeated swap is a standard change.","Emergency changes are high impact and urgent, such as correcting a major outage. One keyboard swap is low impact.","Major changes are managed as dedicated projects through a CAB. A repeatable keyboard swap is nowhere near that scale."],"optionRefs":["p. 719","p. 719","p. 719","p. 719"],"ref":"pp. 718–719, Change Requests","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-03","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"You are filling in a request-for-change (RFC) form for a new VPN client. Which section should state that 300 laptops and their users are affected, and how success will be judged?","context":"You are filling in a request-for-change (RFC) form for a new VPN client. Which section should state that 300 laptops and their users are affected, and how success will be judged?","options":["The scope of the change, which covers who is affected and the success factors.","The purpose of the change, which covers the business case and its benefits.","The type of change, which says whether it is standard, normal, or emergency.","The implementation schedule, which covers the date and maintenance window."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The scope of the change covers the number of devices, users, or customers affected, and can include costs, timescales, sub-tasks, and stakeholders. It should also include the factors by which success or failure will be judged.","optionWhy":["Correct. Scope lists the devices, users, or customers affected and how success or failure will be judged.","Purpose is the business case and the benefits, plus risk analysis. The number of devices affected belongs in scope.","Type of change records priority and category. It does not list the devices and users affected.","The schedule covers when the change happens, maintenance windows, and change freezes, not how many devices are affected."],"optionRefs":["p. 719","p. 719","p. 719","p. 719"],"ref":"pp. 718–719, Change Requests","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-04","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"A plan to move every department to a new email platform will affect the whole company and needs a large budget. Who should approve it?","context":"A plan to move every department to a new email platform will affect the whole company and needs a large budget. Who should approve it?","options":["A Change Advisory Board (CAB) that reviews the business case and the risks.","The technician's own supervisor, who can approve any change on their own.","The end users, who must sign an acceptance form before any work can begin.","The colleague who will perform the peer review once the change is finished."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Major changes are usually managed as dedicated projects and approved through a Change Advisory Board (CAB). The CAB assesses the business case and the technical merits and risks, and includes affected stakeholders, those proposing the change, the technicians who will implement it, and managers who can authorize the budget.","optionWhy":["Correct. Major changes go to a CAB, which assesses the business case, technical merits, and risks.","A supervisor or department manager might approve normal or minor changes. A major, company-wide change needs a CAB.","End-user acceptance comes after the change is implemented, confirming the system works. It is not the approval step.","Peer review checks that the change was carried out correctly after implementation. It does not approve the plan."],"optionRefs":["p. 719","p. 719","p. 720","p. 720"],"ref":"pp. 719–720, Change Board Approvals","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-05","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"Halfway through a firmware upgrade on a core switch, the device starts dropping connections and the change cannot be completed. What part of the change plan should you use now?","context":"Halfway through a firmware upgrade on a core switch, the device starts dropping connections and the change cannot be completed. What part of the change plan should you use now?","options":["The rollback plan, which reverses the change and restores the original setup.","The end-user acceptance step, which records that the users accept the issue.","The peer review, which lets a colleague finish the upgrade after a check.","The risk analysis, which is carried out once the outage has already begun."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A rollback plan reverses the change and restores the system to its original configuration. It should include a backup plan: a system and data backup used to restore the system if an error occurs during the change.","optionWhy":["Correct. A rollback plan reverses the change and restores the original configuration, backed by a backup plan.","End-user acceptance confirms the change works and the client is satisfied. It is not used when a change fails.","Peer review checks that a completed change was done correctly. It does not undo a failed change.","Risk analysis is done for each change request before implementation, to identify and mitigate risks in advance."],"optionRefs":["p. 720","p. 720","p. 720","p. 719"],"ref":"p. 720, Implementation and Acceptance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-06","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"A technician has finished updating the firewall rules for an approved change. Before the service goes back online, a colleague checks each rule against the approved request and finds a typo. What step is this?","context":"A technician has finished updating the firewall rules for an approved change. Before the service goes back online, a colleague checks each rule against the approved request and finds a typo. What step is this?","options":["A peer review, which catches mistakes before the system is back online.","End-user acceptance, which has the customer sign off that the change works.","A rollback, which returns the firewall to how it was before the change.","A sandbox test, which tries the rules on a non-production test network."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A peer review has a colleague confirm the change was implemented correctly in line with the approval. It lets mistakes such as skipped steps or misspellings be caught before the system or service is brought back online.","optionWhy":["Correct. Peer review lets a colleague catch skipped steps or misspellings before the service returns.","End-user acceptance is the customer or client confirming the system is fully functional, not a colleague checking rules.","A rollback reverses a change when the process runs into issues. Here a colleague is checking the work instead.","Sandbox testing happens before implementation in a non-production environment, not as a check after the change."],"optionRefs":["p. 720","p. 720","p. 720","p. 719"],"ref":"p. 720, Implementation and Acceptance","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-07","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"A two-year-old laptop has a failing motherboard, and you want the manufacturer to repair it. Which details from the asset inventory will help most?","context":"A two-year-old laptop has a failing motherboard, and you want the manufacturer to repair it. Which details from the asset inventory will help most?","options":["Its asset ID or service tag and its purchase date and warranty details.","Its cost, which is needed to claim the repair on the business insurance.","Its software license details and the number of seats that are assigned.","The name of its assigned user, so the vendor can contact that user directly."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Inventory records should include the asset ID or service tag and the dates of purchase and warranty. The purchase date verifies warranty coverage, which is key when support needs to be escalated to the manufacturer.","optionWhy":["Correct. The service tag identifies the unit and the purchase and warranty dates verify coverage with the manufacturer.","Cost is useful for insurance and documenting replacement value, but it doesn't verify warranty cover with the manufacturer.","License records track compliance with software terms. They do not help with a hardware warranty repair.","Listing the assigned user helps internal communication about a system. Warranty claims rely on the tag and purchase dates."],"optionRefs":["pp. 720–721","p. 720","p. 721","p. 721"],"ref":"pp. 720–721, Asset Management","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-08","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change and Inventory Management","question":"Your organization wants one database that tracks owned and leased hardware, software licenses, and each asset's life from procurement to disposal. What is this database called?","context":"Your organization wants one database that tracks owned and leased hardware, software licenses, and each asset's life from procurement to disposal. What is this database called?","options":["A configuration management database (CMDB) for assets and their lifecycle.","A request-for-change (RFC) form that records each new asset that arrives.","A material safety data sheet (MSDS) that lists each asset's components.","A change advisory board (CAB) register of every approved hardware purchase."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Many organizations track assets with a configuration management database (CMDB). It can include software and license information as well as hardware, plus lifecycle data from procurement through to disposal.","optionWhy":["Correct. A CMDB can track hardware, software licenses, and each asset's lifecycle.","An RFC form documents a proposed change and its procedure for approval. It is not an asset database.","An MSDS gives hazard, handling, and disposal information for substances. It does not track an inventory.","A CAB is a group that approves major changes. It is not a database for tracking assets."],"optionRefs":["p. 721","p. 718","p. 733","p. 719"],"ref":"pp. 720–721, Asset Management","difficulty":"Definition","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-09","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You are about to work on a component that may still be connected to power. What should you do before touching it?","context":"You are about to work on a component that may still be connected to power. What should you do before touching it?","options":["Check it with a voltage tester and use insulated tools and insulated gloves.","Put on an anti-ESD wrist strap so any voltage drains away from your body.","Touch an unpainted part of the chassis to drain any current from the device.","Hold the device's power button down while you work so it stays switched off."],"answer":0,"explanation":"When working on electronic equipment, use insulated tools and gloves, and test the component with a voltage tester first. Confirming no voltage is present protects you from unexpected shocks.","optionWhy":["Correct. A voltage tester confirms no voltage is present, and insulated tools and gloves add protection.","An ESD strap dissipates static charge to protect components. It is not protection against live mains voltage.","Touching the chassis drains residual static from your body. It does not make a live circuit safe to work on.","Holding the power button drains residual power after the device is unplugged. It does not prove there's no voltage."],"optionRefs":["p. 723","p. 728","p. 728","p. 725"],"ref":"p. 723, Safety Equipment and Tools","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-10","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A device that should be fitted with a 5 A fuse has been given a 13 A fuse instead. What is the risk?","context":"A device that should be fitted with a 5 A fuse has been given a 13 A fuse instead. What is the risk?","options":["It may not blow when it should and could let too much current pass through.","It will blow too easily and cut power every time the device is turned on.","It lowers the voltage reaching the device and causes repeated brownouts.","It removes the ground path, so metal parts could become live during a fault."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A device must have a fuse suited to its maximum current. A fuse rated too low blows too easily; one rated too high may not blow when it should and lets too much current pass through the device.","optionWhy":["Correct. An over-rated fuse may not blow when it should and lets too much current through the device.","That is the risk of a fuse rated too low. A 13 A fuse in a 5 A device is rated too high.","A fuse breaks the circuit when there is a fault. Brownouts are under-voltage events from the building supply.","Grounding is provided via the power plug and ground wire. The fuse rating does not remove the ground path."],"optionRefs":["p. 724","p. 724","p. 731","p. 724"],"ref":"p. 724, Fuses","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-11","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"During a rack upgrade, a colleague suggests unscrewing the rack's ground wire to make room for new cabling. What is the right response?","context":"During a rack upgrade, a colleague suggests unscrewing the rack's ground wire to make room for new cabling. What is the right response?","options":["Leave it in place; if it must be moved, an electrician should replace it.","Remove it, since servers are grounded through their own power plugs anyway.","Remove it and clip an anti-ESD wrist strap to the rack in its place instead.","Remove it while the rack is powered on, since the ground wire carries no load."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Large metal equipment racks must be grounded, because the ground gives fault current a path of least resistance to flow away harmlessly. Do not disconnect the ground wire; if it must be removed, a professional electrician should replace it.","optionWhy":["Correct. Do not disconnect the ground wire; if removal is unavoidable, a professional electrician must replace it.","PCs and printers are grounded via their plugs, but the guide says the metal racks themselves must also be grounded.","An ESD strap protects components from static discharge. It does not replace electrical safety grounding for a rack.","The ground wire provides a safe path for fault current. Removing it on a live rack removes that protection."],"optionRefs":["p. 724","p. 724","p. 728","p. 724"],"ref":"p. 724, Equipment Grounding","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-12","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You are about to replace the RAM in a laptop. Which preparation is correct?","context":"You are about to replace the RAM in a laptop. Which preparation is correct?","options":["Unplug the AC adapter, remove the battery, then hold the power button down.","Shut down Windows and leave the AC adapter in so the laptop stays grounded.","Put the laptop in sleep mode so the memory is not in use during the swap.","Remove only the battery, since the AC adapter supplies very low voltage."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Before adding or replacing components, disconnect the power: remove the AC plug and the battery if present, then hold down the power button to drain any residual power from the circuits and capacitors.","optionWhy":["Correct. Remove the AC plug and battery, then hold the power button to drain residual power.","Power must be disconnected before replacing components. Leaving the adapter plugged in keeps the circuit powered.","Sleep mode keeps the system powered. The guide says to disconnect all power before replacing components.","The guide says to remove both the AC plug and the battery, then drain residual power with the power button."],"optionRefs":["p. 725","p. 725","p. 725","p. 725"],"ref":"p. 725, Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-13","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A desktop's power supply unit (PSU) has died. A junior technician wants to open it up to look for a blown component. What should you tell them?","context":"A desktop's power supply unit (PSU) has died. A junior technician wants to open it up to look for a blown component. What should you tell them?","options":["Do not open it unless trained, as capacitors can hold a charge for hours.","It is safe to open the unit once it has been unplugged for a few seconds.","Push a screwdriver into the fan first to check whether the fan still turns.","It is safe to open as long as an anti-ESD wrist strap is worn the whole time."],"answer":0,"explanation":"PC power supplies can carry dangerously high voltages, and capacitors can hold a charge for hours after the power is off. Do not open them unless specifically trained, and never remove or break open safety devices that carry warnings.","optionWhy":["Correct. PSU capacitors can stay charged for hours, so only trained staff should open them.","Charges in PSU capacitors can persist for hours after the power is off, not just seconds.","Never insert anything into the PSU fan to make it rotate. It does not work and it is dangerous.","An ESD strap protects components from static. It does not protect you from high voltage stored in capacitors."],"optionRefs":["p. 725","p. 725","p. 725","pp. 725, 728"],"ref":"p. 725, Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-14","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A power cable behind a server rack has caught fire. Which extinguisher is the best choice?","context":"A power cable behind a server rack has caught fire. Which extinguisher is the best choice?","options":["A carbon dioxide (CO2) gas extinguisher, which is safe near live equipment.","A water extinguisher, since water cools the burning cable fastest of all.","A foam extinguisher, since foam smothers the fire and blocks the oxygen.","A dry powder extinguisher, since it is the type least harmful to the servers."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Water and foam extinguishers can be dangerous near live electrical equipment. The best choice is a carbon dioxide (CO2) gas extinguisher. Dry powder can also be used, but it can damage electronic equipment. You should also make sure the electricity supply is turned off.","optionWhy":["Correct. A CO2 gas extinguisher is the best type to use on electrical fires.","Water can be dangerous near live electrical equipment, so it is not suitable here.","Foam, like water, can be dangerous when used near live electrical equipment.","Dry powder can be used on electrical fires, but it can damage electronic equipment. CO2 is the best choice."],"optionRefs":["p. 725","p. 725","p. 725","p. 725"],"ref":"p. 725, Electrical Fire Safety","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-15","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A temporary network cable has to run across a busy office walkway, and there is no other route. What should you do?","context":"A temporary network cable has to run across a busy office walkway, and there is no other route. What should you do?","options":["Cover the cable with a cord protector where it crosses the walkway.","Tape a warning sign to the wall and leave the cable loose on the floor.","Bundle it with Velcro straps and then lay the bundle across the walkway.","Run the cable along the edge of the desks at the height of people's feet."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Cabling should not be run across walkways. If there is no other option, cover the cabling with a cord protector. Cables should otherwise be secured with ties, trays, or Velcro straps.","optionWhy":["Correct. If cabling must cross a walkway, cover it with a cord protector.","A loose cable in a walkway is a trip hazard. The guide says to use a cord protector if crossing is unavoidable.","Velcro straps tidy cables, but a bundle across a walkway is still a trip hazard without a cord protector.","Cables should be placed so they cannot be kicked out by users' feet, not run at foot level."],"optionRefs":["p. 726","p. 726","p. 725","pp. 725–726"],"ref":"pp. 725–726, Trip Hazards","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-16","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You need to lift a heavy UPS off the floor and carry it to a rack. Which technique is correct?","context":"You need to lift a heavy UPS off the floor and carry it to a rack. Which technique is correct?","options":["Bend your knees, keep your back straight, and lift by straightening your legs.","Keep your legs straight and bend at the waist to get a good grip on the unit.","Lift it with a quick jerk so the weight is on your back for as little time as possible.","Twist your upper body toward the rack while you lift so you save a step later."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Plant your feet around the object, bend your knees while keeping your back straight and chin up, grip firmly, and lift smoothly by straightening your legs. If it is too heavy or awkward, get help or use a cart.","optionWhy":["Correct. Bend at the knees, keep your back straight and chin up, and lift by straightening your legs.","Bending at the waist strains the back. The guide says to bend your knees and keep your back straight.","Lift smoothly by straightening your legs. The guide specifically says not to jerk the object up.","Plant your feet with one foot toward where you will move and keep your back straight, rather than twisting."],"optionRefs":["p. 726","p. 726","p. 726","p. 726"],"ref":"p. 726, Lifting Techniques","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-17","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A laser printer in an open-plan office has spilled toner inside its case. How should you clean it up?","context":"A laser printer in an open-plan office has spilled toner inside its case. How should you clean it up?","options":["Use a toner-safe vacuum or toner cloth, ideally in a maintenance room.","Blow the toner out with a compressed air can, then wipe the case clean.","Use an ordinary household vacuum cleaner with a new dust bag fitted.","Wipe it out with a wet paper towel, then let the printer dry overnight."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Do not use compressed air on a toner spill or laser printer in an office area, as it blows fine toner dust into the air. Use a PC vacuum only if it is labeled toner-safe, or a toner cloth. Ideally, move the printer to a maintenance room with filters.","optionWhy":["Correct. A toner-safe vacuum or toner cloth contains the particles; a filtered maintenance room is ideal.","Compressed air blows fine toner dust into the atmosphere and creates a health hazard in an office.","The guide says to avoid a domestic vacuum cleaner, because toner can damage the motor and pass through the dust collection bag.","The guide recommends a toner-safe vacuum or a special toner cloth. A wet paper towel is not among its methods."],"optionRefs":["p. 727","p. 727","p. 727","p. 727"],"ref":"pp. 726–727, Dust Cleanup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-18","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A desktop PC's fans and heat sinks are clogged with dust. Which tool should you use to clean inside the system unit?","context":"A desktop PC's fans and heat sinks are clogged with dust. Which tool should you use to clean inside the system unit?","options":["A PC vacuum or a natural bristle brush on the fans and motherboard.","A household vacuum cleaner, since it gives the strongest possible suction.","A damp cloth with cleaning spray wiped directly over the motherboard.","A toner cloth, which is designed to pick up dust from inside a case."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Use a PC vacuum cleaner or a natural bristle brush to remove dust from inside the system unit. Domestic vacuum appliances should not be used because they can produce high levels of static electricity.","optionWhy":["Correct. A PC vacuum or natural bristle brush is the right tool for dust inside the system unit.","Domestic vacuums should not be used because they can produce high levels of static electricity.","Liquids should not come into contact with electrical devices. The guide uses a PC vacuum or brush for dust.","A toner cloth is a special cloth for wiping up loose toner, not for general dust inside a PC."],"optionRefs":["p. 727","p. 727","p. 724","p. 727"],"ref":"pp. 726–727, Dust Cleanup","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-19","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"On a freezing winter day, a new server is delivered to your warm server room. What should you do before unpacking it?","context":"On a freezing winter day, a new server is delivered to your warm server room. What should you do before unpacking it?","options":["Leave it in its packaging for a few hours so it can warm up gradually.","Unpack and power it on right away so its own heat dries any moisture.","Place it next to a radiator so that it warms up to room temperature faster.","Lower the room humidity to 10% so no condensation can form on the server."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Sudden warming can cause condensation. New equipment should be left in its packaging for a few hours, depending on the outside temperature, so it can adjust to room temperature gradually.","optionWhy":["Correct. Leaving it packed for a few hours avoids condensation from sudden warming.","Powering on cold equipment risks condensation from sudden warming. Let it adjust in its packaging first.","Rapid warming is what causes condensation, and computers should not be placed near a radiator.","The guide says low humidity allows static charges to build up, which increases the risk of ESD."],"optionRefs":["p. 728","p. 728","pp. 727–728","p. 728"],"ref":"pp. 727–728, Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-20","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A workshop's air has become very dry this winter, and technicians report more static shocks. Which statement about this is correct?","context":"A workshop's air has become very dry this winter, and technicians report more static shocks. Which statement about this is correct?","options":["Low humidity raises ESD risk; aim for humidity of around 50 percent.","Low humidity lowers ESD risk, since dry air cannot carry any static charge.","High humidity of 90 percent is best, since condensation drains static away.","Humidity has no effect on ESD; only the type of flooring matters for it."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Low humidity lets static charges build up more easily and increases the risk of ESD, especially in dry, cool conditions. High humidity can cause condensation. The ideal level is around 50% (Student Guide p. 727).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says low humidity allows static charges to build up more easily and increases the risk of ESD. The ideal level it gives is around 50%.","The opposite is true: ESD risk rises in dry, cool conditions when humidity is low.","High humidity can cause condensation, which is harmful.","Humidity and climate do affect the likelihood of ESD, as do carpets and synthetic clothing."],"optionRefs":["p. 728","p. 728","p. 728","p. 728"],"ref":"p. 728, Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-21","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You are about to install an expansion card and put on an anti-ESD wrist strap. How should it be worn and connected?","context":"You are about to install an expansion card and put on an anti-ESD wrist strap. How should it be worn and connected?","options":["Snug on bare skin, clipped to an unpainted part of the metal chassis.","Loosely over your sleeve, so the strap is not in direct contact with skin.","Snug on bare skin, clipped to a painted panel of the computer's case.","Snug on bare skin, but with its current-limiting resistor taken out first."],"answer":0,"explanation":"The band should fit snugly so the metal stud touches your skin, not over clothing. It is grounded with a plug into a wall socket or a crocodile clip on a grounded point or unpainted chassis metal. Straps need a working current-limiting resistor and should be tested daily.","optionWhy":["Correct. The stud must touch your skin and the strap must be grounded, for example to unpainted chassis metal.","The strap must not be worn over clothing; the metal stud has to make contact with your skin.","The clip should attach to a grounded point or an unpainted part of the chassis. Paint insulates the connection.","Straps must have a working current-limiting resistor for safety and should be tested daily."],"optionRefs":["p. 728","p. 728","p. 728","p. 729"],"ref":"pp. 728–729, Proper Component Handling","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-22","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You have removed a working RAM module that you want to keep as a spare. How should it be stored?","context":"You have removed a working RAM module that you want to keep as a spare. How should it be stored?","options":["In a shiny gray antistatic bag with the top sealed or folded and sealed.","In a pink dissipative bag, which is designed for static-sensitive chips.","In an open antistatic bag, so trapped charge can escape through the top.","On top of an antistatic bag, since the outer coating protects the module."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Antistatic bags are coated with a conductive material that stops static discharging through the inside of the bag; they are usually shiny gray. Seal the bag or fold the top over and seal it. Pink or blue dissipative packaging is for non-static-sensitive items packed near sensitive ones.","optionWhy":["Correct. Antistatic bags protect sensitive parts; seal the bag or fold over and seal the top.","Pink or blue dissipative packaging is for non-static-sensitive items packed near static-sensitive ones.","To protect the contents fully, seal the bag or at least fold the top over and seal it down.","The coating stops static discharging through the inside of the bag. The module must go inside."],"optionRefs":["p. 730","p. 730","p. 730","p. 730"],"ref":"p. 730, Proper Component Storage","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-23","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"Every time a large lift motor starts in the building, the voltage dips briefly and some PCs power off. What is this event called?","context":"Every time a large lift motor starts in the building, the voltage dips briefly and some PCs power off. What is this event called?","options":["An under-voltage event, also called a brownout, caused by inrush current.","A surge, which is a brief increase in voltage above the normal value.","A blackout, which is a complete loss of power from the building supply.","A spike, which is an intense surge often caused by a lightning strike."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Devices with large motors need a high starting (inrush) current, which can make the building voltage dip briefly. This under-voltage event can power off computers and is sometimes called a brownout.","optionWhy":["Correct. Large motors draw inrush current that can cause a brief under-voltage event, or brownout.","A surge is a brief voltage increase. A dip in voltage is an under-voltage event.","A blackout is a complete loss of power. A brief dip in voltage is a brownout.","A spike is an intense surge, an increase in voltage. Here the voltage drops."],"optionRefs":["p. 731","p. 730","p. 731","p. 730"],"ref":"pp. 730–731, Building Power Issues and Mitigations","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-24","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You are plugging devices into a surge protector rated at 15 A. Using the 80 percent rule of thumb, what is the most the devices together should draw?","context":"You are plugging devices into a surge protector rated at 15 A. Using the 80 percent rule of thumb, what is the most the devices together should draw?","options":["12 A, since you should only use 80 percent of the rated capacity.","15 A, since the rating is the safe continuous load for the protector.","13 A, since that is the maximum load for every power strip socket.","9 A, since you should only use 60 percent of the rated capacity."],"answer":0,"explanation":"As a rule of thumb, you should only use 80% of a surge protector's rated capacity. For a 15 A protector, the connected devices should draw no more than 12 A.","optionWhy":["Correct. Using only 80% of the rated capacity means 15 A × 0.8 = 12 A of total draw.","The rule of thumb is to use only 80% of the rated capacity, not the full rating.","13 A is a typical power strip maximum mentioned elsewhere, but the 80% rule for a 15 A protector gives 12 A.","The guide's rule of thumb is 80% of rated capacity, which gives 12 A for a 15 A protector."],"optionRefs":["p. 731","p. 731","pp. 724, 731","p. 731"],"ref":"p. 731, Surge Suppressors","difficulty":"Calculation","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-25","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"You are comparing two surge protectors. Which pair of specifications gives better protection?","context":"You are comparing two surge protectors. Which pair of specifications gives better protection?","options":["A clamping voltage of 300 V and an energy rating of 600 joules or more.","A clamping voltage of 800 V and an energy rating of 600 joules or more.","A clamping voltage of 300 V and an energy rating of 200 joules or less.","A clamping voltage of 800 V and an energy rating of 200 joules or less."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A lower clamping voltage (such as 400 V or 300 V) means the protection activates sooner, and a joules rating of 600 or more means it can absorb more energy. Each surge event degrades the suppressor's capability.","optionWhy":["Correct. Lower clamping voltages and 600+ joules offer better protection.","A higher clamping voltage means the protection activates later. 400 V or 300 V gives better protection.","The joules rating is the energy the protector can absorb; 600 joules or more offers better protection.","Both are worse: a higher clamping voltage and a low joules rating give weaker protection."],"optionRefs":["p. 731","p. 731","p. 731","p. 731"],"ref":"p. 731, Surge Suppressors","difficulty":"Comparison","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-26","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A small office wants a UPS for a 400 W server and a 50 W switch. Using the watts × 1.67 formula, roughly what is the minimum VA rating needed?","context":"A small office wants a UPS for a 400 W server and a 50 W switch. Using the watts × 1.67 formula, roughly what is the minimum VA rating needed?","options":["About 752 VA, so a 1000 VA UPS model would be enough for the load.","About 450 VA, so a 500 VA UPS model would be enough for the load.","About 270 VA, so a 300 VA UPS model would be enough for the load.","About 900 VA, so a 1000 VA UPS model would be needed for the load."],"answer":0,"explanation":"To find the minimum VA, add up the wattage of all attached devices and multiply by 1.67. Here (400 + 50) × 1.67 ≈ 752 VA, so a 1000 VA (1K VA) unit would be enough.","optionWhy":["Correct. (400 + 50) × 1.67 ≈ 752 VA, so a 1K VA unit is sufficient.","450 is just the total wattage. You must multiply by 1.67 to account for the conversion factor.","That divides the wattage by 1.67 instead of multiplying. The minimum VA is about 752.","That doubles the wattage. The guide's formula multiplies the total wattage by 1.67."],"optionRefs":["p. 732","p. 732","p. 732","p. 732"],"ref":"pp. 731–732, Battery Backups","difficulty":"Calculation","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-27","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A cleaning product has spilled in the workshop and you need to know its health hazards, first aid steps, and how to clean it up and dispose of it. Where should you look?","context":"A cleaning product has spilled in the workshop and you need to know its health hazards, first aid steps, and how to clean it up and dispose of it. Where should you look?","options":["The product's material safety data sheet (MSDS) from the supplier.","The asset record for the product in the configuration management database.","The certificate of destruction issued by the disposal company's staff.","The change request form that was filed when the product was purchased."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Suppliers must provide a material safety data sheet (MSDS) with ingredients, health hazards, precautions, first aid information, what to do if the material spills or leaks, and how to recycle or dispose of it safely.","optionWhy":["Correct. An MSDS covers hazards, precautions, first aid, spills, and safe disposal.","A CMDB tracks hardware, software, and licenses. Hazard and spill information is in the MSDS.","A certificate of destruction shows each drive's make, model, serial number, and how it was destroyed. It gives no hazard or first aid information.","An RFC documents a proposed change. Hazard information for chemicals comes from the supplier's MSDS."],"optionRefs":["p. 733","p. 721","p. 713","p. 718"],"ref":"p. 733, Material Safety Data Sheets","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-28","module":22,"lesson":"22B Common Safety and Environmental Procedures","question":"A user brings in a laptop whose battery has swollen and is pushing the case apart. How should you handle and dispose of the battery?","context":"A user brings in a laptop whose battery has swollen and is pushing the case apart. How should you handle and dispose of the battery?","options":["Wear gloves and goggles, contain it, and use an approved recycling facility.","Puncture it to release the gas, then put it in the normal office trash.","Recharge it fully to make it stable again, then return it to the user.","Send it to the municipal incinerator, since heat safely destroys toxins."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Swollen or leaking batteries must be handled very carefully and stored in appropriate containers. Use gloves and safety goggles to minimize the risk of burns from corrosive material, and dispose of the battery through an approved waste management and recycling facility.","optionWhy":["Correct. Handle it with gloves and goggles, store it in a suitable container, and recycle it through an approved facility.","Batteries must go to an approved waste and recycling facility, never into general waste.","A swollen battery must be handled carefully and disposed of through an approved facility, not reused.","Electronic waste must not go to landfills or incinerators. Use an approved recycling facility."],"optionRefs":["p. 733","p. 733","p. 733","p. 733"],"ref":"p. 733, Proper Disposal","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-29","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"You created backup.sh in your home folder on Linux, but it will not run. The PATH variable does not include that folder. What two things are needed to run it?","context":"You created backup.sh in your home folder on Linux, but it will not run. The PATH variable does not include that folder. What two things are needed to run it?","options":["Set the execute permission on the file and run it as ./backup.sh.","Rename the file to backup.bat and run it by typing only backup.","Add a # line at the top of the file and run it by typing only backup.sh.","Open the file in an IDE and then run it by typing only the word backup."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Linux, a script file must have the execute permission set to run. If no PATH variable points to the script, run it from the working directory by preceding the filename with ./ (for example, ./backup.sh), or use the full path.","optionWhy":["Correct. The file needs execute permission, and without a PATH entry you run it with ./ or the full path.","The .bat extension is for Windows batch files. Linux scripts need execute permission and ./ or a full path.","A # starts a comment. Without a PATH entry, typing only the filename will not find the script.","An IDE helps write and debug code, but execute permission and ./ or a full path are still needed."],"optionRefs":["p. 735","p. 738","p. 736","p. 735"],"ref":"p. 735, Shell Scripts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-30","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A Linux script begins with the line #!/bin/bash. What does this line do?","context":"A Linux script begins with the line #!/bin/bash. What does this line do?","options":["It is the shebang, which tells the OS to run the script in the Bash interpreter.","It is a comment, which the interpreter skips over when the script runs.","It sets the execute permission on the script for the user and the group.","It declares a variable called bash that holds the path to the script file."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Every shell script starts with a shebang line that designates which interpreter to use, such as Bash or Ksh. The statements that make up the script's actions then follow, usually on separate lines.","optionWhy":["Correct. The shebang line designates which interpreter runs the script.","Ordinary comment lines start with #, but #! on the first line is the shebang that picks the interpreter.","Execute permission is set on the file itself. The shebang line only names the interpreter.","Variables are labels for values that can change. The first line here is the shebang, not a variable."],"optionRefs":["p. 735","pp. 735–736","p. 735","p. 736"],"ref":"p. 735, Shell Scripts","difficulty":"Definition","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-31","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"You run a script as ./newuser.sh jsmith sales. Inside the Bash script, how do you refer to the value jsmith?","context":"You run a script as ./newuser.sh jsmith sales. Inside the Bash script, how do you refer to the value jsmith?","options":["As $1, since Bash refers to arguments by the order they are entered.","As $0, since Bash counts the first argument it is given from zero.","As $2, since the script name itself counts as the first argument.","As $jsmith, since Bash creates a named variable for each argument."],"answer":0,"explanation":"An argument (or parameter) is a variable passed to the script when it is executed. In Bash, $1, $2, and so on refer to arguments by position, so jsmith is $1 and sales is $2.","optionWhy":["Correct. The first argument is $1, the second is $2, and so on.","The guide gives $1, $2, and so on for arguments by position. jsmith is the first argument, $1.","The guide refers to the first argument entered as $1, so jsmith is $1 and sales is $2.","Bash refers to arguments by position ($1, $2). Named arguments are supported by other languages."],"optionRefs":["p. 736","p. 736","p. 736","p. 736"],"ref":"p. 736, Variables","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-32","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A payroll script uses a tax percentage that must stay the same for the whole time the script runs. What should it be stored as?","context":"A payroll script uses a tax percentage that must stay the same for the whole time the script runs. What should it be stored as?","options":["A constant, a label for a value that stays the same as the script runs.","A variable, a label for a value that changes as the script is executing.","An argument, a value that is passed to the script when it is executed.","A comment, a line that is skipped by the interpreter when the script runs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A constant is a label for a value that remains the same throughout the execution of the script, such as a tax percentage. A variable is a label for a value that can change as the script runs.","optionWhy":["Correct. Constants hold values that do not change during the script, such as tax percentages.","A variable can change during execution. A value that must stay fixed is a constant.","Arguments are passed in when the script is run and can differ each time. A fixed rate is a constant.","Comments are ignored by the interpreter, so a value stored in a comment could not be used."],"optionRefs":["p. 736","p. 736","p. 736","p. 736"],"ref":"p. 736, Variables","difficulty":"Definition","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-33","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"You need a script that pings every host address from 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.254, one after another. Which construct is the best fit?","context":"You need a script that pings every host address from 192.168.1.1 to 192.168.1.254, one after another. Which construct is the best fit?","options":["A For loop, since the number of times it will repeat is known in advance.","An Until loop, since it repeats until one of the hosts sends back a reply.","An If branch, since it runs different code for each of the host addresses.","A constant, since the address range does not change while the script runs."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A For loop is used when the number of iterations is predictable, such as running ping once for each of 254 host addresses. While or Until loops repeat an indeterminate number of times until a condition is met.","optionWhy":["Correct. A For loop suits a predictable number of iterations, such as 254 addresses.","An Until loop repeats an indeterminate number of times until a condition is met, not once per address.","A branch picks one sequence of instructions based on a test. It does not repeat for each address.","A constant holds a fixed value. Repeating ping for each address needs a loop."],"optionRefs":["p. 737","p. 737","p. 736","p. 736"],"ref":"p. 737, Loops","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-34","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A script uses an Until loop to keep pinging a server until it replies. What condition ends the loop?","context":"A script uses an Until loop to keep pinging a server until it replies. What condition ends the loop?","options":["The server sends a reply, so the logical condition is met","The script has run a fixed number of times, set in advance","The script restarts the server after every failed ping","The user closes the Bash shell window"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A While or Until loop repeats an indeterminate number of times until a logical condition is met. In the guide's example, the condition is a reply from the server (Student Guide p. 737).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a While or Until loop repeats until a logical condition is met. Here the condition is a reply from the server.","A fixed number of repetitions is a For loop. The guide describes an Until loop as repeating an indeterminate number of times.","The guide's Until example only pings the address until a reply is received. It does not restart the server after each failed ping.","Closing the shell is not the logical condition in the guide's Until example. The loop ends when a reply is received."],"optionRefs":["p. 737, Loops","p. 737, Loops","p. 737, Loops","p. 737, Loops"],"ref":"p. 737, Loops","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-35","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"In a Bash test, you want a branch to run only if the disk usage value is greater than or equal to 90. Which switch-notation operator do you use?","context":"In a Bash test, you want a branch to run only if the disk usage value is greater than or equal to 90. Which switch-notation operator do you use?","options":["-ge, which means is greater than or equal to the value it is tested against.","-gt, which means is greater than or equal to the value it is tested against.","-le, which means is greater than or equal to the value it is tested against.","-ne, which means is greater than or equal to the value it is tested against."],"answer":0,"explanation":"In switch notation, -ge means greater than or equal to (>=). The other switches are -eq (==), -ne (!=), -lt (<), -gt (>), and -le (<=).","optionWhy":["Correct. -ge is the switch notation for >=, so it matches 90 and every value above it.","-gt means is greater than (>), so a value of exactly 90 would not match.","-le means less than or equal to (<=), the opposite of what is needed.","-ne means is not equal to (!=). The switch for greater than or equal to is -ge."],"optionRefs":["p. 737","p. 737","p. 737","p. 737"],"ref":"p. 737, Operators","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-36","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A colleague sends you a file named deploy.ps1 to edit and test on Windows. What kind of script is it, and which Microsoft tool is designed for developing it?","context":"A colleague sends you a file named deploy.ps1 to edit and test on Windows. What kind of script is it, and which Microsoft tool is designed for developing it?","options":["A PowerShell script, which you can work on in the PowerShell ISE.","A VBScript file, which you run in the cscript.exe command interpreter.","A batch file, which you run in the basic Windows CMD interpreter shell.","A Python script, which you open in the Python IDLE environment to edit."],"answer":0,"explanation":"PowerShell script files use the .ps1 extension, and Microsoft provides the Windows PowerShell Integrated Scripting Environment (ISE) for rapid development. PowerShell cmdlets use a Verb-Noun naming convention.","optionWhy":["Correct. .ps1 files are PowerShell scripts, and the ISE is Microsoft's tool for developing them.","VBScript files use the .vbs extension. A .ps1 file is a PowerShell script.","Batch files for the CMD interpreter use the .bat extension, not .ps1.","Python scripts use the .py extension. IDLE is Python's development environment, not PowerShell's."],"optionRefs":["p. 738","p. 738","p. 738","pp. 740–741"],"ref":"p. 738, Windows Scripts","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-37","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A legacy VBScript shows its output in pop-up dialog boxes, but you want the output shown in a command prompt window instead. How should you run it?","context":"A legacy VBScript shows its output in pop-up dialog boxes, but you want the output shown in a command prompt window instead. How should you run it?","options":["Run it with cscript.exe, which shows the output in a command prompt.","Run it with wscript.exe, which shows the output in a command prompt.","Rename it to .bat, since batch files can run all VBScript statements.","Run it with pythonw.exe, which shows the output in a command prompt."],"answer":0,"explanation":"VBScript (.vbs) files run with wscript.exe by default, which shows output in a desktop window or dialog. Running the script with cscript.exe shows the output in a command prompt.","optionWhy":["Correct. cscript.exe runs VBScript with output shown in a command prompt.","wscript.exe is the default, and it shows output in a desktop window or dialog.","Batch files are for the CMD interpreter and use a different language. VBScript is run by wscript or cscript.","pythonw.exe is Python's windowed interpreter on Windows. It does not run VBScript."],"optionRefs":["p. 738","p. 738","p. 738","p. 740"],"ref":"p. 738, VBScript","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-38","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"On a Linux server, a script written in Python 3 syntax fails when you run it with the command python script.py. What is the likely fix?","context":"On a Linux server, a script written in Python 3 syntax fails when you run it with the command python script.py. What is the likely fix?","options":["Run it with python3, since python runs scripts in the version 2 interpreter.","Rename it to script.pyw, since .pyw files always run in version 3 instead.","Rewrite it in Python 2 syntax, since version 2 is the current supported release.","Add #!/bin/bash at the top, since Python scripts need Bash to interpret them."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Python 2 and 3 can both be installed. On Linux, the keyword python runs a script as version 2, while python3 uses the version 3 interpreter. Python 2 reached end of life in 2020, so scripts should use version 3 syntax.","optionWhy":["Correct. On Linux, python runs version 2 and python3 runs version 3.","The .pyw extension is associated with pythonw.exe, the windowed interpreter in Windows, not with version 3.","Python 2 has been end of life since 2020, so scripts should be updated to version 3, not back to 2.","A Bash shebang would make the Bash interpreter run the file. Python code needs a Python interpreter."],"optionRefs":["p. 741","p. 740","p. 741","p. 735"],"ref":"p. 741, Python","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-39","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A PowerShell logon script maps drive L: with New-PSDrive, but it fails for users who already have something mapped to L:. How should the script handle this?","context":"A PowerShell logon script maps drive L: with New-PSDrive, but it fails for users who already have something mapped to L:. How should the script handle this?","options":["Add an If check with Test-Path that removes any existing L: mapping first.","Replace New-PSDrive with the mount command, which overwrites existing drives.","Add Restart-Computer -Force so the old mapping is cleared before each run.","Wrap the command in a For loop that keeps on trying until the mapping works."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Mapping a drive to a letter already in use returns an error. You can anticipate this with an If condition: use Test-Path to check for L: and remove the existing mapping before running New-PSDrive. Error handling is key to robust scripts.","optionWhy":["Correct. An If condition using Test-Path removes an existing mapping before New-PSDrive runs.","mount and umount are used in Linux. Drive mapping is a Windows-only concept.","Restarting is not the guide's fix. Anticipating the error with an If condition is the error-handling approach.","Repeating the same command would keep hitting the error and risk an endless loop. Remove the existing mapping first."],"optionRefs":["p. 742","p. 742","p. 742","pp. 737, 742"],"ref":"p. 742, Remapping Network Drives","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-40","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"You want a batch file to install install.msi silently on many PCs without any prompts. Which command line does this?","context":"You want a batch file to install install.msi silently on many PCs without any prompts. Which command line does this?","options":["msiexec C:\\Installs\\install.msi /qn","net use C:\\Installs\\install.msi /qn","wusa.exe C:\\Installs\\install.msi /S","cscript.exe C:\\Installs\\install.msi"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Installers are typically .EXE files or Windows Installer (.MSI) packages. To use a Windows Installer package in a batch file, add the msiexec command, for example msiexec C:\\David\\Downloads\\install.msi /qn.","optionWhy":["Correct. Windows Installer packages are run with msiexec, and /qn is used in the guide's silent example.","net use maps network drives in a batch file. It does not install software.","wusa.exe is called from a batch file to perform update tasks, not to run an MSI package.","cscript.exe runs scripts such as VBScript with output in a command prompt. MSI packages use msiexec."],"optionRefs":["p. 742","p. 742","p. 742","p. 738"],"ref":"p. 742, Installation of Applications","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-41","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"After pushing updates, you want a PowerShell script to reboot the PC even if programs raise warnings. Which command does this?","context":"After pushing updates, you want a PowerShell script to reboot the PC even if programs raise warnings. Which command does this?","options":["Restart-Computer -Force","shutdown -r in PowerShell","New-PSDrive -Force -Root","Write-Host Restart -Force"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In PowerShell, the Restart-Computer cmdlet restarts the machine, and the -Force parameter can be used to ignore any warnings that might be generated. In Bash, the command is shutdown -r.","optionWhy":["Correct. Restart-Computer reboots, and -Force ignores any warnings.","shutdown -r is the command given for restarting a Linux host in Bash, not the PowerShell cmdlet.","New-PSDrive maps a network drive in PowerShell. It does not restart the computer.","Write-Host sends output to the terminal. It would just print text, not restart the PC."],"optionRefs":["p. 742","p. 742","p. 742","p. 738"],"ref":"p. 742, Restarting Machines","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-42","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"You wrote a script that uses robocopy to copy users' files to a server. How can you make it run automatically every night on Windows?","context":"You wrote a script that uses robocopy to copy users' files to a server. How can you make it run automatically every night on Windows?","options":["Schedule the script with Windows Task Scheduler to run every night.","Schedule the script with the cron service built into Windows systems.","Put the script in an Until loop that runs again as soon as it finishes.","Add Restart-Computer to the script so it runs every time the PC restarts."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A simple backup can use file-copy tools such as robocopy in Windows. The script can be set to run automatically using Windows Task Scheduler, or via cron in Linux.","optionWhy":["Correct. Windows Task Scheduler runs scripts automatically; cron does the same job in Linux.","cron is the Linux scheduler. On Windows, scripts are scheduled with Task Scheduler.","An endless loop risks the script hanging and wasting resources. Task Scheduler is the right way to run it on a schedule.","Restart-Computer reboots the machine. It does not schedule a script to run each night."],"optionRefs":["p. 743","p. 743","pp. 737, 744","p. 742"],"ref":"p. 743, Automated Backups","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-43","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A vendor's maintenance script only works if you first disable the antivirus scanner and open extra firewall ports. What should you do?","context":"A vendor's maintenance script only works if you first disable the antivirus scanner and open extra firewall ports. What should you do?","options":["Do not run it, since a script that needs security disabled is not safe.","Run it once with antivirus off, then turn the scanner back on afterward.","Run it with full admin rights so it no longer needs security to be off.","Enable the script interpreter on every host so the script runs everywhere."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Scripts that can only be made to work by disabling security mechanisms are not safe enough to consider running. Test all code in a development environment, and record any required host changes in new configuration baselines.","optionWhy":["Correct. Scripts that only work with security mechanisms disabled are not safe enough to run.","Disabling scanning so a script can run weakens security. The guide says such scripts should not be run.","Scripts should run with the minimum privileges necessary, so full admin rights would increase risk.","Enabling an interpreter that is not a default feature expands the attack surface."],"optionRefs":["p. 744","p. 744","p. 743","p. 743"],"ref":"pp. 743–744, Inadvertent System-Settings Changes","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-44","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"Your team keeps its automation scripts on a shared drive that anyone can edit. What practice would best stop an attacker from changing a script into a Trojan?","context":"Your team keeps its automation scripts on a shared drive that anyone can edit. What practice would best stop an attacker from changing a script into a Trojan?","options":["Put the scripts under access and version controls to block changes.","Give every script full admin rights so that it cannot be tampered with.","Convert every script to PowerShell, since it cannot be used for malware.","Disable antivirus on the drive so it does not block the scripts at run time."],"answer":0,"explanation":"A threat actor could modify script source code to make it act as malware, using the script as a Trojan. To mitigate this, script source code should be under access and version controls, scanned and tested before deployment, and run with minimum privileges.","optionWhy":["Correct. Access and version controls prevent unauthorized changes to script source code.","Scripts should run with the minimum privileges necessary. Admin rights would make a tampered script more dangerous.","Threat actors use environments such as PowerShell to craft fileless malware, so changing language is no fix.","Disabling scanning weakens security. Code should be scanned and tested for vulnerabilities before deployment."],"optionRefs":["p. 743","p. 743","p. 743","pp. 743–744"],"ref":"p. 743, Malware Risks","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-45","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A logging script was deployed without much testing. A week later, a server stops responding because its disk is completely full of the script's log files. What does this show?","context":"A logging script was deployed without much testing. A week later, a server stops responding because its disk is completely full of the script's log files. What does this show?","options":["Accidental DoS from mishandled resources; scripts need testing and monitoring.","A memory-handling flaw that only affects scripting languages like Bash.","A Trojan, since an attacker must have edited the script to fill the disk.","A hallucination, since the script made up data that was never generated."],"answer":0,"explanation":"Scripts can cause accidental DoS by mishandling resources, for example by creating files such as logs or temp files that deplete disk storage. Every script must be tested before deployment and monitored while running.","optionWhy":["Correct. Filling disk storage with log files is mishandling of resources, causing accidental DoS.","Scripting languages are considered safe with respect to memory handling. That issue is with C/C++.","Nothing suggests tampering. The guide describes non-malicious resource mishandling as a common cause.","A hallucination is an AI limitation from Module 21, not a scripting issue. This is resource mishandling."],"optionRefs":["p. 744","p. 744","pp. 743–744","p. 744"],"ref":"p. 744, Browser or System Crashes Due to Mishandling of Resources","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-46","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A web developer hands you a script file named menu.js. What kind of script is it, and where does this language most commonly run?","context":"A web developer hands you a script file named menu.js. What kind of script is it, and where does this language most commonly run?","options":["JavaScript; it runs in web browsers and web servers","A Bash script; it runs mainly on Linux servers","A Python script; it's compiled before it runs","A VBScript file; it runs only in Command Prompt"],"answer":0,"explanation":"JavaScript is designed to implement interactive web-based content and web apps, and most web servers and browsers are configured with a JavaScript interpreter. Script files are identified by the .js extension. Windows Script Host and macOS (JXA) also support it.","optionWhy":["Correct. JavaScript files use the .js extension, and most web servers and browsers include a JavaScript interpreter.","Bash is closely tied to Linux, but .js identifies a JavaScript file, not a Bash script.","Python script files use the .py extension, and Python can run through an interpreter or be compiled as a binary.","VBScript is closely tied to Windows. A .js extension identifies JavaScript, which runs in browsers and web servers."],"optionRefs":["p. 739","p. 739","p. 740","p. 739"],"ref":"p. 739, JavaScript","difficulty":"Scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-51","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user signs in with a Microsoft account and wants to see the files saved to her cloud storage in File Explorer. Which system object shows them?","context":"A user signs in with a Microsoft account and wants to see the files saved to her cloud storage in File Explorer. Which system object shows them?","options":["OneDrive, which holds files saved to your cloud storage","This PC, which lists personal folders and attached drives","Network, which lists computers and shared resources","User account, which holds the profile's personal data folders"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The OneDrive system object shows files and folders saved to cloud storage when the computer is signed in with a Microsoft account.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says OneDrive shows the files and folders saved to your cloud storage service on the Internet if you sign in with a Microsoft account.","This PC shows the personal folders in the profile, plus the fixed disks and removable drives attached to the PC. It does not show cloud storage.","The Network object contains computers and shared resources on the local network. It is not the place for the user's cloud storage.","The user account object contains the personal data folders for the signed-in profile. Cloud files are not described under this object."],"optionRefs":["p. 381, System Objects","p. 381, System Objects","p. 381, System Objects","p. 381, System Objects"],"ref":"p. 381, System Objects","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-52","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A technician needs to tell a colleague which drive holds the data files on a PC. Within the This PC object, how are drives referred to?","context":"A technician needs to tell a colleague which drive holds the data files on a PC. Within the This PC object, how are drives referred to?","options":["By letters and optional labels, such as C: or Data","By a serial number printed on the disk itself","By the IP address of the disk controller in use","By the name of the user account that owns the data"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Within This PC, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels.","The guide does not refer to drives by serial numbers. Within This PC, drives are identified by letters and optional labels.","The guide does not describe drives by IP address. A mapped network folder is shown as a drive letter, not an IP address.","The user account object holds personal folders. The guide says drives are referred to by letters and optional labels."],"optionRefs":["p. 381, Drives and Folders","p. 381, Drives and Folders","p. 381, Drives and Folders","p. 381, Drives and Folders"],"ref":"p. 381, Drives and Folders","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-53","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user wants shortcuts to the built-in virus and threat protection and firewall management pages in one place. Which Windows page provides them?","context":"A user wants shortcuts to the built-in virus and threat protection and firewall management pages in one place. Which Windows page provides them?","options":["Windows Security, with shortcuts to antivirus and firewall","Network and Internet settings, for adapters and connections","Personalization settings, for themes and colors","Time and Language settings, for date and region options"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for Windows Defender virus and threat protection and the firewall.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus and threat protection and firewall.","Network and Internet settings manage adapters and connection status. The guide does not describe them as shortcuts to antivirus and firewall pages.","Personalization settings select and customize themes, such as wallpaper and colors. They do not provide antivirus or firewall shortcuts.","Time and Language settings set the date, time, region, and keyboard options. They are not used for antivirus or firewall management."],"optionRefs":["p. 388, Windows Security","p. 398, Network Settings","p. 379, Personalization Settings","p. 378, Time and Language Settings"],"ref":"p. 388, Windows Security","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-54","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A laptop connects to a public Wi-Fi network in a café. Which network profile type gives the more restrictive firewall policy?","context":"A laptop connects to a public Wi-Fi network in a café. Which network profile type gives the more restrictive firewall policy?","options":["Public, which has the more restrictive firewall policy","Private, which has a less restrictive firewall policy","Domain, which is also less restrictive than public","Cellular, which is an adapter type and not a profile"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A public network is configured with more restrictive firewall policies than a private or domain network.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a public network is configured with more restrictive firewall policies than a private or domain network, and the profile type determines the firewall settings.","A private network is less restrictive than a public network, according to the guide. It is not the most restrictive profile.","A domain network is also less restrictive than a public network in the guide's explanation. It is not the most restrictive profile.","Cellular is an adapter type, not a network profile. The guide says profile type, such as public, private, or domain, sets the firewall policy."],"optionRefs":["p. 398, Network Settings","p. 398, Network Settings","p. 398, Network Settings","p. 398, Network Settings"],"ref":"p. 398, Network Settings","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-55","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows User Settings","question":"A user wants to change the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, and color scheme. Which Windows settings group handles these?","context":"A user wants to change the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, and color scheme. Which Windows settings group handles these?","options":["Personalization Settings, for wallpaper, screen saver, and colors","Ease of Access, for cursor, contrast, and magnifier options","Time and Language Settings, for date, region, and keyboard","Windows Security, for antivirus and firewall shortcuts"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personalization settings select and customize themes, including wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and Start menu and taskbar properties.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Personalization settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and taskbar.","Ease of Access settings configure input and output options, such as cursor indicators, high contrast, and the Magnifier. They do not set wallpaper or colors.","Time and Language settings handle date, time, region, and keyboard options. They do not control the wallpaper or the color scheme.","The Windows Security page provides shortcuts to antivirus and firewall management. It does not change wallpaper or colors."],"optionRefs":["p. 379, Personalization Settings","p. 379, Ease of Access","p. 378, Time and Language Settings","p. 388, Windows Security"],"ref":"p. 379, Personalization Settings","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-GAP-01","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"A technician copies a folder with robocopy but must leave out every file named *.tmp. Which switch excludes files that match specified names?","context":"A technician copies a folder with robocopy but must leave out every file named *.tmp. Which switch excludes files that match specified names?","options":["/xf, which excludes files that match names or paths","/xd, which excludes whole directories instead of files","/S, which copies subdirectories but skips empty ones","/L, which lists what would be copied without copying"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The /xf switch excludes files that match the specified names or paths.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says /xf excludes files that match the specified names or paths.","The guide says /xd excludes directories that match the specified names or paths, not files.","The guide says /S copies subdirectories, excluding empty ones. It does not exclude files by name.","The guide says /L lists the files and directories that would be copied without actually copying them."],"optionRefs":["p. 435, Copying Directory Structures","p. 435, Copying Directory Structures","p. 435, Copying Directory Structures","p. 435, Copying Directory Structures"],"ref":"p. 435, Copying Directory Structures","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-GAP-01","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"A company runs virtual machines and wants a protocol that gives a remote display to monitor and interact with those machine environments. Which protocol is this?","context":"A company runs virtual machines and wants a protocol that gives a remote display to monitor and interact with those machine environments. Which protocol is this?","options":["Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP), a Windows host connection protocol","Secure Shell (SSH), a command-line remote access protocol","Virtual Private Network (VPN), a secure tunnel between networks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"SPICE provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says SPICE provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet.","RDP is a Windows remote connection protocol for a host PC. The guide describes SPICE as the remote display for virtual machine environments.","SSH is a remote access protocol for command-line sessions. It is not the remote display described for virtual machine environments.","A VPN creates a secure tunnel between networks. It is not the remote display described for monitoring virtual machine environments."],"optionRefs":["p. 475, Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","p. 469, Remote Desktop Protocol","p. 476, Secure Shell","p. 475, Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools"],"ref":"p. 475, Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-GAP-01","module":15,"lesson":"15A Logical Security","question":"A user signs in to a banking app. The app sends a code to a trusted phone before access is granted, because the sign-in came from a new device. What is this method?","context":"A user signs in to a banking app. The app sends a code to a trusted phone before access is granted, because the sign-in came from a new device. What is this method?","options":["2-step verification, which uses a soft token to confirm sign-ins","Single sign-on (SSO), which authenticates once for many services","Biometric lock on the device, using a physical trait to unlock","A password only, with no second check on new devices"],"answer":0,"explanation":"2-step verification uses a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic, for example when a new device is detected.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes 2-step verification as using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic, for example when a new device is detected.","Single sign-on lets a user authenticate once for several services. The guide does not describe SSO as checking a new device with a code.","A biometric lock uses a physical trait to unlock a device. The guide describes 2-step verification as a soft-token check.","A password alone does not check a new device with a soft token. The guide's 2-step verification adds a second check."],"optionRefs":["p. 520, 2-Step Verification","p. 521, Single Sign-On","p. 520, 2-Step Verification","p. 520, 2-Step Verification"],"ref":"p. 520, 2-Step Verification","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M15-GAP-02","module":15,"lesson":"15B Windows Security Settings","question":"A team encrypts large nightly backup files and wants a fast method. Which type of encryption uses one secret key to encrypt and decrypt?","context":"A team encrypts large nightly backup files and wants a fast method. Which type of encryption uses one secret key to encrypt and decrypt?","options":["Symmetric encryption, which uses one secret key for both","Asymmetric encryption, which uses a key pair and is slower","Cryptographic hashing, which cannot be decrypted by design","Digital signatures, which prove the origin of a message"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt, and it is much faster than asymmetric encryption.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a symmetric cipher uses one secret key for both encryption and decryption, and it is much faster than an asymmetric cipher.","Asymmetric encryption uses a key pair. The guide describes it as slower than symmetric ciphers, so it is not the fast bulk method here.","A hash is a fixed short representation of data, and it cannot be decrypted. It does not encrypt backup files.","Digital signatures prove the origin of a message. They do not encrypt the backup files for storage."],"optionRefs":["p. 512, Symmetric Encryption","p. 512, Asymmetric Encryption","p. 512, Cryptographic Hashes","p. 512, Digital Signatures and Key Exchange"],"ref":"p. 512, Symmetric Encryption","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-GAP-01","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"A Linux system has been running for a long time. Which process ID is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start?","context":"A Linux system has been running for a long time. Which process ID is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start?","options":["PID 1, the reserved number for the initial daemon","PID 0, a number the guide does not assign to any process","The highest PID number, which belongs to the newest process","The PID of the user's login shell session started at sign-in"],"answer":0,"explanation":"PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, and it is the parent of all other processes.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, and serves as the parent of all other processes.","The guide describes PID 1 as the initial daemon. It does not reserve PID 0 for the initial daemon in this passage.","The guide says PIDs increase with each new process. The highest number is the newest process, not the initial daemon.","The guide says subsequent processes, whether started by the system or a user, receive the next available number. The login shell is not the initial daemon."],"optionRefs":["p. 573, Process Monitoring Commands","p. 573, Process Monitoring Commands","p. 573, Process Monitoring Commands","p. 573, Process Monitoring Commands"],"ref":"p. 573, Process Monitoring Commands","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-GAP-02","module":17,"lesson":"17B Package & Network Mgmt","question":"A Linux computer is powered on and needs to load the kernel into memory before the operating system starts. Which component does this?","context":"A Linux computer is powered on and needs to load the kernel into memory before the operating system starts. Which component does this?","options":["A bootloader, such as GRUB, which loads the kernel","A shell, which gives a command-line environment to users","A text editor, used to create and change plain text files","A desktop environment, which gives a graphical interface"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Before the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system and is responsible for loading the kernel into memory.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a bootloader initializes the system before the OS loads and is responsible for loading the kernel into memory. GRUB is a common Linux bootloader.","A shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS. It starts after the operating system has loaded.","Text editors create and change plain text files. They do not load the kernel into memory.","A desktop environment provides a graphical interface on top of the OS. It is not responsible for loading the kernel."],"optionRefs":["p. 561, Bootloaders","p. 561, Shells and Terminals","p. 562, Text Editors","p. 562, Desktop Environments"],"ref":"p. 561, Bootloaders","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-GAP-01","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"A technician wants a browser that uses the same underlying codebase as Google Chrome. Which Microsoft browser is this?","context":"A technician wants a browser that uses the same underlying codebase as Google Chrome. Which Microsoft browser is this?","options":["Microsoft Edge, which uses the Chromium codebase like Chrome","Internet Explorer, which is no longer supported by Microsoft","Mozilla Firefox, an alternative browser named in the guide","Apple Safari, which is tightly integrated with macOS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Edge, Microsoft's replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium codebase as Google Chrome.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Edge, Microsoft's replacement browser, uses the same underlying Chromium codebase as Google Chrome.","The guide says Internet Explorer used to dominate the market but is no longer supported. It does not use the Chromium codebase.","Firefox is an alternative browser named in the guide. It is not described as using the Chromium codebase.","Safari is tightly integrated within macOS and iOS/iPadOS. The guide does not describe it as using the Chromium codebase."],"optionRefs":["p. 651, Browser Selection and Installation","p. 651, Browser Selection and Installation","p. 651, Browser Selection and Installation","p. 651, Browser Selection and Installation"],"ref":"p. 651, Browser Selection and Installation","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-GAP-01","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"A company asks staff to identify data that could be used to contact or impersonate a person. Which example is personally identifiable information?","context":"A company asks staff to identify data that could be used to contact or impersonate a person. Which example is personally identifiable information?","options":["A cell phone number, which can contact or identify a person","The total number of files stored in a shared folder","The make and model of a printer used in the office","An anonymous, de-identified research dataset with no names"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Personally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual, and a cell phone number is a good example.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says PII is data that can identify, contact, or locate an individual, and gives a cell phone number as a good example.","A file count does not identify, contact, or locate a person, so the guide does not treat it as PII.","A printer's model describes equipment, not an individual. The guide's PII examples are about people.","The guide describes de-identified data sets as not tied to a specific person, so this is not presented as PII."],"optionRefs":["p. 707, Personally Identifiable Information","p. 707, Personally Identifiable Information","p. 707, Personally Identifiable Information","p. 707, Personally Identifiable Information"],"ref":"p. 707, Personally Identifiable Information","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-GAP-02","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"Two companies share sensitive data, but only the company receiving the data must keep it secret. Which kind of NDA is this?","context":"Two companies share sensitive data, but only the company receiving the data must keep it secret. Which kind of NDA is this?","options":["Unilateral NDA, where only the receiving party is bound","Mutual NDA, where both parties are bound to confidentiality","Acceptable use policy, which sets what a service may be used for","Service level agreement, which sets service metrics for a provider"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In a unilateral NDA, one party shares sensitive data with the other party, and only the receiving party is obligated to keep it confidential.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says in a unilateral NDA, one party shares sensitive data and only the receiving party is obligated to keep it confidential.","In a mutual NDA, both parties are obligated to keep the information confidential. The scenario only binds the receiving party.","An AUP sets out what a person may use a service for. It is not an agreement to keep shared sensitive data secret.","An SLA defines service levels from a provider. It does not oblige the receiving party to keep shared data confidential."],"optionRefs":["p. 709, Non-Disclosure Agreements","p. 709, Non-Disclosure Agreements","p. 347, Policy Documentation","p. 343, Service Level Agreements"],"ref":"p. 709, Non-Disclosure Agreements","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-GAP-01","module":22,"lesson":"22A Change Management","question":"A technician is installing a new server rack and leaves a cable across a walkway. What is the hazard and what should be done?","context":"A technician is installing a new server rack and leaves a cable across a walkway. What is the hazard and what should be done?","options":["A trip hazard; secure cabling with ties or management products","No hazard, because cables do not move while in use","An electrical fire risk only, with no pathway issue involved here","A data breach risk from exposed cables in the walkway area"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk, and cabling should be secured with cable ties or cable management products.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk, and cabling should be secured with ties or cable management products.","The guide defines a trip hazard as any object in a walkway, whether it moves or not. Cables across a path are a hazard.","The guide treats the trip hazard as an issue of pathways and cabling. The cable in the walkway is not described as an electrical fire risk here.","The guide describes trip hazards as physical pathway risks. It does not link cables across a walkway to a data breach."],"optionRefs":["p. 725, Trip Hazards","p. 725, Trip Hazards","p. 725, Trip Hazards","p. 725, Trip Hazards"],"ref":"p. 725, Trip Hazards","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-GAP-02","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"Before lifting a heavy server by yourself, what should you check first?","context":"Before lifting a heavy server by yourself, what should you check first?","options":["The maximum safe lifting weight and site restrictions","How fast you can carry the server to the rack position","Whether the server is powered on at the wall outlet","The color of the server's casing and its front panel"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight and any restrictions in your job description or site safety handbook.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight and any restrictions and guidance in your job description or site safety handbook.","The guide's lifting advice is about safe weight and handbook guidance, not speed of carrying.","The guide's lifting advice focuses on safe weight and site guidance. Power state is covered elsewhere in the guide.","The guide's lifting advice does not refer to the color of equipment."],"optionRefs":["p. 726, Lifting Techniques","p. 726, Lifting Techniques","p. 726, Lifting Techniques","p. 726, Lifting Techniques"],"ref":"p. 726, Lifting Techniques","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-GAP-03","module":22,"lesson":"22C Scripting Basics","question":"A technician places a PC in a warehouse corner with high humidity and dust. What does the guide say about this location?","context":"A technician places a PC in a warehouse corner with high humidity and dust. What does the guide say about this location?","options":["It can damage the PC, so avoid damp or dusty areas","Humidity improves cooling, so it is a good location","Dust has no effect while the fan is running at full speed","Location does not affect the lifespan of the equipment at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions, since location affects operation and lifespan.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the location affects operation and lifespan, and equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions.","The guide says equipment should be kept away from damp conditions. It does not describe humidity as beneficial.","The guide explains that dust builds up on components, heat sinks, and fan blades over time, so dust is a problem.","The guide says the location can affect proper operation and lifespan of computer equipment."],"optionRefs":["p. 726, Environmental Impacts","p. 726, Environmental Impacts","p. 726, Dust Cleanup","p. 726, Environmental Impacts"],"ref":"p. 726, Environmental Impacts","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-GAP-04","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"A technician wants to measure how many amps are flowing through a circuit. Which property does the guide describe as measured in amps?","context":"A technician wants to measure how many amps are flowing through a circuit. Which property does the guide describe as measured in amps?","options":["Current, the flow of charge through a conductor","Voltage, the potential difference between two points","Resistance, which opposes the flow of current in a circuit","Frequency, the number of cycles per second in a signal"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I).","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I).","The guide describes voltage as the potential difference between two points. It is measured in volts, not amps.","The guide's electrical properties passage defines current and voltage. Resistance is not the quantity measured in amps here.","The guide's electrical properties passage does not describe frequency. Amps measure current, which is the flow of charge."],"optionRefs":["p. 723, Electrical Safety","p. 723, Electrical Safety","p. 723, Electrical Safety","p. 723, Electrical Safety"],"ref":"p. 723, Electrical Safety","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-01","module":14,"lesson":"14A Network Troubleshooting","question":"A technician must check whether the file sharing port on a server is open and whether clients are connected to it. Which command helps?","context":"A technician must check whether the file sharing port on a server is open and whether clients are connected to it. Which command helps?","options":["netstat, which lists open ports and connections on the host","ping, which tests whether a host replies to echo requests","tracert, which shows each router on the path to a host","nslookup, which queries DNS servers for records"],"answer":0,"explanation":"netstat investigates open ports and connections on the local host, so you can verify whether file sharing or email ports are open.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says netstat can be used to investigate open ports and connections, to verify whether file sharing or email ports are open and whether clients connect.","ping tests reachability by sending echo requests. The guide does not use it to list open ports and connections.","tracert shows each router on the path to a host. It does not list the open ports on the local server.","nslookup queries DNS servers for records. It does not show which ports are open on a server."],"optionRefs":["p. 465, Troubleshoot Network Ports","p. 459, Troubleshoot Connectivity","p. 463, Using tracert in Windows","p. 464, Using nslookup"],"ref":"p. 465, Troubleshoot Network Ports","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-02","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"A firewall administrator wants to allow a Remote Assistance session through a strict firewall. Why is this hard to do securely?","context":"A firewall administrator wants to allow a Remote Assistance session through a strict firewall. Why is this hard to do securely?","options":["It uses a dynamic port from 49152 to 65535, hard to fix in a rule","It only works on Windows Home, which has no firewall at all","It sends its traffic over an unencrypted port 80 by default","It needs a VPN, so no firewall rule is ever needed at all"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Remote Assistance assigns a port dynamically from the ephemeral range 49152 to 65535, which makes a secure firewall rule difficult.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Remote Assistance assigns a port dynamically from the ephemeral range, which makes it difficult to configure a firewall securely.","The guide does not say Remote Assistance is limited to Windows Home. The difficulty described is the dynamic port.","The guide does not describe Remote Assistance traffic on port 80. Quick Assist uses the encrypted HTTPS port instead.","The guide does not say a VPN removes the firewall requirement. The stated difficulty is the dynamic port range."],"optionRefs":["p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","p. 473, Using Remote Assistance"],"ref":"p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-03","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"A technician wants a quick, encrypted remote help session and asks which shortcut opens Quick Assist on Windows 10 and later. Which one is correct?","context":"A technician wants a quick, encrypted remote help session and asks which shortcut opens Quick Assist on Windows 10 and later. Which one is correct?","options":["CTRL+WINDOWS+Q, the Quick Assist shortcut on Windows 10","msra.exe, the older Remote Assistance program it replaces","CTRL+WINDOWS+R, which opens the Run dialog for commands","CTRL+WINDOWS+D, which shows the desktop on the screen"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Quick Assist is opened with CTRL+WINDOWS+Q and is an alternative to msra.exe, and it works over the encrypted HTTPS port.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide gives CTRL+WINDOWS+Q as the shortcut for Quick Assist, which Windows 10 feature updates introduced as an alternative to msra.exe.","msra.exe is the older Remote Assistance program that Quick Assist is an alternative to. It is not the shortcut for Quick Assist.","CTRL+WINDOWS+R is used to open the Run dialog for commands such as perfmon, according to the guide's Performance Monitor steps. It does not open Quick Assist.","The guide does not list CTRL+WINDOWS+D as the Quick Assist shortcut. The correct shortcut is CTRL+WINDOWS+Q."],"optionRefs":["p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","p. 403, Performance Monitor","p. 473, Using Remote Assistance"],"ref":"p. 473, Using Remote Assistance","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-04","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"Event Viewer shows an event saying disk space is running low, but the system is still working. Which severity level fits?","context":"Event Viewer shows an event saying disk space is running low, but the system is still working. Which severity level fits?","options":["Warning, a condition that could lead to errors if not fixed","Critical, the highest priority, for a halted or unresponsive process","Error, a less severe issue to investigate after critical ones","Information, a noteworthy state that needs no action"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A warning is a condition that could lead to errors or critical issues if not addressed, such as low disk space.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes a warning as a condition that could lead to errors or critical issues if not addressed, and gives low disk space as an example.","Critical events are the highest priority and often indicate a halted or unresponsive process. Low disk space alone does not match that description.","Errors are less severe issues to investigate after the critical ones are resolved. A low-space warning is described as a warning, not an error.","Information events are noteworthy operations or states that do not require action. Low disk space needs attention, so it is not information."],"optionRefs":["p. 481, Event Viewer","p. 481, Event Viewer","p. 481, Event Viewer","p. 481, Event Viewer"],"ref":"p. 481, Event Viewer","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-05","module":14,"lesson":"14C Performance Tools","question":"Task Manager shows 50% disk utilization on a PC with two disks. What might that mean?","context":"Task Manager shows 50% disk utilization on a PC with two disks. What might that mean?","options":["One disk may be at 100% while the other is inactive","Both disks are exactly half full of stored data files","The disk controller has failed and needs replacing now","Half of the RAM is in use by the disk cache memory"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Utilization is measured across all disk devices, so 50% might mean one disk is at 100% while another is inactive.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says utilization is measured across all disk devices, so 50% might mean one disk is at 100% while another is inactive.","Utilization in the guide is about activity, not how full the disks are. It is measured across all disk devices.","The guide does not say 50% utilization means a failed controller. It attributes high utilization to several possible causes.","The guide describes disk utilization for disk devices, not RAM. The 50% figure does not describe memory use."],"optionRefs":["p. 483, Performance Tools","p. 483, Performance Tools","p. 483, Performance Tools","p. 483, Performance Tools"],"ref":"p. 483, Performance Tools","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-06","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"In msconfig's Boot tab, the screenshot shows how long the boot menu waits before choosing the default OS. What is the timeout?","context":"In msconfig's Boot tab, the screenshot shows how long the boot menu waits before choosing the default OS. What is the timeout?","options":["30 seconds, the timeout shown on the Boot tab","10 seconds, a shorter timeout than the guide's example","60 seconds, double the timeout shown in the example","5 seconds, a very short wait before the default OS"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Boot tab in the guide's example shows the timeout set to 30 seconds.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide's description of the Boot tab shows the timeout set to 30 seconds.","The guide's Boot tab example shows 30 seconds, not 10 seconds.","The guide's Boot tab example shows 30 seconds, not 60 seconds.","The guide's Boot tab example shows 30 seconds, not 5 seconds."],"optionRefs":["p. 491, System Configuration Utility","p. 491, System Configuration Utility","p. 491, System Configuration Utility","p. 491, System Configuration Utility"],"ref":"p. 491, System Configuration Utility","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-07","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"On the Windows 10 Startup Settings screen, which key selects Safe Mode?","context":"On the Windows 10 Startup Settings screen, which key selects Safe Mode?","options":["F4, which selects Safe Mode on the Startup Settings screen","F1, which is not the Safe Mode key in the guide","F10, which is not listed for Safe Mode in the guide","F12, which is not listed for Safe Mode in the guide"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says to press F4 to select Safe Mode on the Startup Settings screen.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to press F4 to select Safe Mode, which loads only basic drivers and services.","The guide's Startup Settings example uses F4 for Safe Mode. F1 is not listed for Safe Mode there.","The guide's Startup Settings example uses F4 for Safe Mode. F10 is not listed for Safe Mode there.","The guide's Startup Settings example uses F4 for Safe Mode. F12 is not listed for Safe Mode there."],"optionRefs":["p. 495, Windows 10 Startup Settings","p. 495, Windows 10 Startup Settings","p. 495, Windows 10 Startup Settings","p. 495, Windows 10 Startup Settings"],"ref":"p. 495, Windows 10 Startup Settings","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-08","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"A newly updated display driver causes instability after a restart. What is the quickest fix the guide describes?","context":"A newly updated display driver causes instability after a restart. What is the quickest fix the guide describes?","options":["Roll back to the previous driver in Device Manager","Reinstall the whole operating system to clear the driver","Delete the device's registry key to reset the driver","Run sfc /scannow to replace the driver files"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Device Manager lets you revert to the previous driver: right-click the device, select Properties, and use the roll back option.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a new driver may not work properly, and you can use Device Manager to revert to the previous driver by right-clicking the device and selecting Properties.","The guide describes rolling back the driver in Device Manager as the way to revert. A full reinstall is not the described first step.","The guide's driver rollback uses Device Manager. It does not describe deleting registry keys for this problem.","The guide describes driver rollback in Device Manager. sfc is a different tool and is not the rollback described for drivers."],"optionRefs":["p. 499, Programs and Features","p. 499, Programs and Features","p. 499, Programs and Features","p. 499, Programs and Features"],"ref":"p. 499, Programs and Features","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-09","module":14,"lesson":"14D OS Troubleshooting","question":"A non-critical service keeps failing at startup and slowing the boot. Which first step does the guide suggest?","context":"A non-critical service keeps failing at startup and slowing the boot. Which first step does the guide suggest?","options":["Set the service to delayed start, or try starting it manually","Delete the service entry from the registry for good","Disable every service at startup to speed up the boot","Reinstall Windows immediately, before any other step"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If a service is not a critical dependency for other services, setting it to delayed start may help.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that if a service is not a critical dependency for other services, it may help to set it to delayed start, or to try starting it manually.","The guide does not suggest deleting service entries as a first step. It suggests delayed start or a manual start.","Disabling every service would stop critical dependencies. The guide asks you to check whether a disabled service has affected others.","The guide offers service-level steps before any reinstall. Reinstalling is not the first step for a non-critical service."],"optionRefs":["p. 507, Services","p. 507, Services","p. 507, Services","p. 507, Services"],"ref":"p. 507, Services","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M14-EX-10","module":14,"lesson":"14B Remote Access","question":"A branch office needs its network joined securely to the head office network across the Internet. Which technology creates that link?","context":"A branch office needs its network joined securely to the head office network across the Internet. Which technology creates that link?","options":["A VPN, which creates a tunneled link to the remote network","Remote Desktop, which connects to one host's desktop","SSH, which opens a remote shell session to one host only","netstat, which lists open ports and connections on one PC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer or network to a remote network.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a VPN establishes a tunneled link that joins a local computer to a remote network.","The guide says Remote Desktop establishes a connection to a single host, not a link between two networks.","The guide says SSH also connects to a single host. It does not join two networks.","netstat shows open ports and connections on the local host. It does not create a tunnel between networks."],"optionRefs":["p. 477, Virtual Private Networks","p. 477, Virtual Private Networks","p. 477, Virtual Private Networks","p. 465, Troubleshoot Network Ports"],"ref":"p. 477, Virtual Private Networks","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-01","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"A user wants to add a second language and change the Windows display language. Which settings area handles this?","context":"A user wants to add a second language and change the Windows display language. Which settings area handles this?","options":["Language settings, which show the display language and preferred languages","Personalization settings, which control themes and wallpaper","Ease of Access, which adjusts cursor and contrast options","Privacy and Security settings, which control data collection"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Language settings show the display language and the list of preferred languages, and let you add a language.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the Language settings show the Windows display language and the preferred languages, with an option to add a language.","Personalization settings select and customize themes, wallpaper, and colors. The guide does not put language selection there.","Ease of Access covers input and output aids such as cursor and contrast. The guide does not describe language settings there.","Privacy and Security settings govern what usage data Windows may collect. Language selection is not described there."],"optionRefs":["p. 379, Language settings","p. 379, Personalization Settings","p. 379, Ease of Access","p. 377, Privacy and Security Settings"],"ref":"p. 379, Language settings","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-02","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"A user wants File Explorer to open to Quick Access by default. Where is this option set?","context":"A user wants File Explorer to open to Quick Access by default. Where is this option set?","options":["The General tab in File Explorer Options, for the Quick Access setting","The Windows Security page, for antivirus and firewall shortcuts","Device Manager, which manages hardware devices and their drivers","Task Manager, which lists running processes and resource use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The General tab in File Explorer Options has the option to open File Explorer to Quick Access.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the General tab of File Explorer Options has the option to open File Explorer to Quick Access.","Windows Security provides shortcuts to antivirus and firewall pages. It does not set the File Explorer start location.","Device Manager manages hardware and drivers. It does not control where File Explorer opens.","Task Manager lists running processes and resource use. It does not control the File Explorer start location."],"optionRefs":["p. 383, File Explorer Options","p. 388, Windows Security","p. 401, Windows and power user menu","p. 383, File Explorer Options"],"ref":"p. 383, File Explorer Options","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-03","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"A technician wants Command Prompt, Event Viewer, and Disk Cleanup in one folder. Which folder contains these tools?","context":"A technician wants Command Prompt, Event Viewer, and Disk Cleanup in one folder. Which folder contains these tools?","options":["The Windows Tools folder, which groups admin tools like Event Viewer","The Startup folder, which lists apps that start with Windows","The Recovery folder, which holds system recovery options","The Libraries folder, which groups personal document folders"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Windows Tools folder lists tools such as Character Map, Command Prompt, Disk Cleanup, and Event Viewer.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide's Windows Tools folder lists Command Prompt, Disk Cleanup, and Event Viewer among its options.","The Windows Tools list in the guide contains administrative tools such as Command Prompt and Event Viewer, not the Startup folder.","The guide lists Command Prompt, Disk Cleanup, and Event Viewer in the Windows Tools folder, not in a Recovery folder.","The guide lists these tools in the Windows Tools folder. Libraries are not where Command Prompt and Event Viewer are grouped."],"optionRefs":["p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 399, Windows Tools folder"],"ref":"p. 399, Windows Tools folder","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-04","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"Within the Windows Tools folder, which tool manages Hyper-V virtual machines?","context":"Within the Windows Tools folder, which tool manages Hyper-V virtual machines?","options":["Hyper-V Manager, listed in the Windows Tools folder","Local Security Policy, listed in the Windows Tools folder","Dev Home, listed in the Windows Tools folder for developers","Device Manager, which manages hardware and drivers"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Windows Tools folder includes Hyper-V Manager, along with Hyper-V Quick Create.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide's Windows Tools list includes Hyper-V Manager and Hyper-V Quick Create.","Local Security Policy is listed in the Windows Tools folder, but it manages security settings, not virtual machines.","Dev Home is listed in the Windows Tools folder for developer tasks. It is not described as a virtual machine manager.","Device Manager is in the power user menu and manages hardware and drivers. It is not the Hyper-V tool."],"optionRefs":["p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 399, Windows Tools folder","p. 401, Windows and power user menu"],"ref":"p. 399, Windows Tools folder","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-01","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"On a standalone PC, a user has forgotten a local account password. Which console resets it and lets you add the account to a group?","context":"On a standalone PC, a user has forgotten a local account password. Which console resets it and lets you add the account to a group?","options":["Local Users and Groups console, to reset passwords and manage groups","Certificate Manager, which views certificates for the current user","Group Policy Editor, which shows the local password policy settings","Registry Editor, which edits the Windows configuration database"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Local Users and Groups console creates, modifies, disables, and deletes accounts, and resets passwords.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the Local Users and Groups console manages accounts, including resetting passwords, and that security groups group accounts with similar permissions.","Certificate Manager displays and manages certificate stores. The guide does not describe it as a way to reset account passwords.","Group Policy Editor shows and configures settings such as the password policy. It is not the console the guide describes for resetting a single account's password.","Registry Editor changes configuration data directly. The guide describes it for registry keys, not for resetting account passwords."],"optionRefs":["p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","p. 426, Certificate Manager","p. 427, Group Policy Editor","p. 428, Registry Editor"],"ref":"p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-02","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"Why would a technician place several user accounts into one security group?","context":"Why would a technician place several user accounts into one security group?","options":["To give accounts the same permissions, such as editing files","To encrypt the registry keys used by those accounts on the PC","To set the screen resolution for each account on the machine","To store each account's personal certificates in one place"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Security groups let you group user accounts with similar permissions, such as editing files in a shared folder.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says security groups allow you to group user accounts with similar permissions, like editing files in a shared folder.","The guide does not describe security groups as encrypting registry keys. They group accounts with similar permissions.","Screen resolution is a display setting. The guide describes security groups as a way to group accounts by permissions.","Certificate stores are a separate feature in Certificate Manager. Security groups group accounts with similar permissions."],"optionRefs":["p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console"],"ref":"p. 425, Local Users and Groups Console","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-03","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"A technician needs to find the local password policy on a standalone PC. Where is it in Group Policy Editor?","context":"A technician needs to find the local password policy on a standalone PC. Where is it in Group Policy Editor?","options":["Account Policies, in the Security Settings folder","Registry, under the Windows Settings folder of the policy editor","Control Panel, under User Accounts and password settings","The Startup folder, under Programs and startup settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The password policy folder is under Account Policies in the Security Settings folder.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the password policy folder is under the account policies and security settings folders, selected from the menu on the left.","The guide describes Group Policy Editor as a way to configure settings without editing the registry directly. The password policy is not described under a registry folder.","The guide places the local password policy in Group Policy Editor, not in the Control Panel User Accounts settings.","The Startup folder holds startup programs. The guide places the local password policy in Group Policy Editor under account policies."],"optionRefs":["p. 427, Group Policy Editor","p. 427, Group Policy Editor","p. 427, Group Policy Editor","p. 427, Group Policy Editor"],"ref":"p. 427, Group Policy Editor","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-04","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"At the Command Prompt, the prompt shows C:\\Windows. Which entry makes D: the default drive?","context":"At the Command Prompt, the prompt shows C:\\Windows. Which entry makes D: the default drive?","options":["D:, the drive letter followed by a colon","cd D, the drive letter with cd but without a colon","D, the drive letter on its own with no colon","switch D:, using a switch word before the drive"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The active drive is changed by entering the drive letter followed by a colon, so typing D: switches to the D drive.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER, so typing D: makes D the default drive.","The guide's method is the drive letter followed by a colon. A drive letter without a colon is not the described entry.","The guide says to enter the drive letter followed by a colon. The letter on its own does not switch drives.","The guide describes typing the drive letter followed by a colon. The word switch is not part of the command."],"optionRefs":["p. 434, Changing the Current Drive","p. 434, Changing the Current Drive","p. 434, Changing the Current Drive","p. 434, Changing the Current Drive"],"ref":"p. 434, Changing the Current Drive","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-05","module":13,"lesson":"13A Management Consoles","question":"A technician reads that a disk has a logical storage unit assigned to the OS. How does the guide describe a volume?","context":"A technician reads that a disk has a logical storage unit assigned to the OS. How does the guide describe a volume?","options":["A logical storage unit for the OS, often one to one with a partition","A single physical sector on the disk platter surface","A file system type such as NTFS or FAT32 on the disk","A network share that is mapped to a drive letter on the PC"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A volume is a logical storage unit for the OS, often mapped one to one with a partition.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a volume is a logical storage unit for the OS, often mapped 1:1 with a partition, though it can also be built across several devices by a RAID configuration.","The guide describes partitions and volumes as logical storage units, not individual physical sectors.","NTFS and FAT32 are file system types. The guide describes a volume as a storage unit that can hold a file system, not the file system itself.","A mapped network share is a network resource. The guide describes a volume as a logical storage unit for the OS."],"optionRefs":["p. 420, Disk Management","p. 420, Disk Management","p. 420, Disk Management","p. 420, Disk Management"],"ref":"p. 420, Disk Management","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-06","module":13,"lesson":"13B Command-Line Tools","question":"A technician runs a quick format on a drive. What happens to the existing data?","context":"A technician runs a quick format on a drive. What happens to the existing data?","options":["File references are removed, but data isn't erased and may be recoverable","All data is securely erased, so nothing at all can be recovered","The data is encrypted in place with a new key and locked","Files are backed up automatically to another drive before formatting"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Standard and quick formats remove file references but do not erase the actual data, so recovery is possible with third-party tools.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says standard and quick formats remove file references in the volume boot record but do not erase the actual data, and data recovery is possible with third-party tools.","The guide says the quick format doesn't erase the actual data. A secure format utility is described separately for preventing recovery.","The guide does not describe formatting as encrypting data. It describes removed file references and data that can still be recovered.","The guide does not describe formats backing up data. It says existing files can be overwritten and may be recoverable."],"optionRefs":["p. 437, Format","p. 437, Format","p. 437, Format","p. 437, Format"],"ref":"p. 437, Format","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-07","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A laptop joins a coffee shop's Wi-Fi. Which network profile hides the PC from other devices and disables printer and file sharing?","context":"A laptop joins a coffee shop's Wi-Fi. Which network profile hides the PC from other devices and disables printer and file sharing?","options":["Public, which hides the PC and disables printer and file sharing","Private, which allows discovery and sharing on trusted networks","Both profiles, since they hide the PC equally well on every network","Neither profile, because sharing is always turned on for users"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Public profile hides the PC from other devices and disables printer and file sharing.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the Public option hides the PC from other devices and disables printer and file sharing.","The Private option allows discovery and sharing on trusted networks, so it is not the profile that hides the PC.","The guide says only the Public profile hides the PC. Private allows discovery on trusted networks.","The guide describes Public as the profile that disables sharing, so sharing is not always on."],"optionRefs":["p. 448, Network Location","p. 448, Network Location","p. 448, Network Location","p. 448, Network Location"],"ref":"p. 448, Network Location","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-08","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A user wants a home network where the device is discoverable and treated as a trusted environment. Which profile fits?","context":"A user wants a home network where the device is discoverable and treated as a trusted environment. Which profile fits?","options":["Private, a trusted environment where the device is discoverable","Public, which hides the device from other devices on the network","A network with all incoming connections blocked by the firewall","A guest network with sharing turned off for every visitor"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Private option treats the network as a trusted environment where the device is set as discoverable.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes private networks as trusted environments where the device is set as discoverable.","The Public option hides the device from other devices, which is the opposite of the discoverable setting in the scenario.","Blocking all incoming connections is a firewall setting, not a profile that makes the device discoverable on a trusted network.","The guide does not describe a guest network profile. The trusted, discoverable behavior it describes belongs to Private."],"optionRefs":["p. 449, Network Location","p. 448, Network Location","p. 449, Network Location","p. 449, Network Location"],"ref":"p. 449, Network Location","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M13-EX-09","module":13,"lesson":"13C Windows Networking","question":"A technician needs to change an advanced setting for a network adapter. Where in Device Manager are the adapter's settings found?","context":"A technician needs to change an advanced setting for a network adapter. Where in Device Manager are the adapter's settings found?","options":["The Advanced tab in the adapter's Properties in Device Manager","The Power Options applet in Control Panel, under power plans","The Windows Security page, under antivirus and firewall shortcuts","The Task Manager Users tab, which lists signed-in users and usage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Device Manager, right-click the adapter, select Properties, and update settings using the Advanced tab.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to locate the adapter in Device Manager, right-click, select Properties, and update settings using the Advanced tab.","Power Options controls power plans and sleep settings. The guide's adapter steps use Device Manager and the Advanced tab.","The Windows Security page provides antivirus and firewall shortcuts. The guide's adapter steps use the Advanced tab in Device Manager.","The Task Manager Users tab lists signed-in users and their resource use. It is not where adapter settings are changed."],"optionRefs":["p. 441, Network Adapter Settings","p. 393, Power Options","p. 388, Windows Security","p. 484, Task Manager"],"ref":"p. 441, Advanced tab","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-EX-01","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A technician wants to run two commands one after the other on a single line in Bash. Which character separates them?","context":"A technician wants to run two commands one after the other on a single line in Bash. Which character separates them?","options":["A semicolon (;), to run commands in order on one line","A colon (:), which the guide does not use for this","A comma (,), which the guide does not use for this","A forward slash (/), which the guide uses in paths"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The guide says to use a semicolon to execute multiple commands sequentially on a single line.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says to use a semicolon to execute multiple commands sequentially on a single line, and to press ENTER to run them in order.","The guide's multiple-command syntax uses a semicolon. A colon is not described for running commands in sequence.","The guide's multiple-command syntax uses a semicolon. A comma is not described for running commands in sequence.","The guide uses the forward slash in paths. It is not the character that separates multiple commands on one line."],"optionRefs":["p. 563, Multiple Commands","p. 563, Multiple Commands","p. 563, Multiple Commands","p. 563, Multiple Commands"],"ref":"p. 563, Multiple Commands","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-EX-02","module":17,"lesson":"17A Linux Features","question":"A user has two files named Report.txt and report.txt in the same Linux folder. What does the guide say about these names?","context":"A user has two files named Report.txt and report.txt in the same Linux folder. What does the guide say about these names?","options":["Two different files, since Linux names are case-sensitive","The same file, since Linux ignores capital letters in names","Only the first name is valid, and the second is renamed","Both names are invalid in Linux and cannot be used"],"answer":0,"explanation":"In Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that in Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive.","The guide says Linux names are case-sensitive, so capital letters matter. Linux does not ignore them.","The guide does not describe renaming on that basis. Case-sensitive names are treated as different files.","The guide does not say these names are invalid. It says the names are case-sensitive, which makes them different."],"optionRefs":["p. 563, Case Sensitivity","p. 563, Case Sensitivity","p. 563, Case Sensitivity","p. 563, Case Sensitivity"],"ref":"p. 563, Case Sensitivity","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-EX-03","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"A teacher wants to take a photo with an iPhone and have it appear instantly on the Mac. Which macOS feature does this?","context":"A teacher wants to take a photo with an iPhone and have it appear instantly on the Mac. Which macOS feature does this?","options":["Continuity Camera, which sends a phone photo to the Mac","Phone Calls and Text Messages, which sends SMS from the Mac","Instant Hotspot, which shares the iPhone's internet connection","Mission Control, which switches between windows and desktops"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Continuity Camera lets you take a photo or scan a document with your iPhone or iPad and have it appear instantly on your Mac.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes Continuity Camera as enabling you to take a photo or scan a document with your iPhone or iPad and have it appear instantly on your Mac.","The guide describes this feature as making and receiving calls and messages on the Mac. It does not transfer a photo from the phone's camera.","Instant Hotspot shares the iPhone's internet connection with the Mac. The guide does not describe it for photos.","Mission Control switches between windows and manages multiple desktops on the Mac. It does not bring in photos from an iPhone."],"optionRefs":["p. 591, Continuity Camera","p. 591, Phone Calls and Text Messages","p. 591, Instant Hotspot","p. 580, Mission Control"],"ref":"p. 591, Continuity Camera","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-EX-04","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"An app needs access to every file on a Mac's disk. Which Privacy & Security option controls this access?","context":"An app needs access to every file on a Mac's disk. Which Privacy & Security option controls this access?","options":["Full Disk Access, which controls access to the entire disk","Location Services, which controls use of the device's location","Displays, which adjusts scaling, brightness, and color profiles","Trackpad gestures, which control touch swipes and gestures"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Privacy & Security options include Full Disk Access, which controls whether an app can access the entire disk.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists Full Disk Access among the Privacy & Security options that control app access, such as location services and files and folders.","Location Services controls whether apps can use the device's location. It does not control access to the entire disk.","The Displays pane adjusts scaling, brightness, and color profiles. It is not a privacy option.","Trackpad settings control touch gestures such as swipes. They do not control app access to disk files."],"optionRefs":["p. 589, Privacy & Security","p. 589, Privacy & Security","p. 585, Displays","p. 584, Configuring the Trackpad"],"ref":"p. 589, Privacy & Security","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M17-EX-05","module":17,"lesson":"17C macOS Features","question":"On a Mac, a user wants to change the trackpad swipe gestures, such as swiping between full-screen apps. Which tab in the Trackpad settings lists these gestures?","context":"On a Mac, a user wants to change the trackpad swipe gestures, such as swiping between full-screen apps. Which tab in the Trackpad settings lists these gestures?","options":["The More Gestures tab, which lists swipe and gesture options","The Displays pane, which adjusts scaling and brightness","The Finder window, which navigates files and folders","The Privacy & Security pane, which controls app permissions"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The More Gestures tab lists touch gestures such as swiping between pages, swiping between full-screen apps, Notification Centre, and Mission Control.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the More Gestures tab lists touch gestures such as swiping between full-screen apps, Notification Centre, and Mission Control.","The Displays pane adjusts desktop scaling, brightness, and color profiles. It does not list trackpad gestures.","The Finder is the macOS equivalent of File Explorer. It navigates files and folders, not trackpad gestures.","The Privacy & Security pane controls analytics and app permissions. It does not list trackpad gestures."],"optionRefs":["p. 584, Configuring the Trackpad","p. 585, Displays","p. 581, Finder","p. 587, Privacy & Security"],"ref":"p. 584, Configuring the Trackpad","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-05","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"A user deleted a folder by mistake and wants to recover it. Which Windows feature provides this option?","context":"A user deleted a folder by mistake and wants to recover it. Which Windows feature provides this option?","options":["Recycle Bin, which recovers files and folders marked for deletion","Indexing Options, which control how search finds files","Task View, which shows open windows and virtual desktops","Power and Battery settings, which manage sleep and battery use"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Recycle Bin provides an option for recovering files and folders that have been marked for deletion.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the Recycle Bin provides an option for recovering files and folders that have been marked for deletion.","Indexing Options control which locations are indexed for searching. The guide does not describe them as a way to recover deleted files.","Task View shows open windows and desktops. The guide does not describe it for recovering deleted items.","Power and Battery settings manage sleep, battery usage, and battery saver. They do not recover deleted files."],"optionRefs":["p. 381, Recycle Bin","p. 384, Indexing Options","p. 371, Taskbar","p. 374, Windows Settings"],"ref":"p. 381, Recycle Bin","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-06","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"On the Windows 11 taskbar, which area holds icons for background processes, such as notification icons?","context":"On the Windows 11 taskbar, which area holds icons for background processes, such as notification icons?","options":["The notification area, at the right end of the taskbar","The middle part of the taskbar, which shows app icons with open windows","The Task View button, which shows open windows and desktops","The Start menu, which lists installed apps and settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The notification area contains icons for background processes.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says the notification area contains icons for background processes, which is at the right end of the taskbar.","The middle part of the taskbar contains icons for apps that have an open window, so it is not the background-process area.","The Task View button is a separate control that shows windows and desktops. The notification area holds background-process icons.","The Start menu lists installed apps and settings. The guide describes the notification area for background-process icons."],"optionRefs":["p. 371, Taskbar","p. 371, Taskbar","p. 371, Taskbar","p. 371, Taskbar"],"ref":"p. 371, Taskbar","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M12-EX-07","module":12,"lesson":"12A Windows Settings and Control Panel","question":"A user wants to change how long the PC waits before sleeping, and to check battery usage. Which Settings category handles this?","context":"A user wants to change how long the PC waits before sleeping, and to check battery usage. Which Settings category handles this?","options":["Power and Battery, which covers sleep and battery usage","Focus, which covers notifications and automatic rules","Storage, which covers storage space and drives","Devices, which covers Bluetooth and printer settings"],"answer":0,"explanation":"The Power and Battery category covers sleep, battery usage, and battery saver.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide lists Power and Battery as the Settings category for sleep, battery usage, and battery saver.","Focus covers notifications and automatic rules. The guide does not describe it for sleep or battery usage.","Storage covers storage space, drives, and configuration rules. Sleep and battery use are in Power and Battery.","The guide lists Devices as a Settings category for Bluetooth and printers. Sleep and battery usage are under Power and Battery."],"optionRefs":["p. 374, Windows Settings","p. 374, Windows Settings","p. 374, Windows Settings","p. 390, Devices"],"ref":"p. 374, Windows Settings","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-EX-01","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"A router's vendor no longer releases firmware fixes for newly found problems. How does the guide describe this device?","context":"A router's vendor no longer releases firmware fixes for newly found problems. How does the guide describe this device?","options":["A legacy or end-of-life (EOL) system, with no vendor fixes","A configuration baseline, a set of deployment recommendations","A DMZ host, placed outside the firewall protection","A hardened system, with its security measures applied"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A legacy or end-of-life (EOL) system is one where the software vendor no longer provides support or fixes for problems.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says a legacy or end-of-life (EOL) system is one where the software vendor no longer provides support or fixes for problems.","A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer system. It is not the term for a device that has lost vendor support.","A DMZ host is a device placed outside the firewall protection. The guide does not use the term for devices that lost vendor support.","A hardened system has its security measures applied. The guide's term for a device without vendor fixes is legacy or end-of-life."],"optionRefs":["p. 602, Legacy and End-of-Life Systems","p. 601, Configuration Baselines","p. 627, DMZ","p. 601, Configuration Baselines"],"ref":"p. 602, Legacy and End-of-Life Systems","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-EX-02","module":18,"lesson":"18A Attacks & Threats","question":"Before an attack, an intruder scans a company's ports and studies its public websites to learn how the network is set up. Which stage is this?","context":"Before an attack, an intruder scans a company's ports and studies its public websites to learn how the network is set up. Which stage is this?","options":["Reconnaissance, researching a target and scanning its ports","Spoofing, pretending to be another device or user","Denial of service, which makes a service unavailable","Dumpster diving, looking through discarded material for data"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Reconnaissance is research about the target, such as gathering public information and scanning network ports and websites.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes reconnaissance as research about the target, gathering publicly available information, and scanning network ports and websites.","Spoofing is pretending to be another device or user. The guide describes it as a separate attack, after the research stage.","A denial-of-service attack makes a service unavailable. The guide describes it as a separate attack type, not the research stage.","Dumpster diving looks through discarded material for information. The guide's reconnaissance example is port scanning and public websites."],"optionRefs":["p. 606, Reconnaissance","p. 606, Spoofing","p. 607, Denial-of-Service","p. 603, Dumpster Diving"],"ref":"p. 606, Reconnaissance","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-EX-03","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A small office wants its Wi-Fi network name to be hard to target. What does the guide advise about the SSID?","context":"A small office wants its Wi-Fi network name to be hard to target. What does the guide advise about the SSID?","options":["Avoid a meaningful name, such as one that suggests valuable data","Use the company name, so staff can recognize the network","Use a name that describes the department, so it is easy to find","Use the name Accounts, since staff will expect it"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Avoid a meaningful name, because an SSID such as Accounts could be a tempting target for an evil twin attack.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says not to use a meaningful name, since an SSID such as Accounts could be a tempting target for an evil twin attack.","The guide advises against a meaningful name. A company name is one that could attract an evil twin attack.","A department name is a meaningful name. The guide advises against this kind of SSID.","The guide uses Accounts as the example of a tempting target. It is not recommended as an SSID."],"optionRefs":["p. 621, SSID Naming","p. 621, SSID Naming","p. 621, SSID Naming","p. 621, SSID Naming"],"ref":"p. 621, SSID Naming","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M18-EX-04","module":18,"lesson":"18C SOHO Router Security","question":"A home router has a service that nobody uses. What does the guide say should happen to it?","context":"A home router has a service that nobody uses. What does the guide say should happen to it?","options":["Disable it, so it is not accessible in any way","Keep it running as a backup in case it is needed","Leave it open to the Internet for remote support","Allow it only from the local network"],"answer":0,"explanation":"If a service is unused, it should not be accessible in any way.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says that if a service is unused, it should not be accessible in any way.","The guide says an unused service should not be accessible. Keeping it running as a backup goes against that advice.","The guide says an unused service should not be accessible in any way, including from the Internet.","The guide says an unused service should not be accessible in any way. Limiting it to the local network still makes it accessible."],"optionRefs":["p. 625, Port Forwarding and Port Triggering","p. 625, Port Forwarding and Port Triggering","p. 625, Port Forwarding and Port Triggering","p. 625, Port Forwarding and Port Triggering"],"ref":"p. 625, Port Forwarding and Port Triggering","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-EX-01","module":19,"lesson":"19B Workstation Security","question":"Malware persuades a user to bypass the normal checks, without exploiting any software vulnerability. What technique is this?","context":"Malware persuades a user to bypass the normal checks, without exploiting any software vulnerability. What technique is this?","options":["Social engineering, persuading the user to bypass normal checks","Port scanning, a reconnaissance technique to find open ports","Hashing, which converts data into a fixed-length value","Brute force, which tries many passwords in turn"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Social engineering techniques persuade the user to bypass the normal checks, so malware may not need to exploit a vulnerability.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes social engineering techniques that persuade the user to bypass the normal checks, which malware can use.","Port scanning is a reconnaissance technique for finding open ports. It does not persuade the user to bypass checks.","Hashing converts data into a fixed-length value. The guide does not describe it as a way to persuade users.","A brute-force attack tries many passwords. It does not persuade the user to bypass the normal checks."],"optionRefs":["p. 643, Malware Techniques","p. 606, Reconnaissance","p. 608, Password Storage","p. 609, Password Attacks"],"ref":"p. 643, Malware Techniques","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-EX-02","module":19,"lesson":"19C Browser Security","question":"A user asks which AI assistant is enabled by default in the Edge browser. Which feature is it?","context":"A user asks which AI assistant is enabled by default in the Edge browser. Which feature is it?","options":["Copilot, enabled in the Edge browser by default","Internet Explorer mode, a compatibility mode in Edge","Browser add-ons, which add functionality to the browser","Clear browsing data, a way to manage browsing history"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Copilot is enabled in the Edge browser by default.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says Copilot is enabled in the Edge browser by default.","The guide describes Internet Explorer functionality as remaining available as a compatibility mode in Edge. It is not the default AI assistant.","Add-ons are code that adds functionality to a browser. The guide does not describe them as an assistant enabled by default.","Clearing browsing data is a way to manage browsing history. It is not an assistant feature."],"optionRefs":["p. 653, Browser Security","p. 653, Browser Security","p. 654, Browser Add-ons","p. 659, Browsing History"],"ref":"p. 653, Browser Security","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M19-EX-03","module":19,"lesson":"19D Troubleshoot Workstation Security","question":"An antivirus scan finds malware on a PC. Where is the default action that the software attempts set?","context":"An antivirus scan finds malware on a PC. Where is the default action that the software attempts set?","options":["In the software's configuration, as the default action on malware","In the Recovery menu, as a fixed action that cannot change","In the browser's home page setting, under general options","In the firewall's inbound rules, under host protection"],"answer":0,"explanation":"You can configure the default action that software should attempt when it discovers malware as part of a scan.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says you can configure the default action that software should attempt when it discovers malware as part of a scan.","The guide says the default action can be configured, so it is not fixed. The Recovery menu is described separately.","The browser home page is a separate setting. The guide describes the default action in the scanning software.","Firewall inbound rules control network traffic. The guide describes the default action on malware in the scanning software."],"optionRefs":["p. 670, Malware Removal and Scanning","p. 670, Malware Removal and Scanning","p. 653, Browser Security","p. 647, Host Firewall"],"ref":"p. 670, Malware Removal and Scanning","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M16-EX-01","module":16,"lesson":"16A Windows Editions","question":"A user buys a boxed copy of Windows Pro from a store. What is this type of license called?","context":"A user buys a boxed copy of Windows Pro from a store. What is this type of license called?","options":["Retail, a full packaged product (FPP)","OEM, which comes pre-installed on a new device","Volume licensing, which offers bulk-purchase discounts","Pro for Workstations, an edition with more RAM support"],"answer":0,"explanation":"A retail license is a full packaged product (FPP), sold as a boxed copy for one device.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes retail/full packaged product (FPP) as one of the ways Windows Pro is available, sold as a boxed product.","The guide describes an OEM license as one that comes pre-installed on the device, not a boxed copy bought from a store.","The guide describes volume licensing as offering discounts for bulk purchases and custom installation images, not a single boxed copy.","Pro for Workstations is an edition, not a license type. The guide describes it as supporting more RAM and advanced hardware."],"optionRefs":["p. 549, Windows Pro","p. 548, OEM License","p. 549, Windows Pro","p. 549, Windows Pro"],"ref":"p. 549, Windows Pro","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M21-EX-01","module":21,"lesson":"21C Artificial Intelligence","question":"An application can understand and respond to human language. Which AI technique does the guide describe for this?","context":"An application can understand and respond to human language. Which AI technique does the guide describe for this?","options":["Natural Language Processing (NLP), which understands human language","Machine Learning, which makes predictions or decisions from data","Computer Vision, which recognizes images in photos and video frames","Hashing, which converts data into a fixed-length value for storage"],"answer":0,"explanation":"Natural Language Processing lets applications understand and respond to human language, as the guide describes.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide describes Natural Language Processing as the AI feature that lets applications understand and respond to human language.","The guide describes machine learning as making predictions or decisions based on data. The language feature is NLP.","The guide describes computer vision as recognizing images. It is not the feature for understanding human language.","Hashing is a security technique that produces fixed-length values. The guide does not describe it as an AI feature."],"optionRefs":["p. 715, AI Integration","p. 715, AI Integration","p. 715, AI Integration","p. 608, Password Storage"],"ref":"p. 715, AI Integration","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"},{"id":"g-M22-EX-01","module":22,"lesson":"22B Safety & Environment","question":"A technician is cleaning dusty components and must not breathe in dust or harmful particles. Which piece of protective equipment does the guide describe?","context":"A technician is cleaning dusty components and must not breathe in dust or harmful particles. Which piece of protective equipment does the guide describe?","options":["An air filter mask, which keeps dust and particles out of the lungs","Safety goggles, which protect the eyes from debris","An anti-static wrist strap, which drains static charge","Insulated gloves, which protect the hands from shocks"],"answer":0,"explanation":"An air filter mask helps the user avoid ingesting dust, dirt, or harmful particles into the lungs.","optionWhy":["Correct. The guide says air filter masks help a user avoid ingesting dust, dirt, or other harmful particles into their lungs.","Goggles protect the eyes. The guide's question is about breathing in dust, which the air filter mask is for.","An anti-static wrist strap protects components from electrostatic discharge. It does not filter the air a technician breathes.","Gloves protect the hands. The guide describes air filter masks for avoiding dust and particles entering the lungs."],"optionRefs":["p. 727, Personal Protective Equipment","p. 727, Personal Protective Equipment","p. 729, Electrostatic Discharge","p. 727, Personal Protective Equipment"],"ref":"p. 727, Personal Protective Equipment","difficulty":"scenario","src":"guide"}],"flashcards":{"11":[{"term":"Ticket Management","def":"After opening an incident or problem ticket, the troubleshooting process is applied until the issue is resolved. At each stage, the system must track the ownership of the ticket (who is dealing with it) and its status (what has been done)."},{"term":"Service Level Agreements","def":"Service level agreements (SLAs) define the level of service requirements from an internal department or external, third-party vendor. Examples of services that most likely have an SLA in place include: Internal departments of the company that are providing resources to one another such as"},{"term":"Difficult Situations","def":"A difficult situation occurs when either you or the customer becomes, or risks becoming, angry or upset. There are several techniques that you can use to defuse this type of tension."},{"term":"Do not argue or become defensive","def":"When a customer is upset, which communication behavior is specifically recommended?"},{"term":"Professional Appearance","def":"There are many things that contribute to the art of presentation. Your appearance and attire, the words you use, and respecting cultural sensitivities are particularly important."},{"term":"The general process of ticket management is as follows:","def":"1. A user contacts the help desk, perhaps by phone or email, or directly via the ticketing system."},{"term":"Standard Operating Procedure","def":"Employees must understand how to use computers and networked services securely and safely and be aware of their responsibilities. To support this, the organization needs to create written policies and procedures to help staff understand and fulfill their tasks and follow best practices:"},{"term":"Repair and Replace Options","def":"If there is a product issue that cannot be solved remotely, you might offer to repair or replace the product: Repair-—The customer will need clear instructions about how to pack and return the item to a repair center, along with a ticket-tracking number and returned-merchandise authorization"},{"term":"Follow Up","def":"If you have resolved the ticket and tested that the system is operating normally again, you should give the customer a general indication of what caused the issue and what you did to fix it, along with assurance that the problem is now fixed and unlikely to reoccur. Upon leaving"},{"term":"Set Expectations and Timeline","def":"On receiving the request (whether it is a call, email, or face-to-face contact), acknowledge the request and set expectations. For example, repeat the request back to the customer, then state the next steps and establish a timeline. For example, \"I have assigned this problem to"},{"term":"Categories and Severity","def":"Categories Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"term":"Clear Written Communication","def":"Free-form text fields allow ticket requesters and agents to add descriptive information. There are normally three fields to reflect the ticket life cycle: Issue description records the initial request with any detail that could easily be collected at the time."},{"term":"Professional Support Delivery","def":"Respect means that you treat others (and their property) as you would like to be treated."},{"term":"Escalation Levels","def":"Escalation occurs when an agent cannot resolve the ticket. Some of the many reasons for escalation include: The incident is related to a problem and requires analysis by senior technicians or by a third-party/warranty support service."},{"term":"Dealing with Difficult Customers","def":"It is never easy to talk to someone who is unreasonable, abusive, or shouting, but it is important to be able to deal with these situations professionally."},{"term":"Do Not Post Experiences on Social Media","def":"Everyone has bad days when they feel the need to get some difficult situation off their chest."},{"term":"Professional Support Processes","def":"From the point of first contact, the support process must reassure customers that their inquiry will be handled efficiently. If the customer has already encountered a problem with a product, finding that the support process is also faulty will double their poor impression of your"},{"term":"Professional Documentation","def":"Support contact information and hours of operation should be well-advertised so that the customer knows exactly how to open a ticket. The service should have proper documentation so the customer knows what to expect regarding supported items, how long incidents may take"},{"term":"Windows, Linux, macOS, and Chrome OS","def":"Workstation systems (OSs) include Windows, Linux, macOS, and Chrome OS."},{"term":"Windows, Linux, macOS, Chrome OS","def":"Which group is listed as workstation operating systems in the guide?"},{"term":"Professional Communications","def":"You must listen carefully to what is being said to you; it will give you clues to the customer's technical level, enabling you to pace and adapt your replies accordingly."},{"term":"Active Listening","def":"Active listening is the skill of listening to an individual with your full attention without arguing with, commenting on, or misinterpreting what they have said. With active listening, you make a conscious effort to keep your attention focused on what the other person is saying, as"},{"term":"Meet Expectations and Timeline","def":"If possible, the request should be resolved in one call. If this is not possible, the call should be dealt with as quickly as possible and escalated to a senior support team if a solution cannot be found promptly. What is important is that you drive problem acceptance and resolution, either"},{"term":"Maintain a Positive Attitude","def":"Understand that an angry customer is usually frustrated that things are not working properly, or feels let down. Perhaps a technician arrived late and the customer is already irritated. Or perhaps the customer has spent a large amount of money and is now anxious that it has been"},{"term":"Cultural Sensitivity","def":"Cultural sensitivity means being aware of customs and habits used by other people. It is easy to associate culture simply with national elements, such as the difference between the way Americans and Japanese people greet one another. However, within each nation there are many"},{"term":"Knowledge Base","def":"A knowledge base is a self-serve central repository for information. IT service organizations will normally house troubleshooting articles that end users can refer to when attempting to self-correct or diagnose an issue. Frequently asked questions (FAQs) may also be provided to"},{"term":"Be On Time","def":"Ensure that you are on time for each in-person appointment or contact call. If it becomes obvious that you are not going to be on time, inform the customer as soon as possible. Be accountable for your actions, both before you arrive on site and while on site. This means being"},{"term":"Avoid Distractions","def":"A distraction is anything that interrupts you from the task of resolving the ticket. Other than a genuinely critical incident taking priority, do not allow interruptions when you are working at a customer's site. Do not take calls from colleagues unless they are work-related and urgent."},{"term":"Support Documentation and Knowledge Base Articles","def":"It is also useful to link an inventory record to appropriate troubleshooting and support sources."},{"term":"Incident Reporting and Ticketing Systems","def":"A ticketing system manages requests, incidents, and problems. Ticketing systems can be used to support both internal end-users and external customers."},{"term":"Clarifying and Questioning Techniques","def":"There will inevitably be a need to establish some technical facts with the customer. This means directing the customer to answer your questions. There are two broad types of questioning: Open-ended—A question that invites the other person to compose a response. For example,"},{"term":"Policy Documentation","def":"An acceptable use policy (AUP) sets out what someone is allowed to use a particular service or resource for. Such a policy might be used in different contexts. For example, an AUP could be enforced by a business to govern how employees use equipment and services such as"},{"term":"Deal Appropriately with Confidential and Private Materials","def":"You must also demonstrate respect for the customer's property, including any confidential or private data they might have stored on a PC or smartphone, or printed as a document: Do not open data files, email applications, contact managers, web pages that are signed into"},{"term":"Ticket","def":"Which record is used to track a user-reported support issue from opening through resolution?"},{"term":"Using Proper Language","def":"When you greet someone, you should be conscious of making a good first impression. When you arrive on site, make eye contact, greet the customer, and introduce yourself and your company. When you answer the phone, introduce yourself and your department and offer"},{"term":"Typical examples of SOPs are as follows:","def":"Procedures for custom installation of software packages, such as verifying system requirements, validating download/installation source, confirming license validity, adding the software to change control/monitoring processes, and developing support/training documentation"},{"term":"Categories","def":"Categories and subcategories group related tickets together. This is useful for assigning tickets to the relevant support section or technician and for reporting and analysis."},{"term":"Service-level agreement (SLA)","def":"Which document defines the level of service requirements between a provider and a customer or internal department?"}],"12":[{"term":"Network Settings","def":"A Windows host can be configured with one or more types of network adapters. Adapter types include Ethernet, Wi-Fi, cellular radio, and virtual private network (VPN). Each adapter must be configured with Internet Protocol (IP) address information. Each network that an adapter is used"},{"term":"Display and Sound Settings","def":"The principal Display configuration settings are: Scale—A large high-resolution screen can use quite small font sizes for the user interface."},{"term":"Control Panel","def":"Most of the standard Windows 10 and Windows 11 configuration settings can be located within Windows Settings, but not all of them. Some options are still configured via the legacy Control Panel interface."},{"term":"Time and Language settings","def":"Set the date, time, and time zone, and region options such as spelling, keyboard input, and speech recognition (Student Guide p. 378)."},{"term":"Indexing Options","def":"Indexing creates a catalog of file and folder locations and properties, which speeds up searches. Indexing Options controls the indexed locations."},{"term":"Hibernate","def":"Which Windows power option preserves the current system state to disk and powers down?"},{"term":"Privacy and Security Settings","def":"Privacy and Security Settings govern what usage data Windows is permitted to collect and what device functions are enabled and for which apps. The security settings allow a user to change antivirus, browser, and firewall settings. There are multiple settings toggles to determine what"},{"term":"Time and Language","def":"Which Windows settings area is used to configure date, time, and time zone?"},{"term":"Windows Settings and Control Panel","def":"The Windows Settings app and Control Panel are the two main interfaces for administering Windows. Administering an OS means configuring options, setting up user accounts, and adding and removing devices and software. All Windows configuration data is ultimately held in"},{"term":"User Accounts Control Panel Applet","def":"The User Accounts Applet in Control Panel is the legacy interface. Many of its functions are obfuscated or hidden and may not readily be used to add new accounts. It does provide options for adjusting the account name and changing the account privilege level between the"},{"term":"Windows Settings","def":"Windows Settings is a touch-enabled interface for managing Windows. The Settings app is the preferred administrative interface. Configuration option \"pages\" are divided between a few main headings."},{"term":"File Explorer","def":"File management is a critical part of using a computer. As a computer support professional, you will often have to assist users with locating files. In Windows, file management is performed using the File Explorer app. File Explorer enables you to open, copy, move, rename, view, and"},{"term":"Personalization Settings","def":"The Personalization Settings allow you to select and customize themes, which set the appearance of the desktop environment. Personalization and theme settings include the desktop wallpaper, screen saver, color scheme, font, and properties for the Start menu and"},{"term":"Windows 11 Desktop","def":"Windows 11 refreshes the desktop style by introducing a center-aligned taskbar, better spacing for touch control, and rounded corners. It also makes the multiple desktops feature more accessible. Multiple desktops allow the user to set up different workspaces, such as one"},{"term":"Drives and Folders","def":"While the system objects represent logical storage areas, the actual data files are written to disk drives. Within the This PC object, drives are referred to by letters and optional labels. A \"drive\" can be a single physical disk or a partition on a disk, a shared network folder mapped to a drive"},{"term":"Device Settings","def":"Most Windows-compatible hardware devices use Plug and Play. This means that Windows automatically detects when a new device is connected, locates drivers for it, and installs and configures it with minimal user input. In some cases, you may need to install the hardware"},{"term":"Gaming","def":"The Gaming settings page is used to toggle game mode on and off. Game mode suspends Windows Update and dedicates resources to supporting the 3-D performance and frame rate of the active game app rather than other software or background services."},{"term":"Time and Language Settings","def":"The Time and Language settings pages are used for two main purposes: Set the correct date/time and time zone. Keeping the PC synchronized to an accurate time source is important for processes such as authentication and backup."},{"term":"Desktop Settings","def":"The desktop can be configured to use local settings and personalized to adjust its appearance."},{"term":"Windows Update","def":"Windows Update hosts critical updates and security patches plus optional software and hardware device driver updates."},{"term":"Scale","def":"Scaling makes the system use proportionally larger fonts on a large high-resolution screen."},{"term":"System Objects","def":"In Windows, access to data files is typically mediated by system objects. These are shown in the left-hand navigation pane in File Explorer. Some of the main system objects are: User account- Contains personal data folders belonging to the signed-in account profile. For"},{"term":"Windows Account Settings","def":"A Windows account can either be configured as a local-only account or linked to a Microsoft Account. A local account can be used to sign in on a single computer only. A Microsoft account gives access to Microsoft's cloud services and allows sign-in and syncs desktop settings and"},{"term":"Activation","def":"Microsoft Product Activation is an anti-piracy technology that verifies that software products are legitimately purchased. You must activate Windows within a given number of days after installation. After the grace period, certain features will be disabled until the system is activated"},{"term":"System Settings","def":"The System Settings page in the Settings app presents options for configuring input and output devices, power, remote desktop, notifications, and clipboard (data copying). There is also an About page listing key hardware and OS version information."},{"term":"Windows Security","def":"The Windows Security page contains shortcuts to the management pages for the built-in Windows Defender virus/threat protection and firewall product."},{"term":"Which factor should be checked before installing an application?","def":"Application to OS compatibility"},{"term":"Windows Interfaces","def":"An OS is made up of kernel files and device drivers to interface with the hardware plus programs to provide a user interface and configuration tools. The earliest operating systems for PCs, such as Microsoft's Disk Operating System (DOS), used a command-line user interface"},{"term":"Windows 10 Desktop","def":"One of the main functions of an OS is to provide an interface (or shell) for the user to configure and operate the computer hardware and software. Windows has several interface components designed both for general use and for more technical configuration and troubleshooting."},{"term":"Mail","def":"The Mail Applet in Control Panel is added if the Microsoft Outlook client email application is installed on the computer. It can be used to add email accounts/profiles and manage the .OST and .PST data files used to cache and archive messages. Detailed configuration of the email"},{"term":"Videoconferencing","def":"Which cloud productivity category is listed in the guide?"},{"term":"Store apps","def":"Store apps are installed via the Microsoft Store."},{"term":"File Explorer Options","def":"File Explorer has configurable options for view settings and file search."},{"term":"Accounts Settings","def":"A user account controls access to the computer. Each account can be assigned rights or privileges to make OS configuration changes. Accounts can also be assigned permissions on files, folders, and printers."},{"term":"Programs and Features","def":"The Programs and Features Control Panel applet is the legacy software management interface."},{"term":"Power Options","def":"Power management allows Windows to selectively reduce or turn off the power supplied to hardware components. The computer can be configured to enter a power-saving mode automatically; for example, if there is no use of an input device for a set period. This is"},{"term":"Apps Settings","def":"In the Settings app, the Apps Settings group is used to view and remove installed apps and Windows Features. You can also configure which app should act as the default for opening, editing, and printing particular file types and manage which apps run at startup."}],"13":[{"term":"Updating and Troubleshooting Devices","def":"Windows may identify a device's type and function but fail to find a driver, resulting in an \"Unknown Device\" or a \"generic\" type with a yellow exclamation mark in Device Manager. If the device never worked, ensure compatibility of the device and driver with the OS. Manufacturers"},{"term":"There are three ways to use chkdsk :","def":"chkdsk X : (where X is the drive letter but no switch is used) runs in read-only mode, reporting any errors that need repair."},{"term":"Command Syntax","def":"Enter commands at the prompt (>) using the command name, switches, and arguments with proper syntax. Press ENTER to execute the command. Required and optional arguments and switches are listed in the command syntax. Switches are usually preceded by a forward slash. If"},{"term":"Which Windows network configuration value identifies the local subnet?","def":"Subnet mask"},{"term":"Command Prompt","def":"You can execute any command from the Run dialog, but for a series of commands or viewing output, use the command shell. The command prompt processes legacy commands from early Windows versions."},{"term":"Disk Defragmenter","def":"The Defragment and Optimize Drives tool enhances HDD and SSD performance: HDDs: On an HDD, defragmenting reorganizes file data into contiguous clusters, reducing the time the controller needs to seek across the disk to read a file."},{"term":"Changing the Current Directory","def":"Use the cd command to change the working directory. Enter the full path to switch directories, such as cd C:\\Users\\David ."},{"term":"Task Scheduler","def":"The Task Scheduler automates commands and scripts, with many Windows processes having predefined schedules. Tasks can be set to run once at a future date/time or on a recurring schedule. They can involve simple applications (including switches, if necessary), batch files, or"},{"term":"Local Users and Groups Console","def":"The Local Users and Groups console offers an advanced interface for managing user accounts, including creating, modifying, disabling, deleting, and resetting passwords."},{"term":"Administrative Command Prompt","def":"Some commands require elevated privileges. If a command needs elevation, you'll see \"The requested operation requires elevation.\" To run as administrator, right-click the Command Prompt shortcut, select \"Run as administrator,\" and confirm the UAC prompt. Alternatively,"},{"term":"Navigation Commands","def":"The string before the > in the command prompt indicates the current working directory."},{"term":"Editing the Registry","def":"Root keys contain subkeys and data items called value entries, similar to folders and files. A value entry includes the name, data type (such as string or binary), and the value itself. Use the Find tool to search for keys or values."},{"term":"File Management Commands","def":"The move command and copy command allow you to transfer files within a single directory."},{"term":"Device Manager","def":"Device Manager (devmgmt.msc) lets you view and edit installed hardware properties, change configuration settings, update drivers, and remove or disable devices."},{"term":"Certificate Manager","def":"A digital certificate verifies the identity of a user, computer, or service, with validity guaranteed by the issuing certification authority (CA). The certificate manager console displays installed certificates and allows for requesting and importing new ones."},{"term":"Removing, Uninstalling, and Disabling Devices","def":"For Plug and Play, hot-swappable devices, you can remove them without uninstalling. Before removing a storage device, close any active applications, click the Safely Remove Hardware icon in the taskbar, and select the option to stop or eject the device."},{"term":"The format Command","def":"The format command creates a new file system on a drive, deleting all existing data. The basic command is format drive navigation input X: /fs:SYS, where X is the drive letter and SYS is the file system type (e.g., NTFS, FAT32, EXFAT). By default, it scans for bad sectors, which can be"},{"term":"Disk Management Commands","def":"The Disk Management snap-in is easy to use, but there are some circumstances where you may need to manage volumes at a command prompt."},{"term":"cd Documents","def":"To move from C:\\Users\\David to C:\\Users\\David\\Documents, type: cd Documents."},{"term":"Listing Files and Directories","def":"Use the dir command to list files and subdirectories in the current directory or a specified path."},{"term":"Custom Microsoft Management Consoles","def":"A Microsoft Management Console (MMC) is a container for snap-ins like Device Manager, Disk Management, Group Policy Editor, and Certificate Manager. Use the mmc command to customize and create a console with your chosen snap-ins. Save the console as an MSC file in the"},{"term":"Which command displays IP configuration information?","def":"ipconfig"},{"term":"Personal Folder","def":"Personal Folder stores certificates issued to the user account and is used for network authentication."},{"term":"Copying Directory Structures","def":"The robocopy command, or \"robust copy,\" is another file copy utility. Microsoft recommends using robocopy over xcopy for better handling of long file names and NTFS attributes."},{"term":"Disk Management Console","def":"The disk subsystem stores all data generated by the operating system and applications. As the primary store of so much data, ensuring its reliability and performance is a critical management task."},{"term":"Creating a Directory","def":"To create a directory, use the md or mkdir command. For example, type md Data to create a directory named Data in the current directory. To create a directory named Docs within a directory called Data on the A drive, while the current path is C:, type md A:\\Data\\Docs ."},{"term":"Changing the Current Drive","def":"The active, or in focus, drive is managed separately from the directory. To switch drives, enter the drive letter followed by a colon and press ENTER. For example, typing D: switches to the D drive, and the prompt will update to D:\\ , indicating that D is now the default drive."},{"term":"Getting Help","def":"The command prompt includes a basic help system. Type help and then press ENTER at the command prompt to see available commands or type help Command for syntax and switches used for the command. Use the /? switch for command-specific help, e.g., netstat /? displays"},{"term":"Which MMC snap-in is used to manage scheduled tasks?","def":"Task Scheduler (taskschd.msc)"},{"term":"The diskpart Command","def":"The diskpart command serves as the command-line interface for the Disk Management tool."},{"term":"/xf","def":"/xf excludes files that match the specified names or paths."},{"term":"Removing a Directory","def":"To delete an empty directory, use rd Directory or rmdir Directory. If the directory contains files or subdirectories, use the /s switch to remove them. The /q switch can be used to suppress confirmation messages for quiet mode."},{"term":"The chkdsk Command","def":"The chkdsk command scans the file system and disk sectors for faults and attempts to repair detected problems. A version called autochk runs automatically if file system errors are detected at boot."},{"term":"Group Policy Editor","def":"GUI tools like Settings and Control Panel modify user profiles and system configurations stored in the registry, but many registry settings aren't accessible through these tools. The Group Policy Editor offers a robust way to configure these Windows settings without directly editing"},{"term":"Disk Clean-up","def":"The Disk Clean-up tool identifies files safe for deletion to free up space, including those in the Recycle Bin and temporary files. Running it in administrator mode with the Clean up system files option reclaims space from caches like Windows Update and Defender."},{"term":"Registry Editor","def":"The Windows registry is a remotely accessible database for storing configuration information for the OS, devices, and applications. Use the Registry Editor (regedit.msc) to view or edit the registry."},{"term":"Disk Maintenance Tools","def":"Of all the computer's subsystems, disk drives, and the file system probably require the most attention to keep in optimum working order. File storage is subject to three main problems: Fragmentation: On a hard disk, files ideally occupy contiguous clusters. Over time, they"}],"14":[{"term":"Task Manager Performance Monitoring","def":"The Performance tab offers detailed information about the CPU, memory, disk, network, and graphics processing unit (GPU) subsystems. The App History tab displays usage information for Windows Store apps."},{"term":"Video-conferencing Software","def":"Most video conferencing or web-conferencing software, such as Microsoft Teams or Zoom, includes a screen-share client, and some also allow participants to be granted control of the share. The share can be configured as a single window or the whole desktop. The share"},{"term":"Secure Shell","def":"Secure shell is also a remote access protocol, but it connects to a command interpreter rather than a desktop window manager. SSH uses TCP port 22 (by default). SSH uses encryption to protect each session. There are numerous commercial and open-source SSH products available"},{"term":"RDP Server and Security Settings","def":"A Remote Desktop server is not enabled by default. To change remote access settings, open the Remote Desktop page in the Settings app."},{"term":"File Transfer Software","def":"Setting up a network file share can be relatively complex. You need to select a file-sharing protocol that all the connecting hosts can use and that allows configuring permissions on the share and provisioning user accounts that both the server and client recognize. Consequently,"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Local Network Connectivity","def":"If the link and IP configuration both seem to be correct, the problem may not lie with the local machine but somewhere in the overall network topology. You can test connections to servers such as file shares, printers, or email by trying to use them. One drawback of this method is that"},{"term":"Remote Monitoring and Desktop Management Tools","def":"Network visibility refers to the challenge of ensuring that every host communicating on the network is authorized to be there and is running in a secure configuration. It is impractical for a technician to regularly locate and visit each device, so visibility depends on remote monitoring"},{"term":"SPICE","def":"Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments: a remote display system for monitoring and interacting with virtual machine environments across the Internet."},{"term":"Task Manager User Monitoring","def":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you: View all logged-in users."},{"term":"Disk and Network Monitoring","def":"Disk Monitoring The Disk pages report the type and capacity plus statistics for active time, response time, and read/write speeds."},{"term":"Remote Desktop Tools","def":"With remote desktop, the target PC runs a graphical terminal server to accept connections from clients. This allows a user to work at the desktop of a different computer over the network."},{"term":"Troubleshoot IP Configuration","def":"Windows can report several types of error states for a local network adapter. If the connection is reported as unplugged or disconnected, you need to check the cable or wireless network configuration. Two other states are reported if the link is available, but IP is not correctly"},{"term":"Blue screen of death (BSOD)","def":"A Windows system shows a blue screen. Which issue category from Core 2 matches this symptom?"},{"term":"Other Remote Access Tools","def":"Enterprise monitoring suites are designed for environments with large numbers of desktops, and the cost can be prohibitive when managing just a few machines. Other protocols and software tools are available for accepting incoming connections to non-Windows devices and"},{"term":"OS vendors have developed other types of file transfer software:","def":"AirDrop—Supported by Apple iOS and macOS, this uses Bluetooth to establish a Wi-Fi Direct connection between the devices for the duration of the file transfer. The connection is secured by the Bluetooth pairing mechanism and Wi-Fi encryption."},{"term":"Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments (SPICE)","def":"The Simple Protocol for Independent Computing Environments provides a remote display system to monitor and interact with virtual machine environments from across the Internet."},{"term":"Virtual Network Computing","def":"There are alternatives to using RDP for remote access. For example, in macOS, you can use the Screen Sharing feature for remote desktop functionality. Screen Sharing is based on the Virtual Network Computing (VNC) protocol. You can use any VNC client to connect to a Screen Sharing"},{"term":"Network Reset","def":"If there are persistent network problems with either a client or a server, one \"stock\" response is to try restarting the computer hardware. You can also try restarting just the application service."},{"term":"System Information","def":"The System Information tool (msinfo32.exe) generates a comprehensive report on the system's hardware and software components. It provides an inventory of system resources, firmware and OS versions, driver file locations, environment variables, and network status."},{"term":"Task Manager Process Monitoring","def":"Task Manager monitors your PC's key resources. Open it by pressing CTRL+SHIFT+ESC, right- clicking the taskbar or Start, or pressing CTRL+ALT+DEL and selecting Task Manager."},{"term":"Troubleshoot Network Ports","def":"The netstat command can be used to investigate open ports and connections on the local host."},{"term":"Memory Monitoring","def":"The Memory page reports which slots have modules installed and the speed. The usage statistics are broken down as follows: In use refers to system (RAM) usage only."},{"term":"Which Windows tool is designed to monitor system performance?","def":"Performance Monitor (perfmon.msc)"},{"term":"System Log","def":"The System Log records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"term":"Startup Processes and Services Console","def":"The Startup tab allows you to disable programs set to run at startup from the Startup folder or registry. Right-click the headers to view the startup type and each item's impact on boot times."},{"term":"Event Viewer","def":"When Windows detects a problem, it generates an error message to aid troubleshooting. Look up the message in a knowledge base or a support site."},{"term":"Screen-sharing Software","def":"There are many third-party alternatives to the sort of screen sharing and remote-control functionality implemented by MSRA/Quick Assist. Examples include TeamViewer and LogMeIn."},{"term":"Remote Desktop Protocol","def":"Windows uses the Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP) to implement terminal server and client functionality. To connect to a server via Remote Desktop, open the Remote Desktop Connection shortcut or run mstsc.exe . Enter the server's IP address or fully qualified domain name"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Name Resolution","def":"If you cannot identify a problem with basic connectivity, you should start to suspect a problem at a higher layer of processing. There are three main additional \"layers\" where network services fail: Security- A firewall or other security software or hardware might be blocking the connection"},{"term":"RDP","def":"Which remote access technology is listed as a Windows remote desktop protocol?"},{"term":"Disk Monitoring","def":"The Disk pages display type, capacity, and statistics for active time, response time, and read/ write speeds."},{"term":"CPU and GPU Monitoring","def":"The CPU page shows the number of cores and logical processors (HyperThreading), whether the system is multisocket, and whether virtualization is enabled. The statistics show overall utilization, system uptime, and a count of the number of processes, threads, and handles."},{"term":"tracert","def":"Which command is listed for tracing the route packets take to a destination?"},{"term":"Default Log Files","def":"The Windows Logs folder contains four main log files: System Log: Records events affecting the core OS, such as service load failures, hardware conflicts, driver load failures, and network issues."},{"term":"View all logged-in users. Send messages to users.","def":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:"},{"term":"Network Monitoring","def":"The Ethernet or Wi-Fi tab displays send and receive throughput for the active network adapter, along with the IP address and MAC address. For active wireless adapters, it also shows the SSID, connection type (802.11 standard), and signal strength."},{"term":"Troubleshoot Remote Network Connectivity","def":"When a packet is forwarded to a remote network, each router in the path to the network counts as one hop. The path taken by a packet can be used to diagnose routing issues. The tracert command (pronounced trace route) line utility is used to trace the path a packet of information"},{"term":"Microsoft Remote Assistance","def":"Remote Assistance allows a user to ask for help from a technician or co-worker via an invitation file protected by a passcode. The helper can open the file to connect over RDP and join the session with the user. There is a chat feature, and the helper can request control of the desktop."},{"term":"The Users tab in Task Manager lets you:","def":"View all logged-in users. Send messages to users."},{"term":"Virtual Private Networks","def":"Where remote desktop or SSH establishes a connection to a single host over the network, a virtual private network (VPN) establishes a tunneled link that joins your local computer to a remote network. The VPN could be used as an additional layer of security. For example, you"}],"15":[{"term":"Which principle limits users to only the permissions they need?","def":"Principle of least privilege"},{"term":"Time-based OTP (TOTP)","def":"Time-based OTP uses a time-based value for one-time authentication."},{"term":"Authentication Methods","def":"In an access control system, accounts are configured with permissions to access resources and (for privileged accounts) rights to change the system configuration. To access an account, the user must authenticate by supplying the correct credentials, proving that they are the valid"},{"term":"NTFS permissions","def":"Which Windows security concept determines access at the NTFS file-system level?"},{"term":"Multifactor Authentication","def":"Using a single factor makes authentication less reliable. A password could be shared, a device token could be stolen, or a facial recognition system could be spoofed using a photograph."},{"term":"Digital Signatures and Key Exchange","def":"Cryptographic hashes and encryption ciphers have different roles in achieving the information security goals of confidentiality, integrity, and availability. Often two or more of these three different types are used together in the same product or technology."},{"term":"Relationship between vulnerability, threat, and risk","def":"To assist with workstation and network security assessments, you need to understand the types of threats that an organization is exposed to and how vulnerabilities can be exploited to launch attacks."},{"term":"Member Servers","def":"A member server is any server-based system that has been joined to the domain but does not maintain a copy of the Active Directory database. A member server provides file and print and application server services, such as Exchange for email or SQL Server for database or line-of-"},{"term":"Organizational Units","def":"An organizational unit is a way of dividing a domain up into different administrative realms."},{"term":"One-Time Passwords","def":"The soft tokens may also be referred to as a one-time password (OTP). The OTP is only valid for a single login session and a new unique passcode is generated for each login attempt. OTPs add an additional level of security because even if the user's password is compromised, the"},{"term":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML)","def":"Security Assertions Markup Language (SAML) is a special type of SSO. With SAML, an Identity Provider (IdP) is used to pass user credentials to a service provider (SP). This process works as below: The user attempts to login to the service provider, such as Google Workspace."},{"term":"Hashing and Encryption Concepts","def":"Many logical security controls depend to some extent on the use of encryption technologies."},{"term":"Both credentials are knowledge-based","def":"Submitting a username/password (knowledge) and a PIN (knowledge) uses two knowledge-based credentials rather than two different authentication factors."},{"term":"Key Exchange","def":"Key exchange allows two hosts to know the same symmetric encryption key without any other host finding out what it is. A symmetric cipher is much faster than an asymmetric one, so it is often used to protect the actual data exchange in a session. Asymmetric encryption only"},{"term":"Asymmetric Encryption","def":"An asymmetric encryption cipher uses a key pair. A key pair is a private key and a public key that are mathematically linked. For any given message, either key can perform either the encrypt or decrypt operation but not both. Only the paired key can reverse the operation. For"},{"term":"Cryptographic Hashes","def":"A hash is a short representation of data. A hash function takes any amount of data as input and produces a fixed-length value as output. A cryptographic hash performs this process as a one- way function that makes it impossible to recover the original value from the hash. Cryptographic"},{"term":"User Account Control","def":"Just-in-Time (JIT) access is a security practice in which users are granted access to resources only when needed and for only as long as it takes to complete the needed task."},{"term":"Windows authentication involves a complex architecture of","def":"components (docs.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/security/windows-authentication/ credentials-processes-in-windows-authentication), but the following three scenarios are typical: Windows local sign-in - The Local Security Authority (LSA) compares the submitted"},{"term":"User and Group Accounts","def":"A user account is the principal means of controlling access to computer and network resources and assigning rights or privileges. In Windows, a user can be set up with a local account or a Microsoft account: A local account is defined on that computer only. For example, PC1\\David is the"},{"term":"Windows Domains and Active Directory","def":"A local account is only recognized by the local machine and cannot be used to access other computers. For example, if the user David needs access to multiple computers in a workgroup environment, a separate local account must be configured on each computer ( PC1\\David,"},{"term":"Access Control Lists","def":"A permission is a security setting that determines the level of access an account has to a particular resource. A permission is usually implemented as an access control list attached to each resource. Within an ACL, each access control entry (ACE) identifies a subject and the"},{"term":"Username and Password","def":"A username and password credential is configured by creating the user account and choosing a password. The user can change the password by pressing CTRL+ALT+DELETE or using account settings. An administrator can also reset the password using Local Users and Groups."},{"term":"2-Step Verification","def":"2-step verification is a means of using a soft token to check that a sign-in request is authentic."},{"term":"Local Users and Groups","def":"The Local Users and Groups management console provides an interface for managing both user and group accounts. Use the shortcut menus and object Properties dialogs to create, disable, and delete accounts, change account properties, reset user passwords, create custom"},{"term":"Digital Signatures","def":"A digital signature proves that a message or digital certificate has not been altered or spoofed."},{"term":"Hard Token Authentication","def":"A hard token works in the same sort of way as an authenticator app but is implemented as firmware in a smart card or USB thumb drive rather than running on a smartphone. The hard token is first registered with the service or network. When the user needs to authenticate,"},{"term":"Principle of Least Privilege","def":"A complementary principle to implicit deny is that of least privilege. This means that a user should be granted the minimum possible access necessary to perform the job. This can be complex to apply in practice, however. Designing a permissions system that respects the"},{"term":"User Account Control (UAC)","def":"Which Windows security feature prompts for elevation when administrative changes require approval?"},{"term":"Symmetric Encryption","def":"A symmetric encryption cipher uses a single secret key to both encrypt and decrypt data. The secret key is so-called because it must be kept secret. If the key is lost or stolen, the security is breached. Consequently, the main problem with symmetric encryption is secure distribution"},{"term":"Logical Security Controls","def":"A security control is a safeguard or prevention method to avoid, counteract, or minimize risks relating to personal or company property. For example, a firewall is a type of security control because it controls network communications by allowing only traffic that has specifically been"},{"term":"Security Groups","def":"A security group is a collection of user accounts. Security groups are used when assigning permissions and rights, as it is more efficient to assign permissions to a group than to assign them individually to each user. You can set up a number of custom groups with least privilege"},{"term":"Implicit Deny","def":"ACL security is typically founded on the principle of implicit deny. implicit deny means that unless there is a rule specifying that access should be granted, any request for access is denied. This principle can be seen clearly in firewall policies. A firewall filters access requests"},{"term":"Confidentiality, Integrity, and Availability:","def":"Confidentiality ensures that sensitive data is only accessible by authorized users."},{"term":"Single Sign-On","def":"Single sign-on means that a user authenticates once to a device or network to gain access to multiple applications or services. The Kerberos authentication and authorization model for Active Directory domain networks implements SSO. A user who has authenticated with"},{"term":"A PIN","def":"A PIN must be configured to set up Windows Hello. The PIN acts as a backup when another sign-in method is unavailable."},{"term":"Domain Controllers","def":"To create a domain, you need at least one Windows Server computer configured as a domain controller (DC). A DC stores a database of network information called active directory. This database stores user, group, and computer objects."}],"16":[{"term":"Feature update","def":"A Windows update that introduces changes to features and functionality."},{"term":"Windows Enterprise","def":"Windows Enterprise is tailored for large organizations and includes advanced security, deployment, and management features. It is available through volume licensing."},{"term":"Windows Editions for Work and Education","def":"Windows Pro: Designed for small and medium-sized businesses, available through OEM or retail licensing. It offers features like Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host for enhanced network administration. The Pro for Workstations edition supports more advanced"},{"term":"Workgroup","def":"A network where devices share resources but are managed independently."},{"term":"Workgroup vs. Domain Networks","def":"Workgroup: Devices share files and resources but are managed independently."},{"term":"Enterprise volume licensing","def":"The licensing model through which Windows Enterprise and Education are available."},{"term":"Windows Enterprise","def":"Tailored for large organizations with advanced security, deployment, and management features and is available through volume licensing."},{"term":"Windows Home offers two licensing models:","def":"1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"term":"Domain Network Support","def":"The primary distinction between Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions versus Windows Home is domain network support. These editions can join a domain, allowing centralized management of computers, user accounts, and policies via a Domain Controller (DC) server. This is essential"},{"term":"Windows Home","def":"Designed for domestic consumers and SOHO use; it cannot join a Windows domain network."},{"term":"Windows Pro","def":"Designed for small and medium-sized businesses and supports features such as Group Policy, BitLocker, and Remote Desktop host."},{"term":"Windows Home System Limitations","def":"Windows Home supports multicore processing (up to 64 cores) and HyperThreading but does not support multiple CPUs. The 64-bit edition is limited to 128 GB of RAM."},{"term":"Edition Considerations:","def":"You can upgrade within the same or higher edition (e.g., Windows 10 Home to Windows 11 Home or Pro), but you cannot upgrade from Home to Enterprise directly."},{"term":"Clean install","def":"An installation path that does not preserve the existing OS installation in place."},{"term":"Windows Home Edition","def":"Designed for domestic consumers and small office/home office (SOHO) use, Windows Home lacks unique features and has fewer capabilities than other editions, notably unable to join a Windows domain network."},{"term":"OEM license","def":"A Windows Home license pre-installed on a PC and valid only for that device."},{"term":"Windows Versions","def":"Windows 10 and Windows 11, available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions, represent the currently supported versions of the Windows client OS. These editions target different market sectors, such as home users and corporate environments. Additionally, both versions have"},{"term":"32-bit Versus 64-bit","def":"Each version and edition of Windows 10 was initially available in both 32-bit and 64-bit formats."},{"term":"Clean install","def":"Which installation method replaces the existing installation rather than upgrading it in place?"},{"term":"Retail license","def":"A Windows Home license that can be transferred between computers but can be active on only one device at a time."},{"term":"Desktop Styles","def":"The Windows user interface (UI) revolves around the desktop, Start menu, taskbar, and notification area. While these elements remain consistent, Windows versions, editions (such as Home, Pro, Pro for Workstations, and Enterprise), and feature updates often bring changes. For"},{"term":"In-place upgrade","def":"An upgrade that preserves applications, settings, and data when they are compatible."},{"term":"Notable Features of Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education Editions","def":"Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc): Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices, ensuring consistent configurations. Not available in Windows Home."},{"term":"Domain","def":"A network where devices connect to a centralized Domain Controller for management and security policies."},{"term":"Group Policy Editor (gpedit.msc)","def":"Configures and enforces OS and application settings across devices. Included in Windows Pro, Enterprise, and Education editions."},{"term":"64-bit Windows","def":"A Windows architecture that can support more RAM than 32-bit editions, with limits varying by license."},{"term":"Windows Pro and Enterprise Editions:","def":"Windows Pro: Available as an OEM, retail/full packaged product (FPP), or through volume licensing, which offers discounts for bulk purchases and allows custom installation images for quick deployment. Windows Pro for Workstations includes all Pro features but supports"},{"term":"Windows Upgrade Paths and Feature Updates","def":"An in-place upgrade allows you to upgrade to a new Windows version while preserving applications, settings, and data, provided they are compatible."},{"term":"32-bit Windows","def":"A Windows architecture with a 4 GB RAM limit for 32-bit editions."},{"term":"Pro for Workstations","def":"Which Windows edition is listed separately from Home, Pro, and Enterprise for Windows 10?"},{"term":"Remote Desktop Protocol (RDP)","def":"A remote connection protocol supported as a host feature by Windows editions other than Home."},{"term":"Windows Home Licensing","def":"Windows Home offers two licensing models: 1. Original Equipment Manufacturer (OEM) License: This license comes pre-installed on a PC or laptop and is valid only for that device. The computer vendor provides support. Most new devices can upgrade to Windows 11."},{"term":"Trusted Platform Module (TPM)","def":"Which hardware security component is listed as a Windows installation requirement consideration?"},{"term":"BitLocker","def":"A Windows feature that provides disk encryption to protect data."},{"term":"Windows 11","def":"A Windows client OS available in Home, Pro, and Enterprise editions in the guide."},{"term":"Pro for Workstations","def":"A Windows edition designed for advanced users and high-performance hardware."},{"term":"N versions","def":"Windows editions created to comply with EU regulations by excluding certain pre-installed multimedia applications."},{"term":"TPM","def":"A hardware security requirement referenced for Windows installation and upgrade considerations."}],"17":[{"term":"Listing the state of running processes","def":"Various keystrokes execute process management actions, including: ENTER: Refresh the status of all processes."},{"term":"Network Management Commands","def":"In Linux, Ethernet interfaces were traditionally named eth0, eth1, etc., but modern distributions often use names like enp0s3 or ens33, based on the system's hardware topology."},{"term":"Which Linux command lists files and directories?","def":"ls"},{"term":"There are three ways to escape strings:","def":"Backslash (\\): Escapes the next character only. For example,\\* treats * as a literal, and \\\\ treats \\ as a literal."},{"term":"File Permissions Commands","def":"Each file in Linux has a set of permissions that determine user access levels. The permissions system includes three rights: Read (r): Allows viewing the contents of a file or directory."},{"term":"Navigation Commands","def":"In Linux, everything is represented as a file within a unified file system. The first fixed disk is typically /dev/sda , while additional devices, like a USB drive, appear as /dev/sdb ."},{"term":"Console Switching","def":"In systems with a graphical environment, the X server runs on a virtual tty console, typically tty1. Users can switch between consoles using CTRL+ALT+Fx keys, with each console supporting different login prompts and shells."},{"term":"Metacharacters and Escaping","def":"In Linux, escaping means using a special character (usually a backslash \\) to indicate that the following character should be treated as a literal rather than interpreted in its usual, special way. This is necessary when dealing with metacharacters, which have specific meanings in the"},{"term":"For example, using ls -l for a long directory listing:","def":"drwxr-xr-x 2 bobby admins Desktop : The owner (bobby) has full (rwx) permissions, while the group (admins) and others have read and execute (r-x) permissions."},{"term":"Linux commands follow a standard format:","def":"Command: The first \"word\" is the command, which can be a full or relative path to an executable, or simply the name of an executable located in a directory specified by the PATH environment variable. The command is recognized up to the first space character."},{"term":"Examples:","def":"1. Change the owner of a file: chown username file.txt 2. Change both the owner and group: chown username:groupname file.txt 3. Change ownership recursively for a directory and its contents: chown -R username:groupname /path/to/directory 4. Change only the group (using :): chown :groupname file.txt"},{"term":"Modifying permissions using the chmod command","def":"chown Command The chown command allows the superuser or sudoers to change the owner of a file or directory. Regular users cannot use chown , even if they own the file, but they can change the group using the chgrp command."},{"term":"Example entry in /etc/hosts:","def":"127.0.0.1 localhost 192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."},{"term":"Process monitoring keys (ps / top)","def":"ENTER refreshes the status of all processes. SHIFT+N sorts by decreasing PID. M sorts by memory usage, P by CPU usage, u shows processes for a specified user, and q exits."},{"term":"Process Monitoring Commands","def":"Every process in Linux is assigned a unique process ID (PID) upon starting, allowing the system and users to identify it. This PID is a non-negative integer that increments with each new process. PID 1 is reserved for the initial daemon, the first process to start, serving as the parent"},{"term":"Distributions and Repositories","def":"A distribution includes precompiled software packages deemed appropriate by the vendor or sponsor. These packages, along with updates, are posted to software repositories. Vendors often maintain multiple repositories, such as stable, beta, and unsupported packages."},{"term":"127.0.0.1 localhost","def":"192.168.1.10 myserver.local /etc/resolv.conf The /etc/resolv.conf file contains information that defines how DNS (Domain Name System) resolution is handled. It specifies the DNS servers that the system should query to resolve domain names into IP addresses."},{"term":"Search Commands","def":"Linux supports very fast and accurate file system informational search commands."},{"term":"Case Sensitivity","def":"In Linux, commands, parameters, and file and directory names are all case-sensitive. For instance, ls -l file.data and ls -L File.data will yield different results. Typing a command name with incorrect capitalization will result in an error message."},{"term":"Shells, Terminals, and Consoles","def":"So far in this course, you worked mostly with the Microsoft Windows operating system. A CompTIA A+ technician should be capable of supporting diverse OS environments. The various operating systems you might encounter use different interfaces and command syntax, but the"},{"term":"Shells and Terminals","def":"A shell provides a command-line environment for users to interact with the OS and applications."},{"term":"Group Management Commands","def":"Each user account can be assigned to groups to manage file permissions. Use the groupadd, groupmod, and groupdel commands to manage group memberships. A user can belong to multiple groups but has only one effective group ID at a time, listed in /etc/passwd. The"},{"term":"Text Editors","def":"Most Linux files are in plain text format and can be easily edited. There are many text editors available. For those familiar with Windows, the nano editor is a simple option. To open or create a file, use nano filepath , or nano -l filepath to display line numbers. Navigate with the cursor"},{"term":"Help System","def":"To view a Linux command's function and syntax, use the --help option. Since the help output can be lengthy, it's common to pipe it to the more command for viewing one page at a time, e.g.,ls --help | more . Alternatively, use the man command to access detailed manual pages for"},{"term":"Which Linux package manager is listed in the guide?","def":"apt"},{"term":"Example usage:","def":"ip addr show # Display all network interfaces and their IP addresses ip link set enp0s3 up # Bring up the network interface enp0s3 ip route show # Display the routing table /etc/hosts The/etc/hosts file is a simple text file that maps hostnames to IP addresses. It is used for local"},{"term":"Filesystem Management","def":"Filesystem management involves organizing, maintaining, and accessing data stored on disk drives. It includes tasks such as mounting filesystems, checking and repairing them, and configuring their behavior."},{"term":"Bootloaders","def":"Before the operating system loads, a bootloader initializes the system. It is responsible for loading the kernel into memory and starting the operating system. Bootloaders support multi-boot configurations, allowing multiple operating systems on the same device. Common"},{"term":"Providing software packages and updates","def":"Software packages and updates are posted to software repositories. Package managers use configured repository addresses to install, uninstall, and update software."},{"term":"User Account Management","def":"In Linux, the root account, or superuser, has full administrative privileges and can perform any action on the system. It should be used only when absolutely necessary. During setup, most Linux distributions prompt you to create a regular user account for daily tasks. Instead of"},{"term":"Linux","def":"Key commands for navigating the Linux file system include pwd,cd,ls, and cat."},{"term":"Key filesystem management tools include:","def":"Mounting: To access a filesystem, it must be mounted, which means attaching it to a directory in the existing filesystem hierarchy. The mount command is used for this purpose."},{"term":"Which macOS utility is used for disk management?","def":"Disk Utility"},{"term":"Desktop Environments","def":"Linux distros intended for client PCs usually start with a graphical desktop environment. This environment is powered by Xorg, an open-source implementation of the X Window System."},{"term":"File Management Commands","def":"File management commands are used to move, copy, and delete data."},{"term":"User Management Commands","def":"User settings are stored in the /etc/passwd file, while group settings are in the /etc/group file."}],"18":[{"term":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks","def":"Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"term":"On-path Attacks","def":"An is a specific type of spoofing where the threat actor can covertly intercept traffic between two hosts or networks. This allows the threat actor to read and possibly modify the packets. An on-path attack is often designed to try to recover password hashes. An evil twin is one example"},{"term":"Dumpster Diving","def":"To make a pretext seem genuine, the threat actor must obtain privileged information about the organization or an individual. For example, an impersonation pretext is much more effective if the attacker knows the user's name. As most companies are set up toward customer"},{"term":"Smart cards","def":"Which item is a physical access security credential listed in the guide?"},{"term":"Just-in-time access","def":"A company wants users to receive temporary privileged access only when needed. Which concept from Core 2 matches this?"},{"term":"External versus Internal Threats","def":"An external threat actor is one who has no account or authorized access to the target system."},{"term":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES)","def":"Advanced Encryption Standard (AES) is an encryption method that uses symmetric keys and block ciphers to encrypt data. This means that the data is divided into blocks of 128-bits and each block is encrypted independently."},{"term":"Unprotected Systems","def":"A baseline will recommend specific technical security controls to ensure a secure configuration."},{"term":"Data loss prevention (DLP)","def":"Which technology is intended to help prevent sensitive data from leaving controlled systems?"},{"term":"Enterprise Authentication Protocols","def":"The main problems with personal modes of authentication are that the distribution of the passphrase cannot be secured properly and that the access point administrator may choose an insecure passphrase. Personal authentication also fails to provide accounting because all"},{"term":"Password Attacks","def":"On-path and malware attacks can be difficult to perpetrate. Many network intrusions occur because a threat actor simply obtains credentials to access the network. Also, when threat actors gain some sort of access via an on-path or malware attack, they are likely to attempt to"},{"term":"WPA2 Pre-Shared Key Authentication","def":"In WPA2, pre-shared key authentication uses a passphrase to generate the key that is used to encrypt communications. It is also referred to as group authentication because a group of users shares the same passphrase. When the access point is set to WPA2-PSK mode, the"},{"term":"Wi-Fi Protected Access","def":"Wireless LANs require careful configuration to make the connection and transmissions over the link secure. The main problem with wireless is that because it is unguided, there is no way to prevent anything within range from listening to the signals. If the wireless traffic is unencrypted,"},{"term":"Bring Your Own Device Vulnerabilities","def":"Bring your own device is a provisioning model that allows employees to use personal mobile devices to access corporate systems and data. In this scenario, it is very difficult for the security team to identify secure configuration baselines for each type of device and mobile"},{"term":"Unpatched and End of Life Operating Systems","def":"While zero-day exploits can be extremely destructive, they are relatively rare events. A greater threat is the large number of unpatched or legacy systems in use."},{"term":"Phishing and Evil Twins","def":"Phishing uses social engineering techniques to make spoofed electronic communications seem authentic to the victim. A phishing message might try to convince the user to perform some action, such as installing malware disguised as an antivirus program or allowing a threat"},{"term":"Distributed DoS Attacks and Botnets","def":"Network-based DoS attacks are normally accomplished by flooding the server with bogus requests. They rely on the attacker having access to greater bandwidth than the target or on the target being required to devote more resources to each connection than the attacker. This"},{"term":"RADIUS, TACACS+, and Kerberos","def":"Enterprise authentication uses an AAA server and network directory. These components can be implemented by several different protocols."},{"term":"Spoofing Threats","def":"A spoofing threat is any type of attack where the threat actor can masquerade as a trusted user or computer. Spoofing can mean cloning a valid MAC or IP address, using a false digital certificate, creating an email message that imitates a legitimate one, or performing social"},{"term":"Which solution is specifically listed for managing mobile devices?","def":"Mobile device management (MDM)"},{"term":"Non-compliant Systems","def":"A configuration baseline is a set of recommendations for deploying a computer in a hardened configuration to minimize the risk that there could be vulnerabilities. There are baselines for different operating systems and different server and client roles. For example, a web server"},{"term":"Denial of Service Attack","def":"A denial of service attack attack causes a service at a given host to fail or to become unavailable to legitimate users. Typically, a DoS attack tries to overload a service by bombarding it with spoofed requests. It is also possible for DoS attacks to exploit design failures"},{"term":"What is an ACL used for in the security material?","def":"Access control lists (ACLs)"},{"term":"Shoulder Surfing","def":"A shoulder surfing attack means that the threat actor learns a password or PIN (or other secure information) by watching the user type it. Despite the name, the attacker may not have to be in proximity to the target—they could use high-powered binoculars or CCTV to directly observe"},{"term":"Tailgating and Piggybacking","def":"Tailgaiting is a means of entering a secure area without authorization by following closely behind the person who has been allowed to open the door or checkpoint. Piggybacking is a similar situation but means that the attacker enters a secure area with an employee's"},{"term":"Vulnerabilities","def":"A vulnerability is some fault or weakness in a system that could be exploited by a threat actor. Vulnerabilities can arise due to a very wide range of causes. Some of these causes include improperly configured or installed hardware or software, delays in applying and"},{"term":"Wi-Fi Authentication Methods","def":"Wi-Fi authentication comes in three types: open, personal, and enterprise. Within the personal authentication category, there are two methods: WPA2 pre-shared key (PSK) authentication and WPA3 Simultaneous Authentication of Equals."},{"term":"SAML","def":"Which protocol is listed for exchanging authentication and authorization information between an identity provider and a service?"},{"term":"SQL Injection Attacks","def":"A web application is likely to use Structured Query Language (SQL) to read and write information from a database. SQL statements perform operations such as selecting data (SELECT), inserting data (INSERT), deleting data (DELETE), and updating data (UPDATE). In"},{"term":"Bollards","def":"Which physical control is specifically listed as a barrier used to restrict vehicle access?"},{"term":"Software and Zero-day Vulnerabilities","def":"A software vulnerability is a fault in design or in code that can cause an application security system to be circumvented or that will cause the application to crash. The most serious vulnerabilities allow the attacker to execute arbitrary code on the system, which could allow"},{"term":"Biometrics","def":"Which physical security method uses a person’s physical characteristics for authentication?"},{"term":"Single sign-on (SSO)","def":"Which method lets a user authenticate once and then access multiple authorized services?"},{"term":"Principle of least privilege","def":"Which principle gives a user only the permissions needed to perform their task?"},{"term":"Temporal Key Integrity Protocol","def":"The first version of Wi-Fi Protected Access was designed to fix critical vulnerabilities in the earlier wired equivalent privacy (WEP) standard. Like WEP, version 1 of WPA uses the RC4 symmetric cipher to encrypt traffic but adds a mechanism called the Temporal Key Integrity"},{"term":"Impersonation","def":"Impersonation means that the social engineer develops a pretext scenario to allow himself or herself an opportunity to interact with an employee. A classic impersonation pretext is for the threat actor to phone into a department pretending to be calling from IT support, claim"},{"term":"Footprinting Threats","def":"Footprinting is an information-gathering threat in which the attacker attempts to learn about the configuration of the network and security systems. A threat actor will perform reconnaissance and research about the target, gathering publicly available information, scanning network ports"},{"term":"WPA3 Personal Authentication","def":"While WPA3 still uses passphrase-based group authentication of stations in personal mode, it changes the method by which this secret is used to agree session keys. In WPA3, the simultaneous authentication of equals (SAE) protocol replaces the PSK mechanism."},{"term":"Which authentication method can use a time-based one-time password?","def":"Authenticator application"},{"term":"Some phishing variants are referred to by specific names:","def":"Spear-phishing occurs when the attacker has some information that makes the target more likely to be fooled by the attack. The threat actor might know the name of a document that the target is editing, for instance, and send a malicious copy, or the phishing email might"},{"term":"Which technology is associated with privileged access management?","def":"PAM"},{"term":"Social Engineering","def":"Threat actors can use a diverse range of techniques to compromise a security system. A prerequisite of many types of attacks is to obtain information about the network and its security controls. Social engineering—or hacking the human—refers to techniques that persuade or"},{"term":"Threat Types","def":"Historically, cybersecurity techniques were highly dependent on the identification of \"static\" known threats, such as computer viruses. This type of threat leaves a programming code signature in the file that it infects that is relatively straightforward to identify with automated"},{"term":"Hashcat password cracking utility","def":"Cross-site Scripting Attacks Many network services are now deployed as web applications. The HTTP/HTTPS web protocol is based on servers responding to client requests. A developer can extend the basic protocol with software code and information stored in databases to implement a dynamic web app,"},{"term":"WPA3","def":"Which wireless security standard is the newer option listed alongside WPA2?"},{"term":"Spear phishing","def":"Which phishing variant targets a specific person or group with a tailored message?"},{"term":"WPA2","def":"Neither WEP nor the original WPA version is considered secure enough for continued use."}],"19":[{"term":"Distributed denial of service (DDoS)","def":"Which threat involves overwhelming a target with traffic from many sources?"},{"term":"Browser Extensions and Plug-ins","def":"A browser add-on is some type of code that adds to the basic functionality of the software."},{"term":"Windows Defender Antivirus Updated Definitions","def":"It is particularly important that antivirus software be updated regularly. Two types of updates are generally necessary: Definitions are information about new viruses or malware. These updates may be made available daily or even hourly."},{"term":"Add-ons come in several different types:","def":"Browser extensions add or change a browser feature via its application programming interface (API). For example, an extension might install a toolbar or change menu options."},{"term":"Which malware type encrypts data and demands payment?","def":"Ransomware"},{"term":"Password Manager","def":"A typical user might be faced with having to remember dozens of sign-ins for different services and resort to using the same password for each. This is insecure because just one site breach could result in the compromise of all the user's digital identities. Each major browser now"},{"term":"Ransomware and Cryptominers","def":"Ransomware is a type of malware that tries to extort money from the victim. One class of ransomware will display threatening messages, such as requiring Windows to be reactivated or suggesting that the computer has been locked by the police because it was used to view child"},{"term":"System Restore","def":"Which Windows feature should be enabled and given a restore point as part of the listed SOHO malware-removal process?"},{"term":"Browser Selection and Installation","def":"Microsoft’s Internet Explorer (IE) used to be dominant in the browser market, but alternatives such as Google's Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, and Opera have replaced it. IE itself is no longer supported. Edge, Microsoft’s replacement browser, now uses the same underlying Chromium"},{"term":"Windows Defender Firewall","def":"Where the antivirus product protects against threats in the file system, Windows Defender Firewall implements a personal/host firewall to filter inbound and outbound network traffic. The basic Settings app interface allows you to activate or deactivate the firewall for a given network"},{"term":"Sign-in and Browser Data Synchronization","def":"A browser sign-in allows the user to synchronize settings between instances of the browser software on different devices. As well as the browser settings, items that can be synced include bookmarks, history, saved autofill entries, and passwords. Settings and data for specific"},{"term":"Unused Services","def":"Services are used by the operating system and hardware to increase the functionality of the computer. Some services you may be familiar with may include update services, file and print services, and even network services like DHCP and DNS."},{"term":"AutoRun and AutoPlay","def":"One of the problems with legacy versions of Windows is that when an optical disc is inserted or a USB drive is attached, Windows would automatically run commands defined in an autorun.inf file stored in the root of the drive. A typical autorun.inf would define an icon for a disk and"},{"term":"Malware Payloads","def":"Classifying malware by payload is a way of identifying what type of actions the code performs other than simply replicating or persisting on a host."},{"term":"Redirection","def":"A browser repeatedly opens unwanted pages and sends the user somewhere else. Which symptom from Core 2 matches this?"},{"term":"False alerts regarding antivirus protection","def":"A workstation suddenly displays fake antivirus warnings. Which type of security symptom is this?"},{"term":"Troubleshoot PC Security Symptoms","def":"The multiple classifications for malware vectors and payloads mean that there can be very many different symptoms of security issues. In very general terms, any sort of activity or configuration change that was not initiated by the user is a good reason to suspect malware"},{"term":"Password Rules","def":"The following rules are easy for users to apply and make passwords more difficult to crack: Make the password sufficiently long—12+ character length is suitable for an ordinary user account. Administrative accounts should have longer passwords."},{"term":"Which attack uses a malicious lookalike wireless network?","def":"Evil twin"},{"term":"Activating and Deactivating Windows Defender Antivirus","def":"The nature of malware means that there should be no simple means of deactivating an antivirus product, or the malware could easily circumvent it. Defender Antivirus can be disabled temporarily by toggling the Real-time protection button. It will re-activate itself after a short"},{"term":"Which browser symptom is specifically listed as a security issue?","def":"Certificate warnings"},{"term":"Account Policies","def":"Account policies supplement best practice behavior by enforcing requirements as controls imposed by the OS. On a standalone workstation, password and account policies can be configured via the Local Security Policy snap-in ( secpol.msc) or the Group Policy Editor"},{"term":"Execution Control","def":"Authentication and authorization policies give subjects the right to sign on to a computer and network and (potentially) to make changes to the system configuration. This places a certain amount of trust in the user to exercise those rights responsibly. Users can act maliciously,"},{"term":"Encrypting File System","def":"When data is hosted on a file system, it can be protected by the operating system's security model. Each file or folder can be configured with an access control list (ACL), describing the permissions that principals have on the file. These permissions are enforced only when the OS"},{"term":"Which Windows encryption feature is listed for protecting data?","def":"BitLocker"},{"term":"Password Best Practices","def":"One of the first pillars of workstation security is ensuring that only authorized users can operate the computers connected to the network. Effective user security depends on strong credential management, effective account policies, and best practice end-user behavior."},{"term":"Disable Guest Account","def":"A guest account allows unauthenticated access to the computer and may provide some sort of network access too. In current versions of Windows, the Guest account is disabled by default and cannot be used to sign-in. It is only enabled to facilitate password-less file sharing in a"},{"term":"Which malware type can use a legitimate-looking program as a disguise?","def":"Trojan"},{"term":"Adware","def":"Adware is a specialized malware used to display unwanted and unsolicited advertisements on your workstation or device. This can be accomplished by storing cookies in your browser or redirecting connection requests to an advertisement server. These Potentially Unwanted"},{"term":"Browser Patching","def":"Maintaining a system that is up to date helps to ensure it remains protected against new threats."},{"term":"Private-browsing mode","def":"Which browser mode prevents normal browsing history from being retained as part of the session?"},{"term":"Reimage/reinstall","def":"Which recovery method is listed as part of malware remediation?"},{"term":"Malware Vectors","def":"Malware is usually simply defined as software that does something bad, from the perspective of the system owner. The more detailed classification of different malware types helps to identify the likely source and impact of a security incident. Some malware classifications focus on the"},{"term":"Rootkits","def":"In Windows, malware can only be manually installed with local administrator privileges. This means the user must be confident enough in the installer package to enter the credentials or accept the User Account Control (UAC) prompt. Additionally, Windows tries to protect the"},{"term":"Change Default Administrator Account and Password","def":"The root or superuser in Linux or the Administrator user account in Windows is the default system owner. These default accounts have no practical limitations and consequently are the ultimate target for threat actors. In many cases, these default accounts are disabled during the"},{"term":"Untrusted Sources","def":"Using a browser from an untrusted source where the publisher of the installer cannot be verified through a digital signature or hash is a security risk and likely to expose the user to unwanted adverts, search engines, and even spyware and redirection attacks. Some PC"},{"term":"Which malware type can record keystrokes?","def":"Keylogger"},{"term":"End-of-life (EOL)","def":"Which vulnerability is specifically associated with systems that are no longer supported?"},{"term":"Browser Privacy Settings","def":"The marketing value of online advertising has created an entire industry focused on creating profiles of individual search and browsing habits. The main function of privacy controls is to govern sites' use of these tracking tools, such as cookies. A cookie is a text file used to store"},{"term":"Restrict User Permissions","def":"Account management policies are used to determine what rights and privileges each employee should be assigned. These policies should be guided by the principle of least privilege."},{"term":"Disable guest account","def":"Which workstation hardening measure disables an account that is not needed?"},{"term":"Which malware type is designed to hide deeply in a system?","def":"Rootkit"},{"term":"Windows Defender Antivirus","def":"Even with UAC and execution control, there are still plenty of ways for malware to install onto a PC. A program might use particularly effective social engineering techniques to persuade the user to bypass the normal checks. The malware might exploit a vulnerability to execute without"},{"term":"Backdoors","def":"Modern malware is usually designed to implement some type of backdoor, also referred to as a remote access trojan. Once the malware is installed, it allows the threat actor to access the PC, upload/exfiltrate data files, and install additional malware tools. This could allow the attacker to"},{"term":"Which detection approach is listed for endpoint security monitoring?","def":"Endpoint detection and response (EDR)"},{"term":"Secure Connections and Valid Certificates","def":"The web uses Transport Layer Security (TLS) and digital certificate to implement a secure connection. A secure connection validates the identity of the host running a site and encrypts communications to protect against snooping. The identity of a web server computer for a given"},{"term":"Trusted/Untrusted Software Sources","def":"To prevent the spread of malware such as Trojans, it is necessary to restrict the ability of users to run unapproved program code, especially code that can modify the OS, such as an application installer. Windows uses the system of Administrator and Standard user accounts,"},{"term":"Browser Settings","def":"Each browser maintains its own settings that are accessed via its Meatball (…) or Hamburger (⇨) menu button. Alternatively, you can open the internal URL, such as chrome://settings, edge://settings, or about:preferences (Firefox). The settings configure options such as"},{"term":"Update anti-malware software","def":"Which action is listed before scanning and removal techniques in the SOHO malware-removal sequence?"},{"term":"Windows BitLocker and BitLocker To Go","def":"An alternative to file encryption is to use a full disk encryption (FDE) product. The Windows BitLocker disk encryption product is available with all editions of Windows except for the Home edition."},{"term":"Spyware and Keyloggers","def":"Spyware is malware that can perform browser reconfigurations, such as allowing tracking cookies, changing default search providers, opening arbitrary pages at startup, adding bookmarks, and so on. Spyware might also be able to monitor local application activity, take"}],"20":[{"term":"Application source/unofficial application stores","def":"Installing applications from unofficial application stores is listed as what type of mobile security concern?"},{"term":"Mobile Device Troubleshooting Tools","def":"When troubleshooting mobile devices, the Settings app is frequently used. Its layout differs between iOS and Android and can vary with different versions."},{"term":"Two-factor Authentication","def":"Most smartphones and tablets are single-user devices. Access control can be implemented by configuring a screen lock that can only be bypassed using the correct password, personal identification number (PIN), or swipe pattern. Many devices now support biometric"},{"term":"Failed log-in attempts restrictions","def":"Which mobile security control restricts repeated failed login attempts?"},{"term":"BYOD","def":"A deployment model where employees use their own devices subject to company requirements and oversight."},{"term":"OS Fails to Update","def":"An OS update failure can expose the device to security vulnerabilities. Follow these steps: 1. Verify update compatibility with your device model on the vendor's website."},{"term":"Patching/OS Updates","def":"Keeping a mobile OS and its apps updated with patches and new OS versions is crucial, similar to desktop computers."},{"term":"Mobile Data Security","def":"If a mobile device is lost or stolen, mechanisms exist to recover it and prevent misuse or data loss."},{"term":"Mobile firewall app","def":"An app that can monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses."},{"term":"Reboot","def":"Rebooting a mobile device can often resolve temporary performance or stability issues. Unlike leaving the device in sleep mode, powering it off closes all applications and clears RAM without affecting stored data and settings. This soft reset is effective for restoring unresponsive or"},{"term":"Which mobile security technique can locate a missing device?","def":"Locator applications"},{"term":"Fingerprint authentication","def":"A biometric method using a fingerprint sensor for device authentication."},{"term":"High number of ads","def":"Which symptom is listed as a possible sign of a malicious mobile application?"},{"term":"Remote Backup Applications","def":"Most mobile devices are linked to vendor cloud services (iCloud for iOS, Google Sync for Android, OneDrive for Microsoft) for automatic remote backup of data, apps, and settings."},{"term":"2FA","def":"Authentication requiring two different kinds of credentials."},{"term":"Battery Life Issues","def":"Battery life issues on mobile devices could come from a variety of factors. Background app activity can significantly drain the battery, so check which apps consume the most power and disable unnecessary background processes. Adjust screen brightness and timeout settings to"},{"term":"Antivirus/Anti-malware Apps","def":"Modern smartphones are vulnerable to software exploits and malware, especially if apps are installed from untrusted sources. The evolving nature of mobile OS threats makes it challenging to maintain pattern databases or use heuristics effectively."},{"term":"Mobile OS patching","def":"Keeping the mobile operating system updated with patches and new OS versions."},{"term":"Factory Reset","def":"A factory reset erases all user data, apps, and settings from a device. Afterward, the device must be manually set up with a new user account and apps or restored from a backup. Ensure the device is fully charged or connected to a power source before performing a reset."},{"term":"Unexpected application behavior","def":"A mobile device begins behaving unexpectedly after an untrusted application was installed. Which listed symptom matches this?"},{"term":"Mobile device management (MDM)","def":"Which mobile security measure is used to detect and manage device configuration and policy?"},{"term":"Remote wipes","def":"Which security measure can remove data from a lost mobile device remotely?"},{"term":"Locator Apps and Remote Wipe","def":"Most smartphones and many tablets are equipped with GPS receivers, which determine a device's position using satellite information. GPS requires line-of-sight and does not work indoors. High-accuracy location services use GPS along with Wi-Fi access points and Bluetooth"},{"term":"CYOD","def":"A deployment model where employees choose from a list of approved devices."},{"term":"Facial recognition","def":"A biometric method using a 3-D image of the user’s face for authentication."},{"term":"Mobile Device Management","def":"Mobile device management (MDM) is a class of software designed to apply security policies to the use of mobile devices in the enterprise. This software can be used to manage enterprise- owned devices as well as bring your own device (BYOD) user-owned smartphones."},{"term":"High network traffic","def":"Which symptom can indicate a mobile security problem involving excessive communications?"},{"term":"Device Encryption","def":"All but the earliest versions of mobile device OSs for smartphones and tablets provide some type of default encryption."},{"term":"Screen lock","def":"A mobile security mechanism that activates when a device is idle or the power button is pressed."},{"term":"Root access/jailbreak","def":"Which mobile security risk is associated with gaining root access or jailbreaking a device?"},{"term":"Troubleshoot Device and OS Issues","def":"If rebooting doesn't resolve the issue, follow these troubleshooting steps. If problems persist, consider a factory reset."},{"term":"Mobile device encryption","def":"A security mechanism used to protect data on smartphones and tablets if a device is lost or stolen."},{"term":"Mobile Device Management (MDM)","def":"Software designed to apply security policies to mobile devices in an enterprise."},{"term":"Which mobile hardening method encrypts data stored on the device?","def":"Device encryption"},{"term":"Which update practice is part of mobile security hardening?","def":"Application updates"},{"term":"Screen Locks","def":"If threat actors gain access to smartphones or tablets, they can retrieve vast amounts of information to facilitate further attacks. This includes confidential data, cached passwords for email, VPNs, and websites, as well as contacts and message histories (SMS, email, and IM),"},{"term":"Corporate-owned devices","def":"Which deployment choice is compared with BYOD in the guide?"},{"term":"Mobile antivirus/anti-malware","def":"Security software that can filter content, block known phishing sites, and monitor app permissions and usage."},{"term":"Enterprise Mobility Management","def":"Mobile devices have become essential for email, daily management tasks, and accessing business processes and cloud applications. A mobile device deployment model includes the policies and procedures that define how employees receive mobile devices and applications:"},{"term":"Firewall Apps","def":"Firewall apps for mobile devices monitor app activity and prevent connections to specific ports or IP addresses. Firewalls need higher permission levels (root) to control other apps, making installation challenging. \"No-root\" firewalls create a local virtual private network (VPN) interface"},{"term":"Pattern lock","def":"A screen-lock method based on swiping a join-the-dots pattern."},{"term":"COPE","def":"A deployment model where the company owns the device but employees can use it for personal activities under policy."},{"term":"Mobile security software","def":"Software that can protect mobile devices against malware, phishing, and software exploits."},{"term":"Sufficient free space","def":"Check for sufficient free space. On iOS, use Settings > General > Storage; on Android, use Settings > Storage."},{"term":"Pattern","def":"Which mobile screen-lock method is listed in the guide?"},{"term":"PIN","def":"A numeric credential that can be used for mobile screen-lock authentication."},{"term":"Configuration profiles","def":"MDM profiles that define and enforce security requirements and device settings."},{"term":"Limited internet connectivity","def":"Which symptom is listed for a mobile device with a security-related connectivity problem?"},{"term":"COBO","def":"A deployment model where the company owns the device and it is used solely for business purposes."},{"term":"Leaked personal files/data","def":"Which symptom specifically involves personal information being exposed?"}],"21":[{"term":"Which backup type contains changes made since the last full backup?","def":"Differential"},{"term":"A general order of volatility from most to least volatile is:","def":"1. CPU cache and registers - This data is extremely volatile and changes rapidly."},{"term":"End-User License Agreements","def":"Prohibited content also extends to the unauthorized installation and use of software. When you install software, you must accept the license governing its use, often called the end-user license agreement. The terms of the license will vary according to the type of software, but the"},{"term":"Grandfather - Father - Son (GFS) Scheme","def":"The GFS scheme labels the backup tapes in generations. Son tapes store the most recent data and have the shortest retention period (one week, for example). Grandfather tapes are the oldest and have the longest retention period (one year, for example). Assuming a single"},{"term":"Personal Government-issued Information","def":"Personal government-issued information that is issued to individuals by federal or state governments is also PII. Examples include a social security number (SSN), passport, driving license, and birth/marriage certificates. Data collected and held by the US federal government"},{"term":"Order of Volatility","def":"When recovering evidence, it is important to follow the order of volatility. This is the order that data should be collected based on how long it is likely to remain available. The most volatile data should be collected first and then go down the list to the data that is least likely to"},{"term":"Regulated Data Classification","def":"Regulated data is information that must be collected, processed, and stored in compliance with federal and/or state legislation. If a company processes regulated data collected from customers who reside in different countries, it must comply with the relevant legislation for each"},{"term":"Data Retention Requirements","def":"Another issue for regulated data is its retention on both file and database servers and in backup files: Regulation might set a maximum period for the retention of data. For example, if a company collects a customer’s address and credit card information to fulfill an order and the customer"},{"term":"Credit Card Transactions","def":"There are also industry-enforced regulations mandating data security. A good example is the Payment Card Industry Data Security Standard (PCI DSS) which governs the processing of credit card transactions and other bank card payments. It sets out protections that must"},{"term":"Two main factors govern backup operations:","def":"Frequency is the period between backup jobs. The frequency configuration reflects how much lost work can be tolerated. For example, if employees can recall and input the previous day’s work on document files, a daily backup will meet the requirement. If the edits are much"},{"term":"Open-source Licenses","def":"Software released under an open-source license generally makes it free to use, modify, and share and makes the program code used to design it available. The idea is that other programmers can investigate the program and make it more stable and useful. An open-source"},{"term":"Backup Testing and Recovery Best Practices","def":"When you design a backup scheme, test it to make sure it's reliable. To test the backup: Try restoring some of the backed-up data into a test directory, making sure you don't overwrite any data when doing so. Alternatively, use a virtual machine to test recovery"},{"term":"Incremental","def":"Incremental backup selects new files and files modified since the last backup job."},{"term":"Chain of Custody","def":"It is vital that the evidence collected at the crime scene conforms to a valid timeline. Digital information is susceptible to tampering, so access to the evidence must be tightly controlled."},{"term":"Prohibited Content and Licensing Issues","def":"As well as ensuring secure handling of confidential and sensitive data, you need to consider methods for identifying and removing prohibited content and unlicensed software from company workstations."},{"term":"3-2-1 Backup Rule","def":"The 3-2-1 backup rule is a best-practice maxim that you can apply to your backup procedures to verify that you are implementing a solution that can mitigate the widest possible range of disaster scenarios. It states that you should have three copies of your data (including the"},{"term":"Synthetic Full Backup","def":"A synthetic full backup is similar to a Full backup, but instead of scanning the system again to create the full backup, the system will use the original full backup and then add in the data from the incremental backups to create a new full up-to-date backup."},{"term":"Incident Response","def":"While performing technical support, you may have to report or respond to security incidents. A security incident could be one of a wide range of different scenarios, such as: A computer or network infected with viruses, worms, or Trojans."},{"term":"Which backup rotation rule is written as 3-2-1?","def":"3-2-1 backup rule"},{"term":"Low Level Format","def":"Most disk vendors supply low-level formatting tools to reset a disk to its factory condition. Most of these tools will now incorporate some type of sanitize function. You must verify the specific capability of each disk model, but the following functions are typical:"},{"term":"Erasing / Wiping","def":"Disk erasing/wiping software ensures that old data is destroyed by writing to each location on a hard disk drive, either using zeroes or in a random pattern. This leaves the disk in a \"clean\" state ready to be passed to the new owner. This overwriting method is suitable for all but the"},{"term":"Backup Operations","def":"Data backup is a system maintenance task that enables you to store copies of critical data for safekeeping. Backup protects against loss of data due to disasters such as file corruption or hardware failure. Data recovery is a task that enables you to restore user access to lost or"},{"term":"Data Handling Best Practices","def":"Employees should be trained to identify PII and to handle personal or sensitive data appropriately. This means not making unauthorized copies or allowing the data to be seen or captured by any unauthorized persons. Examples of treating sensitive data carelessly include"},{"term":"On Site versus Off Site Storage","def":"On site backup storage means that the production system and backup media are in the same location. This means that if a disaster strikes the facility, there is the risk of losing both the production and backup copies of the data."},{"term":"Non-Disclosure Agreements","def":"Non-disclosure agreements (NDA) are agreements designed to protect sensitive information. A NDA is a legally binding contract that obligates all parties to protect sensitive information that is shared between them. An NDA agreement can be either unilateral or mutual:"},{"term":"Full","def":"Full backup: All selected data is backed up regardless of when it was previously backed up."},{"term":"Personally Identifiable Information","def":"Personally identifiable information (PII) is data that can be used to identify, contact, or locate an individual or, in the case of identity theft, to impersonate them. A cell phone number is a good example of PII. Others include name, date of birth, email address, street address, biometric"},{"term":"Backup Media Requirements","def":"A backup rotation scheme allows some media to be reused once the retention period of the job stored on it has expired. Rotation is most closely associated with the use of tape media but can be applied to disk devices too. There are many backup rotation schemes, but the most widely"},{"term":"Data Destruction Methods","def":"Data destruction and disposal refer to either destroying or decommissioning data storage media, including hard disks, flash drives, tape media, and CDs/DVDs. The problem has become particularly prominent as organizations repurpose and recycle their old computers, either by"},{"term":"Non-disclosure agreement (NDA)","def":"Which agreement is intended to protect sensitive information from disclosure?"},{"term":"Data Integrity and Preservation","def":"Forensics is the science of collecting evidence from computer systems to a standard that will be accepted in a court of law. Like DNA or fingerprints, digital evidence is mostly latent. Latent means that the evidence cannot be seen with the naked eye; rather, it must be interpreted using"},{"term":"Backup Chains","def":"The requirements for backup frequency and retention must be managed against the capacity of the backup media and the time it takes to complete a backup job. These requirements are managed by using different types of jobs in a backup chain. The main types of backups are full"},{"term":"Digital Rights Management","def":"Digital music and video are often subject to copy protection and digital rights management."},{"term":"Recovery Options","def":"When performing a recovery, you can choose to either perform an in-place (overwrite) recovery or recover to an alternate location."},{"term":"Prohibited Content","def":"Employee workstations should only be used for work-related activity and data storage. In this context, prohibited content is any information that is not applicable to work. It can also specifically mean content that is obscene or illegally copied/pirated. The acceptable use"},{"term":"Backup Methods","def":"When considering a file server or database server, the execution and frequency of backups must be carefully planned and guided by policies. Each backup job records data as it was at a certain point in time. As each backup job might take up a lot of space and there is"},{"term":"Healthcare Data","def":"Protected-health-information refers to medical and insurance records plus associated hospital and laboratory test results. Healthcare data may be associated with a specific person or used as an anonymized or de-identified data set for analysis and research, such as in clinical trials to"},{"term":"Which AI limitation is listed in the guide?","def":"Hallucinations"}],"22":[{"term":"Which practice helps reduce ESD risk while servicing components?","def":"Use an ESD strap and ESD mat"},{"term":"Which review is listed as part of change implementation?","def":"Peer review"},{"term":"Electrostatic Discharge Mitigation","def":"Static electricity is a high voltage, low current charge stored in an insulated body. Electrostatic discharge occurs when a path allows electrons to rush from a statically charged body to a component that has no charge. This can occur through touch or even over a small gap if the"},{"term":"Implementation and Acceptance","def":"Implementation is when we have the approval to make a change and we have the authority to make the change. This process may require multiple steps to be followed to accomplish the change task."},{"term":"Shell scripting language","def":"A shell scripting language uses commands that are specific to an operating system."},{"term":"Risk Analysis","def":"Each change being requested should undergo a risk analysis. Risk analysis is a systematic approach to identify possible risks associated with implementing the change. Could the change cause a temporary outage of a critical system or service? Is there a risk of injury or death to the"},{"term":"Temperature, Humidity, and Ventilation Control","def":"A computer that is too hot is likely to be unreliable. A computer must be ventilated so that its fans can draw relatively cool air across the motherboard and expel the warmed air from the rear vents. You must ensure that the room (ambient) temperature is not too high and that there is"},{"term":"Execute permission","def":"The script file must have the execute permission set to run."},{"term":"Other Safety Hazard Mitigations","def":"In addition to electrical hazards, there are other safety hazards that computer technicians must account for."},{"term":"Battery Backups","def":"Sudden power loss is likely to cause file corruption. If there is loss of power due to a brownout or blackout, system operation can be sustained for a few minutes by using battery backup."},{"term":"Proper Power Handling and Personal Safety","def":"Whenever you add or replace components within a PC or laptop, the power must be disconnected first. Remove the AC plug and also remove the battery if present. Hold down the power button on the device to ensure the circuits are drained of any residual power from the"},{"term":"Proper Component Handling","def":"Proper component handling tools and techniques protect electronic components against ESD when you service a PC or mobile device: If possible, work in an uncarpeted area. Ideally, use an ESD-safe floor or chair mat."},{"term":"Warranty and Licensing","def":"Each asset record should include appropriate procurement documentation, such as the invoice and warranty/support contract (along with appropriate contact information). For software, it should record the licensing details with device/user allocations and limits."},{"term":"Trip Hazards","def":"A trip hazard is caused by putting any object in pathways where people walk."},{"term":"Material Safety Data Sheets","def":"Employers are obliged to assess the risk to their workforce from hazardous substances at work and to take steps to eliminate or control that risk. No work with hazardous substances should take place unless an assessment has been made. Employees are within their rights to refuse to"},{"term":"Environmental Impacts","def":"The location in which computer equipment is placed can affect its proper operation and lifespan. All electronic equipment should be kept away from extremes of temperature and damp or dusty conditions."},{"term":"Safety Equipment and Tools","def":"While working with electronic equipment, it would be wise to utilize insulated tools and gloves to protect yourself from harm. You may also want to test the component with a voltage tester before attempting to work on it. Ensuring there is no voltage present can protect you from"},{"term":"Compliance with Regulations","def":"When performing PC maintenance work, you may need to take into account compliance with government regulations. Regulations that typically affect PC maintenance or the installation of new equipment are: Health and safety laws— Keeping the workplace free from hazards."},{"term":"Lifting Techniques","def":"Lifting a heavy object in the wrong way can damage your back or cause muscle strains and ligament damage. You may also drop the object and injure yourself or damage the object. When you need to lift or carry items, be aware of the maximum safe lifting weight as well as any"},{"term":"Equipment Placement","def":"Equipment placement or location is just as important as the equipment itself. Ensuring equipment is located in a secure location near a power source and out of public access ensures that only authorized personnel can access it. Servers and other networking equipment should"},{"term":"Which device provides backup power during a blackout?","def":"Uninterruptible power supply (UPS)"},{"term":"Change Board Approvals","def":"When a change request has been drafted and submitted, it must go through an approval process."},{"term":"Which document starts a formal request to make a change?","def":"Request for change (RFC)"},{"term":"Change Requests","def":"Change management (CM) refers to policies and procedures that reduce the risk of configuration changes causing service downtime. Change management is closely related to configuration management."},{"term":"Building Power Issues and Mitigations","def":"Faults in building power supply cause power problems such as surges, brownouts, and blackouts: Surges— A surge is a brief increase in voltage, while a spike is an intense surge. A surge or spike can be caused by machinery and other high-power devices being turned on or off and"},{"term":"Which file extension is listed for a PowerShell script?","def":".ps1"},{"term":"Equipment Grounding","def":"Electrical equipment must be grounded. If there is a fault that causes metal parts in the equipment to become live in the circuit, the ground provides a path of least resistance for the electrical current to flow away harmlessly. Devices such as PCs and printers are connected to"},{"term":"Proper Disposal","def":"Even with procedures in place to properly maintain IT equipment, eventually, it will need to be decommissioned and either disposed of or recycled. IT equipment contains numerous components and materials that can cause environmental damage if they are disposed of"},{"term":"Surge Suppressors","def":"Passive protection devices can be used to filter out the effects of surges and spikes. The simplest surge suppressor devices come in the form of adapters, trailing sockets, or filter plugs, with the protection circuitry built into the unit. These devices offer low-cost protection to"},{"term":"Dust Cleanup","def":"Dust is drawn into the computer via ventilation holes. Over time, the dust can form a thick layer over components, heat sinks, fan blades, peripheral connection ports, and ventilation slots, preventing effective heat dissipation. It can clog up peripherals such as keyboards and mice."},{"term":"Asset Management","def":"While troubleshooting or implementing changes to a system or service, it will be important to ensure you are working on the correct system or service. Accurate tracking of what physical assets your company or organization has is just as important as fixing the issues with those"},{"term":"Electrical Safety","def":"Electricity flows in a circuit. A circuit is made when conductors form a continuous path between the positive and negative terminals of a power source. An electrical circuit has the following properties: Current is the amount of charge flowing through a conductor, measured in amps (A or I)."},{"term":"Materials Handling and Responsible Disposal","def":"Some of the components and consumables used with computer and printer systems can be hazardous to health and to the environment. You must comply with all relevant regulations when handling and disposing of these substances."},{"term":"Electrical Fire Safety","def":"Faulty electrical equipment can pose a fire risk. If the equipment allows more current to flow through a cable than the cable is rated for, the cable will heat up. This could ignite flammable material close to the cable. If an electrical wire does start a fire, it is important to use the"},{"term":"Fuses","def":"An electrical device must be fitted with a fuse appropriate to its maximum current, such as 3A, 5A, or 13A. A fuse blows if there is a problem with the electrical supply, breaking the circuit to the power source. If the fuse fitted is rated too low, it will blow too easily; if the rating is too"},{"term":"Which scripting use case is listed?","def":"Automated backups"}]}};
